INSERT INTO `flashcards` (`id`, `deck_id`, `front`, `back`, `front_body`, `back_body`, `hint`, `tags`, `difficulty`, `source`, `position`, `created_at`) VALUES
('0a72816b-1b54-4c95-960a-0d420869b436','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Write the de Broglie wavelength relation for matter waves.','$\\lambda = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{h}{mv}$',NULL,NULL,'Connects the wave property ($\\lambda$) with the particle property ($p$).','["physics", "dual-nature", "de-broglie", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6646028e-13d8-4d77-bb84-ba02acdf9fc1','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Express the de Broglie wavelength ($\\lambda$) of a particle in terms of its kinetic energy ($K$).','$\\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mK}}$',NULL,NULL,'Substitute $p = \\sqrt{2mK}$ into the de Broglie relation.','["physics", "dual-nature", "de-broglie"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4a572a78-92e4-4909-a77e-69f002d1b2c6','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What is the Work Function ($\\Phi_0$) of a metal?','The minimum energy required by an electron to just escape from the metal surface.',NULL,NULL,'It is measured in electron volts (eV) and depends on the nature of the metal.','["physics", "dual-nature", "class-12", "definitions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('35507d2f-8055-4473-b2b2-eab13057d741','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What is the value of 1 electron volt (1 eV) in Joules?','$1 \\text{ eV} = 1.602 \\times 10^{-19} \\text{ J}$',NULL,NULL,'Kinetic energy gained by an electron accelerated through a 1V potential.','["physics", "dual-nature", "units"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8d672c89-1e46-4cb1-93df-04364f8a3c48','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What was Hertz\'s observation regarding the photoelectric effect?','He observed that high-voltage sparks across the detector loop were enhanced when the emitter plate was illuminated by ultraviolet (UV) light.',NULL,NULL,'Discovered during his experiments on electromagnetic waves.','["physics", "dual-nature", "history"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3e5b67df-ff3b-4d43-bdf6-f73498f67fe4','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Define Threshold Frequency ($\\nu_0$).','The minimum frequency of incident radiation below which photoelectric emission is not possible, regardless of the light\'s intensity.',NULL,NULL,'Corresponds to the work function: $\\Phi_0 = h\\nu_0$.','["physics", "dual-nature", "definitions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2520fc3f-0e0e-4100-a0b6-fe4b19e0b2f3','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','How does photoelectric current vary with the intensity of incident light?','Photoelectric current is directly proportional to the intensity of incident light (provided $\\nu > \\nu_0$).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 120\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"180\\" y2=\\"100\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"10\\" x2=\\"30\\" y2=\\"100\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"150\\" y2=\\"20\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><text x=\\"70\\" y=\\"115\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Intensity</text><text x=\\"5\\" y=\\"60\\" font-size=\\"10\\" transform=\\"rotate(-90, 15, 60)\\">Photocurrent</text></svg>"}]}','More intensity = more photons = more electrons emitted.','["physics", "dual-nature", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e8f1e096-51ea-4d3a-a77c-befa91a12304','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What is Stopping Potential ($V_0$)?','The minimum negative (retarding) potential given to the anode with respect to the cathode for which the photoelectric current becomes zero.',NULL,NULL,'It measures the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons.','["physics", "dual-nature", "definitions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d83daba5-ed4f-4107-8386-6487939aa17c','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','How does the stopping potential relate to the maximum kinetic energy ($K_{max}$) of emitted electrons?','$K_{max} = eV_0$',NULL,NULL,'Energy conservation: kinetic energy is completely converted to potential energy.','["physics", "dual-nature", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f076b3dd-b4cb-40b6-a67c-877dd6a0223e','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Does stopping potential depend on the intensity of incident light?','No, stopping potential is independent of intensity. It depends ONLY on the frequency of incident light and the nature of the material.',NULL,NULL,'Intensity changes the number of electrons, not their maximum energy.','["physics", "dual-nature", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('72521bbc-0934-437a-b033-d3747797cbb4','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What is the time lag in photoelectric emission?','Photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process. The time lag is less than $10^{-9}$ seconds.',NULL,NULL,'This was a major failure of the classical wave theory of light.','["physics", "dual-nature", "facts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e298f80c-df68-4b29-aaca-cf9e681e3483','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','State Einstein\'s Photoelectric Equation.','$K_{max} = h\\nu - \\Phi_0$',NULL,NULL,'Energy of incident photon = Max Kinetic Energy + Work Function.','["physics", "dual-nature", "formulas", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0a0fd7fe-5971-40c4-93a2-77272cc9bdcb','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','In the photon picture of light, what happens to the energy ($E$) and momentum ($p$) of a photon when it travels through different media?','Its energy ($E = h\\nu$) and momentum ($p = h/\\lambda$) remain constant, because frequency ($\\nu$) does not change when passing from one medium to another.',NULL,NULL,'Velocity and wavelength change, but frequency is a property of the source.','["physics", "dual-nature", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('71328e88-d721-4af6-bc34-22d2bb0e8c8a','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Write the formula for the de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through a potential difference $V$.','$\\lambda = \\frac{1.227}{\\sqrt{V}} \\text{ nm}$',NULL,NULL,'Or $\\frac{12.27}{\\sqrt{V}} \\text{ \\AA}$. Derived from $K = eV$.','["physics", "dual-nature", "de-broglie", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c58486dc-ca8d-4394-8e83-e4e6ad2d448d','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','If an alpha particle and a proton are accelerated through the same potential difference $V$, which will have the shorter de Broglie wavelength?','The alpha particle.',NULL,NULL,'$\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{mq}}$. Alpha has 4x the mass and 2x the charge of a proton.','["physics", "dual-nature", "comparisons"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3fb215b8-0d6a-49bd-8d2b-dbc7dbcc6f85','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','Do all photoelectrons emitted from a metal surface have the same kinetic energy?','No. They are emitted with kinetic energies ranging from zero to a maximum value ($K_{max}$).',NULL,NULL,'Electrons deeper inside the metal lose energy in collisions before escaping.','["physics", "dual-nature", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ec52ba57-1af4-4dac-b51e-4cd9b7c334eb','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What did the Davisson-Germer experiment conclusively prove?','It verified the wave nature of moving electrons (matter waves) by demonstrating electron diffraction.',NULL,NULL,'Nickel crystal was used as a diffraction grating.','["physics", "dual-nature", "experiments"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('214ebc4d-2d0a-4fbf-a4fd-7d280afc1464','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','According to Einstein\'s quantum theory, what does the \'intensity\' of light mean physically?','Intensity is determined by the number of photons crossing a unit area per unit time, NOT the energy of individual photons.',NULL,NULL,'More intensity = more photons. Higher frequency = higher energy per photon.','["physics", "dual-nature", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('aa5bec49-85f4-41f1-9014-e2f7b98b6a00','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What does the slope of the graph between Stopping Potential ($V_0$) and Frequency ($\\nu$) represent?','The slope is $h/e$ (Planck\'s constant divided by electron charge).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The slope is $h/e$ (Planck\'s constant divided by electron charge)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"529\\" height=\\"273\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 529 273\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"529\\" height=\\"273\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','From $eV_0 = h\\nu - \\Phi_0$, rearranging gives $V_0 = (h/e)\\nu - (\\Phi_0/e)$.','["physics", "dual-nature", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929');
