(12110,44,880,'paragraph',NULL,'d','[{"s": "d", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(536,5,58,'paragraph',NULL,'As you notice in Figure 9.1, the heterocyclic compounds in nucleic acids are the nitrogenous bases named adenine, guanine, uracil, cytosine, and thymine. Adenine and Guanine are substituted purines while the rest are substituted pyrimidines. The skeletal heterocyclic ring is called as purine and pyrimidine respectively. The sugar found in polynucleotides is either ribose (a monosaccharide pentose) or 2’ deoxyribose. A nucleic acid containing deoxyribose is called deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) while that which contains ribose is called ribonucleic acid (RNA).','[{"s": "As you notice in Figure 9.1, the heterocyclic compounds in nucleic acids are the nitrogenous bases named adenine, guanine, uracil, cytosine, and thymine.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Adenine and Guanine are substituted purines while the rest are substituted pyrimidines.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The skeletal heterocyclic ring is called as purine and pyrimidine respectively.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The sugar found in polynucleotides is either ribose (a monosaccharide pentose) or 2’ deoxyribose.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A nucleic acid containing deoxyribose is called deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) while that which contains ribose is called ribonucleic acid (RNA).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["91319", "91320", "91328", "91331", "91356"]',NULL),
(537,5,59,'heading',4,'9.7 Structure of Proteins',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(539,5,61,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Biomolecules-image17.png'),
(540,5,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.3 Various levels of Protein Structure','[{"s": "Figure 9.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Various levels of Protein Structure", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(541,5,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Biomolecules-image16.png'),
(542,5,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.4 Cartoon showing : (a) A secondary structure and (b) A tertiary structure of proteins','[{"s": "Figure 9.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cartoon showing : (a) A secondary structure and (b) A tertiary structure of proteins", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(543,5,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Some proteins are an assembly of more than one polypeptide or subunits. The manner in which these individual folded polypeptides or subunits are arranged with respect to each other (e.g. linear string of spheres, spheres arranged one upon each other in the form of a cube or plate etc.) is the architecture of a protein otherwise called the quaternary structure of a protein. Adult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits. Two of these are identical to each other. Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin (Hb).','[{"s": "Some proteins are an assembly of more than one polypeptide or subunits. The manner in which these individual folded polypeptides or subunits are arranged with respect to each other (e.g. linear string of spheres, spheres arranged one upon each other in the form of a cube or plate etc.) is the architecture of a protein otherwise called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "quaternary structure", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a protein.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Adult human haemoglobin consists of 4 subunits. Two of these are identical to each other. Hence, two subunits of α type and two subunits of β type together constitute the human haemoglobin (Hb).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["91344"]',NULL),
(544,5,66,'heading',4,'9.8 Nature of Bond Linking Monomers in a Polymer',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(545,5,67,'paragraph',NULL,'In a polypeptide or a protein, amino acids are linked by a peptide bond which is formed when the carboxyl (-COOH) group of one amino acid reacts with the amino (-NH 2 ) group of the next amino acid with the elimination of a water moiety (the process is called dehydration). In a polysaccharide the individual monosaccharides are linked by a glycosidic bond. This bond is also formed by dehydration. This bond is formed between two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides. In a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide. The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond. As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond (Figure 9.5).','[{"s": "In a polypeptide or a protein, amino acids are linked by a peptide bond which is formed when the carboxyl (-COOH) group of one amino acid reacts with the amino (-NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") group of the next amino acid with the elimination of a water moiety (the process is called dehydration). In a polysaccharide the individual monosaccharides are linked by a glycosidic bond. This bond is also formed by dehydration. This bond is formed between two carbon atoms of two adjacent monosaccharides. In", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "a nucleic acid a phosphate moiety links the 3’-carbon of one sugar of one nucleotide to the 5’-carbon of the sugar of the succeeding nucleotide. The bond between the phosphate and hydroxyl group of sugar is an ester bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As there is one such ester bond on either side, it is called phosphodiester bond (Figure 9.5).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["91208", "91231", "91347"]',NULL),
