(813,8,9,'heading',4,'18.1 Blood',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(814,8,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Blood is a special connective tissue consisting of a fluid matrix, plasma, and formed elements.','[{"s": "Blood is a special connective tissue consisting of a fluid matrix, plasma, and formed elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(815,8,11,'heading',4,'18.1.1 Plasma',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(816,8,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Plasma is a straw coloured, viscous fluid constituting nearly 55 per cent of the blood . 90-92 per cent of plasma is water and proteins contribute 6-8 per cent of it. Fibrinogen, globulins and albumins are the major proteins. Fibrinogens are needed for clotting or coagulation of blood. Globulins primarily are involved in defense mechanisms of the body and the albumins help in osmotic balance. Plasma also contains small amounts of minerals like Na + , Ca ++ , Mg ++ , HCO 3 – , Cl – , etc. Glucose, amino acids, lipids, etc., are also present in the plasma as they are always in transit in the body. Factors for coagulation or clotting of blood are also present in the plasma in an inactive form. Plasma without the clotting factors is called serum.','[{"s": "Plasma is a straw coloured, viscous fluid constituting nearly 55 per cent of the blood", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "90-92 per cent of plasma is water and proteins contribute 6-8 per cent of it.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fibrinogen, globulins and albumins are the major proteins.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fibrinogens are needed for clotting or coagulation of blood. Globulins primarily are involved in defense mechanisms of the body and the albumins help in osmotic balance.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Plasma also contains small amounts of minerals like Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "++", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "++", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", HCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc. Glucose, amino acids, lipids, etc., are also present in the plasma as they are always in transit in the body. Factors for coagulation or clotting of blood are also present in the plasma in an inactive form.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Plasma without the clotting factors is called serum.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181506", "181605", "181642", "181721"]',NULL),
(817,8,13,'heading',4,'18.1.2 Formed Elements',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(818,8,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Erythrocytes, leucocytes and platelets are collectively called formed elements (Figure 18.1) and they constitute nearly 45 per cent of the blood.','[{"s": "Erythrocytes, leucocytes and platelets are collectively called formed elements (Figure 18.1) and they constitute nearly 45 per cent of the blood.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181642"]',NULL),
(819,8,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Erythrocytes or red blood cells (RBC) are the most abundant of all the cells in blood. A healthy adult man has, on an average, 5 millions to 5.5 millions of RBCs mm –3 of blood. RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow in the adults. RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most of the mammals and are biconcave in shape. They have a red coloured, iron containing complex protein called haemoglobin, hence the colour and name of these cells. A healthy individual has 12-16 gms of haemoglobin in every 100 ml of blood. These molecules play a significant role in transport of respiratory gases. RBCs have an average life span of 120 days after which they are destroyed in the spleen (graveyard of RBCs).','[{"s": "Erythrocytes or red blood cells (RBC) are the most abundant of all the cells in blood. A healthy adult man has, on an average, 5 millions to 5.5 millions of RBCs mm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of blood. RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow in the adults.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most of the mammals and are biconcave in shape.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "They have a red coloured, iron containing complex protein called haemoglobin, hence the colour and name of these cells.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A healthy individual has 12-16 gms of haemoglobin in every 100 ml of blood.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These molecules play a significant role in transport of respiratory gases. RBCs have an average life span of 120 days after which they are destroyed in the spleen (graveyard of RBCs).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181503", "181631", "181643", "181722"]',NULL),
(4636,34,700,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 50 Ethers can be prepared by Williamson synthesis in which an alkyl halide is reacted with sodium alkoxide. Di-tert -butyl ether can’t be prepared by this method. Explain,','[{"s": "Q. 50 Ethers can be prepared by Williamson synthesis in which an alkyl halide is reacted with sodium alkoxide. Di-tert -butyl ether can’t be prepared by this method. Explain,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
