(887,9,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanisms of breathing vary among different groups of animals depending mainly on their habitats and levels of organisation. Lower invertebrates like sponges, coelenterates, flatworms, etc., exchange O 2 with CO 2 by simple diffusion over their entire body surface. Earthworms use their moist cuticle and insects have a network of tubes (tracheal tubes) to transport atmospheric air within the body. Special vascularised structures called gills (branchial respiration) are used by most of the aquatic arthropods and molluscs whereas vascularised bags called lungs (pulmonary respiration) are used by the terrestrial forms for the exchange of gases. Among vertebrates, fishes use gills whereas amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals respire through lungs. Amphibians like frogs can respire through their moist skin (cutaneous respiration) also.','[{"s": "Mechanisms of breathing vary among different groups of animals depending mainly on their habitats and levels of organisation. Lower invertebrates like sponges, coelenterates, flatworms, etc., exchange O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by simple diffusion over their entire body surface. Earthworms use their moist cuticle and insects have a network of tubes (tracheal tubes) to transport atmospheric air within the body. Special vascularised structures called gills (branchial respiration) are used by most of the aquatic arthropods and molluscs whereas vascularised bags called lungs (pulmonary respiration) are used by the terrestrial forms for the exchange of gases. Among vertebrates, fishes use gills whereas amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals respire through lungs. Amphibians like frogs can respire through their moist skin (cutaneous respiration) also.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(888,9,11,'heading',4,'17.1.1 Human Respiratory System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(890,9,13,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Breathing_and_Exchange_of_Gases-image5.png'),
(893,9,16,'paragraph',NULL,'The lungs are situated in the thoracic chamber which is anatomically an air-tight chamber. The thoracic chamber is formed dorsally by the vertebral column, ventrally by the sternum, laterally by the ribs and on the lower side by the dome-shaped diaphragm. The anatomical setup of lungs in thorax is such that any change in the volume of the thoracic cavity will be reflected in the lung (pulmonary) cavity. Such an arrangement is essential for breathing, as we cannot directly alter the pulmonary volume.','[{"s": "The lungs are situated in the thoracic chamber which is anatomically an air-tight chamber. The thoracic chamber is formed dorsally by the vertebral column, ventrally by the sternum, laterally by the ribs and on the lower side by the dome-shaped diaphragm. The anatomical setup of lungs in thorax is such that any change in the volume of the thoracic cavity will be reflected in the lung (pulmonary) cavity. Such an arrangement is essential for breathing, as we cannot directly alter the pulmonary volume.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(894,9,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Respiration involves the following steps:','[{"s": "Respiration involves the following steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(895,9,18,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Breathing or pulmonary ventilation by which atmospheric air is drawn in and CO 2 rich alveolar air is released out.','[{"s": "(i) Breathing or pulmonary ventilation by which atmospheric air is drawn in and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rich alveolar air is released out.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(896,9,19,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Diffusion of gases (O 2 and CO 2 ) across alveolar membrane.','[{"s": "(ii) Diffusion of gases (O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") across alveolar membrane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(897,9,20,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Transport of gases by the blood.','[{"s": "(iii) Transport of gases by the blood.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(898,9,21,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Diffusion of O 2 and CO 2 between blood and tissues.','[{"s": "(iv) Diffusion of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between blood and tissues.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4659,34,723,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 58 Match the starting material given in Column I with the products formed by these. (Column II) in the reaction with HI.','[{"s": "Q. 58 Match the starting material given in Column I with the products formed by these. (Column II) in the reaction with HI.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4660,34,724,'paragraph',NULL,'Column I','[{"s": "Column I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(899,9,22,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Utilisation of O 2 by the cells for catabolic reactions and resultant release of CO 2 (cellular respiration as dealt in the Chapter 14).','[{"s": "(v) Utilisation of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by the cells for catabolic reactions and resultant release of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(cellular respiration as dealt in the Chapter 14).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
