(918,9,41,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Breathing_and_Exchange_of_Gases-image9.png'),
(919,9,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Pressure contributed by an individual gas in a mixture of gases is called partial pressure and is represented as pO 2 for oxygen and pCO 2 for carbon dioxide. Partial pressures of these two gases in the atmospheric air and the two sites of diffusion are given in Table 17.1 and in Figure 17.3. The data given in the table clearly indicates a concentration gradient for oxygen from alveoli to blood and blood to tissues.','[{"s": "Pressure contributed by an individual gas in a mixture of gases is called partial pressure and is represented as pO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for oxygen and pCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for carbon dioxide. Partial pressures of these two gases in the atmospheric air and the two sites of diffusion are given in Table 17.1 and in Figure 17.3. The data given in the table clearly indicates a concentration gradient for oxygen from alveoli to blood and blood to tissues.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(921,9,44,'image',NULL,'17.1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Breathing_and_Exchange_of_Gases-image8.png'),
(922,9,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, a gradient is present for CO 2 in the opposite direction, i.e., from tissues to blood and blood to alveoli. As the solubility of CO 2 is 20-25 times higher than that of O 2 , the amount of CO 2 that can diffuse through the diffusion membrane per unit difference in partial pressure is much higher compared to that of O 2 . The diffusion membrane is made up of three major layers (Figure 17.4) namely, the thin squamous epithelium of alveoli, the endothelium of alveolar capillaries and the basement substance (composed of a thin basement membrane supporting the squamous epithelium and the basement membrane surrounding the single layer endothelial cells of capillaries) in between them.','[{"s": "Similarly, a gradient is present for CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the opposite direction, i.e., from tissues to blood and blood to alveoli. As the solubility of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 20-25 times higher than that of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the amount of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that can diffuse through the diffusion membrane per unit difference in partial pressure is much higher compared to that of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The diffusion membrane is made up of three major layers (Figure 17.4) namely, the thin squamous epithelium of alveoli, the endothelium of alveolar capillaries and the basement substance (composed of a thin basement membrane supporting the squamous epithelium and the basement membrane surrounding the single layer endothelial cells of capillaries) in between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(923,9,46,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Breathing_and_Exchange_of_Gases-image2.png'),
(924,9,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 17.3 Diagrammatic representation of exchange of gases at the alveolus and the body tissues with blood and transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide','[{"s": "Figure 17.3 Diagrammatic representation of exchange of gases at the alveolus and the body tissues with blood and transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(925,9,48,'paragraph',NULL,'However, its total thickness is much less than a millimetre. Therefore, all the factors in our body are favourable for diffusion of O2 from alveoli to tissues and that of CO2 from tissues to alveoli.','[{"s": "However, its total thickness is much less than a millimetre. Therefore, all the factors in our body are favourable for diffusion of O2 from alveoli to tissues and that of CO2 from tissues to alveoli.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
