(4717,34,781,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 3 −CH−O−CH 2 −CH 2 −CH 3','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−CH−O−CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4718,34,782,'paragraph',NULL,'I','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1200,12,42,'paragraph',NULL,'The adrenal cortex can be divided into three layers, called zona reticularis (inner layer), zona fasciculata (middle layer) and zona glomerulosa (outer layer). The adrenal cortex secretes many hormones, commonly called as corticoids . The corticoids, which are involved in carbohydrate metabolism are called glucocorticoids. In our body, cortisol is the main glucocorticoid. Corticoids, which regulate the balance of water and electrolytes in our body are called mineralocorticoids. Aldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid in our body.','[{"s": "The adrenal cortex can be divided into three layers, called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "zona reticularis", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(inner layer),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "zona fasciculata", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(middle layer) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "zona glomerulosa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(outer layer).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The adrenal cortex secretes many hormones, commonly called as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "corticoids", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". The corticoids, which are involved in carbohydrate metabolism are called glucocorticoids. In our body, cortisol is the main glucocorticoid.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Corticoids, which regulate the balance of water and electrolytes in our body are called mineralocorticoids. Aldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid in our body.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["221709"]',NULL),
(1202,12,44,'heading',4,'22.2.8 Pancreas',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1203,12,45,'paragraph',NULL,'-cells and β -cells. The α-cells secrete a hormone called glucagon , while the β-cells secrete insulin.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "-cells", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-cells.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "α-cells secrete a hormone called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "glucagon", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", while the β-cells secrete", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "insulin.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221728", "222012"]',NULL),
(1204,12,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Glucagon is a peptide hormone, and plays an important role in maintaining the normal blood glucose levels. Glucagon acts mainly on the liver cells (hepatocytes) and stimulates glycogenolysis resulting in an increased blood sugar ( hyperglycemia ). In addition, this hormone stimulates the process of gluconeogenesis which also contributes to hyperglycemia. Glucagon reduces the cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. Thus, glucagon is a hyperglycemic hormone.','[{"s": "Glucagon is a peptide hormone, and plays an important role in maintaining the normal blood glucose levels.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Glucagon acts mainly on the liver cells (hepatocytes) and stimulates glycogenolysis resulting in an increased blood sugar (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hyperglycemia", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In addition, this hormone stimulates the process of gluconeogenesis which also contributes to hyperglycemia. Glucagon reduces the cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. Thus, glucagon is a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyperglycemic hormone.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["222013"]',NULL),
(4719,34,783,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 3','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4720,34,784,'paragraph',NULL,'Is 2-ethoxy-2-methylethane','[{"s": "Is 2-ethoxy-2-methylethane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4721,34,785,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 64 Assertion (A) Bond angle in ethers is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle.','[{"s": "Q. 64 Assertion (A) Bond angle in ethers is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4722,34,786,'paragraph',NULL,'Reason (R) There is a repulsion between the two bulky (— R) groups.','[{"s": "Reason (R) There is a repulsion between the two bulky (— R) groups.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4723,34,787,'paragraph',NULL,'Reason (R) Lewis acid polarises the bromine molecule.','[{"s": "Reason (R) Lewis acid polarises the bromine molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4724,34,788,'paragraph',NULL,'Reason (R) m and p-nitrophenols exist as associated molecules.','[{"s": "Reason (R) m and p-nitrophenols exist as associated molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1205,12,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Insulin is a peptide hormone, which plays a major role in the regulation of glucose homeostasis. Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes (cells of adipose tissue), and enhances cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. As a result, there is a rapid movement of glucose from blood to hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in decreased blood glucose levels ( hypoglycemia ). Insulin also stimulates conversion of glucose to glycogen ( glycogenesis ) in the target cells. The glucose homeostasis in blood is thus maintained jointly by the two – insulin and glucagons.','[{"s": "Insulin is a peptide hormone, which plays a major role in the regulation of glucose homeostasis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes (cells of adipose tissue), and enhances cellular glucose uptake and utilisation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "As a result, there is a rapid movement of glucose from blood to hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in decreased blood glucose levels (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hypoglycemia", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Insulin also stimulates conversion of glucose to glycogen (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "glycogenesis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in the target cells.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The glucose homeostasis in blood is thus maintained jointly by the two – insulin and glucagons.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221609", "222014"]',NULL),
(1206,12,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Prolonged hyperglycemia leads to a complex disorder called diabetes mellitus which is associated with loss of glucose through urine and formation of harmful compounds known as ketone bodies. Diabetic patients are successfully treated with insulin therapy.','[{"s": "Prolonged hyperglycemia leads to a complex disorder called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "diabetes mellitus", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which is associated with loss of glucose through urine and formation of harmful compounds known as ketone bodies.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Diabetic patients are successfully treated with insulin therapy.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["221602"]',NULL),
(1207,12,49,'heading',4,'22.2.9 Testis',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1208,12,50,'paragraph',NULL,'A pair of testis is present in the scrotal sac (outside abdomen) of male individuals (Figure 22.1). Testis performs dual functions as a primary sex organ as well as an endocrine gland. Testis is composed of seminiferous tubules and stromal or interstitial tissue. The Leydig cells or interstitial cells , which are present in the intertubular spaces produce a group of hormones called androgens mainly testosterone.','[{"s": "A pair of testis is present in the scrotal sac (outside abdomen) of male individuals (Figure 22.1). Testis performs dual functions as a primary sex organ as well as an endocrine gland. Testis is composed of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seminiferous tubules", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stromal or interstitial tissue.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Leydig cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "interstitial cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", which are present in the intertubular spaces produce a group of hormones called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "androgens", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "mainly", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "testosterone.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221725", "221727"]',NULL),
(1209,12,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Androgens regulate the development, maturation and functions of the male accessory sex organs like epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate gland, urethra etc. These hormones stimulate muscular growth, growth of facial and axillary hair, aggressiveness, low pitch of voice etc. Androgens play a major stimulatory role in the process of spermatogenesis (formation of spermatozoa). Androgens act on the central neural system and influence the male sexual behaviour (libido). These hormones produce anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism.','[{"s": "Androgens regulate the development, maturation and functions of the male accessory sex organs like epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate gland, urethra etc. These hormones stimulate muscular growth, growth of facial and axillary hair, aggressiveness, low pitch of voice etc. Androgens play a major stimulatory role in the process of spermatogenesis (formation of spermatozoa). Androgens act on the central neural system and influence the male sexual behaviour (libido). These hormones produce anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1210,12,52,'heading',4,'22.2.10 Ovary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1211,12,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Females have a pair of ovaries located in the abdomen (Figure 22.1). Ovary is the primary female sex organ which produces one ovum during each menstrual cycle. In addition, ovary also produces two groups of steroid hormones called estrogen and progesterone . Ovary is composed of ovarian follicles and stromal tissues. The estrogen is synthesised and secreted mainly by the growing ovarian follicles. After ovulation, the ruptured follicle is converted to a structure called corpus luteum , which secretes mainly progesterone.','[{"s": "Females have a pair of ovaries located in the abdomen (Figure 22.1). Ovary is the primary female sex organ which produces one ovum during each menstrual cycle.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In addition, ovary also produces two groups of steroid hormones called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "estrogen", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "progesterone", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Ovary is composed of ovarian follicles and stromal tissues. The estrogen is synthesised and secreted mainly by the growing ovarian follicles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "After ovulation, the ruptured follicle is converted to a structure called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "corpus luteum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", which secretes mainly", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "progesterone.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221607", "222018"]',NULL),
(1212,12,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Estrogens produce wide ranging actions such as stimulation of growth and activities of female secondary sex organs, development of growing ovarian follicles, appearance of female secondary sex characters (e.g., high pitch of voice, etc.), mammary gland development. Estrogens also regulate female sexual behaviour.','[{"s": "Estrogens produce wide ranging actions such as stimulation of growth and activities of female secondary sex organs, development of growing ovarian follicles, appearance of female secondary sex characters (e.g., high pitch of voice, etc.), mammary gland", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "development.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estrogens also regulate female sexual behaviour.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["221836"]',NULL),
(1213,12,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Progesterone supports pregnancy. Progesterone also acts on the mammary glands and stimulates the formation of alveoli (sac-like structures which store milk) and milk secretion.','[{"s": "Progesterone supports pregnancy. Progesterone also acts on the mammary glands and stimulates the formation of alveoli (sac-like structures which store milk) and milk secretion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1214,12,56,'heading',4,'22.3 Hormones of Heart, Kidney and Gastrointestinal Tract',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12575,44,1345,'paragraph',NULL,'|','[{"s": "|", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12576,44,1346,'paragraph',NULL,'|','[{"s": "|", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1216,12,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The juxtaglomerular cells of kidney produce a peptide hormone called erythropoietin which stimulates erythropoiesis (formation of RBC).','[{"s": "The juxtaglomerular cells of kidney produce a peptide hormone called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "erythropoietin", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which stimulates erythropoiesis (formation of RBC).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221831"]',NULL),
(1218,12,60,'heading',4,'22.4 Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1219,12,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Hormones produce their effects on target tissues by binding to specific proteins called hormone receptors located in the target tissues only. Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are called membrane-bound receptors and the receptors present inside the target cell are called intracellular receptors, mostly nuclear receptors (present in the nucleus). Binding of a hormone to its receptor leads to the formation of a hormone-receptor complex (Figure 22.5 a, b). Each receptor is specific to one hormone only and hence receptors are specific. Hormone-Receptor complex formation leads to certain biochemical changes in the target tissue. Target tissue metabolism and hence physiological functions are regulated by hormones. On the basis of their chemical nature, hormones can be divided into groups :','[{"s": "Hormones produce their effects on target tissues by binding to specific proteins called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hormone receptors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "located in the target tissues only.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are called membrane-bound receptors and the receptors present inside the target cell are called intracellular receptors, mostly nuclear receptors (present in the nucleus).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Binding of a hormone to its receptor leads to the formation of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hormone-receptor complex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Figure 22.5 a, b). Each receptor is specific to one hormone only and hence receptors are specific. Hormone-Receptor complex formation leads to certain biochemical changes in the target tissue. Target tissue metabolism and hence physiological functions are regulated by hormones.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On the basis of their chemical nature, hormones can be divided into groups :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221501", "221502", "221506", "221507", "221508", "221509", "221510"]',NULL),
(1220,12,62,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) peptide, polypeptide, protein hormones (e.g., insulin, glucagon, pituitary hormones, hypothalamic hormones, etc.)','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "peptide, polypeptide, protein hormones", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(e.g., insulin, glucagon, pituitary hormones, hypothalamic hormones, etc.)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221506", "221507", "221508", "221509", "221510"]',NULL),
(1222,12,64,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) iodothyronines (thyroid hormones)','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "iodothyronines", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(thyroid hormones)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221506", "221507", "221508", "221509", "221510"]',NULL),
(1223,12,65,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) amino-acid derivatives (e.g., epinephrine).','[{"s": "(iv)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "amino-acid derivatives", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(e.g., epinephrine).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["221506", "221507", "221508", "221509", "221510"]',NULL),
(1224,12,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Hormones which interact with membrane-bound receptors normally do not enter the target cell, but generate second messengers (e.g., cyclic AMP, IP 3, Ca ++ etc) which in turn regulate cellular metabolism (Figure 22.5a). Hormones which interact with intracellular receptors (e.g., steroid hormones, iodothyronines, etc.) mostly regulate gene expression or chromosome function by the interaction of hormone-receptor complex with the genome. Cumulative biochemical actions result in physiological and developmental effects (Figure 22.5b).','[{"s": "Hormones which interact with membrane-bound receptors normally do not enter the target cell, but generate second messengers (e.g., cyclic AMP, IP", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3, Ca", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "++ etc) which in turn regulate cellular metabolism (Figure 22.5a). Hormones which interact with intracellular receptors (e.g., steroid hormones, iodothyronines, etc.) mostly regulate gene expression or chromosome function by the interaction of hormone-receptor complex with the genome.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cumulative biochemical actions result in physiological and developmental effects (Figure 22.5b).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["221825"]',NULL),
