(1750,18,33,'heading',4,'20.2.2 Mechanism of Muscle Contraction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1761,18,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Axial skeleton comprises 80 bones distributed along the main axis of the body. The skull, vertebral column, sternum and ribs constitute axial skeleton. The skull (Figure 20.6) is composed of two sets of bones – cranial and facial, that totals to 22 bones. Cranial bones are 8 in number. They form the hard protective outer covering, cranium for the brain. The facial region is made up of 14 skeletal elements which form the front part of the skull. A single U-shaped bone called hyoid is present at the base of the buccal cavity and it is also included in the skull. Each middle ear contains three tiny bones – Malleus, Incus and Stapes, collectively called Ear Ossicles. The skull region articulates with the superior region of the vertebral column with the help of two occipital condyles (dicondylic skull).','[{"s": "Axial skeleton comprises 80 bones distributed along the main axis of the body. The skull, vertebral column, sternum and ribs constitute axial skeleton. The skull (Figure 20.6) is composed of two sets of bones – cranial and facial, that totals to 22 bones. Cranial bones are 8 in number. They form the hard protective outer covering, cranium for the brain. The facial region is made up of 14 skeletal elements which form the front part of the skull. A single U-shaped bone called hyoid is present at the base of the buccal cavity and it is also included in the skull. Each middle ear contains three tiny bones – Malleus, Incus and Stapes, collectively called Ear Ossicles. The skull region articulates with the superior region of the vertebral column with the help of two occipital condyles (dicondylic skull).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1762,18,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Locomotion_and_Movement-image4.png'),
(1763,18,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 20.7 Vertebral column (right lateral view)','[{"s": "Figure 20.7 Vertebral column (right lateral view)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1764,18,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Our vertebral column (Figure 20.7) is formed by 26 serially arranged units called vertebrae and is dorsally placed. It extends from the base of the skull and constitutes the main framework of the trunk. Each vertebra has a central hollow portion (neural canal) through which the spinal cord passes. First vertebra is the atlas and it articulates with the occipital condyles. The vertebral column is differentiated into cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (1-fused) and coccygeal (1-fused) regions starting from the skull. The number of cervical vertebrae are seven in almost all mammals including human beings. The vertebral column protects the spinal cord, supports the head and serves as the point of attachment for the ribs and musculature of the back. Sternum is a flat bone on the ventral midline of thorax.','[{"s": "Our vertebral column (Figure 20.7) is formed by 26 serially arranged units called vertebrae and is dorsally placed. It extends from the base of the skull and constitutes the main framework of the trunk. Each vertebra has a central hollow portion (neural canal) through which the spinal cord passes. First vertebra is the atlas and it articulates with the occipital condyles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The vertebral column is differentiated into cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (1-fused) and coccygeal (1-fused) regions starting from the skull.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The number of cervical vertebrae are seven in almost all mammals including human beings. The vertebral column protects the spinal cord, supports the head and serves as the point of attachment for the ribs and musculature of the back.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Sternum is a flat bone on the ventral midline of thorax.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["200639", "200640", "200641", "200642", "200643", "200644", "200654"]',NULL),
(1765,18,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Locomotion_and_Movement-image3.png'),
(1766,18,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 20.8 Ribs and rib cage','[{"s": "Figure 20.8 Ribs and rib cage", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1768,18,51,'paragraph',NULL,'The bones of the limbs alongwith their girdles constitute the appendicular skeleton. Each limb is made of 30 bones. The bones of the hand (fore limb) are humerus, radius and ulna, carpals (wrist bones – 8 in number), metacarpals (palm bones – 5 in number) and phalanges (digits – 14 in number) (Figure 20.9). Femur (thigh bone – the longest bone), tibia and fibula, tarsals (ankle bones – 7 in number), metatarsals (5 in number) and phalanges (digits – 14 in number) are the bones of the legs (hind limb) (Figure 20.10). A cup shaped bone called patella cover the knee ventrally (knee cap).','[{"s": "The bones of the limbs alongwith their girdles constitute the appendicular skeleton.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each limb is made of 30 bones.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The bones of the hand (fore limb) are humerus, radius and ulna, carpals (wrist bones – 8 in number), metacarpals (palm bones – 5 in number) and phalanges (digits – 14 in number) (Figure 20.9). Femur (thigh bone – the longest bone), tibia and fibula, tarsals (ankle bones – 7 in number), metatarsals (5 in number) and phalanges (digits – 14 in number) are the bones of the legs (hind limb) (Figure 20.10). A cup shaped bone called patella cover the knee ventrally (knee cap).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1769,18,52,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Locomotion_and_Movement-image6.png'),
