(6321,37,453,'paragraph',NULL,'At temperature T 1 , equation (4.19) is','[{"s": "At temperature T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", equation (4.19) is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2214,21,93,'paragraph',NULL,'A flower is a modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to floral meristem. Internodes do not elongate and the axis gets condensed. The apex produces different kinds of floral appendages laterally at successive nodes instead of leaves. When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, it is always solitary. The arrangement of flowers on the floral axis is termed as inflorescence. Depending on whether the apex gets developed into a flower or continues to grow, two major types of inflorescences are defined – racemose and cymose. In racemose type of inflorescences the main axis continues to grow, the flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession (Figure 5.11).','[{"s": "A flower is a modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to floral meristem. Internodes do not elongate and the axis gets condensed.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The apex produces different kinds of floral appendages laterally at successive nodes instead of leaves. When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, it is always solitary. The arrangement of flowers on the floral axis is termed as inflorescence. Depending on whether the apex gets developed into a flower or continues to grow, two major types of inflorescences are defined – racemose and cymose. In racemose type of inflorescences the main axis continues to grow, the flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession (Figure 5.11).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["51069"]',NULL),
(2215,21,94,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Morphology_Of_Flowering_Plants-image5.png'),
(2216,21,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.11 Racemose inflorescence','[{"s": "Figure 5.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Racemose inflorescence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2218,21,97,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Morphology_Of_Flowering_Plants-image2.png'),
(2219,21,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.12 Cymose inflorescence','[{"s": "Figure 5.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cymose inflorescence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2220,21,99,'heading',4,'5.5 The Flower',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2221,21,100,'paragraph',NULL,'The flower is the reproductive unit in the angiosperms. It is meant for sexual reproduction. A typical flower has four different kinds of whorls arranged successively on the swollen end of the stalk or pedicel, called thalamus or receptacle . These are calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium. Calyx and corolla are accessory organs, while androecium and gynoecium are reproductive organs. In some flowers like lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct and are termed as perianth. When a flower has both androecium and gynoecium, it is bisexual . A flower having either only stamens or only carpels is unisexual .','[{"s": "The flower is the reproductive unit in the angiosperms. It is meant for sexual reproduction. A typical flower has four different kinds of whorls arranged successively on the swollen end of the stalk or pedicel, called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "thalamus or receptacle", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These are calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium. Calyx and corolla are accessory organs, while androecium and gynoecium are reproductive organs. In some flowers like lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct and are termed as perianth. When a flower has both androecium and gynoecium, it is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bisexual", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A flower having either only stamens or only carpels is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unisexual", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2223,21,102,'paragraph',NULL,'A flower may be trimerous, tetramerous or pentamerous when the floral appendages are in multiple of 3, 4 or 5, respectively. Flowers with bracts-reduced leaf found at the base of the pedicel-are called bracteate and those without bracts, ebracteate .','[{"s": "A flower may be trimerous, tetramerous or pentamerous when the floral appendages are in multiple of 3, 4 or 5, respectively. Flowers with bracts-reduced leaf found at the base of the pedicel-are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bracteate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and those without bracts,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ebracteate", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
