(2347,21,226,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2348,21,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Flowering plants exhibit enormous variation in shape, size, structure, mode of nutrition, life span, habit and habitat. They have well developed root and shoot systems. Root system is either tap root or fibrous. Generally, dicotyledonous plants have tap roots while monocotyledonous plants have fibrous roots. The roots in some plants get modified for storage of food, mechanical support and respiration. The shoot system is differentiated into stem, leaves, flowers and fruits. The morphological features of stems like the presence of nodes and internodes, multicellular hair and positively phototropic nature help to differentiate the stems from roots. Stems also get modified to perform diverse functions such as storage of food, vegetative propagation and protection under different conditions. Leaf is a lateral outgrowth of stem developed exogeneously at the node. These are green in colour to perform the function of photosynthesis. Leaves exhibit marked variations in their shape, size, margin, apex and extent of incisions of leaf blade (lamina). Like other parts of plants, the leaves also get modified into other structures such as tendrils, spines for climbing and protection respectively.','[{"s": "Flowering plants exhibit enormous variation in shape, size, structure, mode of nutrition, life span, habit and habitat. They have well developed root and shoot systems. Root system is either tap root or fibrous. Generally, dicotyledonous plants have tap roots while monocotyledonous plants have fibrous roots. The roots in some plants get modified for storage of food, mechanical support and respiration. The shoot system is differentiated into stem, leaves, flowers and fruits. The morphological features of stems like the presence of nodes and internodes, multicellular hair and positively phototropic nature help to differentiate the stems from roots. Stems also get modified to perform diverse functions such as storage of food, vegetative propagation and protection under different conditions. Leaf is a lateral outgrowth of stem developed exogeneously at the node. These are green in colour to perform the function of photosynthesis. Leaves exhibit marked variations in their shape, size, margin, apex and extent of incisions of leaf blade (lamina). Like other parts of plants, the leaves also get modified into other structures such as tendrils, spines for climbing and protection respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9166,41,698,'paragraph',NULL,'C. MnCl 2 . 4H 2 O','[{"s": "C.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2349,21,228,'paragraph',NULL,'The flower is a modified shoot, meant for sexual reproduction . The flowers are arranged in different types of inflorescences. They exhibit enormous variation in structure, symmetry, position of ovary in relation to other parts, arrangement of petals, sepals, ovules etc. After fertilisation, the ovary is modified into fruits and ovules into seeds. Seeds either may be monocotyledonous or dicotyledonous. They vary in shape, size and period of viability. The floral characteristics form the basis of classification and identification of flowering plants. This can be illustrated through semi-technical descriptions of families. Hence, a flowering plant is described in a definite sequence by using scientific terms. The floral features are represented in the summarised form as floral diagrams and floral formula','[{"s": "The flower is a modified shoot, meant for sexual reproduction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The flowers are arranged in different types of inflorescences. They exhibit enormous variation in structure, symmetry, position of ovary in relation to other parts, arrangement of petals, sepals, ovules etc. After fertilisation, the ovary is modified into fruits and ovules into seeds. Seeds either may be monocotyledonous or dicotyledonous.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "They vary in shape, size and period of viability.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The floral characteristics form the basis of classification and identification of flowering plants. This can be illustrated through semi-technical descriptions of families. Hence, a flowering plant is described in a definite sequence by using scientific terms. The floral features are represented in the summarised form as floral diagrams and floral formula", "t": "text"}]','marked','["51069", "51077"]',NULL),
(2350,22,0,'heading',3,'Chapter 21',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2351,22,1,'heading',1,'Neural Control and Coordination',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2352,22,2,'paragraph',NULL,'As you know, the functions of the organs/organ systems in our body must be coordinated to maintain homeostasis. Coordination is the process through which two or more organs interact and complement the functions of one another. For example, when we do physical exercises, the energy demand is increased for maintaining an increased muscular activity. The supply of oxygen is also increased. The increased supply of oxygen necessitates an increase in the rate of respiration, heart beat and increased blood flow via blood vessels. When physical exercise is stopped, the activities of nerves, lungs, heart and kidney gradually return to their normal conditions. Thus, the functions of muscles, lungs, heart, blood vessels, kidney and other organs are coordinated while performing physical exercises. In our body the neural system and the endocrine system jointly coordinate and integrate all the activities of the organs so that they function in a synchronised fashion.','[{"s": "As you know, the functions of the organs/organ systems in our body must be coordinated to maintain homeostasis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coordination", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the process through which two or more organs interact and complement the functions of one another. For example, when we do physical exercises, the energy demand is increased for maintaining an increased muscular activity. The supply of oxygen is also increased. The increased supply of oxygen necessitates an increase in the rate of respiration, heart beat and increased blood flow via blood vessels. When physical exercise is stopped, the activities of nerves, lungs, heart and kidney gradually return to their normal conditions. Thus, the functions of muscles, lungs, heart, blood vessels, kidney and other organs are coordinated while performing physical exercises. In our body the neural system and the endocrine system jointly coordinate and integrate all the activities of the organs so that they function in a synchronised fashion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2353,22,3,'heading',4,'21.1 Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2354,22,4,'heading',4,'21.2 Human Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2355,22,5,'heading',4,'21.3 Neuron as Structural and Functional Unit of Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2356,22,6,'heading',4,'21.4 Central Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2357,22,7,'heading',4,'21.5 Reflex Action and Reflex Arc',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2358,22,8,'heading',4,'21.6 Sensory Reception and Processing',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2359,22,9,'paragraph',NULL,'The neural system provides an organised network of point-to-point connections for a quick coordination. The endocrine system provides chemical integration through hormones. In this chapter, you will learn about the neural system of human, mechanisms of neural coordination like transmission of nerve impulse, impulse conduction across a synapse and the physiology of reflex action.','[{"s": "The neural system provides an organised network of point-to-point connections for a quick coordination. The endocrine system provides chemical integration through hormones. In this chapter, you will learn about the neural system of human, mechanisms of neural coordination like transmission of nerve impulse, impulse conduction across a synapse and the physiology of reflex action.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2360,22,10,'heading',4,'21.1 Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2361,22,11,'paragraph',NULL,'The neural system of all animals is composed of highly specialised cells called neurons which can detect, receive and transmit different kinds of stimuli.','[{"s": "The neural system of all animals is composed of highly specialised cells called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "neurons", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which can detect, receive and transmit different kinds of stimuli.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2362,22,12,'paragraph',NULL,'The neural organisation is very simple in lower invertebrates. For example, in Hydra it is composed of a network of neurons. The neural system is better organised in insects, where a brain is present along with a number of ganglia and neural tissues. The vertebrates have a more developed neural system.','[{"s": "The neural organisation is very simple in lower invertebrates. For example, in Hydra it is composed of a network of neurons. The neural system is better organised in insects, where a brain is present along with a number of ganglia and neural tissues. The vertebrates have a more developed neural system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2363,22,13,'heading',4,'21.2 Human Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2364,22,14,'paragraph',NULL,'The human neural system is divided into two parts :','[{"s": "The human neural system is divided into two parts :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2365,22,15,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the central neural system (CNS)','[{"s": "(i) the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "central neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(CNS)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2366,22,16,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the peripheral neural system (PNS)','[{"s": "(ii) the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "peripheral neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(PNS)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6328,37,460,'paragraph',NULL,'ln k 2 – ln k 1 = Chemical_Kinetics-image94.png – Chemical_Kinetics-image53.png','[{"s": "ln k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ln k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_Kinetics-image94.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_Kinetics-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6329,37,461,'image',NULL,'4a9',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image1.png'),
(2367,22,17,'paragraph',NULL,'The CNS includes the brain and the spinal cord and is the site of information processing and control. The PNS comprises of all the nerves of the body associated with the CNS (brain and spinal cord). The nerve fibres of the PNS are of two types :','[{"s": "The CNS includes the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "brain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spinal cord", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is the site of information processing and control. The PNS comprises of all the nerves of the body associated with the CNS (brain and spinal cord). The nerve fibres of the PNS are of two types :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2368,22,18,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) afferent fibres','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "afferent fibres", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2369,22,19,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) efferent fibres','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "efferent fibres", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2370,22,20,'paragraph',NULL,'The afferent nerve fibres transmit impulses from tissues/organs to the CNS and the efferent fibres transmit regulatory impulses from the CNS to the concerned peripheral tissues/organs.','[{"s": "The afferent nerve fibres transmit impulses from tissues/organs to the CNS and the efferent fibres transmit regulatory impulses from the CNS to the concerned peripheral tissues/organs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2371,22,21,'paragraph',NULL,'The PNS is divided into two divisions called somatic neural system and autonomic neural system . The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles while the autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and smooth muscles of the body. The autonomic neural system is further classified into sympathetic neural system and parasympathetic neural system .','[{"s": "The PNS is divided into two divisions called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "somatic neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "autonomic neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles while the autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and smooth muscles of the body. The autonomic neural system is further classified into", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sympathetic neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parasympathetic neural system", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2372,22,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Visceral nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that comprises the whole complex of nerves, fibres, ganglia, and plexuses by which impulses travel from the central nervous system to the viscera and from the viscera to the central nervous system.','[{"s": "Visceral nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that comprises the whole complex of nerves, fibres, ganglia, and plexuses by which impulses travel from the central nervous system to the viscera and from the viscera to the central nervous system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2373,22,23,'heading',4,'21.3 Neuron as Structural and Functional Unit of Neural System',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
