(12601,44,1371,'paragraph',NULL,'i','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2454,23,15,'paragraph',NULL,'He was honoured with the Fellowships of the Indian National Science Academy and World Academy of Arts and Science, and the prestigious Sanjay Gandhi Award in Environment and Ecology. Due to his efforts, the Government of India established the National Committee for Environmental Planning and Coordination (1972) which, in later years, paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984).','[{"s": "He was honoured with the Fellowships of the Indian National Science Academy and World Academy of Arts and Science, and the prestigious Sanjay Gandhi Award in Environment and Ecology. Due to his efforts, the Government of India established the National Committee for Environmental Planning and Coordination (1972) which, in later years, paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2455,23,16,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Organisms_and_Population-image6.png'),
(2456,23,17,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Organisms_and_Population-image5.png'),
(2457,23,18,'heading',4,'Chapter 13',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2458,23,19,'heading',2,'Organisms and Populations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2459,23,20,'paragraph',NULL,'13.1 Organism and Its Environment','[{"s": "13.1 Organism and Its Environment", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2460,23,21,'paragraph',NULL,'13.2 Populations','[{"s": "13.2 Populations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2461,23,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Our living world is fascinatingly diverse and amazingly complex. We can try to understand its complexity by investigating processes at various levels of biological organisation–macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes. At any level of biological organisation we can ask two types of questions – for example, when we hear the bulbul singing early morning in the garden, we may ask – ‘How does the bird sing?’ Or, ‘Why does the bird sing ?’ The ‘how-type’ questions seek the mechanism behind the process while the ‘why-type’ questions seek the significance of the process. For the first question in our example, the answer might be in terms of the operation of the voice box and the vibrating bone in the bird, whereas for the second question the answer may lie in the bird’s need to communicate with its mate during breeding season. When you observe nature around you with a scientific frame of mind you will certainly come up with many interesting questions of both types - Why are night-blooming flowers generally white? How does the bee know which flower has nectar? Why does cactus have so many thorns? How does the chick spures recognise her own mother?, and so on.','[{"s": "Our living world is fascinatingly diverse and amazingly complex. We can try to understand its complexity by investigating processes at various levels of biological organisation–macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes. At any level of biological organisation we can ask two types of questions – for example, when we hear the bulbul singing early morning in the garden, we may ask – ‘How does the bird sing?’ Or, ‘Why does the bird sing ?’ The ‘how-type’ questions seek the mechanism behind the process while the ‘why-type’ questions seek the significance of the process. For the first question in our example, the answer might be in terms of the operation of the voice box and the vibrating bone in the bird, whereas for the second question the answer may lie in the bird’s need to communicate with its mate during breeding season. When you observe nature around you with a scientific frame of mind you will certainly come up with many interesting questions of both types - Why are night-blooming flowers generally white? How does the bee know which flower has nectar? Why does cactus have so many thorns? How does the chick spures recognise her own mother?, and so on.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2462,23,23,'paragraph',NULL,'You have already learnt in previous classes that Ecology is a subject which studies the interactions among organisms and between the organism and its physical (abiotic) environment.','[{"s": "You have already learnt in previous classes that Ecology is a subject which studies the interactions among organisms and between the organism and its physical (abiotic) environment.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2463,23,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Ecology is basically concerned with four levels of biological organisation – organisms, populations, communities and biomes. In this chapter we explore ecology at organismic and population levels.','[{"s": "Ecology is basically concerned with four levels of biological organisation – organisms, populations, communities and biomes. In this chapter we explore ecology at organismic and population levels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2464,23,25,'heading',2,'13.1 Organism and Its Environment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2465,23,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Ecology at the organismic level is essentially physiological ecology which tries to understand how different organisms are adapted to their environments in terms of not only survival but also reproduction. You may have learnt in earlier classes how the rotation of our planet around the Sun and the tilt of its axis cause annual variations in the intensity and duration of temperature, resulting in distinct seasons. These variations together with annual variation in precipitation (remember precipitation includes both rain and snow) account for the formation of major biomes such as desert, rain forest and tundra (Figure 13.1).','[{"s": "Ecology at the organismic level is essentially physiological ecology which tries to understand how different organisms are adapted to their environments in terms of not only survival but also reproduction. You may have learnt in earlier classes how the rotation of our planet around the Sun and the tilt of its axis cause annual variations in the intensity and duration of temperature, resulting in distinct seasons. These variations together with annual variation in precipitation (remember precipitation includes both rain and snow) account for the formation of major biomes such as desert, rain forest and tundra (Figure 13.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2466,23,27,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Organisms_and_Population-image15.png'),
(2467,23,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.1 Biome distribution with respect to annual temperature and precipitation','[{"s": "Figure 13.1 Biome distribution with respect to annual temperature and precipitation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2468,23,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of habitats. Major biomes of India are shown in Figure 13.2. On planet Earth, life exists not just in a few favourable habitats but even in extreme and harsh habitats – scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams, permafrost (snow laden) polar regions, high mountain tops, thermal springs, and stinking compost pits, to name a few. Even our intestine is a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes.','[{"s": "Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of habitats. Major biomes of India are shown in Figure 13.2. On planet Earth, life exists not just in a few favourable habitats but even in extreme and harsh habitats – scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams, permafrost (snow laden) polar regions, high mountain tops, thermal springs, and stinking compost pits, to name a few. Even our intestine is a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2469,23,30,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Organisms_and_Population-image8.png'),
(2470,23,31,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2471,23,32,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Organisms_and_Population-image14.png'),
