(3097,27,20,'heading',2,'5.1 Mendel\'s Laws of Inheritance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3098,27,21,'heading',2,'5.2 Inheritance of One Gene',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3099,27,22,'heading',2,'5.3 Inheritance of Two Genes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3100,27,23,'heading',2,'5.4 Sex Determination',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3101,27,24,'heading',2,'5.5 Mutation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3102,27,25,'heading',2,'5.6 Genetic Disorders',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3103,27,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Have you ever wondered why an elephant always gives birth only to a baby elephant and not some other animal? Or why a mango seed forms only a mango plant and not any other plant?','[{"s": "Have you ever wondered why an elephant always gives birth only to a baby elephant and not some other animal? Or why a mango seed forms only a mango plant and not any other plant?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3104,27,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Given that they do, are the offspring identical to their parents? Or do they show differences in some of their characteristics? Have you ever wondered why siblings sometimes look so similar to each other? Or sometimes even so different?','[{"s": "Given that they do, are the offspring identical to their parents? Or do they show differences in some of their characteristics? Have you ever wondered why siblings sometimes look so similar to each other? Or sometimes even so different?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3105,27,28,'paragraph',NULL,'These and several related questions are dealt with, scientifically, in a branch of biology known as Genetics. This subject deals with the inheritance, as well as the variation of characters from parents to offspring. Inheritance is the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny; it is the basis of heredity. Variation is the degree by which progeny differ from their parents.','[{"s": "These and several related questions are dealt with, scientifically, in a branch of biology known as Genetics. This subject deals with the inheritance, as well as the variation of characters from parents to offspring. Inheritance is the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny; it is the basis of heredity. Variation is the degree by which progeny differ from their parents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3106,27,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Humans knew from as early as 8000-1000 B.C. that one of the causes of variation was hidden in sexual reproduction. They exploited the variations that were naturally present in the wild populations of plants and animals to selectively breed and select for organisms that possessed desirable characters. For example, through artificial selection and domestication from ancestral wild cows, we have well-known Indian breeds, e.g., Sahiwal cows in Punjab. We must, however, recognise that though our ancestors knew about the inheritance of characters and variation, they had very little idea about the scientific basis of these phenomena.','[{"s": "Humans knew from as early as 8000-1000 B.C. that one of the causes of variation was hidden in sexual reproduction. They exploited the variations that were naturally present in the wild populations of plants and animals to selectively breed and select for organisms that possessed desirable characters. For example, through artificial selection and domestication from ancestral wild cows, we have well-known Indian breeds, e.g., Sahiwal cows in Punjab. We must, however, recognise that though our ancestors knew about the inheritance of characters and variation, they had very little idea about the scientific basis of these phenomena.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3107,27,30,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image21.png'),
(3109,27,32,'heading',2,'5.1 Mendel’s Laws of Inheritance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3110,27,33,'paragraph',NULL,'It was during the mid-nineteenth century that headway was made in the understanding of inheritance. Gregor Mendel, conducted hybridisation experiments on garden peas for seven years (1856-1863) and proposed the laws of inheritance in living organisms. During Mendel’s investigations into inheritance patterns it was for the first time that statistical analysis and mathematical logic were applied to problems in biology. His experiments had a large sampling size, which gave greater credibility to the data that he collected. Also, the confirmation of his inferences from experiments on successive generations of his test plants, proved that his results pointed to general rules of inheritance rather than being unsubstantiated ideas. Mendel investigated characters in the garden pea plant that were manifested as two opposing traits, e.g., tall or dwarf plants, yellow or green seeds. This allowed him to set up a basic framework of rules governing inheritance, which was expanded on by later scientists to account for all the diverse natural observations and the complexity inherent in them.','[{"s": "It was during the mid-nineteenth century that headway was made in the understanding of inheritance.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gregor Mendel, conducted hybridisation experiments on garden peas for seven years (1856-1863) and proposed the laws of inheritance in living organisms.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "During Mendel’s investigations into inheritance patterns it was for the first time that statistical analysis and mathematical logic were applied to problems in biology. His experiments had a large sampling size, which gave greater credibility to the data that he collected. Also, the confirmation of his inferences from experiments on successive generations of his test plants, proved that his results pointed to general rules of inheritance rather than being unsubstantiated ideas. Mendel investigated characters in the garden pea plant that were manifested as two opposing traits, e.g., tall or dwarf plants, yellow or green seeds. This allowed him to set up a basic framework of rules governing inheritance, which was expanded on by later scientists to account for all the diverse natural observations and the complexity inherent in them.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271724"]',NULL),
(3111,27,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Mendel conducted such artificial pollination/cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines. A true-breeding line is one that, having undergone continuous self-pollination, shows the stable trait inheritance and expression for several generations. Mendel selected 14 true-breeding pea plant varieties, as pairs which were similar except for one character with contrasting traits. Some of the contrasting traits selected were smooth or wrinkled seeds, yellow or green seeds, inflated (full) or constricted green or yellow pods and tall or dwarf plants (Figure 5.1, Table 5.1).','[{"s": "Mendel conducted such artificial pollination/cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A true-breeding line is one that, having undergone continuous self-pollination, shows the stable trait inheritance and expression for several generations.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Mendel selected 14 true-breeding pea plant varieties, as pairs which were similar except for one character with contrasting traits.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Some of the contrasting traits selected were smooth or wrinkled seeds, yellow or green seeds, inflated (full) or constricted green or yellow pods and tall or dwarf plants (Figure 5.1, Table 5.1).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271712", "271745"]',NULL),
(3112,27,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 5.1: Contrasting Traits Studied by Mendel in Pea','[{"s": "Table 5.1: Contrasting Traits Studied by Mendel in Pea", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271746"]',NULL),
(3113,27,36,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image13.png'),
(3114,27,37,'heading',2,'5.2 Inheritance of One Gene',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3115,27,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us take the example of one such hybridisation experiment carried out by Mendel where he crossed tall and dwarf pea plants to study the inheritance of one gene (Figure 5.2). He collected the seeds produced as a result of this cross and grew them to generate plants of the first hybrid generation. This generation is also called the Filial 1 progeny or the F 1 . Mendel observed that all the F1 progeny plants were tall, like one of its parents; none were dwarf (Figure 5.3). He made similar observations for the other pairs of traits – he found that the F 1 always resembled either one of the parents, and that the trait of the other parent was not seen in them.','[{"s": "Let us take the example of one such hybridisation experiment carried out by Mendel where he crossed tall and dwarf pea plants to study the inheritance of one gene (Figure 5.2). He collected the seeds produced as a result of this cross and grew them to generate plants of the first hybrid generation. This generation is also called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Filial", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "progeny", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Mendel observed that all the F1 progeny plants were tall, like one of its parents; none were dwarf (Figure 5.3). He made similar observations for the other pairs of traits – he found that the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "always resembled either one of the parents, and that the trait of the other parent was not seen in them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3116,27,39,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image19.png'),
(3117,27,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.2 Steps in making a cross in pea','[{"s": "Figure 5.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Steps in making a cross in pea", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3118,27,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Mendel then self-pollinated the tall F 1 plants and to his surprise found that in the Filial2 generation some of the offspring were ‘dwarf’; the character that was not seen in the F 1 2 plants while 3/4th of the F 2 plants were tall. The tall and dwarf traits were identical to their parental type and did not show any blending, that is all the offspring were either tall or dwarf, none were of in-between height (Figure 5.3).','[{"s": "Mendel then self-pollinated the tall F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants and to his surprise found that in the Filial2 generation some of the offspring were ‘dwarf’; the character that was not seen in the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants while 3/4th of the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants were tall. The tall and dwarf traits were identical to their parental type and did not show any blending, that is all the offspring were either tall or dwarf, none were of in-between height (Figure 5.3).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3119,27,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Similar results were obtained with the other traits that he studied: only one of the parental traits was expressed in the F 1 generation while at the F 2 stage both the traits were expressed in the proportion 3:1. The contrasting traits did not show any blending at either F 1 or F 2 stage.','[{"s": "Similar results were obtained with the other traits that he studied: only one of the parental traits was expressed in the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "generation while at the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stage both the traits were expressed in the proportion 3:1. The contrasting traits did not show any blending at either F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stage.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3121,27,44,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image14.png'),
(3122,27,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.3 Diagrammatic representation of monohybrid cross','[{"s": "Figure 5.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Diagrammatic representation of monohybrid cross", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3123,27,46,'paragraph',NULL,'If we use alphabetical symbols for each gene, then the capital letter is used for the trait expressed at the F 1 stage and the small alphabet for the other trait. For example, in case of the character of height, T is used for the Tall trait and t for the ‘dwarf’, and T and t are alleles of each other. Hence, in plants the pair of alleles for height would be TT , Tt or tt . Mendel also proposed that in a true breeding, tall or dwarf pea variety the allelic pair of genes for height are identical or homozygous , TT and tt , respectively. TT and tt are called the genotype of the plant while the descriptive terms tall and dwarf are the phenotype . What then would be the phenotype of a plant that had a genotype Tt ?','[{"s": "If we use alphabetical symbols for each gene, then the capital letter is used for the trait expressed at the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stage and the small alphabet for the other trait. For example, in case of the character of height, T is used for the Tall trait and t for the ‘dwarf’, and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are alleles of each other. Hence, in plants the pair of alleles for height would be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Mendel also proposed that in a true breeding, tall or dwarf pea variety the allelic pair of genes for height are identical or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homozygous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and tt are called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the plant while the descriptive terms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tall", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dwarf", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "phenotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What then would be the phenotype of a plant that had a genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12616,44,1386,'paragraph',NULL,'|','[{"s": "|", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3125,27,48,'paragraph',NULL,'It is convenient (and logical) to use the capital and lower case of an alphabetical symbol to remember this concept of dominance and recessiveness. (Do not use T for tall and d for dwarf because you will find it difficult to remember whether T and d are alleles of the same gene/character or not). Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes TT and tt or can be dissimilar as in the case of the heterozygote Tt . Since the Tt plant is heterozygous for genes controlling one character (height), it is a monohybrid and the cross between TT and tt is a monohybrid cross.','[{"s": "It is convenient (and logical) to use the capital and lower case of an alphabetical symbol to remember this concept of dominance and recessiveness. (Do not use", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for tall and d for dwarf because you will find it difficult to remember whether", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are alleles of the same gene/character or not). Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or can be dissimilar as in the case of the heterozygote", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since the Tt plant is heterozygous for genes controlling one character (height), it is a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "monohybrid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the cross between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and tt is a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "monohybrid cross.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3126,27,49,'paragraph',NULL,'From the observation that the recessive parental trait is expressed without any blending in the F 2 generation, we can infer that, when the tall and dwarf plant produce gametes, by the process of meiosis, the alleles of the parental pair separate or segregate from each other and only one allele is transmitted to a gamete. This segregation of alleles is a random process and so there is a 50 per cent chance of a gamete containing either allele, as has been verified by the results of the crossings. In this way the gametes of the tall TT plants have the allele T and the gametes of the dwarf tt plants have the allele t . During fertilisation the two alleles, T from one parent say, through the pollen, and t from the other parent, then through the egg, are united to produce zygotes that have one T allele and one t allele. In other words the hybrids have Tt . Since these hybrids contain alleles which express contrasting traits, the plants are heterozygous . The production of gametes by the parents, the formation of the zygotes, the F 1 and F 2 plants can be understood from a diagram called Punnett Square as shown in Figure 5.4. It was developed by a British geneticist, Reginald C. Punnett. It is a graphical representation to calculate the probability of all possible genotypes of offspring in a genetic cross. The possible gametes are written on two sides, usually the top row and left columns. All possible combinations are represented in boxes below in the squares, which generates a square output form.','[{"s": "From the observation that the recessive parental trait is expressed without any blending in the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "generation, we can infer that, when the tall and dwarf plant produce gametes, by the process of meiosis, the alleles of the parental pair separate or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "segregate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from each other and only one allele is transmitted to a gamete. This segregation of alleles is a random process and so there is a 50 per cent chance of a gamete containing either allele, as has been verified by the results of the crossings. In this way the gametes of the tall", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants have the allele", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the gametes of the dwarf tt plants have the allele", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". During fertilisation the two alleles,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from one parent say, through the pollen, and t from the other parent, then through the egg, are united to produce zygotes that have one T allele and one t allele. In other words the hybrids have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since these hybrids contain alleles which express contrasting traits, the plants are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "heterozygous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The production of gametes by the parents, the formation of the zygotes, the F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "plants can be understood from a diagram called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Punnett Square", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "as shown in Figure 5.4.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It was developed by a British geneticist, Reginald C. Punnett.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is a graphical representation to calculate the probability of all possible genotypes of offspring in a genetic cross. The possible gametes are written on two sides, usually the top row and left columns. All possible combinations are represented in boxes below in the squares, which generates a square output form.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271722", "271747"]',NULL),
(3127,27,50,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image4.png'),
(3128,27,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.4 A Punnett square used to understand a typical monohybrid cross conducted by Mendel between true-breeding tall plants and true-breeding dwarf plants','[{"s": "Figure 5.4 A Punnett square used to understand a typical monohybrid cross conducted by Mendel between true-breeding tall plants and true-breeding dwarf plants", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271733", "271739"]',NULL),
(3129,27,52,'paragraph',NULL,'The Punnett Square shows the parental tall TT (male) and dwarf tt (female) plants, the gametes produced by them and, the F 1 Tt progeny. The F 1 plants of genotype Tt are self-pollinated. The symbols Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image12.png are used to denote the female (eggs) and male (pollen) of the F 1 generation, respectively. The F 1 plant of the genotype Tt when self-pollinated, produces gametes of the genotype T and t in equal proportion. When fertilisation takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 per cent chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T , as well as of genotype t. Also pollen grains of genotype t have a 50 per cent chance of pollinating eggs of genotype T , as well as of genotype t . As a result of random fertilisation, the resultant zygotes can be of the genotypes TT , Tt or tt .','[{"s": "The Punnett Square shows the parental tall", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(male) and dwarf tt (female) plants, the gametes produced by them and, the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "progeny. The F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants of genotype Tt are self-pollinated. The symbols", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image12.png", "t": "img", "alt": "symbol"}, {"s": "are used to denote the female (eggs) and male (pollen) of the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "generation, respectively. The F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plant of the genotype Tt when self-pollinated, produces gametes of the genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in equal proportion. When fertilisation takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 per cent chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as well as of genotype t. Also pollen grains of genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have a 50 per cent chance of pollinating eggs of genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as well as of genotype", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As a result of random fertilisation, the resultant zygotes can be of the genotypes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
