(7739,39,459,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Solid, non-metallic','[{"s": "(d) Solid, non-metallic", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3130,27,53,'paragraph',NULL,'From the Punnett square it is easily seen that 1/4th of the random fertilisations lead to TT , 1/2 lead to Tt and 1/4th to tt. Though the F 1 have a genotype of Tt , but the phenotypic character seen is ‘tall’. At F 2 , 3/4th of the plants are tall, where some of them are TT while others are Tt . Externally it is not possible to distinguish between the plants with the genotypes TT and Tt . Hence, within the genopytic pair Tt only one character ‘ T ’ tall is expressed. Hence the character T or ‘tall’ is said to dominate over the other allele t or ‘dwarf’ character. It is thus due to this dominance of one character over the other that all the F1 are tall (though the genotype is Tt ) and in the F 2 3/4th of the plants are tall (though genotypically 1/2 are Tt and only 1/4th are TT ). This leads to a phenotypic ratio of 3/4th tall : (1/4 TT + 1/2 Tt ) and 1/4th tt , i.e., a 3:1 ratio, but a genotypic ratio of 1:2:1.','[{"s": "From the Punnett square it is easily seen that 1/4th of the random fertilisations lead to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 1/2 lead to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 1/4th to tt. Though the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have a genotype of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", but the phenotypic character seen is ‘tall’. At F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 3/4th of the plants are tall, where some of them are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "while others are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Externally it is not possible to distinguish between the plants with the genotypes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence, within the genopytic pair", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "only one character ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’ tall is expressed. Hence the character", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or ‘tall’ is said to dominate over the other allele t or ‘dwarf’ character. It is thus due to this dominance of one character over the other that all the F1 are tall (though the genotype is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and in the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/4th of the plants are tall (though genotypically 1/2 are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and only 1/4th are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). This leads to a phenotypic ratio of 3/4th tall : (1/4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and 1/4th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e., a 3:1 ratio, but a genotypic ratio of 1:2:1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3132,27,55,'paragraph',NULL,'(1/2 T + 1/2 t )2 = (1/2T + 1/2 t ) X (1/2 T + 1/2 t ) = 1/4 TT + 1/2 Tt + 1/4 tt','[{"s": "(1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")2 = (1/2T + 1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") X (1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 1/4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3133,27,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Mendel self-pollinated the F 2 plants and found that dwarf F 2 plants continued to generate dwarf plants in F 3 and F 4 generations. He concluded that the genotype of the dwarfs was homozygous – tt . What do you think he would have got had he self-pollinated a tall F 2 plant?','[{"s": "Mendel self-pollinated the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants and found that dwarf F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants continued to generate dwarf plants in F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "generations. He concluded that the genotype of the dwarfs was homozygous –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What do you think he would have got had he self-pollinated a tall F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plant?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3134,27,57,'paragraph',NULL,'From the preceeding paragraphs it is clear that though the genotypic ratios can be calculated using mathematical probability, by simply looking at the phenotype of a dominant trait, it is not possible to know the genotypic composition. That is, for example, whether a tall plant from F 1 or F 2 has TT or Tt composition, cannot be predicted. Therefore, to determine the genotype of a tall plant at F 2 , Mendel crossed the tall plant from F 2 with a dwarf plant. This he called a test cross . In a typical test cross an organism (pea plants here) showing a dominant phenotype (and whose genotype is to be determined) is crossed with the recessive parent instead of self-crossing. The progenies of such a cross can easily be analysed to predict the genotype of the test organism. Figure 5.5 shows the results of typical test cross where violet colour flower (W) is dominant over white colour flower (w).','[{"s": "From the preceeding paragraphs it is clear that though the genotypic ratios can be calculated using mathematical probability, by simply looking at the phenotype of a dominant trait, it is not possible to know the genotypic composition. That is, for example, whether a tall plant from F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "composition, cannot be predicted.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Therefore, to determine the genotype of a tall plant at F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Mendel crossed the tall plant from F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "with a dwarf plant. This he called a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "test cross", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a typical test cross an organism (pea plants here) showing a dominant phenotype (and whose genotype is to be determined) is crossed with the recessive parent instead of self-crossing. The progenies of such a cross can easily be analysed to predict the genotype of the test organism. Figure 5.5 shows the results of typical test cross where violet colour flower (W) is dominant over white colour flower (w).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271748", "271749", "271750", "271751", "271752", "271753", "271754"]',NULL),
