(3140,27,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Based on his observations on monohybrid crosses Mendel proposed two general rules to consolidate his understanding of inheritance in monohybrid crosses. Today these rules are called the Principles or Laws of Inheritance : the First Law or Law of Dominance and the Second Law or Law of Segregation.','[{"s": "Based on his observations on monohybrid crosses Mendel proposed two general rules to consolidate his understanding of inheritance in monohybrid crosses. Today these rules are called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principles or Laws of Inheritance", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": the First Law or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of Dominance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the Second Law or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of Segregation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3141,27,64,'heading',3,'5.2.1 Law of Dominance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3142,27,65,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.','[{"s": "(i) Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271755"]',NULL),
(3143,27,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Factors occur in pairs.','[{"s": "(ii) Factors occur in pairs.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271755"]',NULL),
(3144,27,67,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) In a dissimilar pair of factors one member of the pair dominates (dominant) the other (recessive).','[{"s": "(iii) In a dissimilar pair of factors one member of the pair dominates (dominant) the other (recessive).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271755"]',NULL),
(3145,27,68,'paragraph',NULL,'The law of dominance is used to explain the expression of only one of the parental characters in a monohybrid cross in the F1 and the expression of both in the F2. It also explains the proportion of 3:1 obtained at the F2.','[{"s": "The law of dominance is used to explain the expression of only one of the parental characters in a monohybrid cross in the F1 and the expression of both in the F2.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It also explains the proportion of 3:1 obtained at the F2.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271756"]',NULL),
(3146,27,69,'heading',3,'5.2.2 Law of Segregation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3147,27,70,'paragraph',NULL,'This law is based on the fact that the alleles do not show any blending and that both the characters are recovered as such in the F2 generation though one of these is not seen at the F1 stage. Though the parents contain two alleles during gamete formation, the factors or alleles of a pair segregate from each other such that a gamete receives only one of the two factors. Of course, a homozygous parent produces all gametes that are similar while a heterozygous one produces two kinds of gametes each having one allele with equal proportion.','[{"s": "This law is based on the fact that the alleles do not show any blending and that both the characters are recovered as such in the F2 generation though one of these is not seen at the F1 stage.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Though the parents contain two alleles during gamete formation, the factors or alleles of a pair segregate from each other such that a gamete receives only one of the two factors.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Of course, a homozygous parent produces all gametes that are similar while a heterozygous one produces two kinds of gametes each having one allele with equal proportion.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271714"]',NULL),
(3148,27,71,'heading',4,'5.2.2.1 Incomplete Dominance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3149,27,72,'paragraph',NULL,'When experiments on peas were repeated using other traits in other plants, it was found that sometimes the F 1 had a phenotype that did not resemble either of the two parents and was in between the two. The inheritance of flower colour in the dog flower ( snapdragon or Antirrhinum sp .) is a good example to understand incomplete dominance. In a cross between true-breeding red-flowered ( RR ) and true-breeding white-flowered plants ( rr ), the F 1 ( Rr ) was pink (Figure 5.6). When the F1 was self-pollinated the F 2 resulted in the following ratio 1 ( RR ) Red: 2 ( Rr ) Pink: 1 ( rr ) White. Here the genotype ratios were exactly as we would expect in any mendelian monohybrid cross, but the phenotype ratios had changed from the 3:1 dominant : recessive ratio. What happened was that R was not completely dominant over r and this made it possible to distinguish Rr as pink from RR (red) and rr (white) .','[{"s": "When experiments on peas were repeated using other traits in other plants, it was found that sometimes the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "had a phenotype that did not resemble either of the two parents and was in between the two.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The inheritance of flower colour in the dog flower (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "snapdragon", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Antirrhinum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".) is a good example to understand incomplete dominance. In a cross between true-breeding red-flowered (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "RR", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and true-breeding white-flowered plants (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "rr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "), the F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Rr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") was pink (Figure 5.6). When the F1 was self-pollinated the F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "resulted in the following ratio 1 (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "RR", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") Red: 2 (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Rr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") Pink: 1 (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "rr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") White.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Here the genotype ratios were exactly as we would expect in any mendelian monohybrid cross, but the phenotype ratios had changed from the 3:1 dominant : recessive ratio. What happened was that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was not completely dominant over", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and this made it possible to distinguish", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as pink from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(red) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(white) .", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271714"]',NULL),
(3150,27,73,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image1.png'),
(3151,27,74,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.6 Results of monohybrid cross in the plant Snapdragon, where one allele is incompletely dominant over the other allele','[{"s": "Figure 5.6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Results of monohybrid cross in the plant Snapdragon, where one allele is incompletely dominant over the other allele", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271714", "271757", "271758"]',NULL),
(3152,27,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Explanation of the concept of dominance: What exactly is dominance? Why are some alleles dominant and some recessive? To tackle these questions, we must understand what a gene does. Every gene, as you know by now, contains the information to express a particular trait. In a diploid organism, there are two copies of each gene, i.e., as a pair of alleles. Now, these two alleles need not always be identical, as in a heterozygote. One of them may be different due to some changes that it has undergone (about which you will read further on, and in the next chapter) which modifies the information that particular allele contains.','[{"s": "Explanation of the concept of dominance:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What exactly is dominance? Why are some alleles dominant and some recessive? To tackle these questions, we must understand what a gene does. Every gene, as you know by now, contains the information to express a particular trait. In a diploid organism, there are two copies of each gene, i.e., as a pair of alleles. Now, these two alleles need not always be identical, as in a heterozygote. One of them may be different due to some changes that it has undergone (about which you will read further on, and in the next chapter) which modifies the information that particular allele contains.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12619,44,1389,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12620,44,1390,'paragraph',NULL,'5','[{"s": "5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3153,27,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Let’s take an example of a gene that contains the information for producing an enzyme. Now there are two copies of this gene, the two allelic forms. Let us assume (as is more common) that the normal allele produces the normal enzyme that is needed for the transformation of a substrate S. Theoretically, the modified allele could be responsible for production of –','[{"s": "Let’s take an example of a gene that contains the information for producing an enzyme. Now there are two copies of this gene, the two allelic forms. Let us assume (as is more common) that the normal allele produces the normal enzyme that is needed for the transformation of a substrate S. Theoretically, the modified allele could be responsible for production of –", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3154,27,77,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the normal/less efficient enzyme, or','[{"s": "(i) the normal/less efficient enzyme, or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3155,27,78,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) a non-functional enzyme, or','[{"s": "(ii) a non-functional enzyme, or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3156,27,79,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) no enzyme at all','[{"s": "(iii) no enzyme at all", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3157,27,80,'paragraph',NULL,'In the first case, the modified allele is equivalent to the unmodified allele, i.e., it will produce the same phenotype/trait, i.e., result in the transformation of substrate S. Such equivalent allele pairs are very common. But, if the allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme, the phenotype may be effected. The phenotype/trait will only be dependent on the functioning of the unmodified allele. The unmodified (functioning) allele, which represents the original phenotype is the dominant allele and the modified allele is generally the recessive allele. Hence, in the example above the recessive trait is seen due to non-functional enzyme or because no enzyme is produced.','[{"s": "In the first case, the modified allele is equivalent to the unmodified allele, i.e., it will produce the same phenotype/trait, i.e., result in the transformation of substrate S. Such equivalent allele pairs are very common. But, if the allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme, the phenotype may be effected. The phenotype/trait will only be dependent on the functioning of the unmodified allele. The unmodified (functioning) allele, which represents the original phenotype is the dominant allele and the modified allele is generally the recessive allele. Hence, in the example above the recessive trait is seen due to non-functional enzyme or because no enzyme is produced.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3158,27,81,'heading',4,'5.2.2.2 Co-dominance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3162,27,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image5.png'),
(3164,27,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene or the product that it has information for. It depends as much on the gene product and the production of a particular phenotype from this product as it does on the particular phenotype that we choose to examine, in case more than one phenotype is influenced by the same gene.','[{"s": "Therefore, dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene or the product that it has information for. It depends as much on the gene product and the production of a particular phenotype from this product as it does on the particular phenotype that we choose to examine, in case more than one phenotype is influenced by the same gene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3165,27,88,'heading',2,'5.3 Inheritance of Two Genes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7741,39,461,'paragraph',NULL,'25. All transition elements are d-block elements, but all d-block elements are not transition elements. Explain.','[{"s": "25. All transition elements are d-block elements, but all d-block elements are not transition elements. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3166,27,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Mendel also worked with and crossed pea plants that differed in two characters, as is seen in the cross between a pea plant that has seeds with yellow colour and round shape and one that had seeds of green colour and wrinkled shape (Figure5.7). Mendel found that the seeds resulting from the crossing of the parents, had yellow coloured and round shaped seeds. Here can you tell which of the characters in the pairs yellow/green colour and round/wrinkled shape was dominant?','[{"s": "Mendel also worked with and crossed pea plants that differed in two characters, as is seen in the cross between a pea plant that has seeds with yellow colour and round shape and one that had seeds of green colour and wrinkled shape (Figure5.7). Mendel found that the seeds resulting from the crossing of the parents, had yellow coloured and round shaped seeds. Here can you tell which of the characters in the pairs yellow/green colour and round/wrinkled shape was dominant?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3167,27,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, yellow colour was dominant over green and round shape dominant over wrinkled. These results were identical to those that he got when he made separate monohybrid crosses between yellow and green seeded plants and between round and wrinkled seeded plants.','[{"s": "Thus, yellow colour was dominant over green and round shape dominant over wrinkled. These results were identical to those that he got when he made separate monohybrid crosses between yellow and green seeded plants and between round and wrinkled seeded plants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3168,27,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us use the genotypic symbols Y for dominant yellow seed colour and y for recessive green seed colour, R for round shaped seeds and r for wrinkled seed shape. The genotype of the parents can then be written as RRYY and rryy . The cross between the two plants can be written down as in Figure 5.7 showing the genotypes of the parent plants. The gametes RY and ry unite on fertilisation to produce the F 1 hybrid RrYy . When Mendel self hybridised the F 1 plants he found that 3/4th of F 2 plants had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green. The yellow and green colour segregated in a 3:1 ratio. Round and wrinkled seed shape also segregated in a 3:1 ratio; just like in a monohybrid cross.','[{"s": "Let us use the genotypic symbols", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for dominant yellow seed colour and y for recessive green seed colour,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for round shaped seeds and r for wrinkled seed shape. The genotype of the parents can then be written as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RRYY", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rryy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The cross between the two plants can be written down as in Figure 5.7 showing the genotypes of the parent plants. The gametes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RY", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ry", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unite on fertilisation to produce the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RrYy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When Mendel self hybridised the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants he found that 3/4th of F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plants had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green. The yellow and green colour segregated in a 3:1 ratio. Round and wrinkled seed shape also segregated in a 3:1 ratio; just like in a monohybrid cross.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3169,27,92,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image23.png'),
(3171,27,94,'heading',3,'5.3.1 Law of Independent Assortment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3172,27,95,'paragraph',NULL,'In the dihybrid cross (Figure 5.7), the phenotypes round,yellow; wrinkled, yellow; round, green and wrinkled, green appeared in the ratio 9:3:3:1. Such a ratio was observed for several pairs of characters that Mendel studied.','[{"s": "In the dihybrid cross (Figure 5.7), the phenotypes round,yellow; wrinkled, yellow; round, green and wrinkled, green appeared in the ratio 9:3:3:1. Such a ratio was observed for several pairs of characters that Mendel studied.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271736", "271739"]',NULL),
(3173,27,96,'paragraph',NULL,'(3 Round : 1 Wrinkled) (3 Yellow : 1 Green) = 9 Round, Yellow : 3 Wrinkled, Yellow: 3 Round, Green : 1 Wrinkled, Green','[{"s": "(3 Round : 1 Wrinkled) (3 Yellow : 1 Green) = 9 Round, Yellow : 3 Wrinkled, Yellow: 3 Round, Green : 1 Wrinkled, Green", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271736", "271739"]',NULL),
(3174,27,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Based upon such observations on dihybrid crosses (crosses between plants differing in two traits) Mendel proposed a second set of generalisations that we call Mendel’s Law of Independent Assortment. The law states that ‘when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters’.','[{"s": "Based upon such observations on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dihybrid crosses", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(crosses between plants differing in two traits) Mendel proposed a second set of generalisations that we call Mendel’s Law of Independent Assortment. The law states that ‘when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3175,27,98,'paragraph',NULL,'The Punnett square can be effectively used to understand the independent segregation of the two pairs of genes during meiosis and the production of eggs and pollen in the F 1 RrYy plant. Consider the segregation of one pair of genes R and r . Fifty per cent of the gametes have the gene R and the other 50 per cent have r . Now besides each gamete having either R or r , it should also have the allele Y or y . The important thing to remember here is that segregation of 50 per cent R and 50 per cent r is independent from the segregation of 50 per cent Y and 50 per cent y. Therefore, 50 per cent of the r bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 per cent has y . Similarly, 50 per cent of the R bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 per cent has y . Thus there are four genotypes of gametes (four types of pollen and four types of eggs). The four types are RY , Ry , rY and ry each with a frequency of 25 per cent or 1/4th of the total gametes produced. When you write down the four types of eggs and pollen on the two sides of a Punnett square it is very easy to derive the composition of the zygotes that give rise to the F 2 plants (Figure 5.7). Although there are 16 squares how many different types of genotypes and phenotypes are formed? Note them down in the format given.','[{"s": "The Punnett square can be effectively used to understand the independent segregation of the two pairs of genes during meiosis and the production of eggs and pollen in the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RrYy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plant. Consider the segregation of one pair of genes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Fifty per cent of the gametes have the gene", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the other 50 per cent have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Now besides each gamete having either", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", it should also have the allele", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The important thing to remember here is that segregation of 50 per cent R and 50 per cent r is independent from the segregation of 50 per cent", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 50 per cent y. Therefore, 50 per cent of the r bearing gametes has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the other 50 per cent has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Similarly, 50 per cent of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 per cent has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus there are four genotypes of gametes (four types of pollen and four types of eggs).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The four types are", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "RY", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Ry", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "rY", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ry", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "each with a frequency of 25 per cent or 1/4th of the total gametes produced.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When you write down the four types of eggs and pollen on the two sides of a Punnett square it is very easy to derive the composition of the zygotes that give rise to the F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "plants (Figure 5.7).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Although there are 16 squares how many different types of genotypes and phenotypes are formed? Note them down in the format given.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271762", "271763"]',NULL),
(3176,27,99,'paragraph',NULL,'S.No. Genotypes found in F2 Their expected Phenotypes','[{"s": "S.No. Genotypes found in F2 Their expected Phenotypes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3177,27,100,'heading',3,'5.3.2 Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24128,61,140,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image95.png'),
(3178,27,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Mendel published his work on inheritance of characters in 1865 but for several reasons, it remained unrecognised till 1900. Firstly, communication was not easy (as it is now) in those days and his work could not be widely publicised. Secondly, his concept of genes (or factors , in Mendel’s words) as stable and discrete units that controlled the expression of traits and, of the pair of alleles which did not ‘blend’ with each other, was not accepted by his contemporaries as an explanation for the apparently continuous variation seen in nature. Thirdly, Mendel’s approach of using mathematics to explain biological phenomena was totally new and unacceptable to many of the biologists of his time. Finally, though Mendel’s work suggested that factors (genes) were discrete units, he could not provide any physical proof for the existence of factors or say what they were made of.','[{"s": "Mendel published his work on inheritance of characters in 1865 but for several reasons, it remained unrecognised till 1900. Firstly, communication was not easy (as it is now) in those days and his work could not be widely publicised. Secondly, his concept of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "genes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "factors", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", in Mendel’s words) as stable and discrete units that controlled the expression of traits and, of the pair of alleles which did not ‘blend’ with each other, was not accepted by his contemporaries as an explanation for the apparently continuous variation seen in nature. Thirdly, Mendel’s approach of using mathematics to explain biological phenomena was totally new and unacceptable to many of the biologists of his time. Finally, though Mendel’s work suggested that factors (genes) were discrete units, he could not provide any physical proof for the existence of factors or say what they were made of.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3179,27,102,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1900, three Scientists (de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak) independently rediscovered Mendel’s results on the inheritance of characters. Also, by this time due to advancements in microscopy that were taking place, scientists were able to carefully observe cell division. This led to the discovery of structures in the nucleus that appeared to double and divide just before each cell division. These were called chromosomes ( colored bodies , as they were visualised by staining). By 1902, the chromosome movement during meiosis had been worked out. Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri noted that the behaviour of chromosomes was parallel to the behaviour of genes and used chromosome movement (Figure 5.8) to explain Mendel’s laws (Table 5.3). Recall that you have studied the behaviour of chromosomes during mitosis (equational division) and during meiosis (reduction division). The important things to remember are that chromosomes as well as genes occur in pairs. The two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes.','[{"s": "In 1900, three Scientists (de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak) independently rediscovered Mendel’s results on the inheritance of characters. Also, by this time due to advancements in microscopy that were taking place, scientists were able to carefully observe cell division. This led to the discovery of structures in the nucleus that appeared to double and divide just before each cell division. These were called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chromosomes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "colored", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bodies", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as they were visualised by staining). By 1902, the chromosome movement during meiosis had been worked out.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri noted that the behaviour of chromosomes was parallel to the behaviour of genes and used chromosome movement (Figure 5.8) to explain Mendel’s laws (Table 5.3).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Recall that you have studied the behaviour of chromosomes during mitosis (equational division) and during meiosis (reduction division). The important things to remember are that chromosomes as well as genes occur in pairs. The two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["271764"]',NULL),
(3180,27,103,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Principles_Of_Inheritance_And_Variation-image20.png'),
(3181,27,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.8 Meiosis and germ cell formation in a cell with four chromosomes. Can you see how chromosomes segregate when germ cells','[{"s": "Figure", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Meiosis and germ cell formation in a cell with four chromosomes. Can you see how chromosomes segregate when germ cells", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3182,27,105,'paragraph',NULL,'are formed?','[{"s": "are formed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
