(28996,71,129,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image71.png'),
(3376,29,57,'paragraph',NULL,'F 0 is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the inner membrane. The passage of protons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F 1 component for the production of ATP. For each ATP produced, 2H + passes through F 0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient.','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the inner membrane. The passage of protons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "component for the production of ATP.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For each ATP produced, 2H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "passes through F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["141113", "141115"]',NULL),
(3377,29,58,'heading',4,'14.5 The Respiratory Balance Sheet',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3378,29,59,'paragraph',NULL,'It is possible to make calculations of the net gain of ATP for every glucose molecule oxidised; but in reality this can remain only a theoretical exercise. These calculations can be made only on certain assumptions that:','[{"s": "It is possible to make calculations of the net gain of ATP for every glucose molecule oxidised; but in reality this can remain only a theoretical exercise. These calculations can be made only on certain assumptions that:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3379,29,60,'paragraph',NULL,'• There is a sequential, orderly pathway functioning, with one substrate forming the next and with glycolysis, TCA cycle and ETS pathway following one after another.','[{"s": "• There is a sequential, orderly pathway functioning, with one substrate forming the next and with glycolysis, TCA cycle and ETS pathway following one after another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3382,29,63,'paragraph',NULL,'• Only glucose is being respired – no other alternative substrates are entering in the pathway at any of the intermediary stages.','[{"s": "• Only glucose is being respired – no other alternative substrates are entering in the pathway at any of the intermediary stages.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3384,29,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Now let us compare fermentation and aerobic respiration:','[{"s": "Now let us compare fermentation and aerobic respiration:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3385,29,66,'paragraph',NULL,'• Fermentation accounts for only a partial breakdown of glucose whereas in aerobic respiration it is completely degraded to CO 2 and H 2 O.','[{"s": "• Fermentation accounts for only a partial breakdown of glucose whereas in aerobic respiration it is completely degraded to CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3386,29,67,'paragraph',NULL,'• In fermentation there is a net gain of only two molecules of ATP for each molecule of glucose degraded to pyruvic acid whereas many more molecules of ATP are generated under aerobic conditions.','[{"s": "• In fermentation there is a net gain of only two molecules of ATP for each molecule of glucose degraded to pyruvic acid whereas many more molecules of ATP are generated under aerobic conditions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3387,29,68,'paragraph',NULL,'• NADH is oxidised to NAD + rather slowly in fermentation, however the reaction is very vigorous in case of aerobic respiration.','[{"s": "• NADH is oxidised to NAD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rather slowly in fermentation, however the reaction is very vigorous in case of aerobic respiration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3388,29,69,'paragraph',NULL,'14.6 Amphibolic Pathway','[{"s": "14.6 Amphibolic Pathway", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3483,30,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Majority of flowering plants use a range of animals as pollinating agents. Bees, butterflies, flies, beetles, wasps, ants, moths, birds (sunbirds and humming birds) and bats are the common pollinating agents. (Figure 2.11b). Among the animals, insects, particularly bees are the dominant biotic pollinating agents. Even larger animals such as some primates (lemurs), arboreal (tree-dwelling) rodents, or even reptiles (gecko lizard and garden lizard) have also been reported as pollinators in some species.','[{"s": "Majority of flowering plants use a range of animals as pollinating agents.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bees, butterflies, flies, beetles, wasps, ants, moths, birds (sunbirds and humming birds) and bats are the common pollinating agents. (Figure 2.11b). Among the animals, insects, particularly bees are the dominant biotic pollinating agents.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Even larger animals such as some primates (lemurs), arboreal (tree-dwelling) rodents, or even reptiles (gecko lizard and garden lizard) have also been reported as pollinators in some species.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241642"]',NULL),
(3484,30,77,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image10.png'),
(3485,30,78,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3486,30,79,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image2.png'),
(3390,29,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Since respiration involves breakdown of substrates, the respiratory process has traditionally been considered a catabolic process and the respiratory pathway as a catabolic pathway. But is this understanding correct? We have discussed above, at which points in the respiratory pathway different substrates would enter if they were to be respired and used to derive energy. What is important to recognise is that it is these very compounds that would be withdrawn from the respiratory pathway for the synthesis of the said substrates. Hence, fatty acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway when it is used as a substrate. But when the organism needs to synthesise fatty acids, acetyl CoA would be withdrawn from the respiratory pathway for it. Hence, the respiratory pathway comes into the picture both during breakdown and synthesis of fatty acids. Similarly, during breakdown and synthesis of protein too, respiratory intermediates form the link. Breaking down processes within the living organism is catabolism, and synthesis is anabolism. Because the respiratory pathway is involved in both anabolism and catabolism, it would hence be better to consider the respiratory pathway as an amphibolic pathway rather than as a catabolic one.','[{"s": "Since respiration involves breakdown of substrates, the respiratory process has traditionally been considered a catabolic process and the respiratory pathway as a catabolic pathway. But is this understanding correct? We have discussed above, at which points in the respiratory pathway different substrates would enter if they were to be respired and used to derive energy. What is important to recognise is that it is these very compounds that would be withdrawn from the respiratory pathway for the synthesis of the said substrates. Hence, fatty acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway when it is used as a substrate.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "But when the organism needs to synthesise fatty acids, acetyl CoA would be withdrawn from the respiratory pathway for it.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hence, the respiratory pathway comes into the picture both during breakdown and synthesis of fatty acids. Similarly, during breakdown and synthesis of protein too, respiratory intermediates form the link. Breaking down processes within the living organism is catabolism, and synthesis is anabolism. Because the respiratory pathway is involved in both anabolism and catabolism, it would hence be better to consider the respiratory pathway as an amphibolic pathway rather than as a catabolic one.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["141218", "141307"]',NULL),
(3391,29,72,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Respiration_in_Plants-image3.png'),
(3392,29,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.6 Interrelationship among metabolic pathways showing respiration mediated breakdown of different organic molecules to CO 2 and H 2 0','[{"s": "Figure 14.6 Interrelationship among metabolic pathways showing respiration mediated breakdown of different organic molecules to CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3393,29,74,'heading',4,'14.7 Respiratory Quotient',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3395,29,76,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Respiration_in_Plants-image7.png'),
(3396,29,77,'paragraph',NULL,'The respiratory quotient depends upon the type of respiratory substrate used during respiration.','[{"s": "The respiratory quotient depends upon the type of respiratory substrate used during respiration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3397,29,78,'paragraph',NULL,'When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1, because equal amounts of CO 2 and O 2 are evolved and consumed, respectively, as shown in the equation below :','[{"s": "When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1, because equal amounts of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are evolved and consumed, respectively, as shown in the equation below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3398,29,79,'image',NULL,'p13.5',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Respiration_in_Plants-image5.png'),
(3400,29,81,'image',NULL,'p13.6',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Respiration_in_Plants-image6.png'),
(3401,29,82,'paragraph',NULL,'When proteins are respiratory substrates the ratio would be about 0.9.','[{"s": "When proteins are respiratory substrates the ratio would be about 0.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3402,29,83,'paragraph',NULL,'What is important to recognise is that in living organisms respiratory substrates are often more than one; pure proteins or fats are never used as respiratory substrates.','[{"s": "What is important to recognise is that in living organisms respiratory substrates are often more than one; pure proteins or fats are never used as respiratory substrates.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
