(3414,30,7,'paragraph',NULL,'2.1 Flower – A Fascinating Organ of Angiosperms','[{"s": "2.1 Flower – A Fascinating Organ of Angiosperms", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3415,30,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Are we not lucky that plants reproduce sexually? The myriads of flowers that we enjoy gazing at, the scents and the perfumes that we swoon over, the rich colours that attract us, are all there as an aid to sexual reproduction. Flowers do not exist only for us to be used for our own selfishness. All flowering plants show sexual reproduction. A look at the diversity of structures of the inflorescences, flowers and floral parts, shows an amazing range of adaptations to ensure formation of the end products of sexual reproduction, the fruits and seeds. In this chapter, let us understand the morphology, structure and the processes of sexual reproduction in flowering plants (angiosperms).','[{"s": "Are we not lucky that plants reproduce sexually? The myriads of flowers that we enjoy gazing at, the scents and the perfumes that we swoon over, the rich colours that attract us, are all there as an aid to sexual reproduction. Flowers do not exist only for us to be used for our own selfishness. All flowering plants show sexual reproduction. A look at the diversity of structures of the inflorescences, flowers and floral parts, shows an amazing range of adaptations to ensure formation of the end products of sexual reproduction, the fruits and seeds. In this chapter, let us understand the morphology, structure and the processes of sexual reproduction in flowering plants (angiosperms).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3416,30,9,'paragraph',NULL,'2.1 Flower – A Fascinating Organ of Angiosperms','[{"s": "2.1 Flower – A Fascinating Organ of Angiosperms", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3417,30,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Human beings have had an intimate relationship with flowers since time immemorial. Flowers are objects of aesthetic, ornamental, social, religious and cultural value – they have always been used as symbols for conveying important human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, mourning, etc. List at least five flowers of ornamental value that are commonly cultivated at homes and in gardens. Find out the names of five more flowers that are used in social and cultural celebrations in your family. Have you heard of floriculture – what does it refer to?','[{"s": "Human beings have had an intimate relationship with flowers since time immemorial. Flowers are objects of aesthetic, ornamental, social, religious and cultural value – they have always been used as symbols for conveying important human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, mourning, etc. List at least five flowers of ornamental value that are commonly cultivated at homes and in gardens.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find out the names of five more flowers that are used in social and cultural celebrations in your family. Have you heard of floriculture – what does it refer to?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3418,30,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image16.png'),
(3419,30,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.1 A diagrammatic representation of L.S. of a flower','[{"s": "Figure 2.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A diagrammatic representation of L.S. of a flower", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3420,30,13,'paragraph',NULL,'To a biologist, flowers are morphological and embryological marvels and the sites of sexual reproduction. In class XI, you have read the various parts of a flower. Figure 2.1 will help you recall the parts of a typical flower. Can you name the two parts in a flower in which the two most important units of sexual reproduction develop?','[{"s": "To a biologist, flowers are morphological and embryological marvels and the sites of sexual reproduction. In class XI, you have read the various parts of a flower. Figure 2.1 will help you recall the parts of a typical flower. Can you name the two parts in a flower in which the two most important units of sexual reproduction develop?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3421,30,14,'heading',3,'2.2 Pre-fertilisation: Structures and Events',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3489,30,82,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Insect pollination','[{"s": "(b) Insect pollination", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3422,30,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Much before the actual flower is seen on a plant, the decision that the plant is going to flower has taken place. Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and further development of the floral primordium. Inflorescences are formed which bear the floral buds and then the flowers. In the flower the male and female reproductive structures, the androecium and the gynoecium differentiate and develop. You would recollect that the androecium consists of a whorl of stamens representing the male reproductive organ and the gynoecium represents the female reproductive organ.','[{"s": "Much before the actual flower is seen on a plant, the decision that the plant is going to flower has taken place. Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and further development of the floral primordium. Inflorescences are formed which bear the floral buds and then the flowers. In the flower the male and female reproductive structures, the androecium and the gynoecium differentiate and develop. You would recollect that the androecium consists of a whorl of stamens representing the male reproductive organ and the gynoecium represents the female reproductive organ.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3423,30,16,'heading',4,'2.2.1 Stamen, Microsporangium and Pollen Grain',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3425,30,18,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image20.png'),
(3426,30,19,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.2 (a) A typical stamen; (b) three–dimensional cut section of an anther','[{"s": "Figure 2.2 (a) A typical stamen; (b) three–dimensional cut section of an anther", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(3427,30,20,'paragraph',NULL,'A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed with each lobe having two theca, i.e., they are dithecous (Figure 2.2 b). Often a longitudinal groove runs lengthwise separating the theca. Let us understand the various types of tissues and their organisation in the transverse section of an anther (Figure 2.3 a). The bilobed nature of an anther is very distinct in the transverse section of the anther. The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four microsporangia located at the corners, two in each lobe.','[{"s": "A typical angiosperm anther is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bilobed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with each lobe having two theca, i.e., they are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dithecous", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Figure 2.2 b). Often a longitudinal groove runs lengthwise separating the theca. Let us understand the various types of tissues and their organisation in the transverse section of an anther (Figure 2.3 a). The bilobed nature of an anther is very distinct in the transverse section of the anther. The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four", "t": "text"}, {"s": "microsporangia", "t": "text"}, {"s": "located at the corners, two in each lobe.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3428,30,21,'paragraph',NULL,'The microsporangia develop further and become pollen sacs . They extend longitudinally all through the length of an anther and are packed with pollen grains.','[{"s": "The microsporangia develop further and become", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pollen sacs", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They extend longitudinally all through the length of an anther and are packed with pollen grains.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3430,30,23,'paragraph',NULL,'When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the sporogenous tissue occupies the centre of each microsporangium.','[{"s": "When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sporogenous tissue", "t": "text"}, {"s": "occupies the centre of each microsporangium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3431,30,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Microsporogenesis : As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads. What would be the ploidy of the cells of the tetrad?','[{"s": "Microsporogenesis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What would be the ploidy of the cells of the tetrad?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["242011"]',NULL),
(3432,30,25,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image6.png'),
(3433,30,26,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (b)','[{"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3434,30,27,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image18.png'),
(3435,30,28,'paragraph',NULL,'(c)','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3436,30,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.3 (a) Transverse section of a young anther; (b) Enlarged view of one microsporangium showing wall layers; (c) A mature dehisced anther','[{"s": "Figure 2.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Transverse section of a young anther; (b) Enlarged view of one microsporangium showing wall layers; (c) A mature dehisced anther", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3437,30,30,'paragraph',NULL,'As each cell of the sporogenous tissue is capable of giving rise to a microspore tetrad. Each one is a potential pollen or microspore mo ther cell. The process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) through meiosis is called microsporogenesis . The microspores, as they are formed, are arranged in a cluster of four cells–the microspore tetrad (Figure 2.3 a). As the anthers mature and dehydrate, the microspores dissociate from each other and develop into pollen grains','[{"s": "As each cell of the sporogenous tissue is capable of giving rise to a microspore tetrad. Each one is a potential pollen or microspore mo", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ther cell.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) through meiosis is called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "microsporogenesis", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The microspores, as they are formed, are arranged in a cluster of four cells–the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "microspore tetrad", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Figure 2.3 a). As the anthers mature and dehydrate, the microspores dissociate from each other and develop into", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pollen grains", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["240215", "241520"]',NULL),
(3438,30,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollen grain: The pollen grains represent the male gametophytes . If you touch the opened anthers of Hibiscus or any other flower you would find deposition of yellowish powdery pollen grains on your fingers. Sprinkle these grains on a drop of water taken on a glass slide and observe under a microscope. You will really be amazed at the variety of architecture – sizes, shapes, colours, designs – seen on the pollen grains from different species (Figure 2.4).','[{"s": "Pollen grain:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The pollen grains represent the male gametophytes", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If you touch the opened anthers of Hibiscus or any other flower you would find deposition of yellowish powdery pollen grains on your fingers. Sprinkle these grains on a drop of water taken on a glass slide and observe under a microscope. You will really be amazed at the variety of architecture – sizes, shapes, colours, designs – seen on the pollen grains from different species (Figure 2.4).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241521"]',NULL),
(3439,30,32,'image',NULL,'2.4',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image14.png'),
(3440,30,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.4 Scanning electron micrographs of a few pollen grains','[{"s": "Figure 2.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Scanning electron micrographs of a few pollen grains", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3442,30,35,'image',NULL,'2.4(a)',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image1.png'),
(3443,30,36,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image5.png'),
(3444,30,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.5 (a) Enlarged view of a pollen grain tetrad; (b) stages of a microspore maturing into a pollen grain','[{"s": "Figure 2.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Enlarged view of a pollen grain tetrad; (b) stages of a microspore maturing into a pollen grain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3445,30,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollen grains of many species cause severe allergies and bronchial afflictions in some people often leading to chronic respiratory disorders– asthma, bronchitis, etc. It may be mentioned that Parthenium or carrot grass that came into India as a contaminant with imported wheat, has become ubiquitous in occurrence and causes pollen allergy.','[{"s": "Pollen grains of many species cause severe allergies and bronchial afflictions in some people often leading to chronic respiratory disorders– asthma, bronchitis, etc.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It may be mentioned that Parthenium or carrot grass that came into India as a contaminant with imported wheat, has become ubiquitous in occurrence and causes pollen allergy.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241524"]',NULL),
(3447,30,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image4.png'),
(3448,30,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.6 Pollen products','[{"s": "Figure 2.6 Pollen products", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3490,30,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Often flowers of animal-pollinated plants are specifically adapted for a particular species of animal.','[{"s": "Often flowers of animal-pollinated plants are specifically adapted for a particular species of animal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3450,30,43,'heading',5,'2.2.2 The Pistil, Megasporangium (ovule) and Embryo sac',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3452,30,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image13.png'),
(3453,30,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.7 (a) A dissected flower of Hibiscus showing pistil (other floral parts have been removed); (b) Multicarpellary, syncarpous pistil of Papaver ; (c) A multicarpellary, apocarpous gynoecium of Michelia; (d) A diagrammatic view of a typical anatropous ovule','[{"s": "Figure 2.7 (a) A dissected flower of Hibiscus showing pistil (other floral parts have been removed); (b) Multicarpellary, syncarpous pistil of Papaver ; (c) A multicarpellary, apocarpous gynoecium of Michelia; (d) A diagrammatic view of a typical anatropous ovule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3454,30,47,'paragraph',NULL,'The style is the elongated slender part beneath the stigma. The basal bulged part of the pistil is the ovary . Inside the ovary is the ovarian cavity (locule) . The placenta is located inside the ovarian cavity. Recall the definition and types of placentation that you studied in Class XI. Arising from the placenta are the megasporangia , commonly called ovules . The number of ovules in an ovary may be one (wheat, paddy, mango) to many (papaya, water melon, orchids).','[{"s": "The style is the elongated slender part beneath the stigma. The basal bulged part of the pistil is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ovary", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Inside the ovary is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ovarian cavity (locule)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The placenta", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is located inside the ovarian cavity. Recall the definition and types of placentation that you studied in Class XI. Arising from the placenta are the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "megasporangia", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", commonly called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ovules", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The number of ovules in an ovary may be one (wheat, paddy, mango) to many (papaya, water melon, orchids).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7773,39,493,'paragraph',NULL,'41. The radius of Na + cation is less than that of Na atom. Give reason.','[{"s": "41. The radius of Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cation is less than that of Na atom. Give reason.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7774,39,494,'paragraph',NULL,'42. Among alkali metals which element do you expect to be least electronegative and why?','[{"s": "42. Among alkali metals which element do you expect to be least electronegative and why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
