(3458,30,51,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image3.png'),
(3459,30,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.8 (a) Parts of the ovule showing a large megaspore mother cell, a dyad and a tetrad of megaspores; (b) 2, 4, and 8-nucleate stages of embryo sac and a mature embryo sac; (c) A diagrammatic representation of the mature embryo sac.','[{"s": "Figure 2.8 (a) Parts of the ovule showing a large megaspore mother cell, a dyad and a tetrad of megaspores; (b) 2, 4, and 8-nucleate stages of embryo sac and a mature embryo sac; (c) A diagrammatic representation of the mature embryo sac.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3461,30,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us study formation of the embryo sac in a little more detail. (Figure 2.8b). The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides mitotically to form two nuclei which move to the opposite poles, forming the 2-nucleate embryo sac. Two more sequential mitotic nuclear divisions result in the formation of the 4-nucleate and later the 8-nucleate stages of the embryo sac. It is of interest to note that these mitotic divisions are strictly free nuclear, that is, nuclear divisions are not followed immediately by cell wall formation. After the 8-nucleate stage, cell walls are laid down leading to the organisation of the typical female gametophyte or embryo sac . Observe the distribution of cells inside the embryo sac (Figure 2.8b, c). Six of the eight nuclei are surrounded by cell walls and organised into cells; the remaining two nuclei, called polar nuclei are situated below the egg apparatus in the large central cell.','[{"s": "Let us study formation of the embryo sac in a little more detail. (Figure 2.8b). The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides mitotically to form two nuclei which move to the opposite poles, forming the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2-nucleate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "embryo sac. Two more sequential mitotic nuclear divisions result in the formation of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4-nucleate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and later the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8-nucleate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stages of the embryo sac. It is of interest to note that these mitotic divisions are strictly free nuclear, that is, nuclear divisions are not followed immediately by cell wall formation. After the 8-nucleate stage, cell walls are laid down leading to the organisation of the typical", "t": "text"}, {"s": "female gametophyte", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "embryo sac", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Observe the distribution of cells inside the embryo sac (Figure 2.8b, c). Six of the eight nuclei are surrounded by cell walls and organised into cells; the remaining two nuclei, called polar nuclei are situated below the egg apparatus in the large", "t": "text"}, {"s": "central cell.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3463,30,56,'heading',4,'2.2.3 Pollination',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3464,30,57,'paragraph',NULL,'In the preceding sections you have learnt that the male and female gametes in flowering plants are produced in the pollen grain and embryo sac, respectively. As both types of gametes are non-motile, they have to be brought together for fertilisation to occur. How is this achieved?','[{"s": "In the preceding sections you have learnt that the male and female gametes in flowering plants are produced in the pollen grain and embryo sac, respectively. As both types of gametes are non-motile, they have to be brought together for fertilisation to occur. How is this achieved?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3465,30,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollination is the mechanism to achieve this objective. Transfer of pollen grains (shed from the anther) to the stigma of a pistil is termed pollination . Flowering plants have evolved an amazing array of adaptations to achieve pollination. They make use of external agents to achieve pollination. Can you list the possible external agents?','[{"s": "Pollination", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mechanism to achieve this objective. Transfer of pollen grains (shed from the anther) to the stigma of a pistil is termed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pollination", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Flowering plants have evolved an amazing array of adaptations to achieve pollination. They make use of external agents to achieve pollination. Can you list the possible external agents?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3466,30,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinds of Pollination : Depending on the source of pollen, pollination can be divided into three types.','[{"s": "Kinds of Pollination", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Depending on the source of pollen, pollination can be divided into three types.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3468,30,61,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image7.png'),
(3469,30,62,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3471,30,64,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image8.png'),
(3472,30,65,'paragraph',NULL,'(b)','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3474,30,67,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/biology/images/Sexual_Reproduction_in_Flowering_Plants-image12.png'),
(3475,30,68,'paragraph',NULL,'(c)','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3476,30,69,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.9 (a) Self-pollinated flowers; (b)Cross pollinated flowers; (c)Cleistogamous flowers','[{"s": "Figure 2.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Self-pollinated flowers; (b)Cross pollinated flowers; (c)Cleistogamous flowers", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3477,30,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Agents of Pollination : Plants use two abiotic (wind and water) and one biotic (animals) agents to achieve pollination. Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination. Only a small proportion of plants use abiotic agents. Pollen grains coming in contact with the stigma is a chance factor in both wind and water pollination. To compensate for this uncertainties and associated loss of pollen grains, the flowers produce enormous amount of pollen when compared to the number of ovules available for pollination.','[{"s": "Agents of Pollination :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Plants use two abiotic (wind and water) and one biotic (animals) agents to achieve pollination. Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination. Only a small proportion of plants use abiotic agents. Pollen grains coming in contact with the stigma is a chance factor in both wind and water pollination. To compensate for this uncertainties and associated loss of pollen grains, the flowers produce enormous amount of pollen when compared to the number of ovules available for pollination.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7776,39,496,'paragraph',NULL,'Element','[{"s": "Element", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7777,39,497,'paragraph',NULL,'Atomic radius (pm)','[{"s": "Atomic radius (pm)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7778,39,498,'paragraph',NULL,'Be','[{"s": "Be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7779,39,499,'paragraph',NULL,'74','[{"s": "74", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7780,39,500,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3491,30,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Majority of insect-pollinated flowers are large, colourful, fragrant and rich in nectar. When the flowers are small, a number of flowers are clustered into an inflorescence to make them conspicuous. Animals are attracted to flowers by colour and/or fragrance. The flowers pollinated by flies and beetles secrete foul odours to attract these animals. To sustain animal visits, the flowers have to provide rewards to the animals. Nectar and pollen grains are the usual floral rewards. For harvesting the reward(s) from the flower the animal visitor comes in contact with the anthers and the stigma. The body of the animal gets a coating of pollen grains, which are generally sticky in animal pollinated flowers. When the animal carrying pollen on its body comes in contact with the stigma, it brings about pollination.','[{"s": "Majority of insect-pollinated flowers are large, colourful, fragrant and rich in nectar.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When the flowers are small, a number of flowers are clustered into an inflorescence to make them conspicuous. Animals are attracted to flowers by colour and/or fragrance.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The flowers pollinated by flies and beetles secrete foul odours to attract these animals.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "To sustain animal visits, the flowers have to provide rewards to the animals.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nectar and pollen grains are the usual floral rewards.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For harvesting the reward(s) from the flower the animal visitor comes in contact with the anthers and the stigma. The body of the animal gets a coating of pollen grains, which are generally sticky in animal pollinated flowers. When the animal carrying pollen on its body comes in contact with the stigma, it brings about pollination.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241522", "241642", "242026"]',NULL),
(3492,30,85,'paragraph',NULL,'In some species floral rewards are in providing safe places to lay eggs; an example is that of the tallest flower of Amorphophallusang="en-GB"> (the flower itself is about 6 feet in height). A similar relationship exists between a species of moth and the plant Yucca where both species – moth and the plant – cannot complete their life cycles without each other. The moth deposits its eggs in the locule of the ovary and the flower, in turn, gets pollinated by the moth. The larvae of the moth come out of the eggs as the seeds start developing.','[{"s": "In some species floral rewards are in providing safe places to lay eggs; an example is that of the tallest flower of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Amorphophallusang=\\"en-GB\\">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(the flower itself is about 6 feet in height). A similar relationship exists between a species of moth and the plant Yucca where both species – moth and the plant – cannot complete their life cycles without each other.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The moth deposits its eggs in the locule of the ovary and the flower, in turn, gets pollinated by the moth. The larvae of the moth come out of the eggs as the seeds start developing.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["242027"]',NULL),
