(3964,34,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethers are classified as simple or symmetrical, if the alkyl or aryl groups attached to the oxygen atom are the same, and mixed or unsymmetrical, if the two groups are different. Diethyl ether, C 2 H 5 OC 2 H 5 , is a symmetrical ether whereas C 2 H 5 OCH 3 and C 2 H 5 OC 6 H 5 are unsymmetrical ethers.','[{"s": "Ethers are classified as simple or symmetrical, if the alkyl or aryl groups attached to the oxygen atom are the same, and mixed or unsymmetrical, if the two groups are different. Diethyl ether, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is a symmetrical ether whereas C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are unsymmetrical ethers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3965,34,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3966,34,30,'paragraph',NULL,'11.1 Classify the following as primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols:','[{"s": "11.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Classify the following as primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3967,34,31,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image139.png'),
(3968,34,32,'paragraph',NULL,'11.2 Identify allylic alcohols in the above examples.','[{"s": "11.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Identify allylic alcohols in the above examples.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3969,34,33,'heading',3,'11.2 Nomenclature',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3970,34,34,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Alcohols: The common name of an alcohol is derived from the common name of the alkyl group and adding the word alcohol to it. For example, CH 3 OH is methyl alcohol.','[{"s": "(a) Alcohols:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The common name of an alcohol is derived from the common name of the alkyl group and adding the word alcohol to it. For example, CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH is methyl alcohol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3971,34,35,'paragraph',NULL,'According to IUPAC system (Unit 12, Class XI), the name of an alcohol is derived from the name of the alkane from which the alcohol is derived, by substituting ‘ e ’ of alkane with the suffix ‘ ol ’. The position of substituents are indicated by numerals. For this, the longest carbon chain (parent chain) is numbered starting at the end nearest to the hydroxyl group. The positions of the –OH group and other substituents are indicated by using the numbers of carbon atoms to which these are attached. For naming polyhydric alcohols, the ‘e’ of alkane is retained and the ending ‘ol’ is added. The number of – OH groups is indicated by adding the multiplicative prefix, di, tri, etc., before ‘ol’. The positions of – OH groups are indicated by appropriate locants, e.g., HO–CH 2 –CH 2 –OH is named as ethane–1, 2-diol. Table 11.1 gives common and IUPAC names of a few alcohols as examples.','[{"s": "According to IUPAC system (Unit 12, Class XI), the name of an alcohol is derived from the name of the alkane from which the alcohol is derived, by substituting ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’ of alkane with the suffix ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’. The position of substituents are indicated by numerals. For this, the longest carbon chain (parent chain) is numbered starting at the end nearest to the hydroxyl group. The positions of the –OH group and other substituents are indicated by using the numbers of carbon atoms to which these are attached. For naming polyhydric alcohols, the ‘e’ of alkane is retained and the ending ‘ol’ is added. The number of –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups is indicated by adding the multiplicative prefix, di, tri, etc., before ‘ol’. The positions of –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups are indicated by appropriate locants, e.g., HO–CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–OH is named as ethane–1, 2-diol. Table 11.1 gives common and IUPAC names of a few alcohols as examples.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3972,34,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.1: Common and IUPAC Names of Some Alcohols','[{"s": "Table 11.1: Common and IUPAC Names of Some Alcohols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3973,34,37,'image',NULL,'Img03',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image73.png'),
(3974,34,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Cyclic alcohols are named using the prefix cyclo and considering the —OH group attached to C–1.','[{"s": "Cyclic alcohols are named using the prefix cyclo and considering the —OH group attached to C–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3975,34,39,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image24.png'),
(3976,34,40,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Phenols: The simplest hydroxy derivative of benzene is phenol. It is its common name and also an accepted IUPAC name. As structure of phenol involves a benzene ring, in its substituted compounds the terms ortho (1,2- disubstituted), meta (1,3-disubstituted) and para (1,4-disubstituted) are often used in the common names.','[{"s": "(b) Phenols:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The simplest hydroxy derivative of benzene is phenol. It is its common name and also an accepted IUPAC name. As structure of phenol involves a benzene ring, in its substituted compounds the terms ortho (1,2- disubstituted), meta (1,3-disubstituted) and para (1,4-disubstituted) are often used in the common names.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3977,34,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Common name IUPAC name','[{"s": "Common name IUPAC name", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3978,34,42,'image',NULL,'Img04',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image129.png'),
(3979,34,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Dihydroxy derivatives of benzene are known as 1, 2-, 1, 3- and 1, 4-benzenediol.','[{"s": "Dihydroxy derivatives of benzene are known as 1, 2-, 1, 3- and 1, 4-benzenediol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3980,34,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Common name IUPAC name','[{"s": "Common name IUPAC name", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
