(4004,34,68,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image13.png','[{"s": "(v)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4005,34,69,'heading',3,'11.3 Structures of Functional Groups',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4006,34,70,'paragraph',NULL,'In alcohols, the oxygen of the – OH group is attached to carbon by a sigma (σ ) bond formed by the overlap of a sp 3 hybridised orbital of carbon with a sp 3 hybridised orbital of oxygen. Fig. 11.1 depicts structural aspects of methanol, phenol and methoxymethane.','[{"s": "In alcohols, the oxygen of the –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group is attached to carbon by a sigma (σ ) bond formed by the overlap of a sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised orbital of carbon with a sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised orbital of oxygen. Fig. 11.1 depicts structural aspects of methanol, phenol and methoxymethane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4007,34,71,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image70.png'),
(4008,34,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.1 : Structures of methanol, phenol and methoxymethane','[{"s": "Fig. 11.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Structures of methanol, phenol and methoxymethane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6369,37,501,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, formation of methanol from bromoethane depends upon the orientation of reactant molecules as shown in Fig. 4.12. The proper orientation of reactant molecules lead to bond formation whereas improper orientation makes them simply bounce back and no products are formed.','[{"s": "For example, formation of methanol from bromoethane depends upon the orientation of reactant molecules as shown in Fig. 4.12.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The proper orientation of reactant molecules lead to bond formation whereas improper orientation makes them simply bounce back and no products are formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6370,37,502,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image66.png'),
(6371,37,503,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.12: Diagram showing molecules having proper and improper orientation','[{"s": "Fig. 4.12: Diagram showing molecules having proper and improper orientation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7863,39,583,'paragraph',NULL,'55. Discuss and compare the trend in ionisation enthalpy of the elements of group 1 with those of group 17 elements.','[{"s": "55. Discuss and compare the trend in ionisation enthalpy of the elements of group 1 with those of group 17 elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7864,40,0,'heading',1,'Unit 9',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4009,34,73,'paragraph',NULL,'The bond angle Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image133.png in alcohols is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle (109°-28′). It is due to the repulsion between the unshared electron pairs of oxygen. In phenols, the –OH group is attached to sp 2 hybridised carbon of an aromatic ring. The carbon– oxygen bond length (136 pm) in phenol is slightly less than that in methanol. This is due to (i) partial double bond character on account of the conjugation of unshared electron pair of oxygen with the aromatic ring (Section 11.4.4) and (ii) sp 2 hybridised state of carbon to which oxygen is attached.','[{"s": "The bond angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image133.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "in alcohols is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle (109°-28′). It is due to the repulsion between the unshared electron pairs of oxygen. In phenols, the –OH group is attached to sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised carbon of an aromatic ring. The carbon– oxygen bond length (136 pm) in phenol is slightly less than that in methanol. This is due to (i) partial double bond character on account of the conjugation of unshared electron pair of oxygen with the aromatic ring (Section 11.4.4) and (ii) sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised state of carbon to which oxygen is attached.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4010,34,74,'paragraph',NULL,'In ethers, the four electron pairs, i.e., the two bond pairs and two lone pairs of electrons on oxygen are arranged approximately in a tetrahedral arrangement. The bond angle is slightly greater than the tetrahedral angle due to the repulsive interaction between the two bulky (–R) groups. The C – O bond length (141 pm) is almost the same as in alcohols.','[{"s": "In ethers, the four electron pairs, i.e., the two bond pairs and two lone pairs of electrons on oxygen are arranged approximately in a tetrahedral arrangement. The bond angle is slightly greater than the tetrahedral angle due to the repulsive interaction between the two bulky (–R) groups. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond length (141 pm) is almost the same as in alcohols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4011,34,75,'heading',3,'11.4 Alcohols and Phenols',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4012,34,76,'heading',4,'11.4.1 Preparation of Alcohols',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4013,34,77,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols are prepared by the following methods:','[{"s": "Alcohols are prepared by the following methods:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4014,34,78,'paragraph',NULL,'1. From alkenes','[{"s": "1. From alkenes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4015,34,79,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) By acid catalysed hydration: Alkenes react with water in the presence of acid as catalyst to form alcohols. In case of unsymmetrical alkenes, the addition reaction takes place in accordance with Markovnikov’s rule (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "(i) By acid catalysed hydration: Alkenes react with water in the presence of acid as catalyst to form alcohols. In case of unsymmetrical alkenes, the addition reaction takes place in accordance with Markovnikov’s rule (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4016,34,80,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image98.png'),
(4017,34,81,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image99.png'),
(4018,34,82,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanism','[{"s": "Mechanism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4019,34,83,'paragraph',NULL,'The mechanism of the reaction involves the following three steps:','[{"s": "The mechanism of the reaction involves the following three steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4020,34,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: Protonation of alkene to form carbocation by electrophilic attack of H 3 O + .','[{"s": "Step 1: Protonation of alkene to form carbocation by electrophilic attack of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4021,34,85,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 O + H + → H 3 O +','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4022,34,86,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image142.png'),
(4023,34,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: Nucleophilic attack of water on carbocation.','[{"s": "Step 2: Nucleophilic attack of water on carbocation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4024,34,88,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image1.png'),
(4025,34,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Deprotonation to form an alcohol.','[{"s": "Step 3: Deprotonation to form an alcohol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4026,34,90,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image12.png'),
(4027,34,91,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) By hydroboration–oxidation: Diborane (BH 3 ) 2 reacts with alkenes to give trialkyl boranes as addition product. This is oxidised to alcohol by hydrogen peroxide in the presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide.','[{"s": "(ii) By hydroboration–oxidation: Diborane (BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reacts with alkenes to give trialkyl boranes as addition product.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is oxidised to alcohol by hydrogen peroxide in the presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250969"]',NULL),
(4028,34,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydroboration - oxidation was first reported by H.C. Brown in 1959. For his studies on boron containing organic compounds, Brown shared the 1979 Nobel prize in Chemistry with G. Wittig.','[{"s": "Hydroboration - oxidation was first reported by H.C. Brown in 1959. For his studies on boron containing organic compounds, Brown shared the 1979 Nobel prize in Chemistry with G. Wittig.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4029,34,93,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image147.png'),
(4030,34,94,'paragraph',NULL,'The addition of borane to the double bond takes place in such a manner that the boron atom gets attached to the sp 2 carbon carrying greater number of hydrogen atoms. The alcohol so formed looks as if it has been formed by the addition of water to the alkene in a way opposite to the Markovnikov’s rule. In this reaction, alcohol is obtained in excellent yield.','[{"s": "The addition of borane to the double bond takes place in such a manner that the boron atom gets attached to the sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "carbon carrying greater number of hydrogen atoms. The alcohol so formed looks as if it has been formed by the addition of water to the alkene in a way opposite to the Markovnikov’s rule. In this reaction, alcohol is obtained in excellent yield.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7865,40,1,'heading',2,'Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4031,34,95,'paragraph',NULL,'2. From carbonyl compounds','[{"s": "2. From carbonyl compounds", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4032,34,96,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) By reduction of aldehydes and ketones: Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to the corresponding alcohols by addition of hydrogen in the presence of catalysts (catalytic hydrogenation). The usual catalyst is a finely divided metal such as platinum, palladium or nickel. It is also prepared by treating aldehydes and ketones with sodium borohydride (NaBH 4 ) or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH 4 ). Aldehydes yield primary alcohols whereas ketones give secondary alcohols.','[{"s": "(i) By reduction of aldehydes and ketones: Aldehydes and ketones are reduced to the corresponding alcohols by addition of hydrogen in the presence of catalysts (catalytic hydrogenation). The usual catalyst is a finely divided metal such as platinum, palladium or nickel. It is also prepared by treating aldehydes and ketones with sodium borohydride (NaBH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Aldehydes yield primary alcohols whereas ketones give secondary alcohols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4033,34,97,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image57.png'),
(4034,34,98,'paragraph',NULL,'The numbers in front of the reagents along the arrow indicate that the second reagent is added only when the reaction with first is complete.','[{"s": "The numbers in front of the reagents along the arrow indicate that the second reagent is added only when the reaction with first is complete.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4035,34,99,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) By reduction of carboxylic acids and esters: Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols in excellent yields by lithium aluminium hydride, a strong reducing agent.','[{"s": "(ii) By reduction of carboxylic acids and esters: Carboxylic acids are reduced to primary alcohols in excellent yields by lithium aluminium hydride, a strong reducing agent.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4036,34,100,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image75.png'),
(4037,34,101,'paragraph',NULL,'However, LiAlH 4 is an expensive reagent, and therefore, used for preparing special chemicals only. Commercially, acids are reduced to alcohols by converting them to the esters (Section 11.4.4), followed by their reduction using hydrogen in the presence of catalyst (catalytic hydrogenation).','[{"s": "However, LiAlH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an expensive reagent, and therefore, used for preparing special chemicals only. Commercially, acids are reduced to alcohols by converting them to the esters (Section 11.4.4), followed by their reduction using hydrogen in the presence of catalyst (catalytic hydrogenation).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4038,34,102,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image89.png'),
(4039,34,103,'paragraph',NULL,'3. From Grignard reagents','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From Grignard reagents", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250918"]',NULL),
(4040,34,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols are produced by the reaction of Grignard reagents (Unit 10, Class XII) with aldehydes and ketones.','[{"s": "Alcohols are produced by the reaction of Grignard reagents (Unit 10, Class XII) with aldehydes and ketones.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4041,34,105,'paragraph',NULL,'The first step of the reaction is the nucleophilic addition of Grignard reagent to the carbonyl group to form an adduct. Hydrolysis of the adduct yields an alcohol.','[{"s": "The first step of the reaction is the nucleophilic addition of Grignard reagent to the carbonyl group to form an adduct.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hydrolysis of the adduct yields an alcohol.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250964"]',NULL),
(4042,34,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image80.png ....(i)','[{"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image80.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "....(i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4043,34,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image158.png .....(ii)','[{"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image158.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".....(ii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4044,34,108,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction of Grignard reagents with methanal produces a primary alcohol, with other aldehydes, secondary alcohols and with ketones, tertiary alcohols.','[{"s": "The reaction of Grignard reagents with methanal produces a primary alcohol, with other aldehydes, secondary alcohols and with ketones, tertiary alcohols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4045,34,109,'paragraph',NULL,'The overall reactions using different aldehydes and ketones are as follows:','[{"s": "The overall reactions using different aldehydes and ketones are as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4046,34,110,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image8.png'),
(4047,34,111,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image130.png'),
(4048,34,112,'paragraph',NULL,'You will notice that the reaction produces a primary alcohol with methanal, a secondary alcohol with other aldehydes and tertiary alcohol with ketones.','[{"s": "You will notice that the reaction produces a primary alcohol with methanal, a secondary alcohol with other aldehydes and tertiary alcohol with ketones.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4049,34,113,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.2','[{"s": "Example 11.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4050,34,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Give the structures and IUPAC names of the products expected from the following reactions:','[{"s": "Give the structures and IUPAC names of the products expected from the following reactions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4051,34,115,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Catalytic reduction of butanal.','[{"s": "(a) Catalytic reduction of butanal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4052,34,116,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Hydration of propene in the presence of dilute sulphuric acid.','[{"s": "(b) Hydration of propene in the presence of dilute sulphuric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4053,34,117,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Reaction of propanone with methylmagnesium bromide followed by hydrolysis.','[{"s": "(c) Reaction of propanone with methylmagnesium bromide followed by hydrolysis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4054,34,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4055,34,119,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image32.png','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image32.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4056,34,120,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image96.png','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image96.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4057,34,121,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image3.png','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4058,34,122,'heading',4,'11.4.2 Preparation of Phenols',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4059,34,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Phenol, also known as carbolic acid, was first isolated in the early nineteenth century from coal tar. Nowadays, phenol is commercially produced synthetically. In the laboratory, phenols are prepared from benzene derivatives by any of the following methods:','[{"s": "Phenol, also known as carbolic acid, was first isolated in the early nineteenth century from coal tar. Nowadays, phenol is commercially produced synthetically. In the laboratory, phenols are prepared from benzene derivatives by any of the following methods:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4060,34,124,'paragraph',NULL,'1. From haloarenes','[{"s": "1. From haloarenes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4061,34,125,'paragraph',NULL,'Chlorobenzene is fused with NaOH at 623K and 320 atmospheric pressure. Phenol is obtained by acidification of sodium phenoxide so produced (Unit 10, Class XII).','[{"s": "Chlorobenzene is fused with NaOH at 623K and 320 atmospheric pressure. Phenol is obtained by acidification of sodium phenoxide so produced (Unit 10, Class XII).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4062,34,126,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image119.png'),
(4063,34,127,'paragraph',NULL,'2. From benzenesulphonic acid','[{"s": "2. From benzenesulphonic acid", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4064,34,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene is sulphonated with oleum and benzene sulphonic acid so formed is converted to sodium phenoxide on heating with molten sodium hydroxide. Acidification of the sodium salt gives phenol.','[{"s": "Benzene is sulphonated with oleum and benzene sulphonic acid so formed is converted to sodium phenoxide on heating with molten sodium hydroxide. Acidification of the sodium salt gives phenol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4065,34,129,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image117.png'),
(4066,34,130,'paragraph',NULL,'3. From diazonium salts','[{"s": "3. From diazonium salts", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4067,34,131,'paragraph',NULL,'A diazonium salt is formed by treating an aromatic primary amine with nitrous acid (NaNO 2 + HCl) at 273-278 K. Diazonium salts are hydrolysed to phenols by warming with water or by treating with dilute acids (Unit 13, Class XII).','[{"s": "A diazonium salt is formed by treating an aromatic primary amine with nitrous acid (NaNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ HCl) at 273-278 K. Diazonium salts are hydrolysed to phenols by warming with water or by treating with dilute acids (Unit 13, Class XII).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4068,34,132,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image104.png'),
(4069,34,133,'paragraph',NULL,'Most of the worldwide production of phenol is from cumene.','[{"s": "Most of the worldwide production of phenol is from cumene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4070,34,134,'paragraph',NULL,'4. From cumene','[{"s": "4. From cumene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4071,34,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Phenol is manufactured from the hydrocarbon, cumene . Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is oxidised in the presence of air to cumene hydroperoxide. It is converted to phenol and acetone by treating it with dilute acid. Acetone, a by-product of this reaction, is also obtained in large quantities by this method.','[{"s": "Phenol is manufactured from the hydrocarbon, cumene", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is oxidised in the presence of air to cumene hydroperoxide. It is converted to phenol and acetone by treating it with dilute acid. Acetone, a by-product of this reaction, is also obtained in large quantities by this method.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250901"]',NULL),
(4072,34,136,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image47.png'),
(4073,34,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4074,34,138,'paragraph',NULL,'11.4 Show how are the following alcohols prepared by the reaction of a suitable Grignard reagent on methanal ?','[{"s": "11.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show how are the following alcohols prepared by the reaction of a suitable Grignard reagent on methanal ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4075,34,139,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image64.png'),
(4076,34,140,'paragraph',NULL,'11.5 Write structures of the products of the following reactions:','[{"s": "11.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write structures of the products of the following reactions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4077,34,141,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image159.png','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image159.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4078,34,142,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image33.png','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4079,34,143,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image54.png','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image54.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4080,34,144,'paragraph',NULL,'11.4.3 Physical Properties','[{"s": "11.4.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Physical Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4081,34,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols and phenols consist of two parts, an alkyl/aryl group and a hydroxyl group. The properties of alcohols and phenols are chiefly due to the hydroxyl group. The nature of alkyl and aryl groups simply modify these properties.','[{"s": "Alcohols and phenols consist of two parts, an alkyl/aryl group and a hydroxyl group. The properties of alcohols and phenols are chiefly due to the hydroxyl group. The nature of alkyl and aryl groups simply modify these properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4082,34,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Boiling Points','[{"s": "Boiling Points", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4083,34,147,'paragraph',NULL,'The boiling points of alcohols and phenols increase with increase in the number of carbon atoms (increase in van der Waals forces). In alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain (because of decrease in van der Waals forces with decrease in surface area).','[{"s": "The boiling points of alcohols and phenols increase with increase in the number of carbon atoms (increase in van der Waals forces). In alcohols, the boiling points decrease with increase of branching in carbon chain (because of decrease in van der Waals forces with decrease in surface area).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4084,34,148,'paragraph',NULL,'The –OH group in alcohols and phenols is involved in intermolecular hydrogen bonding as shown below:','[{"s": "The –OH group in alcohols and phenols is involved in intermolecular hydrogen bonding as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4085,34,149,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image17.png'),
(4086,34,150,'paragraph',NULL,'It is interesting to note that boiling points of alcohols and phenols are higher in comparison to other classes of compounds, namely hydrocarbons, ethers, haloalkanes and haloarenes of comparable molecular masses. For example, ethanol and propane have comparable molecular masses but their boiling points differ widely. The boiling point of methoxymethane is intermediate of the two boiling points.','[{"s": "It is interesting to note that boiling points of alcohols and phenols are higher in comparison to other classes of compounds, namely hydrocarbons, ethers, haloalkanes and haloarenes of comparable molecular masses. For example, ethanol and propane have comparable molecular masses but their boiling points differ widely. The boiling point of methoxymethane is intermediate of the two boiling points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4087,34,151,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image112.png'),
(4088,34,152,'paragraph',NULL,'The high boiling points of alcohols are mainly due to the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding in them which is lacking in ethers and hydrocarbons.','[{"s": "The high boiling points of alcohols are mainly due to the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding in them which is lacking in ethers and hydrocarbons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4089,34,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Solubility','[{"s": "Solubility", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4090,34,154,'paragraph',NULL,'Solubility of alcohols and phenols in water is due to their ability to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules as shown. The solubility decreases with increase in size of alkyl/aryl (hydro-phobic) groups. Several of the lower molecular mass alcohols are miscible with water in all proportions.','[{"s": "Solubility of alcohols and phenols in water is due to their ability to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules as shown. The solubility decreases with increase in size of alkyl/aryl (hydro-phobic) groups. Several of the lower molecular mass alcohols are miscible with water in all proportions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4091,34,155,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image114.png'),
(4092,34,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.3','[{"s": "Example 11.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4093,34,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Arrange the following sets of compounds in order of their increasing boiling points:','[{"s": "Arrange the following sets of compounds in order of their increasing boiling points:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4094,34,158,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Pentan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, ethanol, propan-1-ol, methanol.','[{"s": "(a) Pentan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, ethanol, propan-1-ol, methanol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4095,34,159,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Pentan-1-ol, n-butane, pentanal, ethoxyethane.','[{"s": "(b) Pentan-1-ol, n-butane, pentanal, ethoxyethane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4096,34,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4097,34,161,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Methanol, ethanol, propan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, butan-1-ol, pentan-1-ol.','[{"s": "(a) Methanol, ethanol, propan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, butan-1-ol, pentan-1-ol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4098,34,162,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) n-Butane, ethoxyethane, pentanal and pentan-1-ol.','[{"s": "(b) n-Butane, ethoxyethane, pentanal and pentan-1-ol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4099,34,163,'heading',4,'11.4.4 Chemical Reactions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4100,34,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols are versatile compounds. They react both as nucleophiles and electrophiles. The bond between O–H is broken when alcohols react as nucleophiles.','[{"s": "Alcohols are versatile compounds. They react both as nucleophiles and electrophiles. The bond between O–H is broken when alcohols react as nucleophiles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4101,34,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols as nucleophiles','[{"s": "Alcohols as nucleophiles", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4102,34,166,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image14.png','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4103,34,167,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The bond between C–O is broken when they react as electrophiles. Protonated alcohols react in this manner.','[{"s": "(ii) The bond between C–O is broken when they react as electrophiles. Protonated alcohols react in this manner.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4104,34,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Protonated alcohols as electrophiles','[{"s": "Protonated alcohols as electrophiles", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4105,34,169,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image22.png'),
(4106,34,170,'paragraph',NULL,'Based on the cleavage of O–H and C–O bonds, the reactions of alcohols and phenols may be divided into two groups:','[{"s": "Based on the cleavage of O–H and C–O bonds, the reactions of alcohols and phenols may be divided into two groups:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4107,34,171,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Reactions involving cleavage of O–H bond','[{"s": "(a) Reactions involving cleavage of O–H bond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4108,34,172,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Acidity of alcohols and phenols','[{"s": "1. Acidity of alcohols and phenols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4109,34,173,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Reaction with metals: Alcohols and phenols react with active metals such as sodium, potassium and aluminium to yield corresponding alkoxides/phenoxides and hydrogen.','[{"s": "(i) Reaction with metals: Alcohols and phenols react with active metals such as sodium, potassium and aluminium to yield corresponding alkoxides/phenoxides and hydrogen.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250970"]',NULL),
(4110,34,174,'image',NULL,'Img09',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image27.png'),
(4111,34,175,'paragraph',NULL,'In addition to this, phenols react with aqueous sodium hydroxide to form sodium phenoxides.','[{"s": "In addition to this, phenols react with aqueous sodium hydroxide to form sodium phenoxides.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4112,34,176,'image',NULL,'Img10',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image124.png'),
(4113,34,177,'paragraph',NULL,'The above reactions show that alcohols and phenols are acidic in nature. In fact, alcohols and phenols are Brönsted acids i.e., they can donate a proton to a stronger base (B:).','[{"s": "The above reactions show that alcohols and phenols are acidic in nature. In fact, alcohols and phenols are Brönsted acids i.e., they can donate a proton to a stronger base (B:).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4114,34,178,'image',NULL,'Img11',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image62.png'),
(7866,40,2,'image',NULL,'12085CH09',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image17.png'),
(9198,41,730,'paragraph',NULL,'D. Element with highest heat of atomisation','[{"s": "D. Element with highest heat of atomisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4115,34,179,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Acidity of alcohols: The acidic character of alcohols is due to the polar nature of O–H bond. An electron-releasing group (–CH 3 , –C 2 H 5 ) increases electron density on oxygen tending to decrease the polarity of O-H bond. This decreases the acid strength. For this reason, the acid strength of alcohols decreases in the following order:','[{"s": "(ii) Acidity of alcohols: The acidic character of alcohols is due to the polar nature of O–H bond.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "An electron-releasing group (–CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") increases electron density on oxygen tending to decrease the polarity of O-H bond. This decreases the acid strength. For this reason, the acid strength of alcohols decreases in the following order:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250971"]',NULL),
(4116,34,180,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image152.png'),
(4117,34,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols are, however, weaker acids than water. This can be illustrated by the reaction of water with an alkoxide.','[{"s": "Alcohols are, however, weaker acids than water. This can be illustrated by the reaction of water with an alkoxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4118,34,182,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image71.png'),
(4119,34,183,'paragraph',NULL,'This reaction shows that water is a better proton donor (i.e., stronger acid) than alcohol. Also, in the above reaction, we note that an alkoxide ion is a better proton acceptor than hydroxide ion, which suggests that alkoxides are stronger bases (sodium ethoxide is a stronger base than sodium hydroxide).','[{"s": "This reaction shows that water is a better proton donor (i.e., stronger acid) than alcohol. Also, in the above reaction, we note that an alkoxide ion is a better proton acceptor than hydroxide ion, which suggests that alkoxides are stronger bases (sodium ethoxide is a stronger base than sodium hydroxide).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4120,34,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols act as Bronsted bases as well. It is due to the presence of unshared electron pairs on oxygen, which makes them proton acceptors.','[{"s": "Alcohols act as Bronsted bases as well. It is due to the presence of unshared electron pairs on oxygen, which makes them proton acceptors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4121,34,185,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Acidity of phenols: The reactions of phenol with metals (e.g., sodium, aluminium) and sodium hydroxide indicate its acidic nature. 2 The hydroxyl group, in phenol is directly attached to the sp 2 hybridised carbon of benzene ring which acts as an electron withdrawing group. Due to this, the charge distribution in phenol molecule, as depicted in its resonance structures, causes the oxygen of –OH group to be positive.','[{"s": "(iii) Acidity of phenols: The reactions of phenol with metals (e.g., sodium, aluminium) and sodium hydroxide indicate its acidic nature.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The hydroxyl group, in phenol is directly attached to the sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised carbon of benzene ring which acts as an electron withdrawing group. Due to this, the charge distribution in phenol molecule, as depicted in its resonance structures, causes the oxygen of –OH group to be positive.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250924", "250971"]',NULL),
(4122,34,186,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image155.png'),
(4123,34,187,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction of phenol with aqueous sodium hydroxide indicates that phenols are stronger acids than alcohols and water. Let us examine how a compound in which hydroxyl group attached to an aromatic ring is more acidic than the one in which hydroxyl group is attached to an alkyl group.','[{"s": "The reaction of phenol with aqueous sodium hydroxide indicates that phenols are stronger acids than alcohols and water. Let us examine how a compound in which hydroxyl group attached to an aromatic ring is more acidic than the one in which hydroxyl group is attached to an alkyl group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4124,34,188,'paragraph',NULL,'The ionisation of an alcohol and a phenol takes place as follows:','[{"s": "The ionisation of an alcohol and a phenol takes place as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4125,34,189,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image163.png'),
(4126,34,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to the higher electronegativity of sp 2 hybridised carbon of phenol to which –OH is attached, electron density decreases on oxygen. This increases the polarity of O–H bond and results in an increase in ionisation of phenols than that of alcohols. Now let us examine the stabilities of alkoxide and phenoxide ions. In alkoxide ion, the negative charge is localised on oxygen while in phenoxide ion, the charge is delocalised.','[{"s": "Due to the higher electronegativity of sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised carbon of phenol to which –OH is attached, electron density decreases on oxygen. This increases the polarity of O–H bond and results in an increase in ionisation of phenols than that of alcohols. Now let us examine the stabilities of alkoxide and phenoxide ions. In alkoxide ion, the negative charge is localised on oxygen while in phenoxide ion, the charge is delocalised.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4127,34,191,'paragraph',NULL,'The delocalisation of negative charge (structures I-V) makes phenoxide ion more stable and favours the ionisation of phenol. Although there is also charge delocalisation in phenol, its resonance structures have charge separation due to which the phenol molecule is less stable than phenoxide ion.','[{"s": "The delocalisation of negative charge (structures I-V) makes phenoxide ion more stable and favours the ionisation of phenol.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Although there is also charge delocalisation in phenol, its resonance structures have charge separation due to which the phenol molecule is less stable than phenoxide ion.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250899"]',NULL),
(4128,34,192,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image69.png'),
(6372,37,504,'paragraph',NULL,'To account for effective collisions, another factor P, called the probability or steric factor is introduced. It takes into account the fact that in a collision, molecules must be properly oriented i.e.,','[{"s": "To account for effective collisions, another factor P, called the probability or steric factor is introduced. It takes into account the fact that in a collision, molecules must be properly oriented i.e.,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6373,37,505,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image114.png'),
(6374,37,506,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in collision theory activation energy and proper orientation of the molecules together determine the criteria for an effective collision and hence the rate of a chemical reaction.','[{"s": "Thus, in collision theory activation energy and proper orientation of the molecules together determine the criteria for an effective collision and hence the rate of a chemical reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4129,34,193,'paragraph',NULL,'In substituted phenols, the presence of electron withdrawing groups such as nitro group, enhances the acidic strength of phenol. This effect is more pronounced when such a group is present at ortho and para positions. It is due to the effective delocalisation of negative charge in phenoxide ion when substituent is at ortho or para position. On the other hand, electron releasing groups, such as alkyl groups, in general, do not favour the formation of phenoxide ion resulting in decrease in acid strength. Cresols, for example, are less acidic than phenol.','[{"s": "In substituted phenols, the presence of electron withdrawing groups such as nitro group, enhances the acidic strength of phenol.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This effect is more pronounced when such a group is present at ortho and para positions. It is due to the effective delocalisation of negative charge in phenoxide ion when substituent is at ortho or para position. On the other hand, electron releasing groups, such as alkyl groups, in general, do not favour the formation of phenoxide ion resulting in decrease in acid strength. Cresols, for example, are less acidic than phenol.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250971"]',NULL),
(4130,34,194,'paragraph',NULL,'The greater the pK a value, the weaker the acid.','[{"s": "The greater the pK", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "value, the weaker the acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4131,34,195,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.3: pK a Values of some Phenols and Ethanol','[{"s": "Table 11.3:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pK", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Values of some Phenols and Ethanol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
