(4140,34,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols and phenols react with carboxylic acids, acid chlorides and acid anhydrides to form esters.','[{"s": "Alcohols and phenols react with carboxylic acids, acid chlorides and acid anhydrides to form esters.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4141,34,205,'image',NULL,'Img13',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image122.png'),
(4142,34,206,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image74.png'),
(4143,34,207,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction with carboxylic acid and acid anhydride is carried out in the presence of a small amount of concentrated sulphuric acid. The reaction is reversible, and therefore, water is removed as soon as it is formed. The reaction with acid chloride is carried out in the presence of a base (pyridine) so as to neutralise HCl which is formed during the reaction. It shifts the equilibrium to the right hand side. The introduction of acetyl (CH 3 CO) group in alcohols or phenols is known as acetylation. Acetylation of salicylic acid produces aspirin.','[{"s": "The reaction with carboxylic acid and acid anhydride is carried out in the presence of a small amount of concentrated sulphuric acid. The reaction is reversible, and therefore, water is removed as soon as it is formed. The reaction with acid chloride is carried out in the presence of a base (pyridine) so as to neutralise HCl which is formed during the reaction. It shifts the equilibrium to the right hand side. The introduction of acetyl (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CO) group in alcohols or phenols is known as acetylation. Acetylation of salicylic acid produces aspirin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4144,34,208,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image156.png'),
(4145,34,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Aspirin possesses analgesic, anti-inflammatory and antipyretic properties.','[{"s": "Aspirin possesses analgesic, anti-inflammatory and antipyretic properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4146,34,210,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Reactions involving cleavage of carbon – oxygen (C–O) bond in alcohols','[{"s": "(b) Reactions involving cleavage of carbon – oxygen (C–O) bond in alcohols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4147,34,211,'paragraph',NULL,'The reactions involving cleavage of C–O bond take place only in alcohols. Phenols show this type of reaction only with zinc.','[{"s": "The reactions involving cleavage of C–O bond take place only in alcohols. Phenols show this type of reaction only with zinc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4148,34,212,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Reaction with hydrogen halides: Alcohols react with hydrogen halides to form alkyl halides (Refer Unit 10, Class XII).','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Reaction with hydrogen halides:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Alcohols react with hydrogen halides to form alkyl halides (Refer Unit 10, Class XII).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250973"]',NULL),
(4149,34,213,'paragraph',NULL,'ROH + HX → R–X + H 2 O','[{"s": "ROH + HX → R–X + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6375,37,507,'paragraph',NULL,'Collision theory also has certain drawbacks as it considers atoms/molecules to be hard spheres and ignores their structural aspect. You will study details about this theory and more on other theories in your higher classes.','[{"s": "Collision theory also has certain drawbacks as it considers atoms/molecules to be hard spheres and ignores their structural aspect. You will study details about this theory and more on other theories in your higher classes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6376,37,508,'paragraph',NULL,'* Threshold energy = Activation Energy + energy possessed by reacting species.','[{"s": "* Threshold energy = Activation Energy + energy possessed by reacting species.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6377,37,509,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4150,34,214,'paragraph',NULL,'The difference in reactivity of three classes of alcohols with HCl distinguishes them from one another ( Lucas test ) . Alcohols are soluble in Lucas reagent (conc. HCl and ZnCl 2 ) while their halides are immiscible and produce turbidity in solution. In case of tertiary alcohols, turbidity is produced immediately as they form the halides easily. Primary alcohols do not produce turbidity at room temperature.','[{"s": "The difference in reactivity of three classes of alcohols with HCl distinguishes them from one another (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Lucas test", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols are soluble in Lucas reagent (conc. HCl and ZnCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") while their halides are immiscible and produce turbidity in solution. In case of tertiary alcohols, turbidity is produced immediately as they form the halides easily. Primary alcohols do not produce turbidity at room temperature.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250898"]',NULL),
(4151,34,215,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Reaction with phosphorus trihalides: Alcohols are converted to alkyl bromides by reaction with phosphorus tribromide (Refer Unit 10, Class XII).','[{"s": "2. Reaction with phosphorus trihalides:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols are converted to alkyl bromides by reaction with phosphorus tribromide (Refer Unit 10, Class XII).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4152,34,216,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Dehydration: Alcohols undergo dehydration (removal of a molecule of water) to form alkenes on treating with a protic acid e.g., concentrated H 2 SO 4 or H 3 PO 4 , or catalysts such as anhydrous zinc chloride or alumina (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "3. Dehydration:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols undergo dehydration (removal of a molecule of water) to form alkenes on treating with a protic acid e.g., concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", or catalysts such as anhydrous zinc chloride or alumina (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4153,34,217,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image123.png'),
(4154,34,218,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethanol undergoes dehydration by heating it with concentrated H 2 SO 4 at 443 K.','[{"s": "Ethanol undergoes dehydration by heating it with concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 443 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4155,34,219,'image',NULL,'Img14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image51.png'),
(4156,34,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Secondary and tertiary alcohols are dehydrated under milder conditions. For example','[{"s": "Secondary and tertiary alcohols are dehydrated under milder conditions. For example", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4157,34,221,'image',NULL,'Img15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image149.png'),
(4158,34,222,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the relative ease of dehydration of alcohols follows the following order:','[{"s": "Thus, the relative ease of dehydration of alcohols follows the following order:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250974"]',NULL),
(4159,34,223,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image108.png'),
(4160,34,224,'paragraph',NULL,'The mechanism of dehydration of ethanol involves the following steps:','[{"s": "The mechanism of dehydration of ethanol involves the following steps:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250897"]',NULL),
(4161,34,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Tertiary carbocations are more stable and therefore are easier to form than secondary and primary carbocations; tertiary alcohols are the easiest to dehydrate.','[{"s": "Tertiary carbocations are more stable and therefore are easier to form than secondary and primary carbocations; tertiary alcohols are the easiest to dehydrate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4162,34,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanism','[{"s": "Mechanism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4163,34,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: Formation of protonated alcohol.','[{"s": "Step 1: Formation of protonated alcohol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4164,34,228,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image105.png'),
(4165,34,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: Formation of carbocation: It is the slowest step and hence, the rate determining step of the reaction.','[{"s": "Step 2: Formation of carbocation: It is the slowest step and hence, the rate determining step of the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4166,34,230,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image153.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(4167,34,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Formation of ethene by elimination of a proton.','[{"s": "Step 3: Formation of ethene by elimination of a proton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4168,34,232,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image121.png'),
(4169,34,233,'paragraph',NULL,'The acid used in step 1 is released in step 3. To drive the equilibrium to the right, ethene is removed as it is formed.','[{"s": "The acid used in step 1 is released in step 3. To drive the equilibrium to the right, ethene is removed as it is formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4170,34,234,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Oxidation: Oxidation of alcohols involves the formation of a carbon-oxygen double bond with cleavage of an O-H and C-H bonds.','[{"s": "4. Oxidation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oxidation of alcohols involves the formation of a carbon-oxygen double bond with cleavage of an O-H and C-H bonds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4171,34,235,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image78.png'),
(4172,34,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Such a cleavage and formation of bonds occur in oxidation reactions. These are also known as dehydrogenation reactions as these involve loss of dihydrogen from an alcohol molecule. Depending on the oxidising agent used, a primary alcohol is oxidised to an aldehyde which in turn is oxidised to a carboxylic acid.','[{"s": "Such a cleavage and formation of bonds occur in oxidation reactions. These are also known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dehydrogenation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reactions as these involve loss of dihydrogen from an alcohol molecule. Depending on the oxidising agent used, a primary alcohol is oxidised to an aldehyde which in turn is oxidised to a carboxylic acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4173,34,237,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image140.png'),
(6378,37,510,'paragraph',NULL,'4.7 What will be the effect of temperature on rate constant ?','[{"s": "4.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What will be the effect of temperature on rate constant ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9199,41,731,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Ni','[{"s": "5. Ni", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4174,34,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Strong oxidising agents such as acidified potassium permanganate are used for getting carboxylic acids from alcohols directly. CrO 3 in anhydrous medium is used as the oxidising agent for the isolation of aldehydes.','[{"s": "Strong oxidising agents such as acidified potassium permanganate are used for getting carboxylic acids from alcohols directly. CrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in anhydrous medium is used as the oxidising agent for the isolation of aldehydes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4175,34,239,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image77.png'),
(4176,34,240,'paragraph',NULL,'A better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC), a complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine and HCl.','[{"s": "A better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC), a complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine and HCl.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250975"]',NULL),
(4177,34,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image109.png'),
(4178,34,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Secondary alcohols are oxidised to ketones by chromic anhyride (CrO 3 ).','[{"s": "Secondary alcohols are oxidised to ketones by chromic anhyride (CrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4179,34,243,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image136.png'),
(4180,34,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Tertiary alcohols do not undergo oxidation reaction . Under strong reaction conditions such as strong oxidising agents (KMnO 4 ) and elevated temperatures, cleavage of various C-C bonds takes place and a mixture of carboxylic acids containing lesser number of carbon atoms is formed.','[{"s": "Tertiary alcohols do not undergo oxidation reaction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Under strong reaction conditions such as strong oxidising agents (KMnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and elevated temperatures, cleavage of various C-C bonds takes place and a mixture of carboxylic acids containing lesser number of carbon atoms is formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250929"]',NULL),
(4181,34,245,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image87.png'),
(4182,34,246,'paragraph',NULL,'When the vapours of a primary or a secondary alcohol are passed over heated copper at 573 K, dehydrogenation takes place and an aldehyde or a ketone is formed while tertiary alcohols undergo dehydration. 2','[{"s": "When the vapours of a primary or a secondary alcohol are passed over heated copper at 573 K, dehydrogenation takes place and an aldehyde or a ketone is formed while tertiary alcohols undergo dehydration.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250976", "250977"]',NULL),
(4183,34,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Biological oxidation of methanol and ethanol in the body produces the corresponding aldehyde followed by the acid. At times the alcoholics, by mistake, drink ethanol, mixed with methanol also called denatured alcohol. In the body, methanol is oxidised first to methanal and then to methanoic acid, which may cause blindness and death. A methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving intravenous infusions of diluted ethanol. The enzyme responsible for oxidation of aldehyde (HCHO) to acid is swamped allowing time for kidneys to excrete methanol.','[{"s": "Biological oxidation of methanol and ethanol in the body produces the corresponding aldehyde followed by the acid. At times the alcoholics, by mistake, drink ethanol, mixed with methanol also called denatured alcohol. In the body, methanol is oxidised first to methanal and then to methanoic acid, which may cause blindness and death. A methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving intravenous infusions of diluted ethanol. The enzyme responsible for oxidation of aldehyde (HCHO) to acid is swamped allowing time for kidneys to excrete methanol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4184,34,248,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Reactions of phenols','[{"s": "(c) Reactions of phenols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4185,34,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Following reactions are shown by phenols only.','[{"s": "Following reactions are shown by phenols only.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4186,34,250,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Electrophilic aromatic substitution','[{"s": "1. Electrophilic aromatic substitution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4187,34,251,'paragraph',NULL,'In phenols, the reactions that take place on the aromatic ring are electrophilic substitution reactions (Unit 13, Class XI). The –OH group attached to the benzene ring activates it towards electrophilic substitution. Also, it directs the incoming group to ortho and para positions in the ring as these positions become electron rich due to the resonance effect caused by –OH group. The resonance structures are shown under acidity of phenols.','[{"s": "In phenols, the reactions that take place on the aromatic ring are electrophilic substitution reactions (Unit 13, Class XI). The –OH group attached to the benzene ring activates it towards electrophilic substitution. Also, it directs the incoming group to ortho and para positions in the ring as these positions become electron rich due to the resonance effect caused by –OH group.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The resonance structures are shown under acidity of phenols.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250978"]',NULL),
(4188,34,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Common electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions taking place in phenol are as follows:','[{"s": "Common electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions taking place in phenol are as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4189,34,253,'paragraph',NULL,'2 groups which facilitate the release of hydrogen ion.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups which facilitate the release of hydrogen ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4190,34,254,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Nitration: With dilute nitric acid at low temperature (298 K), phenol yields a mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols.','[{"s": "(i) Nitration: With dilute nitric acid at low temperature (298 K), phenol yields a mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4191,34,255,'image',NULL,'bond-71',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image39.png'),
(4192,34,256,'paragraph',NULL,'The ortho and para isomers can be separated by steam distillation. o-Nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding while p-nitrophenol is less volatile due to intermolecular hydrogen bonding which causes the association of molecules.','[{"s": "The ortho and para isomers can be separated by steam distillation. o-Nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding while p-nitrophenol is less volatile due to intermolecular hydrogen bonding which causes the association of molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4193,34,257,'image',NULL,'bond-72',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image101.png'),
(12628,44,1398,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) (iii) < (ii) < (i)','[{"s": "(c) (iii) < (ii) < (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4194,34,258,'paragraph',NULL,'With concentrated nitric acid, phenol is converted to 2,4,6-trinitrophenol. The product is commonly known as picric acid. The yield of the reaction product is poor.','[{"s": "With concentrated nitric acid, phenol is converted to 2,4,6-trinitrophenol. The product is commonly known as picric acid. The yield of the reaction product is poor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4195,34,259,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image25.png'),
(4196,34,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Nowadays picric acid is prepared by treating phenol first with concentrated sulphuric acid which converts it to phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid, and then with concentrated nitric acid to get 2,4,6-trinitrophenol. Can you write the equations of the reactions involved?','[{"s": "Nowadays picric acid is prepared by treating phenol first with concentrated sulphuric acid which converts it to phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid, and then with concentrated nitric acid to get 2,4,6-trinitrophenol. Can you write the equations of the reactions involved?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4197,34,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Halogenation: On treating phenol with bromine, different reaction products are formed under different experimental conditions.','[{"s": "(ii) Halogenation: On treating phenol with bromine, different reaction products are formed under different experimental conditions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4198,34,262,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) When the reaction is carried out in solvents of low polarity such as CHCl 3 or CS 2 and at low temperature, monobromophenols are formed.','[{"s": "(a) When the reaction is carried out in solvents of low polarity such as CHCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or CS", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and at low temperature, monobromophenols are formed.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250979"]',NULL),
(4199,34,263,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image2.png'),
(4200,34,264,'paragraph',NULL,'The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid, such as FeBr 3 (Unit 10, Class XII), which polarises the halogen molecule. In case of phenol, the polarisation of bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of Lewis acid. It is due to the highly activating effect of –OH group attached to the benzene ring.','[{"s": "The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid, such as FeBr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Unit 10, Class XII), which polarises the halogen molecule.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In case of phenol, the polarisation of bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of Lewis acid. It is due to the highly activating effect of –OH group attached to the benzene ring.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["250972"]',NULL),
(4201,34,265,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) When phenol is treated with bromine water, 2,4,6-tribromophenol is formed as white precipitate.','[{"s": "(b) When phenol is treated with bromine water, 2,4,6-tribromophenol is formed as white precipitate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4202,34,266,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image50.png'),
(4203,34,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.5','[{"s": "Example 11.5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
