(4233,34,297,'heading',3,'11.5 Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4234,34,298,'paragraph',NULL,'Methanol and ethanol are among the two commercially important alcohols.','[{"s": "Methanol and ethanol are among the two commercially important alcohols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4235,34,299,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Methanol','[{"s": "1. Methanol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4236,34,300,'paragraph',NULL,'Methanol, CH 3 OH, also known as ‘wood spirit’, was produced by destructive distillation of wood. Today, most of the methanol is produced by catalytic hydrogenation of carbon monoxide at high pressure and temperature and in the presence of ZnO – Cr 2 O 3 catalyst.','[{"s": "Methanol, CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH, also known as ‘wood spirit’, was produced by destructive distillation of wood. Today, most of the methanol is produced by catalytic hydrogenation of carbon monoxide at high pressure and temperature and in the presence of ZnO – Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "catalyst.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4237,34,301,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image79.png'),
(4238,34,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Methanol is a colourless liquid and boils at 337 K. It is highly poisonous in nature. Ingestion of even small quantities of methanol can cause blindness and large quantities causes even death. Methanol is used as a solvent in paints, varnishes and chiefly for making formaldehyde.','[{"s": "Methanol is a colourless liquid and boils at 337 K. It is highly poisonous in nature. Ingestion of even small quantities of methanol can cause blindness and large quantities causes even death. Methanol is used as a solvent in paints, varnishes and chiefly for making formaldehyde.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4239,34,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Ingestion of ethanol acts on the central nervous system. In moderate amounts, it affects judgment and lowers inhibitions. Higher concentrations cause nausea and loss of consciousness. Even at higher concentrations, it interferes with spontaneous respiration and can be fatal.','[{"s": "Ingestion of ethanol acts on the central nervous system. In moderate amounts, it affects judgment and lowers inhibitions. Higher concentrations cause nausea and loss of consciousness. Even at higher concentrations, it interferes with spontaneous respiration and can be fatal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4240,34,304,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Ethanol','[{"s": "2. Ethanol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4241,34,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethanol, C 2 H 5 OH, is obtained commercially by fermentation, the oldest method is from sugars. The sugar in molasses, sugarcane or fruits such as grapes is converted to glucose and fructose, (both of which have the formula C 6 H 12 O 6 ), in the presence of an enzyme, invertase. Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.','[{"s": "Ethanol, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH, is obtained commercially by fermentation, the oldest method is from sugars. The sugar in molasses, sugarcane or fruits such as grapes is converted to glucose and fructose, (both of which have the formula C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), in the presence of an enzyme, invertase. Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4242,34,306,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image143.png'),
(4243,34,307,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image43.png'),
(6379,37,511,'paragraph',NULL,'4.8 The rate of the chemical reaction doubles for an increase of 10K in absolute temperature from 298K. Calculate E a .','[{"s": "4.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The rate of the chemical reaction doubles for an increase of 10K in absolute temperature from 298K. Calculate E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6380,37,512,'paragraph',NULL,'4.9 The activation energy for the reaction','[{"s": "4.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The activation energy for the reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4244,34,308,'paragraph',NULL,'In wine making, grapes are the source of sugars and yeast. As grapes ripen, the quantity of sugar increases and yeast grows on the outer skin. When grapes are crushed, sugar and the enzyme come in contact and fermentation starts. Fermentation takes place in anaerobic conditions i.e. in absence of air. Carbon dioxide is released during fermentation.','[{"s": "In wine making, grapes are the source of sugars and yeast. As grapes ripen, the quantity of sugar increases and yeast grows on the outer skin. When grapes are crushed, sugar and the enzyme come in contact and fermentation starts. Fermentation takes place in anaerobic conditions i.e. in absence of air. Carbon dioxide is released during fermentation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4245,34,309,'paragraph',NULL,'The action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds 14 percent. If air gets into fermentation mixture, the oxygen of air oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid which in turn destroys the taste of alcoholic drinks.','[{"s": "The action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds 14 percent. If air gets into fermentation mixture, the oxygen of air oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid which in turn destroys the taste of alcoholic drinks.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4246,34,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethanol is a colourless liquid with boiling point 351 K. It is used as a solvent in paint industry and in the preparation of a number of carbon compounds. The commercial alcohol is made unfit for drinking by mixing in it some copper sulphate (to give it a colour) and pyridine (a foul smelling liquid). It is known as denaturation of alcohol.','[{"s": "Ethanol is a colourless liquid with boiling point 351 K. It is used as a solvent in paint industry and in the preparation of a number of carbon compounds. The commercial alcohol is made unfit for drinking by mixing in it some copper sulphate (to give it a colour) and pyridine (a foul smelling liquid). It is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denaturation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of alcohol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4247,34,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Nowadays, large quantities of ethanol are obtained by hydration of ethene (Section 11.4).','[{"s": "Nowadays, large quantities of ethanol are obtained by hydration of ethene (Section 11.4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4248,34,312,'heading',3,'11.6 Ethers',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4249,34,313,'heading',4,'11.6.1 Preparation of Ethers',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4250,34,314,'paragraph',NULL,'1. By dehydration of alcohols','[{"s": "1. By dehydration of alcohols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4251,34,315,'paragraph',NULL,'Alcohols undergo dehydration in the presence of protic acids (H 2 SO 4 , H 3 PO 4 ) . The formation of the reaction product, alkene or ether depends on the reaction conditions. For example, ethanol is dehydrated to ethene in the presence of sulphuric acid at 443 K. At 413 K, ethoxyethane is the main product.','[{"s": "Alcohols undergo dehydration in the presence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "protic acids (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The formation of the reaction product, alkene or ether depends on the reaction conditions.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, ethanol is dehydrated to ethene in the presence of sulphuric acid at 443 K. At 413 K, ethoxyethane is the main product.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250984"]',NULL),
(4252,34,316,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image128.png'),
(4253,34,317,'paragraph',NULL,'Diethyl ether has been used widely as an inhalation anaesthetic. But due to its slow effect and an unpleasant recovery period, it has been replaced, as an anaesthetic, by other compounds.','[{"s": "Diethyl ether has been used widely as an inhalation anaesthetic. But due to its slow effect and an unpleasant recovery period, it has been replaced, as an anaesthetic, by other compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4254,34,318,'paragraph',NULL,'The formation of ether is a nucleophilic bimolecular reaction (S n 2) involving the attack of alcohol molecule on a protonated alcohol, as indicated below:','[{"s": "The formation of ether is a nucleophilic bimolecular reaction (S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2) involving the attack of alcohol molecule on a protonated alcohol, as indicated below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4255,34,319,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image118.png'),
(4256,34,320,'paragraph',NULL,'Acidic dehydration of alcohols, to give an alkene is also associated with substitution reaction to give an ether.','[{"s": "Acidic dehydration of alcohols, to give an alkene is also associated with substitution reaction to give an ether.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4257,34,321,'paragraph',NULL,'The method is suitable for the preparation of ethers having primary alkyl groups only. The alkyl group should be unhindered and the temperature be kept low. Otherwise the reaction favours the formation of alkene. The reaction follows S N 1 pathway when the alcohol is secondary or tertiary about which you will learn in higher classes. However, the dehydration of secondary and tertiary alcohols to give corresponding ethers is unsuccessful as elimination competes over substitution and as a consequence, alkenes are easily formed.','[{"s": "The method is suitable for the preparation of ethers having primary alkyl groups only. The alkyl group should be unhindered and the temperature be kept low. Otherwise the reaction favours the formation of alkene. The reaction follows S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 pathway when the alcohol is secondary or tertiary about which you will learn in higher classes. However, the dehydration of secondary and tertiary alcohols to give corresponding ethers is unsuccessful as elimination competes over substitution and as a consequence, alkenes are easily formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4258,34,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Can you explain why is bimolecular dehydration not appropriate for the preparation of ethyl methyl ether?','[{"s": "Can you explain why is bimolecular dehydration not appropriate for the preparation of ethyl methyl ether?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4259,34,323,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Williamson synthesis5','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Williamson synthesis5", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["250954", "250970", "250985", "250986"]',NULL),
(4260,34,324,'paragraph',NULL,'It is an important laboratory method for the preparation of symmetrical and unsymmetrical ethers. In this method, an alkyl halide is allowed to react with sodium alkoxide.','[{"s": "It is an important laboratory method for the preparation of symmetrical and unsymmetrical ethers. In this method, an alkyl halide is allowed to react with sodium alkoxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4261,34,325,'image',NULL,'Img16',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image135.png'),
(12629,44,1399,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) (ii) < (iii) < (i)','[{"s": "(d) (ii) < (iii) < (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4262,34,326,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethers containing substituted alkyl groups (secondary or tertiary) may also be prepared by this method. The reaction involves S n 2 attack of an alkoxide ion on primary alkyl halide.','[{"s": "Ethers containing substituted alkyl groups (secondary or tertiary) may also be prepared by this method. The reaction involves S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 attack of an alkoxide ion on primary alkyl halide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4263,34,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Alexander William Williamson (1824–1904) was born in London of Scottish parents. In 1849, he became Professor of Chemistry at University College, London.','[{"s": "Alexander William Williamson (1824–1904) was born in London of Scottish parents. In 1849, he became Professor of Chemistry at University College, London.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4264,34,328,'image',NULL,'Img18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image90.png'),
(4265,34,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Better results are obtained if the alkyl halide is primary. In case of secondary and tertiary alkyl halides, elimination competes over substitution. If a tertiary alkyl halide is used, an alkene is the only reaction product and no ether is formed. For example, the reaction of CH 3 ONa with (CH 3 ) 3 C–Br gives exclusively 2-methylpropene.','[{"s": "Better results are obtained if the alkyl halide is primary. In case of secondary and tertiary alkyl halides, elimination competes over substitution. If a tertiary alkyl halide is used, an alkene is the only reaction product and no ether is formed. For example, the reaction of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ONa with (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C–Br gives exclusively 2-methylpropene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4266,34,330,'image',NULL,'Img31',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image111.png'),
(4267,34,331,'paragraph',NULL,'It is because alkoxides are not only nucleophiles but strong bases as well. They react with alkyl halides leading to elimination reactions.','[{"s": "It is because alkoxides are not only nucleophiles but strong bases as well. They react with alkyl halides leading to elimination reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
