(4272,34,336,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Write a suitable reaction for the preparation of t-butylethyl ether.','[{"s": "(ii) Write a suitable reaction for the preparation of t-butylethyl ether.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4273,34,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4274,34,338,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The major product of the given reaction is 2-methylprop-1-ene. It is because sodium ethoxide is a strong nucleophile as well as a strong base. Thus elimination reaction predominates over substitution.','[{"s": "(i) The major product of the given reaction is 2-methylprop-1-ene. It is because sodium ethoxide is a strong nucleophile as well as a strong base. Thus elimination reaction predominates over substitution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4275,34,339,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image138.png','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image138.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4276,34,340,'paragraph',NULL,'Phenols are also converted to ethers by this method. In this, phenol is used as the phenoxide moiety.','[{"s": "Phenols are also converted to ethers by this method. In this, phenol is used as the phenoxide moiety.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4277,34,341,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image6.png'),
(4278,34,342,'heading',4,'11.6.2 Physical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4279,34,343,'paragraph',NULL,'The C-O bonds in ethers are polar and thus, ethers have a net dipole moment. The weak polarity of ethers do not appreciably affect their boiling points which are comparable to those of the alkanes of comparable molecular masses but are much lower than the boiling points of alcohols as shown in the following cases:','[{"s": "The C-O bonds in ethers are polar and thus, ethers have a net dipole moment. The weak polarity of ethers do not appreciably affect their boiling points which are comparable to those of the alkanes of comparable molecular masses but are much lower than the boiling points of alcohols as shown in the following cases:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4280,34,344,'image',NULL,'Img19',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image81.png'),
(4281,34,345,'paragraph',NULL,'The large difference in boiling points of alcohols and ethers is due to the presence of hydrogen bonding in alcohols.','[{"s": "The large difference in boiling points of alcohols and ethers is due to the presence of hydrogen bonding in alcohols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4282,34,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The miscibility of ethers with water resembles those of alcohols of the same molecular mass. Both ethoxyethane and butan-1-ol are miscible to almost the same extent i.e., 7.5 and 9 g per 100 mL water, respectively while pentane is essentially immiscible with water. Can you explain this observation ? This is due to the fact that just like alcohols, oxygen of ether can also form hydrogen bonds with water molecule as shown:','[{"s": "The miscibility of ethers with water resembles those of alcohols of the same molecular mass. Both ethoxyethane and butan-1-ol are miscible to almost the same extent i.e., 7.5 and 9 g per 100 mL water, respectively while pentane is essentially immiscible with water. Can you explain this observation ? This is due to the fact that just like alcohols, oxygen of ether can also form hydrogen bonds with water molecule as shown:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4283,34,347,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Alcohols_Phenols_and_Ethers-image76.png'),
