(4779,35,47,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) α-Methoxypropionaldehyde (ii) 3-Hydroxybutanal','[{"s": "(i) α-Methoxypropionaldehyde (ii) 3-Hydroxybutanal", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4780,35,48,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) 2-Hydroxycyclopentane carbaldehyde (iv) 4-Oxopentanal','[{"s": "(iii) 2-Hydroxycyclopentane carbaldehyde (iv) 4-Oxopentanal", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4781,35,49,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Di-sec. butyl ketone (vi) 4-Fluoroacetophenone','[{"s": "(v) Di-sec. butyl ketone (vi) 4-Fluoroacetophenone", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4782,35,50,'heading',3,'12.2 Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4783,35,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Some important methods for the preparation of aldehydes and ketones are as follows:','[{"s": "Some important methods for the preparation of aldehydes and ketones are as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4784,35,52,'heading',4,'12.2.1 Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4785,35,53,'paragraph',NULL,'1. By oxidation of alcohols','[{"s": "1. By oxidation of alcohols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4786,35,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols, respectively (Unit 11, Class XII).','[{"s": "Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols, respectively (Unit 11, Class XII).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["261204"]',NULL),
(4787,35,55,'paragraph',NULL,'2. By dehydrogenation of alcohols','[{"s": "2. By dehydrogenation of alcohols", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4788,35,56,'paragraph',NULL,'This method is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. In this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). Primary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively (Unit 11, Class XII).','[{"s": "This method is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. In this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). Primary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively (Unit 11, Class XII).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4789,35,57,'paragraph',NULL,'3. From hydrocarbons','[{"s": "3. From hydrocarbons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4790,35,58,'paragraph',NULL,'( i ) By ozonolysis of alkenes: As we know, ozonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes, ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "(", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "By ozonolysis of alkenes:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "As we know, ozonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes, ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130824"]',NULL),
(4791,35,59,'paragraph',NULL,'( ii ) By hydration of alkynes: Addition of water to ethyne in the presence of H 2 SO 4 and HgSO 4 gives acetaldehyde. All other alkynes give ketones in this reaction (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ii", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "By hydration of alkynes:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Addition of water to ethyne in the presence of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HgSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gives acetaldehyde. All other alkynes give ketones in this reaction (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4792,35,60,'heading',4,'12.2.2 Preparation of Aldehydes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4793,35,61,'paragraph',NULL,'1. From acyl chloride (acid chloride)','[{"s": "1. From acyl chloride (acid chloride)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4794,35,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate . This reaction is called Rosenmund reduction','[{"s": "Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This reaction is called Rosenmund reduction", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["261518"]',NULL),
(4795,35,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image54.png'),
(4796,35,64,'paragraph',NULL,'2. From nitriles and esters','[{"s": "2. From nitriles and esters", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4797,35,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.','[{"s": "Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4798,35,66,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image35.png'),
(4799,35,67,'paragraph',NULL,'This reaction is called Stephen reaction.','[{"s": "This reaction is called Stephen reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4800,35,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternatively, nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes:','[{"s": "Alternatively, nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4801,35,69,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image44.png'),
(4802,35,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, esters are also reduced to aldehydes with DIBAL-H.','[{"s": "Similarly, esters are also reduced to aldehydes with DIBAL-H.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4803,35,71,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image79.png'),
(4804,35,72,'paragraph',NULL,'3. From hydrocarbons','[{"s": "3. From hydrocarbons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4805,35,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde and its derivatives) are prepared from aromatic hydrocarbons by the following methods:','[{"s": "Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde and its derivatives) are prepared from aromatic hydrocarbons by the following methods:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4806,35,74,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) By oxidation of methylbenzene','[{"s": "(i) By oxidation of methylbenzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4807,35,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Strong oxidising agents oxidise toluene and its derivatives to benzoic acids. However, it is possible to stop the oxidation at the aldehyde stage with suitable reagents that convert the methyl group to an intermediate that is difficult to oxidise further. The following methods are used for this purpose.','[{"s": "Strong oxidising agents oxidise toluene and its derivatives to benzoic acids. However, it is possible to stop the oxidation at the aldehyde stage with suitable reagents that convert the methyl group to an intermediate that is difficult to oxidise further. The following methods are used for this purpose.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4808,35,76,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Use of chromyl chloride (CrO 2 Cl 2 ) : Chromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.','[{"s": "(a) Use of chromyl chloride (CrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Chromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4809,35,77,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image115.png'),
(4810,35,78,'paragraph',NULL,'This reaction is called Etard reaction.','[{"s": "This reaction is called Etard reaction.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["261302"]',NULL),
(4811,35,79,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Use of chromic oxide (CrO 3 ) : Toluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. The benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.','[{"s": "(b) Use of chromic oxide (CrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Toluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. The benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4812,35,80,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image77.png'),
(4813,35,81,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis','[{"s": "(ii) By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["261201"]',NULL),
(4814,35,82,'paragraph',NULL,'Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. This is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde.','[{"s": "Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. This is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4815,35,83,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image31.png'),
(4816,35,84,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) By Gatterman – Koch reaction','[{"s": "(iii) By Gatterman – Koch reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4817,35,85,'paragraph',NULL,'When benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminum chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.','[{"s": "When benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminum chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4818,35,86,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image58.png'),
(4819,35,87,'paragraph',NULL,'This reaction is known as Gatterman-Koch reaction.','[{"s": "This reaction is known as Gatterman-Koch reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4820,35,88,'heading',4,'12.2.3 Preparation of Ketones',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4821,35,89,'paragraph',NULL,'1. From acyl chlorides','[{"s": "1. From acyl chlorides", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4822,35,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkylcadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.','[{"s": "Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkylcadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4823,35,91,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image16.png'),
(4824,35,92,'paragraph',NULL,'2. From nitriles','[{"s": "2. From nitriles", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4825,35,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Treating a nitrile with Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis yields a ketone.','[{"s": "Treating a nitrile with Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis yields a ketone.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["261265"]',NULL),
(4826,35,94,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image106.png'),
(4827,35,95,'paragraph',NULL,'3. From benzene or substituted benzenes','[{"s": "3. From benzene or substituted benzenes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4828,35,96,'paragraph',NULL,'When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. This reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.','[{"s": "When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. This reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(4829,35,97,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Aldehydes_Ketones_and_Carboxylic_Acids-image90.png'),
(4830,35,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 12.1','[{"s": "Example 12.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
