(5115,35,383,'paragraph',NULL,'The p K a of hydrochloric acid is –7.0, where as p K a of trifluoroacetic acid (the strongest carboxylic acid), benzoic acid and acetic acid are 0.23, 4.19 and 4.76, respectively.','[{"s": "The p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of hydrochloric acid is –7.0, where as p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of trifluoroacetic acid (the strongest carboxylic acid), benzoic acid and acetic acid are 0.23, 4.19 and 4.76, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5116,35,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Smaller the p K a , the stronger the acid ( the better it is as a proton donor). Strong acids have p K a values < 1, the acids with p K a values between 1 and 5 are considered to be moderately strong acids, weak acids have p K a values between 5 and 15, and extremely weak acids have p K a values >15.','[{"s": "Smaller the p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the stronger the acid ( the better it is as a proton donor). Strong acids have p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values < 1, the acids with p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values between 1 and 5 are considered to be moderately strong acids, weak acids have p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values between 5 and 15, and extremely weak acids have p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values >15.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9208,41,740,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 65 When a chromite ore (A) is fused with sodium carbonate in free excess of air and the product is dissolved in water, a yellow solution of compound (B) is obtained. After treatment of this yellow solution with sulphuric acid, compound (C) can be crystallised from the solution. When compound (C) is treated with KCL, orange crystals of compound (D) crystallise out. Identify A to D and also explain the reactions.','[{"s": "Q. 65 When a chromite ore (A) is fused with sodium carbonate in free excess of air and the product is dissolved in water, a yellow solution of compound (B) is obtained. After treatment of this yellow solution with sulphuric acid, compound (C) can be crystallised from the solution. When compound (C) is treated with KCL, orange crystals of compound (D) crystallise out. Identify A to D and also explain the reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5117,35,385,'paragraph',NULL,'Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (p K a is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol). In fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds you have studied so far. You already know why phenols are more acidic than alcohols. The higher acidity of carboxylic acids as compared to phenols can be understood similarly. The conjugate base of carboxylic acid, a carboxylate ion, is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom. The conjugate base of phenol, a phenoxide ion, has non-equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the less electronegative carbon atom. Therefore, resonance in phenoxide ion is not as important as it is in carboxylate ion. Further, the negative charge is delocalised over two electronegative oxygen atoms in carboxylate ion whereas it is less effectively delocalised over one oxygen atom and less electronegative carbon atoms in phenoxide ion (Unit 11, Class XII). Thus, the carboxylate ion is more stabilised than phenoxide ion, so carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols.','[{"s": "Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol). In fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds you have studied so far. You already know why phenols are more acidic than alcohols. The higher acidity of carboxylic acids as compared to phenols can be understood similarly. The conjugate base of carboxylic acid, a carboxylate ion, is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom. The conjugate base of phenol, a phenoxide ion, has non-equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the less electronegative carbon atom. Therefore, resonance in phenoxide ion is not as important as it is in carboxylate ion. Further, the negative charge is delocalised over two electronegative oxygen atoms in carboxylate ion whereas it is less effectively delocalised over one oxygen atom and less electronegative carbon atoms in phenoxide ion (Unit 11, Class XII). Thus, the carboxylate ion is more stabilised than phenoxide ion, so carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5118,35,386,'paragraph',NULL,'Effect of substituents on the acidity of carboxylic acids: Substituents may affect the stability of the conjugate base and thus, also affect the acidity of the carboxylic acids. Electron withdrawing groups increase the acidity of carboxylic acids by stabilising the conjugate base through delocalisation of the negative charge by inductive and/or resonance effects. Conversely, electron donating groups decrease the acidity by destabilising the conjugate base.','[{"s": "Effect of substituents on the acidity of carboxylic acids:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Substituents may affect the stability of the conjugate base and thus, also affect the acidity of the carboxylic acids.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electron withdrawing groups increase the acidity of carboxylic acids by stabilising the conjugate base through delocalisation of the negative charge by inductive and/or resonance effects.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Conversely, electron donating groups decrease the acidity by destabilising the conjugate base.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["261277"]',NULL),
