(5339,36,45,'heading',4,'14.1.2.1 Glucose',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5340,36,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Glucose occurs freely in nature as well as in the combined form. It is present in sweet fruits and honey. Ripe grapes also contain glucose in large amounts. It is prepared as follows:','[{"s": "Glucose occurs freely in nature as well as in the combined form. It is present in sweet fruits and honey. Ripe grapes also contain glucose in large amounts. It is prepared as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5341,36,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation of Glucose','[{"s": "Preparation of Glucose", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5342,36,48,'paragraph',NULL,'1. From sucrose (Cane sugar) : If sucrose is boiled with dilute HCl or H 2 SO 4 in alcoholic solution, glucose and fructose are obtained in equal amounts.','[{"s": "1. From sucrose (Cane sugar)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": If sucrose is boiled with dilute HCl or H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in alcoholic solution, glucose and fructose are obtained in equal amounts.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5343,36,49,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image1.png'),
(5344,36,50,'paragraph',NULL,'2. From starch : Commercially glucose is obtained by hydrolysis of starch by boiling it with dilute H 2 SO 4 at 393 K under pressure.','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From starch", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ":", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Commercially glucose is obtained by hydrolysis of starch by boiling it with dilute H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "at 393 K under pressure.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["281271"]',NULL),
(5345,36,51,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image31.png'),
(5346,36,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Starch or cellulose Glucose','[{"s": "Starch or cellulose Glucose", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5347,36,53,'heading',4,'Structure of Glucose',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5348,36,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Glucose is an aldohexose and is also known as dextrose. It is the monomer of many of the larger carbohydrates, namely starch, cellulose. It is probably the most abundant organic compound on earth. It was assigned the structure given below on the basis of the following evidences:','[{"s": "Glucose is an aldohexose and is also known as dextrose. It is the monomer of many of the larger carbohydrates, namely starch, cellulose. It is probably the most abundant organic compound on earth. It was assigned the structure given below on the basis of the following evidences:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5349,36,55,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image28.png'),
(5350,36,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Glucose','[{"s": "Glucose", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5351,36,57,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Its molecular formula was found to be C 6 H 12 O 6 .','[{"s": "1. Its molecular formula was found to be C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5352,36,58,'paragraph',NULL,'2. On prolonged heating with HI, it forms n-hexane, suggesting that all the six carbon atoms are linked in a straight chain.','[{"s": "2. On prolonged heating with HI, it forms n-hexane, suggesting that all the six carbon atoms are linked in a straight chain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5353,36,59,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image57.png'),
(5354,36,60,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime and adds a molecule of hydrogen cyanide to give cyanohydrin. These reactions confirm the presence of a carbonyl group (>C = O) in glucose.','[{"s": "3. Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime and adds a molecule of hydrogen cyanide to give cyanohydrin.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These reactions confirm the presence of a carbonyl group (>C = O) in glucose.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["281267"]',NULL),
(5355,36,61,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image41.png'),
(5356,36,62,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Glucose gets oxidised to six carbon carboxylic acid (gluconic acid) on reaction with a mild oxidising agent like bromine water. This indicates that the carbonyl group is present as an aldehydic group.','[{"s": "4. Glucose gets oxidised to six carbon carboxylic acid (gluconic acid) on reaction with a mild oxidising agent like bromine water. This indicates that the carbonyl group is present as an aldehydic group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5357,36,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image45.png'),
(5358,36,64,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Acetylation of glucose with acetic anhydride gives glucose pentaacetate which confirms the presence of five –OH groups. Since it exists as a stable compound, five –OH groups should be attached to different carbon atoms.','[{"s": "5. Acetylation of glucose with acetic anhydride gives glucose pentaacetate which confirms the presence of five –OH groups. Since it exists as a stable compound, five –OH groups should be attached to different carbon atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5359,36,65,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image27.png'),
(5360,36,66,'paragraph',NULL,'6. On oxidation with nitric acid, glucose as well as gluconic acid both yield a dicarboxylic acid, saccharic acid. This indicates the presence of a primary alcoholic (–OH) group in glucose.','[{"s": "6. On oxidation with nitric acid, glucose as well as gluconic acid both yield a dicarboxylic acid, saccharic acid. This indicates the presence of a primary alcoholic (–OH) group in glucose.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5361,36,67,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image47.png'),
(5362,36,68,'paragraph',NULL,'The exact spatial arrangement of different —OH groups was given by Fischer after studying many other properties. Its configuration is correctly represented as I. So gluconic acid is represented as II and saccharic acid as III.','[{"s": "The exact spatial arrangement of different —OH groups was given by Fischer after studying many other properties. Its configuration is correctly represented as I. So gluconic acid is represented as II and saccharic acid as III.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5363,36,69,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image12.png'),
