(12117,44,887,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (i) <(ii) < (iii) <(iv)','[{"s": "(a) (i) <(ii) < (iii) <(iv)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12118,44,888,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) (i) < (iii) < (iv) <(ii)','[{"s": "(b) (i) < (iii) < (iv) <(ii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5426,36,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Amino acids are classified as acidic, basic or neutral depending upon the relative number of amino and carboxyl groups in their molecule. Equal number of amino and carboxyl groups makes it neutral; more number of amino than carboxyl groups makes it basic and more carboxyl groups as compared to amino groups makes it acidic. The amino acids, which can be synthesised in the body, are known as non-essential amino acids. On the other hand, those which cannot be synthesised in the body and must be obtained through diet, are known as essential amino acids (marked with asterisk in Table 14.2).','[{"s": "Amino acids are classified as acidic, basic or neutral depending upon the relative number of amino and carboxyl groups in their molecule. Equal number of amino and carboxyl groups makes it neutral; more number of amino than carboxyl groups makes it basic and more carboxyl groups as compared to amino groups makes it acidic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The amino acids, which can be synthesised in the body, are known as non-essential amino acids.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "On the other hand, those which cannot be synthesised in the body and must be obtained through diet, are known as essential amino acids (marked with asterisk in Table 14.2).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281205"]',NULL),
(5427,36,133,'paragraph',NULL,'Amino acids are usually colourless, crystalline solids. These are water-soluble, high melting solids and behave like salts rather than simple amines or carboxylic acids. This behaviour is due to the presence of both acidic (carboxyl group) and basic (amino group) groups in the same molecule. In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group can lose a proton and amino group can accept a proton, giving rise to a dipolar ion known as zwitter ion. This is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.','[{"s": "Amino acids are usually colourless, crystalline solids. These are water-soluble, high melting solids and behave like salts rather than simple amines or carboxylic acids. This behaviour is due to the presence of both acidic (carboxyl group) and basic (amino group) groups in the same molecule.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group can lose a proton and amino group can accept a proton, giving rise to a dipolar ion known as zwitter ion.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is neutral but contains both positive and negative charges.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281207"]',NULL),
(5428,36,134,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image35.png'),
(5429,36,135,'paragraph',NULL,'In zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react both with acids and bases.','[{"s": "In zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react both with acids and bases.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["281207"]',NULL),
(5430,36,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Except glycine, all other naturally occurring α-amino acids are optically active, since the α-carbon atom is asymmetric. These exist both in \'D\' and \'L\' forms. Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration. L-Aminoacids are represented by writing the –NH2 group on left hand side.','[{"s": "Except glycine, all other naturally occurring α-amino acids are optically active, since the α-carbon atom is asymmetric.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These exist both in \'D\' and \'L\' forms. Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration. L-Aminoacids are represented by writing the –NH2 group on left hand side.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281232"]',NULL),
(5431,36,137,'heading',4,'14.2.3 Structure of Proteins',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5432,36,138,'paragraph',NULL,'You have already read that proteins are the polymers of α-amino acids and they are connected to each other by peptide bond or peptide linkage . Chemically, peptide linkage is an amide formed between','[{"s": "You have already read that proteins are the polymers of α-amino acids and they are connected to each other by peptide bond or peptide linkage", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Chemically, peptide linkage is an amide formed between", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281208"]',NULL),
(5433,36,139,'paragraph',NULL,'–COOH group and –NH 2 group. The reaction between two molecules of similar or different amino acids, proceeds through the combination of the amino group of one molecule with the carboxyl group of the other. This results in the elimination of a water molecule and formation of a peptide bond –CO–NH–. 2 The product of the reaction is called a dipeptide because it is made up of two amino acids. For example, when carboxyl group of glycine combines with the amino group of alanine we get a dipeptide, glycylalanine.','[{"s": "–COOH group and –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group. The reaction between two molecules of similar or different amino acids, proceeds through the combination of the amino group of one molecule with the carboxyl group of the other.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This results in the elimination of a water molecule and formation of a peptide bond –CO–NH–.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The product of the reaction is called a dipeptide because it is made up of two amino acids. For example, when carboxyl group of glycine combines with the amino group of alanine we get a dipeptide, glycylalanine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5434,36,140,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image25.png'),
(5435,36,141,'paragraph',NULL,'If a third amino acid combines to a dipeptide, the product is called a tripeptide. A tripeptide contains three amino acids linked by two peptide linkages. Similarly when four, five or six amino acids are linked, the respective products are known as tetrapeptide, pentapeptide or hexapeptide, respectively. When the number of such amino acids is more than ten, then the products are called polypeptides. A polypeptide with more than hundred amino acid residues, having molecular mass higher than 10,000u is called a protein. However, the distinction between a polypeptide and a protein is not very sharp. Polypeptides with fewer amino acids are likely to be called proteins if they ordinarily have a well defined conformation of a protein such as insulin which contains 51 amino acids.','[{"s": "If a third amino acid combines to a dipeptide, the product is called a tripeptide. A tripeptide contains three amino acids linked by two peptide linkages. Similarly when four, five or six amino acids are linked, the respective products are known as tetrapeptide, pentapeptide or hexapeptide, respectively. When the number of such amino acids is more than ten, then the products are called polypeptides. A polypeptide with more than hundred amino acid residues, having molecular mass higher than 10,000u is called a protein. However, the distinction between a polypeptide and a protein is not very sharp. Polypeptides with fewer amino acids are likely to be called proteins if they ordinarily have a well defined conformation of a protein such as insulin which contains 51 amino acids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5436,36,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Proteins can be classified into two types on the basis of their molecular shape.','[{"s": "Proteins can be classified into two types on the basis of their molecular shape.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5437,36,143,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Fibrous proteins','[{"s": "(a) Fibrous proteins", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5438,36,144,'paragraph',NULL,'When the polypeptide chains run parallel and are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds, then fibre– like structure is formed. Such proteins are generally insoluble in water. Some common examples are keratin (present in hair, wool, silk) and myosin (present in muscles), etc.','[{"s": "When the polypeptide chains run parallel and are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds, then fibre– like structure is formed. Such proteins are generally insoluble in water. Some common examples are keratin (present in hair, wool, silk) and myosin (present in muscles), etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5439,36,145,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Globular proteins','[{"s": "(b) Globular proteins", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5440,36,146,'paragraph',NULL,'This structure results when the chains of polypeptides coil around to give a spherical shape. These are usually soluble in water. Insulin and albumins are the common examples of globular proteins.','[{"s": "This structure results when the chains of polypeptides coil around to give a spherical shape. These are usually soluble in water. Insulin and albumins are the common examples of globular proteins.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5441,36,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Structure and shape of proteins can be studied at four different levels, i.e., primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary, each level being more complex than the previous one.','[{"s": "Structure and shape of proteins can be studied at four different levels, i.e., primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary, each level being more complex than the previous one.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5442,36,148,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Primary structure of proteins : Proteins may have one or more polypeptide chains. Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence and it is this sequence of amino acids that is said to be the primary structure of that protein. Any change in this primary structure i.e., the sequence of amino acids creates a different protein.','[{"s": "(i) Primary structure of proteins", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Proteins may have one or more polypeptide chains. Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence and it is this sequence of amino acids that is said to be the primary structure of that protein. Any change in this primary structure i.e., the sequence of amino acids creates a different protein.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5443,36,149,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Secondary structure of proteins : The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures viz. α-helix and β-pleated sheet structure. These structures arise due to the regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide chain due to hydrogen bonding between Biomolecules_Chemistry-image16.png and –NH– groups of the peptide bond.','[{"s": "(ii) Secondary structure of proteins", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures viz. α-helix and β-pleated sheet structure. These structures arise due to the regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide chain due to hydrogen bonding between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Biomolecules_Chemistry-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and –NH– groups of the peptide bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5444,36,150,'paragraph',NULL,'α-Helix is one of the most common ways in which a polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds by twisting into a right handed screw (helix) with the –NH group of each amino acid residue hydrogen bonded to the Biomolecules_Chemistry-image51.png of an adjacent turn of the helix as shown in Fig.14.1.','[{"s": "α-Helix is one of the most common ways in which a polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds by twisting into a right handed screw (helix) with the –NH group of each amino acid residue hydrogen bonded to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Biomolecules_Chemistry-image51.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "of an adjacent turn of the helix as shown in Fig.14.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5445,36,151,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image36.png'),
(5446,36,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.1: α-Helix structure of proteins','[{"s": "Fig. 14.1: α-Helix structure of proteins", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5447,36,153,'paragraph',NULL,'In β-structure all peptide chains are stretched out to nearly maximum extension and then laid side by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds. The structure resembles the pleated folds of drapery and therefore is known as β-pleated sheet.','[{"s": "In β-structure all peptide chains are stretched out to nearly maximum extension and then laid side by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds. The structure resembles the pleated folds of drapery and therefore is known as β-pleated sheet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5448,36,154,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image29.png'),
(5449,36,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.2: β-Pleated sheet structure of proteins','[{"s": "Fig. 14.2: β-Pleated sheet structure of proteins", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5450,36,156,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Tertiary structure of proteins : The tertiary structure of proteins represents overall folding of the polypeptide chains i.e., further folding of the secondary structure. It gives rise to two major molecular shapes viz. fibrous and globular. The main forces which stabilise the 2° and 3° structures of proteins are hydrogen bonds, disulphide linkages, van der Waals and electrostatic forces of attraction.','[{"s": "(iii) Tertiary structure of proteins", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ":", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The tertiary structure of proteins represents overall folding of the polypeptide chains i.e., further folding of the secondary structure.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It gives rise to two major molecular shapes viz. fibrous and globular. The main forces which stabilise the 2° and 3° structures of proteins are hydrogen bonds, disulphide linkages, van der Waals and electrostatic forces of attraction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281231"]',NULL),
(5451,36,157,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Quaternary structure of proteins : Some of the proteins are composed of two or more polypeptide chains referred to as sub-units. The spatial arrangement of these subunits with respect to each other is known as quaternary structure.','[{"s": "(iv) Quaternary structure of proteins", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some of the proteins are composed of two or more polypeptide chains referred to as sub-units. The spatial arrangement of these subunits with respect to each other is known as quaternary structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5452,36,158,'paragraph',NULL,'A diagrammatic representation of all these four structures is given in Figure 14.3 where each coloured ball represents an amino acid.','[{"s": "A diagrammatic representation of all these four structures is given in Figure 14.3 where each coloured ball represents an amino acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5453,36,159,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image14.png'),
(5454,36,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.3 : Diagrammatic representation of protein structure (two sub-units of two types in quaternary structure)','[{"s": "Fig. 14.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Diagrammatic representation of protein structure (two sub-units of two types in quaternary structure)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5455,36,161,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image22.png'),
(5456,36,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.4 : Primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structures of haemoglobin','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14.4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": Primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structures of haemoglobin", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["281276"]',NULL),
(5457,36,163,'heading',4,'14.2.4 Denaturation of Proteins',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5458,36,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Protein found in a biological system with a unique three-dimensional structure and biological activity is called a native protein. When a protein in its native form, is subjected to physical change like change in temperature or chemical change like change in pH, the hydrogen bonds are disturbed. Due to this, globules unfold and helix get uncoiled and protein loses its biological activity. This is called denaturation of protein. During denaturation 2° and 3° structures are destroyed but 1º structure remains intact. The coagulation of egg white on boiling is a common example of denaturation. Another example is curdling of milk which is caused due to the formation of lactic acid by the bacteria present in milk.','[{"s": "Protein found in a biological system with a unique three-dimensional structure and biological activity is called a native protein. When a protein in its native form, is subjected to physical change like change in temperature or chemical change like change in pH, the hydrogen bonds are disturbed. Due to this, globules unfold and helix get uncoiled and protein loses its biological activity. This is called denaturation of protein. During denaturation 2° and 3° structures are destroyed but 1º structure remains intact. The coagulation of egg white on boiling is a common example of denaturation. Another example is curdling of milk which is caused due to the formation of lactic acid by the bacteria present in milk.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5459,36,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5460,36,166,'paragraph',NULL,'14.4 The melting points and solubility in water of amino acids are generally higher than that of the corresponding halo acids. Explain.','[{"s": "14.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The melting points and solubility in water of amino acids are generally higher than that of the corresponding halo acids. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5461,36,167,'paragraph',NULL,'14.5 Where does the water present in the egg go after boiling the egg?','[{"s": "14.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Where does the water present in the egg go after boiling the egg?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5462,36,168,'heading',3,'14.3 Enzymes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5463,36,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Life is possible due to the coordination of various chemical reactions in living organisms. An example is the digestion of food, absorption of appropriate molecules and ultimately production of energy. This process involves a sequence of reactions and all these reactions occur in the body under very mild conditions. This occurs with the help of certain biocatalysts called enzymes . Almost all the enzymes are globular proteins. Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and for a particular substrate. They are generally named after the compound or class of compounds upon which they work. For example, the enzyme that catalyses hydrolysis of maltose into glucose is named as maltase.','[{"s": "Life is possible due to the coordination of various chemical reactions in living organisms. An example is the digestion of food, absorption of appropriate molecules and ultimately production of energy. This process involves a sequence of reactions and all these reactions occur in the body under very mild conditions. This occurs with the help of certain biocatalysts called enzymes", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Almost all the enzymes are globular proteins. Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and for a particular substrate. They are generally named after the compound or class of compounds upon which they work.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, the enzyme that catalyses hydrolysis of maltose into glucose is named as maltase.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["190610"]',NULL),
(5464,36,170,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Biomolecules_Chemistry-image50.png'),
(5465,36,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Sometimes enzymes are also named after the reaction, where they are used. For example, the enzymes which catalyse the oxidation of one substrate with simultaneous reduction of another substrate are named as oxidoreductase enzymes. The ending of the name of an enzyme is -ase.','[{"s": "Sometimes enzymes are also named after the reaction, where they are used.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, the enzymes which catalyse the oxidation of one substrate with simultaneous reduction of another substrate are named as oxidoreductase enzymes. The ending of the name of an enzyme is -ase.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["281277"]',NULL),
(5466,36,172,'heading',4,'14.3.1 Mechanism of Enzyme Action',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5467,36,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Enzymes are needed only in small quantities for the progress of a reaction. Similar to the action of chemical catalysts, enzymes are said to reduce the magnitude of activation energy. For example, activation energy for acid hydrolysis of sucrose is 6.22 kJ mol –1 , while the activation energy is only 2.15 kJ mol –1 when hydrolysed by the enzyme, sucrase. Mechanism for the enzyme action has been discussed in Unit 5.','[{"s": "Enzymes are needed only in small quantities for the progress of a reaction. Similar to the action of chemical catalysts, enzymes are said to reduce the magnitude of activation energy. For example, activation energy for acid hydrolysis of sucrose is 6.22 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", while the activation energy is only 2.15 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "when hydrolysed by the enzyme, sucrase. Mechanism for the enzyme action has been discussed in Unit 5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5468,36,174,'heading',3,'14.4 Vitamins',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9220,41,752,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 71 A violet compound of manganese (A) decomposes on heating to liberate oxygen and compounds (B) and (C) of manganese are formed. Compound (C) reacts with KOH in the presence of potassium nitrate to give compound (B). On heating compound (C) with conc. H 2 SO 4 Thinking Process','[{"s": "Q. 71 A violet compound of manganese (A) decomposes on heating to liberate oxygen and compounds (B) and (C) of manganese are formed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Compound (C) reacts with KOH in the presence of potassium nitrate to give compound (B).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "On heating compound (C) with conc.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Thinking Process", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82496"]',NULL),
(5469,36,175,'paragraph',NULL,'It has been observed that certain organic compounds are required in small amounts in our diet but their deficiency causes specific diseases. These compounds are called vitamins. Most of the vitamins cannot be synthesised in our body but plants can synthesise almost all of them, so they are considered as essential food factors. However, the bacteria of the gut can produce some of the vitamins required by us. All the vitamins are generally available in our diet. Different vitamins belong to various chemical classes and it is difficult to define them on the basis of structure. They are generally regarded as organic compounds required in the diet in small amounts to perform specific biological functions for normal maintenance of optimum growth and health of the organism . Vitamins are designated by alphabets A, B, C, D, etc. Some of them are further named as sub-groups e.g. B 1 , B 2 , B 6 , B 12 , etc. Excess of vitamins is also harmful and vitamin pills should not be taken without the advice of doctor.','[{"s": "It has been observed that certain organic compounds are required in small amounts in our diet but their deficiency causes specific diseases. These compounds are called vitamins. Most of the vitamins cannot be synthesised in our body but plants can synthesise almost all of them, so they are considered as essential food factors. However, the bacteria of the gut can produce some of the vitamins required by us. All the vitamins are generally available in our diet. Different vitamins belong to various chemical classes and it is difficult to define them on the basis of structure. They are generally regarded as organic compounds required in the diet in small amounts to perform specific biological functions for normal maintenance of optimum growth and health of the organism", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Vitamins are designated by alphabets A, B, C, D, etc. Some of them are further named as sub-groups e.g. B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc. Excess of vitamins is also harmful and vitamin pills should not be taken without the advice of doctor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5470,36,176,'paragraph',NULL,'The term “Vitamine” was coined from the word vital + amine since the earlier identified compounds had amino groups. Later work showed that most of them did not contain amino groups, so the letter ‘e’ was dropped and the term vitamin is used these days.','[{"s": "The term “Vitamine” was coined from the word vital + amine since the earlier identified compounds had amino groups. Later work showed that most of them did not contain amino groups, so the letter ‘e’ was dropped and the term vitamin is used these days.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5471,36,177,'heading',4,'14.4.1 Classification of Vitamins',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5472,36,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Vitamins are classified into two groups depending upon their solubility in water or fat.','[{"s": "Vitamins are classified into two groups depending upon their solubility in water or fat.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5473,36,179,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Fat soluble vitamins : Vitamins which are soluble in fat and oils but insoluble in water are kept in this group. These are vitamins A, D, E and K. They are stored in liver and adipose (fat storing) tissues.','[{"s": "(i) Fat soluble vitamins", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Vitamins which are soluble in fat and oils but insoluble in water are kept in this group. These are vitamins A, D, E and K. They are stored in liver and adipose (fat storing) tissues.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5474,36,180,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Water soluble vitamins : B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water so they are grouped together. Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B 12 ) in our body. 3','[{"s": "(ii) Water soluble vitamins", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water so they are grouped together.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "12", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") in our body.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["281241", "281242", "281243"]',NULL),
(5475,36,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Some important vitamins, their sources and diseases caused by their deficiency are listed in Table 14.3.','[{"s": "Some important vitamins, their sources and diseases caused by their deficiency are listed in Table 14.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
