(5911,37,43,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be seen (Table 4.1) that the average rate falls from 1.90× 0 -4 mol L -1 s -1 to 0.4 × 10 -4 mol L -1 s -1 . However, average rate cannot be used to predict the rate of a reaction at a particular instant as it would be constant for the time interval for which it is calculated. So, to express the rate at a particular moment of time we determine the instantaneous rate . It is obtained when we consider the average rate at the smallest time interval say dt ( i.e. when ∆t approaches zero). Hence, mathematically for an infinitesimally small dt instantaneous rate is given by','[{"s": "It can be seen (Table 4.1) that the average rate falls from 1.90× 0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 0.4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However, average rate cannot be used to predict the rate of a reaction at a particular instant as it would be constant for the time interval for which it is calculated. So, to express the rate at a particular moment of time we determine the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "instantaneous rate", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is obtained when we consider the average rate at the smallest time interval say dt ( i.e. when ∆t approaches zero). Hence, mathematically for an infinitesimally small dt instantaneous rate is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
