(5948,37,80,'paragraph',NULL,'4.1 For the reaction R → P, the concentration of a reactant changes from 0.03M to 0.02M in 25 minutes. Calculate the average rate of reaction using units of time both in minutes and seconds.','[{"s": "4.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For the reaction R → P, the concentration of a reactant changes from 0.03M to 0.02M in 25 minutes. Calculate the average rate of reaction using units of time both in minutes and seconds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5949,37,81,'paragraph',NULL,'4.2 In a reaction, 2A → Products, the concentration of A decreases from 0.5 mol L –1 to 0.4 mol L –1 in 10 minutes. Calculate the rate during this interval?','[{"s": "4.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a reaction, 2A → Products, the concentration of A decreases from 0.5 mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 0.4 mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in 10 minutes. Calculate the rate during this interval?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5950,37,82,'heading',3,'4.2 Factors Influencing Rate of a Reaction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5951,37,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate of reaction depends upon the experimental conditions such as concentration of reactants (pressure in case of gases), temperature and catalyst.','[{"s": "Rate of reaction depends upon the experimental conditions such as concentration of reactants (pressure in case of gases), temperature and catalyst.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181062"]',NULL),
(5952,37,84,'heading',4,'4.2.1 Dependence of Rate on Concentration',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5953,37,85,'paragraph',NULL,'The rate of a chemical reaction at a given temperature may depend on the concentration of one or more reactants and products. The representation of rate of reaction in terms of concentration of the reactants is known as rate law . It is also called as rate equation or rate expression.','[{"s": "The rate of a chemical reaction at a given temperature may depend on the concentration of one or more reactants and products. The representation of rate of reaction in terms of concentration of the reactants is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rate law", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is also called as rate equation or rate expression.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5954,37,86,'heading',4,'4.2.2 Rate Expression and Rate Constant',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12131,44,901,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12132,44,902,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5955,37,87,'paragraph',NULL,'The results in Table 4.1 clearly show that rate of a reaction decreases with the passage of time as the concentration of reactants decrease. Conversely, rates generally increase when reactant concentrations increase. So, rate of a reaction depends upon the concentration of reactants.','[{"s": "The results in Table 4.1 clearly show that rate of a reaction decreases with the passage of time as the concentration of reactants decrease. Conversely, rates generally increase when reactant concentrations increase. So, rate of a reaction depends upon the concentration of reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5956,37,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a general reaction','[{"s": "Consider a general reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5957,37,89,'paragraph',NULL,'aA + bB → cC + dD','[{"s": "aA + bB → cC + dD", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5958,37,90,'paragraph',NULL,'where a, b, c and d are the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants and products.','[{"s": "where a, b, c and d are the stoichiometric coefficients of reactants and products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5959,37,91,'paragraph',NULL,'The rate expression for this reaction is','[{"s": "The rate expression for this reaction is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181015"]',NULL),
(5960,37,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate ∝ [A] x [B] y (4.4)','[{"s": "Rate ∝ [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5961,37,93,'paragraph',NULL,'where exponents x and y may or may not be equal to the stoichiometric coefficients (a and b) of the reactants. Above equation can also be written as','[{"s": "where exponents x and y may or may not be equal to the stoichiometric coefficients (a and b) of the reactants. Above equation can also be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5962,37,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate = k [A] x [B] y (4.4a)','[{"s": "Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.4a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5963,37,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical_Kinetics-image79.png (4.4b)','[{"s": "Chemical_Kinetics-image79.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(4.4b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5964,37,96,'paragraph',NULL,'This form of equation (4.4 b) is known as differential rate equation, where k is a proportionality constant called rate constant . For example: The equation like (4.4), which relates the rate of a reaction to concentration of reactants is called rate law or rate expression. Thus, rate law is the expression in which reaction rate is given in terms of molar concentration of reactants with each term raised to some power, which may or may not be same as the stoichiometric coefficient of the reacting species in a balanced chemical equation . 8','[{"s": "This form of equation (4.4 b) is known as differential rate equation, where k is a proportionality constant called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rate constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The equation like (4.4), which relates the rate of a reaction to concentration of reactants is called rate law or rate expression.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "rate law is the expression in which reaction rate is given in terms of molar concentration of reactants with each term raised to some power, which may or may not be same as the stoichiometric coefficient of the reacting species in a balanced chemical equation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "8", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181001", "181002", "181003", "181006", "181018", "181019", "181021"]',NULL),
(5965,37,97,'paragraph',NULL,'2NO(g) + O 2 (g) → 2NO 2 (g)','[{"s": "2NO(g) + O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(g) → 2NO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181009", "181020"]',NULL),
(5966,37,98,'paragraph',NULL,'We can measure the rate of this reaction as a function of initial concentrations either by keeping the concentration of one of the reactants constant and changing the concentration of the other reactant or by changing the concentration of both the reactants. The following results are obtained (Table 4.2).','[{"s": "We can measure the rate of this reaction as a function of initial concentrations either by keeping the concentration of one of the reactants constant and changing the concentration of the other reactant or by changing the concentration of both the reactants. The following results are obtained (Table 4.2).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5967,37,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.2: Initial rate of formation of NO 2','[{"s": "Table 4.2: Initial rate of formation of NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
