(5976,37,108,'paragraph',NULL,'In these reactions, the exponents of the concentration terms are not the same as their stoichiometric coefficients. Thus, we can say that:','[{"s": "In these reactions, the exponents of the concentration terms are not the same as their stoichiometric coefficients. Thus, we can say that:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5977,37,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate law for any reaction cannot be predicted by merely looking at the balanced chemical equation, i.e., theoretically but must be determined experimentally.','[{"s": "Rate law for any reaction cannot be predicted by merely looking at the balanced chemical equation, i.e., theoretically but must be determined experimentally.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5978,37,110,'heading',4,'4.2.3 Order of a Reaction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5979,37,111,'paragraph',NULL,'In the rate equation (4.4)','[{"s": "In the rate equation (4.4)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181063"]',NULL),
(5980,37,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate = k [A] x [B] y','[{"s": "Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5981,37,113,'paragraph',NULL,'x and y indicate how sensitive the rate is to the change in concentration of A and B. Sum of these exponents, i.e., x + y in (4.4) gives the overall order of a reaction whereas x and y represent the order with respect to the reactants A and B respectively.','[{"s": "x and y indicate how sensitive the rate is to the change in concentration of A and B.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Sum of these exponents, i.e., x + y in (4.4) gives the overall order of a reaction whereas x and y represent the order with respect to the reactants A and B respectively.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["181023"]',NULL),
(5982,37,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, the sum of powers of the concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression is called the order of that chemical reaction .','[{"s": "Hence,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the sum of powers of the concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression is called the order of that chemical reaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5983,37,115,'paragraph',NULL,'Order of a reaction can be 0, 1, 2, 3 and even a fraction. A zero order reaction means that the rate of reaction is independent of the concentration of reactants.','[{"s": "Order of a reaction can be 0, 1, 2, 3 and even a fraction. A zero order reaction means that the rate of reaction is independent of the concentration of reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5984,37,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.3','[{"s": "Example 4.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5985,37,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Calculate the overall order of a reaction which has the rate expression','[{"s": "Calculate the overall order of a reaction which has the rate expression", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5986,37,118,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Rate = k [A] 1/2 [B] 3/2','[{"s": "(a) Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5987,37,119,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Rate = k [A] 3/2 [B] –1','[{"s": "(b) Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5988,37,120,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5989,37,121,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Rate = k [A] x [B] y','[{"s": "(a) Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5990,37,122,'paragraph',NULL,'order = x + y','[{"s": "order = x + y", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5991,37,123,'paragraph',NULL,'So order = 1/2 + 3/2 = 2, i.e., second order','[{"s": "So order = 1/2 + 3/2 = 2, i.e., second order", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5992,37,124,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) order = 3/2 + (–1) = 1/2, i.e., half order.','[{"s": "(b) order = 3/2 + (–1) = 1/2, i.e., half order.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5993,37,125,'paragraph',NULL,'A balanced chemical equation never gives us a true picture of how a reaction takes place since rarely a reaction gets completed in one step. The reactions taking place in one step are called elementary reactions . When a sequence of elementary reactions (called mechanism) gives us the products, the reactions are called complex reactions . These may be consecutive reactions (e.g., oxidation of ethane to CO 2 and H 2 O passes through a series of intermediate steps in which alcohol, aldehyde and acid are formed), reverse reactions and side reactions (e.g., nitration of phenol yields o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol).','[{"s": "A balanced chemical equation never gives us a true picture of how a reaction takes place since rarely a reaction gets completed in one step. The reactions taking place in one step are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "elementary reactions", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When a sequence of elementary reactions (called mechanism) gives us the products, the reactions are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "complex reactions", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These may be consecutive reactions (e.g., oxidation of ethane to CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O passes through a series of intermediate steps in which alcohol, aldehyde and acid are formed), reverse reactions and side reactions (e.g., nitration of phenol yields o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5994,37,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Units of rate constant','[{"s": "Units of rate constant", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5995,37,127,'paragraph',NULL,'For a general reaction','[{"s": "For a general reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5996,37,128,'paragraph',NULL,'aA + bB → cC + dD','[{"s": "aA + bB → cC + dD", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5997,37,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Rate = k [A] x [B] y','[{"s": "Rate = k [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(5998,37,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Where x + y = n = order of the reaction','[{"s": "Where x + y = n = order of the reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
