(6290,37,422,'paragraph',NULL,'4.5 A first order reaction has a rate constant 1.15 × 10 -3 s -1 . How long will 5 g of this reactant take to reduce to 3 g?','[{"s": "4.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A first order reaction has a rate constant 1.15 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "How long will 5 g of this reactant take to reduce to 3 g?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181044"]',NULL),
(6291,37,423,'paragraph',NULL,'4.6 Time required to decompose SO 2 Cl 2 to half of its initial amount is 60 minutes. If the decomposition is a first order reaction, calculate the rate constant of the reaction.','[{"s": "4.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Time required to decompose SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to half of its initial amount is 60 minutes. If the decomposition is a first order reaction, calculate the rate constant of the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6292,37,424,'heading',3,'4.5 Temperature Dependence of the Rate of a Reaction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6293,37,425,'paragraph',NULL,'Most of the chemical reactions are accelerated by increase in temperature. For example, in decomposition of N 2 O 5 , the time taken for half of the original amount of material to decompose is 12 min at 50 o C, 5 h at 25 o C and 10 days at 0 o C. You also know that in a mixture of potassium permanganate (KMnO 4 ) and oxalic acid (H 2 C 2 O 4 ), potassium permanganate gets decolourised faster at a higher temperature than that at a lower temperature.','[{"s": "Most of the chemical reactions are accelerated by increase in temperature. For example, in decomposition of N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the time taken for half of the original amount of material to decompose is 12 min at 50", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C, 5 h at 25", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C and 10 days at 0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C. You also know that in a mixture of potassium permanganate (KMnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and oxalic acid (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), potassium permanganate gets decolourised faster at a higher temperature than that at a lower temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6294,37,426,'paragraph',NULL,'It has been found that for a chemical reaction with rise in temperature by 10°, the rate constant is nearly doubled .','[{"s": "It has been found that", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "for a chemical reaction with rise in temperature by 10°, the rate constant is nearly doubled", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181050"]',NULL),
(6295,37,427,'paragraph',NULL,'The temperature dependence of the rate of a chemical reaction can be accurately explained by Arrhenius equation (4.18). It was first proposed by Dutch chemist, J.H. van’t Hoff but Swedish chemist, Arrhenius provided its physical justification and interpretation.','[{"s": "The temperature dependence of the rate of a chemical reaction can be accurately explained by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Arrhenius equation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.18). It was first proposed by Dutch chemist, J.H.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "van’t Hoff but Swedish chemist, Arrhenius provided its physical justification and interpretation.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181051"]',NULL),
(6296,37,428,'paragraph',NULL,'k = A e -Ea /RT (4.18)','[{"s": "k = A e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-Ea /RT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
