(6297,37,429,'paragraph',NULL,'where A is the Arrhenius factor or the frequency factor . 3 It is also called pre-exponential factor. It is a constant specific to a particular reaction. R is gas constant and E a is activation energy measured in joules/mole (J mol –1 ).','[{"s": "where A is the Arrhenius factor or the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "frequency factor", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is also called pre-exponential factor. It is a constant specific to a particular reaction. R is gas constant and E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is activation energy measured in joules/mole (J mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]','marked','["181052", "181053", "181054"]',NULL),
(6298,37,430,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be understood clearly using the following simple reaction','[{"s": "It can be understood clearly using the following simple reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6299,37,431,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image2.png'),
(6300,37,432,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Arrhenius, this reaction can take place only when a molecule of hydrogen and a molecule of iodine collide to form an unstable intermediate (Fig. 4.6). It exists for a very short time and then breaks up to form two molecules of hydrogen iodide.','[{"s": "According to Arrhenius, this reaction can take place only when a molecule of hydrogen and a molecule of iodine collide to form an unstable intermediate (Fig. 4.6). It exists for a very short time and then breaks up to form two molecules of hydrogen iodide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6301,37,433,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image112.png'),
(6302,37,434,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.6: Formation of HI through the intermediate','[{"s": "Fig. 4.6: Formation of HI through the intermediate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6303,37,435,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy required to form this intermediate, called activated complex (C), is known as activation energy ( Ea ) . Fig. 4.7 is obtained by plotting potential energy vs reaction coordinate. Reaction coordinate represents the profile of energy change when reactants change into products.','[{"s": "The energy required to form this intermediate, called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "activated complex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(C), is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "activation energy (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ea", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Fig. 4.7 is obtained by plotting potential energy vs reaction coordinate. Reaction coordinate represents the profile of energy change when reactants change into products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6304,37,436,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image42.png'),
(6305,37,437,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.7: Diagram showing plot of potential energy vs reaction coordinate. 3','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4.7: Diagram showing plot of potential energy vs reaction coordinate.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["181055", "181075", "181076"]',NULL),
(6306,37,438,'paragraph',NULL,'Some energy is released when the complex decomposes to form products. So, the final enthalpy of the reaction depends upon the nature of reactants and products.','[{"s": "Some energy is released when the complex decomposes to form products. So, the final enthalpy of the reaction depends upon the nature of reactants and products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6307,37,439,'paragraph',NULL,'All the molecules in the reacting species do not have the same kinetic energy. Since it is difficult to predict the behaviour of any one molecule with precision, Ludwig Boltzmann and James Clark Maxwell used statistics to predict the behaviour of large number of molecules. According to them, the distribution of kinetic energy may be described by plotting the fraction of molecules (N E /N T ) with a given kinetic energy (E) vs kinetic energy (Fig. 4.8). Here, N E is the number of molecules with energy E and N T is total number of molecules.','[{"s": "All the molecules in the reacting species do not have the same kinetic energy. Since it is difficult to predict the behaviour of any one molecule with precision, Ludwig Boltzmann and James Clark Maxwell used statistics to predict the behaviour of large number of molecules. According to them, the distribution of kinetic energy may be described by plotting the fraction of molecules (N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") with a given kinetic energy (E) vs kinetic energy (Fig. 4.8). Here, N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the number of molecules with energy E and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is total number of molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6308,37,440,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image61.png'),
(6309,37,441,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.8: Distribution curve showing energies among gaseous molecules','[{"s": "Fig. 4.8: Distribution curve showing energies among gaseous molecules", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6310,37,442,'paragraph',NULL,'The peak of the curve corresponds to the most probable kinetic energy, i.e., kinetic energy of maximum fraction of molecules. There are decreasing number of molecules with energies higher or lower than this value. When the temperature is raised, the maximum of the curve moves to the higher energy value (Fig. 4.9) and the curve broadens out, i.e., spreads to the right such that there is a greater proportion of molecules with much higher energies. The area under the curve must be constant since total probability must be one at all times. We can mark the position of E a on Maxwell Boltzmann distribution curve (Fig. 4.9).','[{"s": "The peak of the curve corresponds to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "most", "t": "text"}, {"s": "probable kinetic energy,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e., kinetic energy of maximum fraction of molecules. There are decreasing number of molecules with energies higher or lower than this value. When the temperature is raised, the maximum of the curve moves to the higher energy value (Fig. 4.9) and the curve broadens out, i.e., spreads to the right such that there is a greater proportion of molecules with much higher energies. The area under the curve must be constant since total probability must be one at all times. We can mark the position of E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on Maxwell Boltzmann distribution curve (Fig. 4.9).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6311,37,443,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_Kinetics-image49.png'),
(6312,37,444,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.9: Distribution curve showing temperature dependence of rate of a reaction','[{"s": "Fig. 4.9: Distribution curve showing temperature dependence of rate of a reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12160,44,930,'paragraph',NULL,'��3 |(���) �3�—�—��3 | ��','[{"s": "��3 |(���) �3�—�—��3 | ��", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12161,44,931,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (ii) < (i) < (iii)','[{"s": "(a) (ii) < (i) < (iii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6384,37,516,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical kinetics is the study of chemical reactions with respect to reaction rates, effect of various variables, rearrangement of atoms and formation of intermediates. The rate of a reaction is concerned with decrease in concentration of reactants or increase in the concentration of products per unit time. It can be expressed as instantaneous rate at a particular instant of time and average rate over a large interval of time. A number of factors such as temperature, concentration of reactants, catalyst, affect the rate of a reaction. Mathematical representation of rate of a reaction is given by rate law . It has to be determined experimentally and cannot be predicted. Order of a reaction with respect to a reactant is the power of its concentration which appears in the rate law equation. The order of a reaction is the sum of all such powers of concentration of terms for different reactants. Rate constant is the proportionality factor in the rate law. Rate constant and order of a reaction can be determined from rate law or its integrated rate equation. Molecularity is defined only for an elementary reaction. Its values are limited from 1 to 3 whereas order can be 0, 1, 2, 3 or even a fraction. Molecularity and order of an elementary reaction are same.','[{"s": "Chemical kinetics", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the study of chemical reactions with respect to reaction rates, effect of various variables, rearrangement of atoms and formation of intermediates. The rate of a reaction is concerned with decrease in concentration of reactants or increase in the concentration of products per unit time. It can be expressed as instantaneous rate at a particular instant of time and average rate over a large interval of time. A number of factors such as temperature, concentration of reactants, catalyst, affect the rate of a reaction. Mathematical representation of rate of a reaction is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rate law", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It has to be determined experimentally and cannot be predicted.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Order of a reaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to a reactant is the power of its concentration which appears in the rate law equation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The order of a reaction is the sum of all such powers of concentration of terms for different reactants.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rate constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the proportionality factor in the rate law. Rate constant and order of a reaction can be determined from rate law or its integrated rate equation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Molecularity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined only for an elementary reaction. Its values are limited from 1 to 3 whereas order can be 0, 1, 2, 3 or even a fraction. Molecularity and order of an elementary reaction are same.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6385,37,517,'paragraph',NULL,'Temperature dependence of rate constants is described by Arrhenius equation (k = Ae – E a/ RT). E a corresponds to the activation energy and is given by the energy difference between activated complex and the reactant molecules, and A (Arrhenius factor or pre-exponential factor) corresponds to the collision frequency. The equation clearly shows that increase of temperature or lowering of E a will lead to an increase in the rate of reaction and presence of a catalyst lowers the activation energy by providing an alternate path for the reaction. According to collision theory, another factor P called steric factor which refers to the orientation of molecules which collide, is important and contributes to effective collisions, thus, modifying the Arrhenius equation to Chemical_Kinetics-image9.png .','[{"s": "Temperature dependence of rate constants is described by Arrhenius equation (k = Ae", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT). E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corresponds to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "activation energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is given by the energy difference between activated complex and the reactant molecules, and A (Arrhenius factor or pre-exponential factor) corresponds to the collision frequency. The equation clearly shows that increase of temperature or lowering of E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will lead to an increase in the rate of reaction and presence of a catalyst lowers the activation energy by providing an alternate path for the reaction. According to collision theory, another factor P called steric factor which refers to the orientation of molecules which collide, is important and contributes to effective collisions, thus, modifying the Arrhenius equation to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_Kinetics-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
