(6786,38,36,'paragraph',NULL,'[He] 2s 2 2p 5 [He] 2s 2 2p 6 or [Ne]','[{"s": "[He] 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[He] 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or [Ne]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6787,38,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Ca 2+ + 2F – → CaF 2 or Ca 2+ (F – ) 2','[{"s": "Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ CaF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6788,38,38,'paragraph',NULL,'The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion. Thus, calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one.','[{"s": "The bond formed, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the positive and negative ions was termed as the electrovalent bond. The electrovalence is thus equal to the number of unit charge(s) on the ion.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, calcium is assigned a positive electrovalence of two, while chlorine a negative electrovalence of one.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6789,38,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. His views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. At the same time he did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts.','[{"s": "Kössel’s postulations provide the basis for the modern concepts regarding ion-formation by electron transfer and the formation of ionic crystalline compounds. His views have proved to be of great value in the understanding and systematisation of the ionic compounds. At the same time he did recognise the fact that a large number of compounds did not fit into these concepts.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6790,38,40,'heading',4,'4.1.1 Octet Rule',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12165,44,935,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 9 In which of the following molecules carbon atom marked with asterisk (*)','[{"s": "Q. 9 In which of the following molecules carbon atom marked with asterisk (*)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12166,44,936,'paragraph',NULL,'is asymmetric?','[{"s": "is asymmetric?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6791,38,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Kössel and Lewis in 1916 developed an important theory of chemical combination between atoms known as electronic theory of chemical bonding . According to this, atoms can combine either by transfer of valence electrons from one atom to another (gaining or losing) or by sharing of valence electrons in order to have an octet in their valence shells. This is known as octet rule .','[{"s": "Kössel and Lewis in 1916 developed an important theory of chemical combination between atoms known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronic theory", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chemical bonding", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to this, atoms can combine either by transfer of valence electrons from one atom to another (gaining or losing) or by sharing of valence electrons in order to have an octet in their valence shells. This is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "octet rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6792,38,42,'heading',4,'4.1.2 Covalent Bond',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6793,38,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Langmuir (1919) refined the Lewis postulations by abandoning the idea of the stationary cubical arrangement of the octet, and by introducing the term covalent bond . The Lewis-Langmuir theory can be understood by considering the formation of the chlorine molecule,Cl 2 . The Cl atom with electronic configuration, [Ne]3s 2 3p 5 , is one electron short of the argon configuration. The formation of the Cl­ 2 molecule can be understood in terms of the sharing of a pair of electrons between the two chlorine atoms, each chlorine atom contributing one electron to the shared pair. In the process both chlorine atoms attain the outer shell octet of the nearest noble gas (i.e., argon).','[{"s": "Langmuir", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1919) refined the Lewis postulations by abandoning the idea of the stationary cubical arrangement of the octet, and by introducing the term", "t": "text"}, {"s": "covalent bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The Lewis-Langmuir theory can be understood by considering the formation of the chlorine molecule,Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The Cl atom with electronic configuration, [Ne]3s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is one electron short of the argon configuration. The formation of the Cl­", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule can be understood in terms of the sharing of a pair of electrons between the two chlorine atoms, each chlorine atom contributing one electron to the shared pair. In the process both chlorine atoms attain the outer shell octet of the nearest noble gas (i.e., argon).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6794,38,44,'paragraph',NULL,'or Cl – Cl','[{"s": "or Cl – Cl", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6795,38,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image50.png'),
(6796,38,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Covalent bond between two Cl atoms','[{"s": "Covalent bond between two Cl atoms", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6797,38,47,'paragraph',NULL,'The dots represent electrons. Such structures are referred to as Lewis dot structures.','[{"s": "The dots represent electrons. Such structures are referred to as Lewis dot structures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6798,38,48,'paragraph',NULL,'The Lewis dot structures can be written for other molecules also, in which the combining atoms may be identical or different. The important conditions being that:','[{"s": "The Lewis dot structures can be written for other molecules also, in which the combining atoms may be identical or different. The important conditions being that:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6799,38,49,'paragraph',NULL,'• Each bond is formed as a result of sharing of an electron pair between the atoms.','[{"s": "• Each bond is formed as a result of sharing of an electron pair between the atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6800,38,50,'paragraph',NULL,'• Each combining atom contributes at least one electron to the shared pair.','[{"s": "• Each combining atom contributes at least one electron to the shared pair.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6801,38,51,'paragraph',NULL,'• The combining atoms attain the outer-shell noble gas configurations as a result of the sharing of electrons.','[{"s": "• The combining atoms attain the outer-shell noble gas configurations as a result of the sharing of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6802,38,52,'paragraph',NULL,'• Thus in water and carbon tetrachloride molecules, formation of covalent bonds can be represented as:','[{"s": "• Thus in water and carbon tetrachloride molecules, formation of covalent bonds can be represented as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6803,38,53,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image57.png'),
(6804,38,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, when two atoms share one electron pair they are said to be joined by a single covalent bond. In many compounds we have multiple bonds between atoms. The formation of multiple bonds envisages sharing of more than one electron pair between two atoms. If two atoms share two pairs of electrons, the covalent bond between them is called a double bond. For example, in the carbon dioxide molecule, we have two double bonds between the carbon and oxygen atoms. Similarly in ethene molecule the two carbon atoms are joined by a double bond.','[{"s": "Thus, when two atoms share one electron pair they are said to be joined by a single covalent bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In many compounds we have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiple bonds", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between atoms. The formation of multiple bonds envisages sharing of more than one electron pair between two atoms.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If two atoms share two pairs of electrons, the covalent bond between them is called a double bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, in the carbon dioxide molecule, we have two double bonds between the carbon and oxygen atoms. Similarly in ethene molecule the two carbon atoms are joined by a double bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6805,38,55,'paragraph',NULL,'When combining atoms share three electron pairs as in the case of two nitrogen atoms in the N 2 molecule and the two carbon atoms in the ethyne molecule, a triple bond is formed.','[{"s": "When combining atoms share three electron pairs as in the case of two nitrogen atoms in the N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule and the two carbon atoms in the ethyne molecule, a triple bond is formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6806,38,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image52.png'),
(6807,38,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Double bonds in CO 2 molecule','[{"s": "Double bonds in CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
