(6811,38,61,'paragraph',NULL,'N 2 molecule','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6812,38,62,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image29.png'),
(6813,38,63,'paragraph',NULL,'C 2 H 2 molecule','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6814,38,64,'paragraph',NULL,'4.1.3 Lewis Representation of Simple Molecules (the Lewis Structures)','[{"s": "4.1.3 Lewis Representation of Simple Molecules (the Lewis Structures)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6815,38,65,'paragraph',NULL,'The Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule. While such a picture may not explain the bonding and behaviour of a molecule completely, it does help in understanding the formation and properties of a molecule to a large extent. Writing of Lewis dot structures of molecules is, therefore, very useful. The Lewis dot structures can be written by adopting the following steps:','[{"s": "The Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "While such a picture may not explain the bonding and behaviour of a molecule completely, it does help in understanding the formation and properties of a molecule to a large extent. Writing of Lewis dot structures of molecules is, therefore, very useful. The Lewis dot structures can be written by adopting the following steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6816,38,66,'paragraph',NULL,'• The total number of electrons required for writing the structures are obtained by adding the valence electrons of the combining atoms. For example, in the CH 4 molecule there are eight valence electrons available for bonding (4 from carbon and 4 from the four hydrogen atoms).','[{"s": "• The total number of electrons required for writing the structures are obtained by adding the valence electrons of the combining atoms. For example, in the CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule there are eight valence electrons available for bonding (4 from carbon and 4 from the four hydrogen atoms).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6817,38,67,'paragraph',NULL,'• For anions, each negative charge would mean addition of one electron. For cations, each positive charge would result in subtraction of one electron from the total number of valence electrons. For example, for the CO 3 2– ion, the two negative charges indicate that there are two additional electrons than those provided by the neutral atoms. For NH 4 + ion, one positive charge indicates the loss of one electron from the group of neutral atoms.','[{"s": "• For anions, each negative charge would mean addition of one electron. For cations, each positive charge would result in subtraction of one electron from the total number of valence electrons. For example, for the CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion, the two negative charges indicate that there are two additional electrons than those provided by the neutral atoms. For NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion, one positive charge indicates the loss of one electron from the group of neutral atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6818,38,68,'paragraph',NULL,'• Knowing the chemical symbols of the combining atoms and having knowledge of the skeletal structure of the compound (known or guessed intelligently), it is easy to distribute the total number of electrons as bonding shared pairs between the atoms in proportion to the total bonds.','[{"s": "• Knowing the chemical symbols of the combining atoms and having knowledge of the skeletal structure of the compound (known or guessed intelligently), it is easy to distribute the total number of electrons as bonding shared pairs between the atoms in proportion to the total bonds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6819,38,69,'paragraph',NULL,'• In general the least electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion. For example in the NF 3 and CO 3 2– , nitrogen and carbon are the central atoms whereas fluorine and oxygen occupy the terminal positions.','[{"s": "• In general the least electronegative atom occupies the central position in the molecule/ion. For example in the NF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", nitrogen and carbon are the central atoms whereas fluorine and oxygen occupy the terminal positions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6820,38,70,'paragraph',NULL,'• After accounting for the shared pairs of electrons for single bonds, the remaining electron pairs are either utilized for multiple bonding or remain as the lone pairs. The basic requirement being that each bonded atom gets an octet of electrons.','[{"s": "• After accounting for the shared pairs of electrons for single bonds, the remaining electron pairs are either utilized for multiple bonding or remain as the lone pairs. The basic requirement being that each bonded atom gets an octet of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6821,38,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Lewis representations of a few molecules/ ions are given in Table 4.1.','[{"s": "Lewis representations of a few molecules/ ions are given in Table 4.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6822,38,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.1 The Lewis Representation of Some Molecules','[{"s": "Table 4.1 The Lewis Representation of Some Molecules", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6823,38,73,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image72.png'),
(6824,38,74,'paragraph',NULL,'* Each H atom attains the configuration of helium (a duplet of electrons)','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each H atom attains the configuration of helium (a duplet of electrons)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6825,38,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 4.1','[{"s": "Problem 4.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6826,38,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Write the Lewis dot structure of CO molecule.','[{"s": "Write the Lewis dot structure of CO molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6827,38,77,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6828,38,78,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1. Count the total number of valence electrons of carbon and oxygen atoms. The outer (valence) shell configurations of carbon and oxygen atoms are: 2s 2 2p 2 and 2s 2 2p 4 , respectively. The valence electrons available are 4 + 6 =10.','[{"s": "Step 1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Count the total number of valence electrons of carbon and oxygen atoms. The outer (valence) shell configurations of carbon and oxygen atoms are: 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. The valence electrons available are 4 + 6 =10.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6829,38,79,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2. The skeletal structure of CO is written as: C O','[{"s": "Step 2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The skeletal structure of CO is written as: C O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6830,38,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3. Draw a single bond (one shared electron pair) between C and O and complete the octet on O, the remaining two electrons are the lone pair on C.','[{"s": "Step 3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Draw a single bond (one shared electron pair) between C and O and complete the octet on O, the remaining two electrons are the lone pair on C.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6831,38,81,'paragraph',NULL,'This does not complete the octet on carbon and hence we have to resort to multiple bonding (in this case a triple bond) between C and O atoms. This satisfies the octet rule condition for both atoms.','[{"s": "This does not complete the octet on carbon and hence we have to resort to multiple bonding (in this case a triple bond) between C and O atoms. This satisfies the octet rule condition for both atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6832,38,82,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image36.png'),
(6833,38,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 4.2','[{"s": "Problem 4.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6834,38,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Write the Lewis structure of the nitrite ion, NO 2 – .','[{"s": "Write the Lewis structure of the nitrite ion, NO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41937"]',NULL),
(6835,38,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6836,38,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1. Count the total number of valence electrons of the nitrogen atom, the oxygen atoms and the additional one negative charge (equal to one electron).','[{"s": "Step 1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Count the total number of valence electrons of the nitrogen atom, the oxygen atoms and the additional one negative charge (equal to one electron).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6837,38,87,'paragraph',NULL,'N(2s 2 2p 3 ), O (2s 2 2p 4 )','[{"s": "N(2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), O (2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6838,38,88,'paragraph',NULL,'5 + (2 × 6) +1 = 18 electrons','[{"s": "5 + (2 × 6) +1 = 18 electrons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6839,38,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2. The skeletal structure of NO 2 – is written as : O N O','[{"s": "Step 2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The skeletal structure of NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is written as : O N O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6840,38,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3. Draw a single bond (one shared electron pair) between the nitrogen and each of the oxygen atoms completing the octets on oxygen atoms. This, however, does not complete the octet on nitrogen if the remaining two electrons constitute lone pair on it.','[{"s": "Step 3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Draw a single bond (one shared electron pair) between the nitrogen and each of the oxygen atoms completing the octets on oxygen atoms. This, however, does not complete the octet on nitrogen if the remaining two electrons constitute lone pair on it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6841,38,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence we have to resort to multiple bonding between nitrogen and one of the oxygen atoms (in this case a double bond). This leads to the following Lewis dot structures.','[{"s": "Hence we have to resort to multiple bonding between nitrogen and one of the oxygen atoms (in this case a double bond). This leads to the following Lewis dot structures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6842,38,92,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image40.png'),
(6843,38,93,'heading',4,'4.1.4 Formal Charge',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6844,38,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Lewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules. In case of polyatomic ions, the net charge is possessed by the ion as a whole and not by a particular atom. It is, however, feasible to assign a formal charge on each atom. The formal charge of an atom in a polyatomic molecule or ion may be defined as the difference between the number of valence electrons of that atom in an isolated or free state and the number of electrons assigned to that atom in the Lewis structure. It is expressed as :','[{"s": "Lewis dot structures, in general, do not represent the actual shapes of the molecules. In case of polyatomic ions, the net charge is possessed by the ion as a whole and not by a particular atom. It is, however, feasible to assign a formal charge on each atom.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The formal charge of an atom in a polyatomic molecule or ion may be defined as the difference between the number of valence electrons of that atom in an isolated or free state and the number of electrons assigned to that atom in the Lewis structure.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is expressed as :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["42005"]',NULL),
(6845,38,95,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-04 12-12-06',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image20.png'),
(6846,38,96,'paragraph',NULL,'The counting is based on the assumption that the atom in the molecule owns one electron of each shared pair and both the electrons of a lone pair.','[{"s": "The counting is based on the assumption that the atom in the molecule owns one electron of each shared pair and both the electrons of a lone pair.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6847,38,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider the ozone molecule (O 3 ). The Lewis structure of O 3 may be drawn as :','[{"s": "Let us consider the ozone molecule (O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The Lewis structure of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may be drawn as :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["214254"]',NULL),
(6848,38,98,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image12.png'),
(6849,38,99,'paragraph',NULL,'The atoms have been numbered as 1, 2 and 3. The formal charge on:','[{"s": "The atoms have been numbered as 1, 2 and 3. The formal charge on:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6850,38,100,'paragraph',NULL,'• The central O atom marked 1','[{"s": "• The central O atom marked 1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6851,38,101,'paragraph',NULL,'= 6 – 2 – Chemical_bonding-image4.png (6) = +1','[{"s": "= 6 – 2 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_bonding-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-04 12-14-22"}, {"s": "(6) = +1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6852,38,102,'paragraph',NULL,'• The end O atom marked 2','[{"s": "• The end O atom marked 2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12167,44,937,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (i) , (ii), (iii) and (iv)','[{"s": "(a) (i) , (ii), (iii) and (iv)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6853,38,103,'paragraph',NULL,'= 6 – 4 – Chemical_bonding-image4.png (4) = 0','[{"s": "= 6 – 4 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_bonding-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-04 12-14-22"}, {"s": "(4) = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6854,38,104,'paragraph',NULL,'• The end O atom marked 3','[{"s": "• The end O atom marked 3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
