(6950,38,200,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 4.4','[{"s": "Problem 4.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6951,38,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Explain the structure of CO 2 molecule.','[{"s": "Explain the structure of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6952,38,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6953,38,203,'paragraph',NULL,'The experimentally determined carbon to oxygen bond length in CO 2 is 115 pm. The lengths of a normal carbon to oxygen double bond (C=O) and carbon to oxygen triple bond (C≡O) are 121 pm and 110 pm respectively. The carbon-oxygen bond lengths in CO 2 (115 pm) lie between the values for C=O and C≡O. Obviously, a single Lewis structure cannot depict this position and it becomes necessary to write more than one Lewis structures and to consider that the structure of CO 2 is best described as a hybrid of the canonical or resonance forms I, II and III.','[{"s": "The experimentally determined carbon to oxygen bond length in CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 115 pm. The lengths of a normal carbon to oxygen double bond (C=O) and carbon to oxygen triple bond (C≡O) are 121 pm and 110 pm respectively. The carbon-oxygen bond lengths in CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(115 pm) lie between the values for C=O and C≡O. Obviously, a single Lewis structure cannot depict this position and it becomes necessary to write more than one Lewis structures and to consider that the structure of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is best described as a hybrid of the canonical or resonance forms I, II and III.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6954,38,204,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image22.png'),
(6955,38,205,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.5 Resonance in CO 2 molecule, I, II and III represent the three canonical forms.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.5 Resonance in CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule, I, II and III represent the three canonical forms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6956,38,206,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, it may be stated that','[{"s": "In general, it may be stated that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6957,38,207,'paragraph',NULL,'• Resonance stabilizes the molecule as the energy of the resonance hybrid is less than the energy of any single cannonical structure; and,','[{"s": "• Resonance stabilizes the molecule as the energy of the resonance hybrid is less than the energy of any single cannonical structure; and,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41933"]',NULL),
(10540,43,526,'heading',4,'7.10.1 Arrhenius Concept of Acids and Bases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6958,38,208,'paragraph',NULL,'• Resonance averages the bond characteristics as a whole.','[{"s": "• Resonance averages the bond characteristics as a whole.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6959,38,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus the energy of the O 3 resonance hybrid is lower than either of the two cannonical froms I and II (Fig 4.3).','[{"s": "Thus the energy of the O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resonance hybrid is lower than either of the two cannonical froms I and II (Fig 4.3).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6960,38,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Many misconceptions are associated with resonance and the same need to be dispelled. You should remember that :','[{"s": "Many", "t": "text"}, {"s": "misconceptions", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are associated with resonance and the same need to be dispelled. You should remember that :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6961,38,211,'paragraph',NULL,'• The cannonical forms have no real existence.','[{"s": "• The cannonical forms have no real existence.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6962,38,212,'paragraph',NULL,'• The molecule does not exist for a certain fraction of time in one cannonical form and for other fractions of time in other cannonical forms.','[{"s": "• The molecule does not exist for a certain fraction of time in one cannonical form and for other fractions of time in other cannonical forms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6963,38,213,'paragraph',NULL,'• There is no such equilibrium between the cannonical forms as we have between tautomeric forms (keto and enol) in tautomerism.','[{"s": "• There is no such equilibrium between the cannonical forms as we have between tautomeric forms (keto and enol) in tautomerism.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6964,38,214,'paragraph',NULL,'• The molecule as such has a single structure which is the resonance hybrid of the cannonical forms and which cannot as such be depicted by a single Lewis structure.','[{"s": "• The molecule as such has a single structure which is the resonance hybrid of the cannonical forms and which cannot as such be depicted by a single Lewis structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6965,38,215,'heading',4,'4.3.6 Polarity of Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6966,38,216,'paragraph',NULL,'The existence of a hundred percent ionic or covalent bond represents an ideal situation. In reality no bond or a compound is either completely covalent or ionic. Even in case of covalent bond between two hydrogen atoms, there is some ionic character.','[{"s": "The existence of a hundred percent ionic or covalent bond represents an ideal situation. In reality no bond or a compound is either completely covalent or ionic. Even in case of covalent bond between two hydrogen atoms, there is some ionic character.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6967,38,217,'paragraph',NULL,'When covalent bond is formed between two similar atoms, for example in H 2 , O 2 , Cl 2 , N 2 or F 2 , the shared pair of electrons is equally attracted by the two atoms. As a result electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei. The bond so formed is called nonpolar covalent bond. Contrary to this in case of a heteronuclear molecule like HF, the shared electron pair between the two atoms gets displaced more towards fluorine since the electronegativity of fluorine (Unit 3) is far greater than that of hydrogen. The resultant covalent bond is a polar covalent bond.','[{"s": "When covalent bond is formed between two similar atoms, for example in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the shared pair of electrons is equally attracted by the two atoms. As a result electron pair is situated exactly between the two identical nuclei. The bond so formed is called nonpolar covalent bond. Contrary to this in case of a heteronuclear molecule like HF, the shared electron pair between the two atoms gets displaced more towards fluorine since the electronegativity of fluorine (Unit 3) is far greater than that of hydrogen. The resultant covalent bond is a polar covalent bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6968,38,218,'paragraph',NULL,'As a result of polarisation, the molecule possesses the dipole moment (depicted below) which can be defined as the product of the magnitude of the charge and the distance between the centres of positive and negative charge. It is usually designated by a Greek letter ‘µ’. Mathematically, it is expressed as follows :','[{"s": "As a result of polarisation, the molecule possesses the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dipole moment", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(depicted below) which can be defined as the product of the magnitude of the charge and the distance between the centres of positive and negative charge. It is usually designated by a Greek letter ‘µ’. Mathematically, it is expressed as follows :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6969,38,219,'paragraph',NULL,'Dipole moment (µ) = charge (Q) × distaTable 4.2 Average Bond Lengths for Some Single, Double and Triple Bonds Bond Type Covalent Bond Lengthnce of separation (r)','[{"s": "Dipole moment (µ) = charge (Q) × distaTable 4.2 Average Bond Lengths for Some Single, Double and Triple Bonds Bond Type Covalent Bond Lengthnce of separation (r)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6970,38,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Dipole moment is usually expressed in Debye units (D). The conversion factor is','[{"s": "Dipole moment is usually expressed in Debye units (D). The conversion factor is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6971,38,221,'paragraph',NULL,'1 D = 3.33564 × 10 –30 C m','[{"s": "1 D = 3.33564 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6972,38,222,'paragraph',NULL,'where C is coulomb and m is meter.','[{"s": "where C is coulomb and m is meter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6973,38,223,'paragraph',NULL,'Further dipole moment is a vector quantity and by convention it is depicted by a small arrow with tail on the negative centre and head pointing towards the positive centre. But in chemistry presence of dipole moment is represented by the crossed arrow ( Chemical_bonding-image2.png ) put on Lewis structure of the molecule. The cross is on positive end and arrow head is on negative end. For example the dipole moment of HF may be represented as :','[{"s": "Further dipole moment is a vector quantity and by convention it is depicted by a small arrow with tail on the negative centre and head pointing towards the positive centre. But in chemistry presence of dipole moment is represented by the crossed arrow (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_bonding-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") put on Lewis structure of the molecule. The cross is on positive end and arrow head is on negative end. For example the dipole moment of HF may be represented as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6974,38,224,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-04 14-36-15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image42.png'),
(12175,44,945,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 11 Which of the following is an example of vic-dihalide?','[{"s": "Q. 11 Which of the following is an example of vic-dihalide?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6975,38,225,'paragraph',NULL,'This arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule. Note that the direction of crossed arrow is opposite to the conventional direction of dipole moment vector.','[{"s": "This arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density in the molecule. Note that the direction of crossed arrow is opposite to the conventional direction of dipole moment vector.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6976,38,226,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of polyatomic molecules the dipole moment not only depend upon the individual dipole moments of bonds known as bond dipoles but also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule. In such case, the dipole moment of a molecule is the vector sum of the dipole moments of various bonds. For example in H 2 O molecule, which has a bent structure, the two O–H bonds are oriented at an angle of 104.5 0 . Net dipole moment of 6.17 × 10 –30 C m (1D = 3.33564 × 10 –30 C m) is the resultant of the dipole moments of two O–H bonds.','[{"s": "In case of polyatomic molecules the dipole moment not only depend upon the individual dipole moments of bonds known as bond dipoles but also on the spatial arrangement of various bonds in the molecule. In such case, the dipole moment of a molecule is the vector sum of the dipole moments of various bonds. For example in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecule, which has a bent structure, the two O–H bonds are oriented at an angle of 104.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Net dipole moment of 6.17 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m (1D = 3.33564 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m) is the resultant of the dipole moments of two O–H bonds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6977,38,227,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image38.png'),
(6978,38,228,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image31.png'),
(6979,38,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Peter Debye, the Dutch chemist received Nobel prize in 1936 for his work on X-ray diffraction and dipole moments. The magnitude of the dipole moment is given in Debye units in order to honour him.','[{"s": "Peter Debye, the Dutch chemist received Nobel prize in 1936 for his work on X-ray diffraction and dipole moments. The magnitude of the dipole moment is given in Debye units in order to honour him.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6980,38,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Net Dipole moment, µ = 1.85 D = 1.85 × 3.33564 × 10 –30 C m = 6.17 ×10 –30 C m','[{"s": "Net Dipole moment, µ = 1.85 D = 1.85 × 3.33564 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m = 6.17 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6981,38,231,'paragraph',NULL,'The dipole moment in case of BeF 2 is zero. This is because the two equal bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel the effect of each other.','[{"s": "The dipole moment in case of BeF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is because the two equal bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel the effect of each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6982,38,232,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image11.png'),
(6983,38,233,'paragraph',NULL,'In tetra-atomic molecule, for example in BF 3 , the dipole moment is zero although the B – F bonds are oriented at an angle of 120 o to one another, the three bond moments give a net sum of zero as the resultant of any two is equal and opposite to the third.','[{"s": "In tetra-atomic molecule, for example in BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the dipole moment is zero although the B – F bonds are oriented at an angle of 120", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to one another, the three bond moments give a net sum of zero as the resultant of any two is equal and opposite to the third.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6984,38,234,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image33.png'),
(6985,38,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us study an interesting case of NH 3 and NF 3 molecule. 2 Both the molecules have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the resultant dipole moment of NH 3 ( 4.90 × 10 –30 C m) is greater than that of NF 3 (0.8 × 10 –30 C m). This is because, in case of NH 3 the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N – H bonds, whereas in NF 3 the orbital dipole is in the direction opposite to the resultant dipole moment of the three N–F bonds. The orbital dipole because of lone pair decreases the effect of the resultant N – F bond moments, which results in the low dipole moment of NF 3 as represented below :','[{"s": "Let us study an interesting case of NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and NF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Both the molecules have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the resultant dipole moment of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( 4.90 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m) is greater than that of NF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is because, in case of NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N – H bonds, whereas in NF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "the orbital dipole is in the direction opposite to the resultant dipole moment of the three N–F bonds.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The orbital dipole because of lone pair decreases the effect of the resultant N – F bond moments, which results in the low dipole moment of NF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as represented below :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41905", "41919", "41935"]',NULL),
(6986,38,236,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image75.png'),
(6987,38,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Dipole moments of some molecules are shown in Table 4.5.','[{"s": "Dipole moments of some molecules are shown in Table 4.5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6988,38,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Just as all the covalent bonds have some partial ionic character, the ionic bonds also have partial covalent character. in terms of the following rules:','[{"s": "Just as all the covalent bonds have some partial ionic character, the ionic bonds also have partial covalent character.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "in terms of the following rules:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6989,38,239,'paragraph',NULL,'• The smaller the size of the cation and the larger the size of the anion, the greater the covalent character of an ionic bond. 2','[{"s": "• The smaller the size of the cation and the larger the size of the anion, the greater the covalent character of an ionic bond.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41956", "42250"]',NULL),
(6990,38,240,'paragraph',NULL,'• The greater the charge on the cation, the greater the covalent character of the ionic bond.','[{"s": "• The greater the charge on the cation, the greater the covalent character of the ionic bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12176,44,946,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Dichloromethane (b) 1, 2-dichloroethane','[{"s": "(a) Dichloromethane (b) 1, 2-dichloroethane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12177,44,947,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Lthylidene chloride (d) Allyl chloride','[{"s": "(c) Lthylidene chloride (d) Allyl chloride", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6991,38,241,'paragraph',NULL,'n ns o , typical of transition metals, is more polarising than the one with a noble gas configuration, ns 2 np 6 , typical of alkali and alkaline earth metal cations.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", typical of transition metals, is more polarising than the one with a noble gas configuration, ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", typical of alkali and alkaline earth metal cations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6992,38,242,'paragraph',NULL,'The cation polarises the anion, pulling the electronic charge toward itself and thereby increasing the electronic charge between the two. This is precisely what happens in a covalent bond, i.e., buildup of electron charge density between the nuclei. The polarising power of the cation, the polarisability of the anion and the extent of distortion (polarisation) of anion are the factors, which determine the per cent covalent character of the ionic bond.','[{"s": "The cation polarises the anion, pulling the electronic charge toward itself and thereby increasing the electronic charge between the two. This is precisely what happens in a covalent bond, i.e., buildup of electron charge density between the nuclei. The polarising power of the cation, the polarisability of the anion and the extent of distortion (polarisation) of anion are the factors, which determine the per cent covalent character of the ionic bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6993,38,243,'heading',3,'4.4 The Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion (VSEPR) Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6994,38,244,'paragraph',NULL,'As already explained, Lewis concept is unable to explain the shapes of molecules. This theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules. Sidgwick and Powell in 1940, proposed a simple theory based on the repulsive interactions of the electron pairs in the valence shell of the atoms. It was further developed and redefined by Nyholm and Gillespie (1957).','[{"s": "As already explained, Lewis concept is unable to explain the shapes of molecules.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules. Sidgwick and Powell in 1940, proposed a simple theory based on the repulsive interactions of the electron pairs in the valence shell of the atoms. It was further developed and redefined by Nyholm and Gillespie (1957).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41921"]',NULL),
(6995,38,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.5 Dipole Moments of Selected Molecules','[{"s": "Table 4.5 Dipole Moments of Selected Molecules", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41919"]',NULL),
(6996,38,246,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-04 14-50-34',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image21.png'),
(6997,38,247,'paragraph',NULL,'The main postulates of VSEPR theory are as follows:','[{"s": "The main postulates of VSEPR theory are as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6998,38,248,'paragraph',NULL,'• The shape of a molecule depends upon the number of valence shell electron pairs (bonded or nonbonded) around the central atom.','[{"s": "• The shape of a molecule depends upon the number of valence shell electron pairs (bonded or nonbonded) around the central atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(6999,38,249,'paragraph',NULL,'• Pairs of electrons in the valence shell repel one another since their electron clouds are negatively charged.','[{"s": "• Pairs of electrons in the valence shell repel one another since their electron clouds are negatively charged.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7000,38,250,'paragraph',NULL,'• These pairs of electrons tend to occupy such positions in space that minimise repulsion and thus maximise distance between them.','[{"s": "• These pairs of electrons tend to occupy such positions in space that minimise repulsion and thus maximise distance between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7001,38,251,'paragraph',NULL,'• The valence shell is taken as a sphere with the electron pairs localising on the spherical surface at maximum distance from one another.','[{"s": "• The valence shell is taken as a sphere with the electron pairs localising on the spherical surface at maximum distance from one another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7002,38,252,'paragraph',NULL,'• A multiple bond is treated as if it is a single electron pair and the two or three electron pairs of a multiple bond are treated as a single super pair.','[{"s": "• A multiple bond is treated as if it is a single electron pair and the two or three electron pairs of a multiple bond are treated as a single super pair.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7003,38,253,'paragraph',NULL,'• Where two or more resonance structures can represent a molecule, the VSEPR model is applicable to any such structure.','[{"s": "• Where two or more resonance structures can represent a molecule, the VSEPR model is applicable to any such structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7004,38,254,'paragraph',NULL,'The repulsive interaction of electron pairs decrease in the order:','[{"s": "The repulsive interaction of electron pairs decrease in the order:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41907"]',NULL),
(7005,38,255,'paragraph',NULL,'Lone pair (lp) – Lone pair (lp) > Lone pair (lp) – Bond pair (bp) > Bond pair (bp) – Bond pair (bp)','[{"s": "Lone pair (lp) – Lone pair (lp) > Lone pair (lp) – Bond pair (bp) > Bond pair (bp) – Bond pair (bp)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7006,38,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Nyholm and Gillespie (1957) refined the VSEPR model by explaining the important difference between the lone pairs and bonding pairs of electrons. While the lone pairs are localised on the central atom, each bonded pair is shared between two atoms. As a result, the lone pair electrons in a molecule occupy more space as compared to the bonding pairs of electrons. This results in greater repulsion between lone pairs of electrons as compared to the lone pair - bond pair and bond pair - bond pair repulsions. These repulsion effects result in deviations from idealised shapes and alterations in bond angles in molecules.','[{"s": "Nyholm and Gillespie (1957) refined the VSEPR model by explaining the important difference between the lone pairs and bonding pairs of electrons. While the lone pairs are localised on the central atom, each bonded pair is shared between two atoms. As a result, the lone pair electrons in a molecule occupy more space as compared to the bonding pairs of electrons. This results in greater repulsion between lone pairs of electrons as compared to the lone pair - bond pair and bond pair - bond pair repulsions. These repulsion effects result in deviations from idealised shapes and alterations in bond angles in molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10541,43,527,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Arrhenius theory, OH – (aq). The ionization of an acid HX (aq) can be represented by the following equations:','[{"s": "According to Arrhenius theory,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq). The ionization of an acid HX (aq) can be represented by the following equations:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7007,38,257,'paragraph',NULL,'For the prediction of geometrical shapes of molecules with the help of VSEPR theory, it is convenient to divide molecules into two categories as (i) molecules in which the central atom has no lone pair and (ii) molecules in which the central atom has one or more lone pairs.','[{"s": "For the prediction of geometrical shapes of molecules with the help of VSEPR theory, it is convenient to divide molecules into two categories as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(i) molecules in which the central atom has no lone pair and (ii) molecules in which the central atom has one or more lone pairs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7008,38,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.6 (page114) shows the arrangement of electron pairs about a central atom A (without any lone pairs) and geometries of some molecules/ions of the type AB. Table 4.7 (page 115) shows shapes of some simple molecules and ions in which the central atom has one or more lone pairs. Table 4.8 (page 116) explains the reasons for the distortions in the geometry of the molecule.','[{"s": "Table 4.6 (page114) shows the arrangement of electron pairs about a central atom A (without any lone pairs) and geometries of some molecules/ions of the type AB. Table 4.7 (page 115) shows shapes of some simple molecules and ions in which the central atom has one or more lone pairs.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Table 4.8 (page 116) explains the reasons for the distortions in the geometry of the molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7009,38,259,'paragraph',NULL,'As depicted in Table 4.6, in the compounds of AB 2 , AB 3 , AB 4 , AB 5 and AB 6 , the arrangement of electron pairs and the B atoms around the central atom A are : linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal-bipyramidal and octahedral, respectively. Such arrangement can be seen in the molecules like BF 3 (AB 3 ), CH 4 (AB 4 ) and PCl 5 (AB 5 ) as depicted below by their ball and stick models.','[{"s": "As depicted in Table 4.6, in the compounds of AB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", AB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", AB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", AB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and AB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", the arrangement of electron pairs and the B atoms around the central atom A are :", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal-bipyramidal and octahedral,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "respectively.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Such arrangement can be seen in the molecules like BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") as depicted below by their ball and stick models.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41942"]',NULL),
(7010,38,260,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image48.png'),
(7011,38,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.6 The shapes of molecules in which central atom has no lone pair','[{"s": "Fig. 4.6 The shapes of molecules in which central atom has no lone pair", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7012,38,262,'paragraph',NULL,'The VSEPR Theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of p-block elements accurately. It is also quite successful in determining the geometry quite-accurately even when the energy difference between possible structures is very small. The theoretical basis of the VSEPR theory regarding the effects of electron pair repulsions on molecular shapes is not clear and continues to be a subject of doubt and discussion.','[{"s": "The VSEPR Theory is able to predict geometry of a large number of molecules, especially the compounds of p-block elements accurately.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is also quite successful in determining the geometry quite-accurately even when the energy difference between possible structures is very small. The theoretical basis of the VSEPR theory regarding the effects of electron pair repulsions on molecular shapes is not clear and continues to be a subject of doubt and discussion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7013,38,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.6 Geometry of Molecules in which the Central Atom has No Lone Pair of Electrons 7','[{"s": "Table 4.6 Geometry of Molecules in which the Central Atom has No Lone Pair of Electrons", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41903", "41911", "41917", "41944", "41957", "41958", "41959"]',NULL),
(7014,38,264,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image6.png'),
(7015,38,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.7 Shape (geometry) of Some Simple Molecules/Ions with Central Ions having One or More Lone Pairs of Electrons(E).','[{"s": "Table 4.7 Shape (geometry) of Some Simple Molecules/Ions with Central Ions having One or More Lone Pairs of Electrons(E).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41943"]',NULL),
(7016,38,266,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image24.png'),
(7017,38,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 4.8 Shapes of Molecules containing Bond Pair and Lone Pair 2','[{"s": "Table 4.8 Shapes of Molecules containing Bond Pair and Lone Pair", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41904", "41945"]',NULL),
(7018,38,268,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-04 15-17-11',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image34.png'),
(7019,38,269,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-04 15-17-55',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image37.png'),
(7020,38,270,'heading',3,'4.5 Valence Bond Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7021,38,271,'paragraph',NULL,'As we know that Lewis approach helps in writing the structure of molecules but it fails to explain the formation of chemical bond. It also does not give any reason for the difference in bond dissociation enthalpies and bond lengths in molecules like H 2 (435.8 kJ mol -1 , 74 pm) and F 2 (155 kJ mol -1 , 144 pm), although in both the cases a single covalent bond is formed by the sharing of an electron pair between the respective atoms. It also gives no idea about the shapes of polyatomic molecules.','[{"s": "As we know that Lewis approach helps in writing the structure of molecules but it fails to explain the formation of chemical bond. It also does not give any reason for the difference in bond dissociation enthalpies and bond lengths in molecules like H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(435.8 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 74 pm) and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(155 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 144 pm), although in both the cases a single covalent bond is formed by the sharing of an electron pair between the respective atoms. It also gives no idea about the shapes of polyatomic molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10542,43,528,'paragraph',NULL,'HX (aq) → H + (aq) + X – (aq)','[{"s": "HX (aq) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7022,38,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly the VSEPR theory gives the geometry of simple molecules but theoretically, it does not explain them and also it has limited applications. To overcome these limitations the two important theories based on quantum mechanical principles are introduced. These are valence bond (VB) theory and molecular orbital (MO) theory.','[{"s": "Similarly the VSEPR theory gives the geometry of simple molecules but theoretically, it does not explain them and also it has limited applications. To overcome these limitations the two important theories based on quantum mechanical principles are introduced. These are valence bond (VB) theory and molecular orbital (MO) theory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7023,38,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Valence bond theory was introduced by Heitler and London (1927) and developed further by Pauling and others. A discussion of the valence bond theory is based on the knowledge of atomic orbitals, electronic configurations of elements (Units 2), the overlap criteria of atomic orbitals, the hybridization of atomic orbitals and the principles of variation and superposition. A rigorous treatment of the VB theory in terms of these aspects is beyond the scope of this book. Therefore, for the sake of convenience, valence bond theory has been discussed in terms of qualitative and non-mathematical treatment only. To start with, let us consider the formation of hydrogen molecule which is the simplest of all molecules.','[{"s": "Valence bond theory", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was introduced by Heitler and London (1927) and developed further by Pauling and others. A discussion of the valence bond theory is based on the knowledge of atomic orbitals, electronic configurations of elements (Units 2), the overlap criteria of atomic orbitals, the hybridization of atomic orbitals and the principles of variation and superposition. A rigorous treatment of the VB theory in terms of these aspects is beyond the scope of this book. Therefore, for the sake of convenience, valence bond theory has been discussed in terms of qualitative and non-mathematical treatment only. To start with, let us consider the formation of hydrogen molecule which is the simplest of all molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7024,38,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider two hydrogen atoms A and B approaching each other having nuclei N A and N B and electrons present in them are represented by e A and e B . When the two atoms are at large distance from each other, there is no interaction between them. As these two atoms approach each other, new attractive and repulsive forces begin to operate.','[{"s": "Consider two hydrogen atoms A and B approaching each other having nuclei N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and electrons present in them are represented by e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When the two atoms are at large distance from each other, there is no interaction between them. As these two atoms approach each other, new attractive and repulsive forces begin to operate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7025,38,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Attractive forces arise between:','[{"s": "Attractive forces arise between:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7026,38,276,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) nucleus of one atom and its own electron that is N A – e A and N B – e B .','[{"s": "(i) nucleus of one atom and its own electron that is N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7027,38,277,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) nucleus of one atom and electron of other atom i.e., N A – e B , N B – e A .','[{"s": "(ii) nucleus of one atom and electron of other atom i.e., N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7028,38,278,'paragraph',NULL,'A – e B A – N B .','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7029,38,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Attractive forces tend to bring the two atoms close to each other whereas repulsive forces tend to push them apart (Fig. 4.7).','[{"s": "Attractive forces tend to bring the two atoms close to each other whereas repulsive forces tend to push them apart (Fig. 4.7).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7030,38,280,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image41.png'),
(7031,38,281,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.7 Forces of attraction and repulsion during the formation of H 2 molecule.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.7 Forces of attraction and repulsion during the formation of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7032,38,282,'paragraph',NULL,'Experimentally it has been found that the magnitude of new attractive force is more than the new repulsive forces. As a result, two atoms approach each other and potential energy decreases. Ultimately a stage is reached where the net force of attraction balances the force of repulsion and system acquires minimum energy. At this stage two hydrogen atoms are said to be bonded together to form a stable molecule having the bond length of 74 pm.','[{"s": "Experimentally it has been found that the magnitude of new attractive force is more than the new repulsive forces. As a result, two atoms approach each other and potential energy decreases. Ultimately a stage is reached where the net force of attraction balances the force of repulsion and system acquires minimum energy. At this stage two hydrogen atoms are said to be bonded together to form a stable molecule having the bond length of 74 pm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7033,38,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the energy gets released when the bond is formed between two hydrogen atoms, the hydrogen molecule is more stable than that of isolated hydrogen atoms. The energy so released is called as bond enthalpy , which is corresponding to minimum in the curve depicted in Fig. 4.8. Conversely, 435.8 kJ of energy is required to dissociate one mole of H 2 molecule.','[{"s": "Since the energy gets released when the bond is formed between two hydrogen atoms, the hydrogen molecule is more stable than that of isolated hydrogen atoms. The energy so released is called as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond enthalpy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is corresponding to minimum in the curve depicted in Fig. 4.8. Conversely, 435.8 kJ of energy is required to dissociate one mole of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7034,38,284,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 (g) + 435.8 kJ mol –1 → H(g) + H(g)','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 435.8 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ H(g) + H(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7035,38,285,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image80.png'),
(7036,38,286,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.8 The potential energy curve for the formation of H 2 molecule as a function of internuclear distance of the H atoms. The minimum in the curve corresponds to the most stable state of H 2 .','[{"s": "Fig. 4.8 The potential energy curve for the formation of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule as a function of internuclear distance of the H atoms. The minimum in the curve corresponds to the most stable state of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7037,38,287,'heading',4,'4.5.1 Orbital Overlap Concept',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7038,38,288,'paragraph',NULL,'In the formation of hydrogen molecule, there is a minimum energy state when two hydrogen atoms are so near that their atomic orbitals undergo partial interpenetration. This partial merging of atomic orbitals is called overlapping of atomic orbitals which results in the pairing of electrons. The extent of overlap decides the strength of a covalent bond. In general, greater the overlap the stronger is the bond formed between two atoms. Therefore, according to orbital overlap concept, the formation of a covalent bond between two atoms results by pairing of electrons present in the valence shell having opposite spins.','[{"s": "In the formation of hydrogen molecule, there is a minimum energy state when two hydrogen atoms are so near that their atomic orbitals undergo partial interpenetration. This partial merging of atomic orbitals is called overlapping of atomic orbitals which results in the pairing of electrons. The extent of overlap decides the strength of a covalent bond. In general, greater the overlap the stronger is the bond formed between two atoms. Therefore, according to orbital overlap concept, the formation of a covalent bond between two atoms results by pairing of electrons present in the valence shell having opposite spins.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7039,38,289,'heading',4,'4.5.2 Directional Properties of Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7040,38,290,'paragraph',NULL,'As we have already seen, the covalent bond is formed by overlapping of atomic orbitals. The molecule of hydrogen is formed due to the overlap of 1s-orbitals of two H atoms.','[{"s": "As we have already seen, the covalent bond is formed by overlapping of atomic orbitals. The molecule of hydrogen is formed due to the overlap of 1s-orbitals of two H atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7041,38,291,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of polyatomic molecules like CH 4 , NH 3 and H 2 O, the geometry of the molecules is also important in addition to the bond formation. For example why is it so that CH 4 molecule has tetrahedral shape and HCH bond angles are 109.5°? Why is the shape of NH 3 molecule pyramidal ?','[{"s": "In case of polyatomic molecules like CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, the geometry of the molecules is also important in addition to the bond formation. For example why is it so that CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule has tetrahedral shape and HCH bond angles are 109.5°? Why is the shape of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule pyramidal ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7042,38,292,'paragraph',NULL,'The valence bond theory explains the shape, the formation and directional properties of bonds in polyatomic molecules like CH 4 , NH 3 and H 2 O, etc. in terms of overlap and hybridisation of atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "The valence bond theory explains the shape, the formation and directional properties of bonds in polyatomic molecules like CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, etc. in terms of overlap and hybridisation of atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7043,38,293,'heading',4,'4.5.3 Overlapping of Atomic Orbitals',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7044,38,294,'paragraph',NULL,'When orbitals of two atoms come close to form bond, their overlap may be positive, negative or zero depending upon the sign (phase) and direction of orientation of amplitude of orbital wave function in space (Fig. 4.9). Positive and negative sign on boundary surface diagrams in the Fig. 4.9 show the sign (phase) of orbital wave function and are not related to charge. Orbitals forming bond should have same sign (phase) and orientation in space. This is called positive overlap. Various overlaps of s and p orbitals are depicted in Fig. 4.9.','[{"s": "When orbitals of two atoms come close to form bond, their overlap may be positive, negative or zero depending upon the sign (phase) and direction of orientation of amplitude of orbital wave function in space (Fig. 4.9). Positive and negative sign on boundary surface diagrams in the Fig. 4.9 show the sign (phase) of orbital wave function and are not related to charge. Orbitals forming bond should have same sign (phase) and orientation in space. This is called positive overlap. Various overlaps of s and p orbitals are depicted in Fig. 4.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7045,38,295,'paragraph',NULL,'The criterion of overlap, as the main factor for the formation of covalent bonds applies uniformly to the homonuclear/heteronuclear diatomic molecules and polyatomic molecules. We know that the shapes of CH 4 , NH 3 , and H 2 O molecules are tetrahedral, pyramidal and bent respectively. It would be therefore interesting to use VB theory to find out if these geometrical shapes can be explained in terms of the orbital overlaps.','[{"s": "The criterion of overlap, as the main factor for the formation of covalent bonds applies uniformly to the homonuclear/heteronuclear diatomic molecules and polyatomic molecules. We know that the shapes of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecules are tetrahedral, pyramidal and bent respectively. It would be therefore interesting to use VB theory to find out if these geometrical shapes can be explained in terms of the orbital overlaps.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7046,38,296,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us first consider the CH 4 (methane) molecule. The electronic configuration of carbon in its ground state is [He]2s 2 2p 2 which in the excited state becomes [He] 2s 1 2p x 1 2p y 1 2p z 1 . The energy required for this excitation is compensated by the release of energy due to overlap between the orbitals of carbon and the hydrogen.The four atomic orbitals of carbon, each with an unpaired electron can overlap with the 1s orbitals of the four H atoms which are also singly occupied. This will result in the formation of four C-H bonds. It will, however, be observed that while the three p orbitals of carbon are at 90 ° to one another, the HCH angle for these will also be 90 ° . That is three C-H bonds will be oriented at 90 ° to one another. The 2s orbital of carbon and the 1s orbital of H are spherically symmetrical and they can overlap in any direction. Therefore the direction of the fourth C-H bond cannot be ascertained. This description does not fit in with the tetrahedral HCH angles of 109.5 ° . Clearly, it follows that simple atomic orbital overlap does not account for the directional characteristics of bonds in CH 4 . Using similar procedure and arguments, it can be seen that in the case of NH 3 and H 2 O molecules, the HNH and HOH angles should be 90 ° . This is in disagreement with the actual bond angles of 107 ° and 104.5 ° in the NH 3 and H 2 O molecules respectively.','[{"s": "Let us first consider the CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(methane) molecule. The electronic configuration of carbon in its ground state is [He]2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which in the excited state becomes [He] 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The energy required for this excitation is compensated by the release of energy due to overlap between the orbitals of carbon and the hydrogen.The four atomic orbitals of carbon, each with an unpaired electron can overlap with the 1s orbitals of the four H atoms which are also singly occupied. This will result in the formation of four C-H bonds. It will, however, be observed that while the three p orbitals of carbon are at 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to one another, the HCH angle for these will also be 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". That is three C-H bonds will be oriented at 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to one another. The 2s orbital of carbon and the 1s orbital of H are spherically symmetrical and they can overlap in any direction. Therefore the direction of the fourth C-H bond cannot be ascertained. This description does not fit in with the tetrahedral HCH angles of 109.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Clearly, it follows that simple atomic orbital overlap does not account for the directional characteristics of bonds in CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Using similar procedure and arguments, it can be seen that in the case of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecules, the HNH and HOH angles should be 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is in disagreement with the actual bond angles of 107", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "°", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and 104.5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "°", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "in the NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O molecules respectively.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41904"]',NULL),
(7047,38,297,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image7.png'),
(7048,38,298,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.9 Positive, negative and zero overlaps of s and p atomic orbitals','[{"s": "Fig.4.9 Positive, negative and zero overlaps of s and p atomic orbitals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7049,38,299,'heading',4,'4.5.4 Types of Overlapping and Nature of Covalent Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7050,38,300,'paragraph',NULL,'The covalent bond may be classified into two types depending upon the types of overlapping:','[{"s": "The covalent bond may be classified into two types depending upon the types of overlapping:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41909"]',NULL),
(7051,38,301,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Sigma(σ) bond, and (ii) pi(π) bond','[{"s": "(i) Sigma(σ) bond, and (ii) pi(π) bond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7052,38,302,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Sigma( σ ) bond : This type of covalent bond is formed by the end to end (head-on) overlap of bonding orbitals along the internuclear axis . This is called as head on overlap or axial overlap. This can be formed by any one of the following types of combinations of atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "(i) Sigma(", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "σ", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") bond :", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This type of covalent bond is formed by the end to end (head-on) overlap of bonding orbitals along the internuclear axis", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is called as head on overlap or axial overlap. This can be formed by any one of the following types of combinations of atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41960"]',NULL),
(7053,38,303,'paragraph',NULL,'• s-s overlapping : In this case, there is overlap of two half filled s-orbitals along the internuclear axis as shown below :','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s-s overlapping :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In this case, there is overlap of two half filled s-orbitals along the internuclear axis as shown below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7054,38,304,'paragraph',NULL,'• s-p overlapping: This type of overlap occurs between half filled s-orbitals of one atom and half filled p-orbitals of another atom.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s-p overlapping:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This type of overlap occurs between half filled s-orbitals of one atom and half filled p-orbitals of another atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7055,38,305,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image17.png'),
(7056,38,306,'paragraph',NULL,'• p–p overlapping : This type of overlap takes place between half filled p-orbitals of the two approaching atoms.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p–p overlapping :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This type of overlap takes place between half filled p-orbitals of the two approaching atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7057,38,307,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image63.png'),
(7058,38,308,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) pi( π ) bond : In the formation of π bond the atomic orbitals overlap in such a way that their axes remain parallel to each other and perpendicular to the internuclear axis. The orbitals formed due to sidewise overlapping consists of two saucer type charged clouds above and below the plane of the participating atoms.','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pi(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") bond :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the formation of π bond the atomic orbitals overlap in such a way that their axes remain parallel to each other and perpendicular to the internuclear axis. The orbitals formed due to sidewise overlapping consists of two saucer type charged clouds above and below the plane of the participating atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7059,38,309,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image62.png'),
(7060,38,310,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image18.png'),
(7061,38,311,'heading',4,'4.5.5 Strength of Sigma and pi Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7062,38,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Basically the strength of a bond depends upon the extent of overlapping. In case of sigma bond, the overlapping of orbitals takes place to a larger extent. Hence, it is stronger as compared to the pi bond where the extent of overlapping occurs to a smaller extent. Further, it is important to note that in the formation of multiple bonds between two atoms of a molecule, pi bond(s) is formed in addition to a sigma bond.','[{"s": "Basically the strength of a bond depends upon the extent of overlapping. In case of sigma bond, the overlapping of orbitals takes place to a larger extent. Hence, it is stronger as compared to the pi bond where the extent of overlapping occurs to a smaller extent. Further, it is important to note that in the formation of multiple bonds between two atoms of a molecule, pi bond(s) is formed in addition to a sigma bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7063,38,313,'heading',3,'4.6 Hybridisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7064,38,314,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to explain the characteristic geometrical shapes of polyatomic molecules like CH 4 , NH 3 and H 2 O etc., Pauling introduced the concept of hybridisation. According to him the atomic orbitals combine to form new set of equivalent orbitals known as hybrid orbitals . Unlike pure orbitals, the hybrid orbitals are used in bond formation. The phenomenon is known as hybridisation which can be defined as the process of intermixing of the orbitals of slightly different energies so as to redistribute their energies, resulting in the formation of new set of orbitals of equivalent energies and shape. For example when one 2s and three 2p-orbitals of carbon hybridise, there is the formation of four new sp 3 hybrid orbitals.','[{"s": "In order to explain the characteristic geometrical shapes of polyatomic molecules like CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O etc., Pauling introduced the concept of hybridisation. According to him the atomic orbitals combine to form new set of equivalent orbitals known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Unlike pure orbitals, the hybrid orbitals are used in bond formation. The phenomenon is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which can be defined as the process of intermixing of the orbitals of slightly different energies so as to redistribute their energies, resulting in the formation of new set of orbitals of equivalent energies and shape. For example when one 2s and three 2p-orbitals of carbon hybridise, there is the formation of four new sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7065,38,315,'paragraph',NULL,'Salient features of hybridisation: The main features of hybridisation are as under :','[{"s": "Salient features of hybridisation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The main features of hybridisation are as under :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10543,43,529,'paragraph',NULL,'or','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12178,44,948,'paragraph',NULL,'Q.12 The position of Br in the compound in','[{"s": "Q.12 The position of Br in the compound in", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7066,38,316,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The number of hybrid orbitals is equal to the number of the atomic orbitals that get hybridised. 2. The hybridised orbitals are always equivalent in energy and shape. 3. The hybrid orbitals are more effective in forming stable bonds than the pure atomic orbitals. 4. These hybrid orbitals are directed in space in some preferred direction to have minimum repulsion between electron pairs and thus a stable arrangement. Therefore, the type of hybridisation indicates the geometry of the molecules.','[{"s": "1. The number of hybrid orbitals is equal to the number of the atomic orbitals that get hybridised.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2. The hybridised orbitals are always equivalent in energy and shape.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3. The hybrid orbitals are more effective in forming stable bonds than the pure atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4. These hybrid orbitals are directed in space in some preferred direction to have minimum repulsion between electron pairs and thus a stable arrangement.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Therefore, the type of hybridisation indicates the geometry of the molecules.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41944"]',NULL),
(7067,38,317,'paragraph',NULL,'Important conditions for hybridisation','[{"s": "Important conditions for hybridisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7068,38,318,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The orbitals present in the valence shell of the atom are hybridised. (ii) The orbitals undergoing hybridisation should have almost equal energy. (iii) Promotion of electron is not essential condition prior to hybridisation. (iv) It is not necessary that only half filled orbitals participate in hybridisation. In some cases, even filled orbitals of valence shell take part in hybridisation.','[{"s": "(i) The orbitals present in the valence shell of the atom are hybridised.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ii) The orbitals undergoing hybridisation should have almost equal energy.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(iii) Promotion of electron is not essential condition prior to hybridisation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(iv) It is not necessary that only half filled orbitals participate in hybridisation. In some cases, even filled orbitals of valence shell take part in hybridisation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7069,38,319,'heading',4,'4.6.1 Types of Hybridisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7070,38,320,'paragraph',NULL,'There are various types of hybridisation involving s, p and d orbitals. The different types of hybridisation are as under:','[{"s": "There are various types of hybridisation involving s, p and d orbitals. The different types of hybridisation are as under:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7071,38,321,'paragraph',NULL,'(I) sp hybridisation: This type of hybridisation involves the mixing of one s and one p orbital resulting in the formation of two equivalent sp hybrid orbitals . The suitable orbitals for sp hybridisation are s and p z , if the hybrid orbitals are to lie along the z-axis. Each sp hybrid orbitals has 50% s-character and 50% p-character. Such a molecule in which the central atom is sp-hybridised and linked directly to two other central atoms possesses linear geometry. This type of hybridisation is also known as diagonal hybridisation.','[{"s": "(I)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sp hybridisation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This type of hybridisation involves the mixing of one s and one p orbital resulting in the formation of two equivalent sp hybrid orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The suitable orbitals for sp hybridisation are s and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", if the hybrid orbitals are to lie along the z-axis. Each sp hybrid orbitals has 50% s-character and 50% p-character. Such a molecule in which the central atom is sp-hybridised and linked directly to two other central atoms possesses linear geometry. This type of hybridisation is also known as diagonal hybridisation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7072,38,322,'paragraph',NULL,'The two sp hybrids point in the opposite direction along the z-axis with projecting positive lobes and very small negative lobes, which provides more effective overlapping resulting in the formation of stronger bonds.','[{"s": "The two sp hybrids point in the opposite direction along the z-axis with projecting positive lobes and very small negative lobes, which provides more effective overlapping resulting in the formation of stronger bonds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7073,38,323,'paragraph',NULL,'Example of molecule having sp hybridisation','[{"s": "Example of molecule having sp hybridisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7074,38,324,'paragraph',NULL,'BeCl 2 : The ground state electronic configuration of Be is 1s 2 2s 2 . In the exited state one of the 2s-electrons is promoted to vacant 2p orbital to account for its bivalency. One 2s and one 2p-orbital gets hybridised to form two sp hybridised orbitals. These two sp hybrid orbitals are oriented in opposite direction forming an angle of 180°.','[{"s": "BeCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ground state electronic configuration of Be is 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the exited state one of the 2s-electrons is promoted to vacant 2p orbital to account for its bivalency. One 2s and one 2p-orbital gets hybridised to form two sp hybridised orbitals.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These two sp hybrid orbitals are oriented in opposite direction forming an angle of 180°.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["130844"]',NULL),
(7075,38,325,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image35.png'),
(7076,38,326,'paragraph',NULL,'(II) sp 2 hybridisation : p-orbitals in order to form three equivalent sp 2 hybridised orbitals. 2 For example, in BCl 3 molecule, the ground state electronic configuration of central boron atom is 1s 2 2s 2 2p 1 . In the excited state, one of the 2s electrons is promoted to vacant 2p orbital as a result boron has three unpaired electrons. These three orbitals (one 2s and two 2p) hybridise to form three sp 2 hybrid orbitals. The three hybrid orbitals so formed are oriented in a trigonal planar arrangement and overlap with 2p orbitals of chlorine to form three B-Cl bonds. Therefore, in BCl 3 (Fig . 4.11), the geometry is trigonal planar with ClBCl bond angle of 120°.','[{"s": "(II) sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "p-orbitals in order to form three equivalent sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybridised orbitals.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, in BCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule, the ground state electronic configuration of central boron atom is 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the excited state, one of the 2s electrons is promoted to vacant 2p orbital as a result boron has three unpaired electrons. These three orbitals (one 2s and two 2p) hybridise to form three sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals. The three hybrid orbitals so formed are oriented in a trigonal planar arrangement and overlap with 2p orbitals of chlorine to form three B-Cl bonds. Therefore, in BCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4.11), the geometry is trigonal planar with ClBCl bond angle of 120°.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41903", "41946"]',NULL),
(7077,38,327,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image46.png'),
(7078,38,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.11 Formation of sp2 hybrids and the BCl 3 molecule','[{"s": "Fig.4.11 Formation of sp2 hybrids and the BCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7079,38,329,'paragraph',NULL,'(III) sp 3 hybridisation: This type of hybridisation can be explained by taking the example of CH 4 molecule in which there is mixing of one s-orbital and three p-orbitals of the valence shell to form four sp 3 hybrid orbital of equivalent energies and shape. There is 25% s-character and 75% p-character in each sp 3 hybrid orbital. The four sp 3 hybrid orbitals so formed are directed towards the four corners of the tetrahedron. The angle between sp 3 hybrid orbital is 109.5° as shown in Fig. 4.12.','[{"s": "(III) sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This type of hybridisation can be explained by taking the example of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule in which there is mixing of one s-orbital and three p-orbitals of the valence shell to form four sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbital of equivalent energies and shape. There is 25% s-character and 75% p-character in each sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbital. The four sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals so formed are directed towards the four corners of the tetrahedron. The angle between sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbital is 109.5° as shown in Fig. 4.12.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7080,38,330,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image70.png'),
(7081,38,331,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.12 Formation of sp 3 hybrids by the combination of s , px , py and pz atomic orbitals of carbon and the formation of CH4 molecule','[{"s": "Fig.4.12 Formation of sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrids by the combination of s , px , py and pz atomic orbitals of carbon and the formation of CH4 molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7082,38,332,'paragraph',NULL,'The structure of NH 3 and H 2 O molecules can also be explained with the help of sp 3 hybridisation. In NH 3 , the valence shell (outer) electronic configuration of nitrogen in the ground state is Chemical_bonding-image44.png having three unpaired electrons in the sp 3 hybrid orbitals and a lone pair of electrons is present in the fourth one. These three hybrid orbitals overlap with 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms to form three N–H sigma bonds. We know that the force of repulsion between a lone pair and a bond pair is more than the force of repulsion between two bond pairs of electrons. The molecule thus gets distorted and the bond angle is reduced to 107° from 109.5°. The geometry of such a molecule will be pyramidal as shown in Fig. 4.13.','[{"s": "The structure of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecules can also be explained with the help of sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation. In NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the valence shell (outer) electronic configuration of nitrogen in the ground state is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical_bonding-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "having three unpaired electrons in the sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals and a lone pair of electrons is present in the fourth one. These three hybrid orbitals overlap with 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms to form three N–H sigma bonds. We know that the force of repulsion between a lone pair and a bond pair is more than the force of repulsion between two bond pairs of electrons. The molecule thus gets distorted and the bond angle is reduced to 107° from 109.5°. The geometry of such a molecule will be pyramidal as shown in Fig. 4.13.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7083,38,333,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image81.png'),
(7084,38,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.13 Formation of NH 3 molecule','[{"s": "Fig.4.13 Formation of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7085,38,335,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of H 2 O molecule, the four oxygen orbitals (one 2s and three 2p) undergo sp 3 hybridisation forming four sp 3 hybrid orbitals out of which two contain one electron each and the other two contain a pair of electrons. These four sp 3 hybrid orbitals acquire a tetrahedral geometry, with two corners occupied by hydrogen atoms while the other two by the lone pairs. The bond angle in this case is reduced to 104.5° from 109.5° (Fig. 4.14) and the molecule thus acquires a V-shape or angular geometry.','[{"s": "In case of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecule, the four oxygen orbitals (one 2s and three 2p) undergo sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation forming four sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals out of which two contain one electron each and the other two contain a pair of electrons. These four sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals acquire a tetrahedral geometry, with two corners occupied by hydrogen atoms while the other two by the lone pairs.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The bond angle in this case is reduced to 104.5° from 109.5° (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4.14) and the molecule thus acquires a V-shape or angular geometry.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41916"]',NULL),
(7086,38,336,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image27.png'),
(7087,38,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.14 Formation of H2O molecule','[{"s": "Fig.4.14 Formation of H2O molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7088,38,338,'heading',4,'4.6.2 Other Examples of sp3, sp2 and sp Hybridisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7089,38,339,'paragraph',NULL,'sp 3 Hybridisation in C2H6 molecule: In ethane molecule both the carbon atoms assume sp 3 hybrid state . One of the four sp 3 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with similar orbitals of other atom to form sp 3 -sp 3 sigma bond while the other three hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used in forming sp 3 –s sigma bonds with hydrogen atoms as discussed in section 4.6.1(iii). Therefore in ethane C–C bond length is 154 pm and each C–H bond length is 109 pm.','[{"s": "sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hybridisation in C2H6 molecule: In ethane molecule both the carbon atoms assume sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybrid state", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "One of the four sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with similar orbitals of other atom to form sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sigma bond while the other three hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used in forming sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–s sigma bonds with hydrogen atoms as discussed in section 4.6.1(iii). Therefore in ethane C–C bond length is 154 pm and each C–H bond length is 109 pm.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41947"]',NULL),
(12179,44,949,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12180,44,950,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12181,44,951,'paragraph',NULL,'3','[{"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7090,38,340,'paragraph',NULL,'sp 2 Hybridisation in C 2 H 4 : In the formation of ethene molecule, one of the sp 2 hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with sp 2 hybridised orbital of another carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond . While the other two sp 2 hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used for making sp 2 –s sigma bond with two hydrogen atoms. The unhybridised orbital (2px or 2py) of one carbon atom overlaps sidewise with the similar orbital of the other carbon atom to form weak π bond, which consists of two equal electron clouds distributed above and below the plane of carbon and hydrogen atoms.','[{"s": "sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hybridisation in C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ":", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In the formation of ethene molecule, one of the sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals of carbon atom overlaps axially with sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybridised orbital of another carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "While the other two sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom are used for making sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–s sigma bond with two hydrogen atoms. The unhybridised orbital (2px or 2py) of one carbon atom overlaps sidewise with the similar orbital of the other carbon atom to form weak π bond, which consists of two equal electron clouds distributed above and below the plane of carbon and hydrogen atoms.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41917"]',NULL),
(7091,38,341,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image53.png'),
(7092,38,342,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.15 Formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethene','[{"s": "Fig. 4.15 Formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7093,38,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in ethene molecule, the carbon-carbon bond consists of one sp 2 –sp 2 sigma bond and one pi (π ) bond between p orbitals which are not used in the hybridisation and are perpendicular to the plane of molecule; the bond length 134 pm. The C–H bond is sp 2 –s sigma with bond length 108 pm. The H–C–H bond angle is 117.6° while the H–C–C angle is 121°. The formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethene is shown in Fig. 4.15.','[{"s": "Thus, in ethene molecule, the carbon-carbon bond consists of one sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sigma bond and one pi (π ) bond between p orbitals which are not used in the hybridisation and are perpendicular to the plane of molecule; the bond length 134 pm. The C–H bond is sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–s sigma with bond length 108 pm. The H–C–H bond angle is 117.6° while the H–C–C angle is 121°. The formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethene is shown in Fig. 4.15.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7094,38,344,'paragraph',NULL,'sp Hybridisation in C 2 H 2 : In the formation of ethyne molecule, both the carbon atoms undergo sp-hybridisation having two unhybridised orbital i.e., 2p y and 2p x .','[{"s": "sp Hybridisation in C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the formation of ethyne molecule, both the carbon atoms undergo sp-hybridisation having two unhybridised orbital i.e., 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7095,38,345,'paragraph',NULL,'One sp hybrid orbital of one carbon atom overlaps axially with sp hybrid orbital of the other carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond, while the other hybridised orbital of each carbon atom overlaps axially with the half filled s orbital of hydrogen atoms forming σ bonds. Each of the two unhybridised p orbitals of both the carbon atoms overlaps sidewise to form two π bonds between the carbon atoms. So the triple bond between the two carbon atoms is made up of one sigma and two pi bonds as shown in Fig. 4.16.','[{"s": "One sp hybrid orbital of one carbon atom overlaps axially with sp hybrid orbital of the other carbon atom to form C–C sigma bond, while the other hybridised orbital of each carbon atom overlaps axially with the half filled s orbital of hydrogen atoms forming σ bonds.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Each of the two unhybridised p orbitals of both the carbon atoms overlaps sidewise to form two π bonds between the carbon atoms. So the triple bond between the two carbon atoms is made up of one sigma and two pi bonds as shown in Fig. 4.16.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41962"]',NULL),
(7096,38,346,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-01-37',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image60.png'),
(7097,38,347,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.16 Formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethyne','[{"s": "Fig.4.16 Formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethyne", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7098,38,348,'paragraph',NULL,'The elements present in the third period contain d orbitals in addition to s and p orbitals. The energy of the 3d orbitals are comparable to the energy of the 3s and 3p orbitals. The energy of 3d orbitals are also comparable to those of 4s and 4p orbitals. As a consequence the hybridisation involving either 3s, 3p and 3d or 3d, 4s and 4p is possible. However, since the difference in energies of 3p and 4s orbitals is significant, no hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s orbitals is possible.','[{"s": "The elements present in the third period contain d orbitals in addition to s and p orbitals. The energy of the 3d orbitals are comparable to the energy of the 3s and 3p orbitals. The energy of 3d orbitals are also comparable to those of 4s and 4p orbitals. As a consequence the hybridisation involving either 3s, 3p and 3d or 3d, 4s and 4p is possible. However, since the difference in energies of 3p and 4s orbitals is significant, no hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s orbitals is possible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7099,38,349,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-02-43',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image61.png'),
(7100,38,350,'paragraph',NULL,'The important hybridisation schemes involving s, p and d orbitals are summarised below:','[{"s": "The important hybridisation schemes involving s, p and d orbitals are summarised below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7101,38,351,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Formation of PCl 5 (sp 3 d hybridisation): The ground state and the excited state outer electronic configurations of phosphorus (Z=15) are represented below.','[{"s": "(i) Formation of PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d hybridisation):", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ground state and the excited state outer electronic configurations of phosphorus (Z=15) are represented below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7102,38,352,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image51.png'),
(12182,44,952,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12183,44,953,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7103,38,353,'paragraph',NULL,'sp 3 d hybrid orbitals filled by electron pairs donated by five Cl atoms.','[{"s": "sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d hybrid orbitals filled by electron pairs donated by five Cl atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7104,38,354,'paragraph',NULL,'Now the five orbitals (i.e., one s, three p and one d orbitals) are available for hybridisation to yield a set of five sp 3 d hybrid orbitals which are directed towards the five corners of a trigonal bipyramidal as depicted in the Fig. 4.17.','[{"s": "Now the five orbitals (i.e., one s, three p and one d orbitals) are available for hybridisation to yield a set of five sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d hybrid orbitals which are directed towards the five corners of a trigonal bipyramidal as depicted in the Fig. 4.17.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["42113"]',NULL),
(7105,38,355,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image39.png'),
(7106,38,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.17 Trigonal bipyramidal geometry of PCl 5 molecule','[{"s": "Fig. 4.17 Trigonal bipyramidal geometry of PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7107,38,357,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be noted that all the bond angles in trigonal bipyramidal geometry are not equivalent. In PCl 5 the five sp 3 d orbitals of phosphorus overlap with the singly occupied p orbitals of chlorine atoms to form five P–Cl sigma bonds. Three P–Cl bond lie in one plane and make an angle of 120° with each other; these bonds are termed as equatorial bonds. The remaining two P–Cl bonds–one lying above and the other lying below the equatorial plane, make an angle of 90° with the plane. These bonds are called axial bonds. As the axial bond pairs suffer more repulsive interaction from the equatorial bond pairs, therefore axial bonds have been found to be slightly longer and hence slightly weaker than the equatorial bonds; which makes PCl 5 molecule more reactive.','[{"s": "It should be noted that all the bond angles in trigonal bipyramidal geometry are not equivalent. In PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the five sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d orbitals of phosphorus overlap with the singly occupied p orbitals of chlorine atoms to form five P–Cl sigma bonds. Three P–Cl bond lie in one plane and make an angle of 120° with each other; these bonds are termed as equatorial bonds. The remaining two P–Cl bonds–one lying above and the other lying below the equatorial plane, make an angle of 90° with the plane. These bonds are called axial bonds. As the axial bond pairs suffer more repulsive interaction from the equatorial bond pairs, therefore axial bonds have been found to be slightly longer and hence slightly weaker than the equatorial bonds; which makes PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule more reactive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7108,38,358,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Formation of SF 6 (sp 3 d 2 hybridisation): In SF 6 the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s 2 3p 4 . In the exited state the available six orbitals i.e., one s, three p and two d are singly occupied by electrons. These orbitals hybridise to form six new sp 3 d 2 hybrid orbitals, which are projected towards the six corners of a regular octahedron in SF 6 . These six sp 3 d 2 hybrid orbitals overlap with singly occupied orbitals of fluorine atoms to form six S–F sigma bonds. Thus SF 6 molecule has a regular octahedral geometry as shown in Fig. 4.18.','[{"s": "(ii) Formation of SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation):", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the central sulphur atom has the ground state outer electronic configuration 3s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the exited state the available six orbitals i.e., one s, three p and two d are singly occupied by electrons. These orbitals hybridise to form six new sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals, which are projected towards the six corners of a regular octahedron in SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These six sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybrid orbitals overlap with singly occupied orbitals of fluorine atoms to form six S–F sigma bonds. Thus SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule has a regular octahedral geometry as shown in Fig. 4.18.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7109,38,359,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image8.png'),
(7110,38,360,'paragraph',NULL,'sp 3 d 2 hybridisation','[{"s": "sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7111,38,361,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image59.png'),
(7112,38,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.18 Octahedral geometry of SF 6 molecule','[{"s": "Fig. 4.18 Octahedral geometry of SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7113,38,363,'heading',3,'4.7 Molecular Orbital Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7114,38,364,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular orbital (MO) theory was developed by F. Hund and R.S. Mulliken in 1932. The salient features of this theory are :','[{"s": "Molecular orbital (MO) theory was developed by F. Hund and R.S. Mulliken in 1932. The salient features of this theory are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(7115,38,365,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The electrons in a molecule are present in the various molecular orbitals as the electrons of atoms are present in the various atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "(i) The electrons in a molecule are present in the various molecular orbitals as the electrons of atoms are present in the various atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7116,38,366,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The atomic orbitals of comparable energies and proper symmetry combine to form molecular orbitals.','[{"s": "(ii) The atomic orbitals of comparable energies and proper symmetry combine to form molecular orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7117,38,367,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) While an electron in an atomic orbital is influenced by one nucleus, in a molecular orbital it is influenced by two or more nuclei depending upon the number of atoms in the molecule. Thus, an atomic orbital is monocentric while a molecular orbital is polycentric.','[{"s": "(iii) While an electron in an atomic orbital is influenced by one nucleus, in a molecular orbital it is influenced by two or more nuclei depending upon the number of atoms in the molecule. Thus, an atomic orbital is monocentric while a molecular orbital is polycentric.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7118,38,368,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) The number of molecular orbital formed is equal to the number of combining atomic orbitals. When two atomic orbitals combine, two molecular orbitals are formed. One is known as bonding molecular orbital while the other is called antibonding molecular orbital .','[{"s": "(iv) The number of molecular orbital formed is equal to the number of combining atomic orbitals. When two atomic orbitals combine, two molecular orbitals are formed. One is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bonding molecular orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": "while the other is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "antibonding molecular orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12184,44,954,'paragraph',NULL,'=','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7119,38,369,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) The bonding molecular orbital has lower energy and hence greater stability than the corresponding antibonding molecular orbital.','[{"s": "(v) The bonding molecular orbital has lower energy and hence greater stability than the corresponding antibonding molecular orbital.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7120,38,370,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) Just as the electron probability distribution around a nucleus in an atom is given by an atomic orbital, the electron probability distribution around a group of nuclei in a molecule is given by a molecular orbital.','[{"s": "(vi) Just as the electron probability distribution around a nucleus in an atom is given by an atomic orbital, the electron probability distribution around a group of nuclei in a molecule is given by a molecular orbital.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7121,38,371,'paragraph',NULL,'(vii) The molecular orbitals like atomic orbitals are filled in accordance with the aufbau principle obeying the Pauli’s exclusion principle and the Hund’s rule.','[{"s": "(vii) The molecular orbitals like atomic orbitals are filled in accordance with the aufbau principle obeying the Pauli’s exclusion principle and the Hund’s rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7122,38,372,'paragraph',NULL,'4.7.1 Formation of Molecular Orbitals Linear Combination of Atomic Orbitals (LCAO)','[{"s": "4.7.1 Formation of Molecular Orbitals Linear Combination of Atomic Orbitals (LCAO)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7123,38,373,'paragraph',NULL,'According to wave mechanics, the atomic orbitals can be expressed by wave functions (ψ ’s) which represent the amplitude of the electron waves. These are obtained from the solution of Schrödinger wave equation. However, since it cannot be solved for any system containing more than one electron, molecular orbitals which are one electron wave functions for molecules are difficult to obtain directly from the solution of Schrödinger wave equation. To overcome this problem, an approximate method known as linear combination of atomic orbitals (LCAO) has been adopted.','[{"s": "According to wave mechanics, the atomic orbitals can be expressed by wave functions (ψ ’s) which represent the amplitude of the electron waves. These are obtained from the solution of Schrödinger wave equation. However, since it cannot be solved for any system containing more than one electron, molecular orbitals which are one electron wave functions for molecules are difficult to obtain directly from the solution of Schrödinger wave equation. To overcome this problem, an approximate method known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "linear combination of atomic orbitals (LCAO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has been adopted.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7124,38,374,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us apply this method to the homonuclear diatomic hydrogen molecule. Consider the hydrogen molecule consisting of two atoms A and B. Each hydrogen atom in the ground state has one electron in 1s orbital. The atomic orbitals of these atoms may be represented by the wave functions ψ A and ψ B . Mathematically, the formation of molecular orbitals may be described by the linear combination of atomic orbitals that can take place by addition and by subtraction of wave functions of individual atomic orbitals as shown below :','[{"s": "Let us apply this method to the homonuclear diatomic hydrogen molecule. Consider the hydrogen molecule consisting of two atoms A and B. Each hydrogen atom in the ground state has one electron in 1s orbital. The atomic orbitals of these atoms may be represented by the wave functions ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Mathematically, the formation of molecular orbitals may be described by the linear combination of atomic orbitals that can take place by addition and by subtraction of wave functions of individual atomic orbitals as shown below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7125,38,375,'paragraph',NULL,'ψ MO = ψ A + ψ B','[{"s": "ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7126,38,376,'paragraph',NULL,'σ = ψ A + ψ B','[{"s": "σ = ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7127,38,377,'paragraph',NULL,'σ* = ψ A – ψ B','[{"s": "σ* = ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7128,38,378,'paragraph',NULL,'The molecular orbital σ formed by the addition of atomic orbitals is called the bonding molecular orbital while the molecular orbital σ* formed by the subtraction of atomic orbital is called antibonding molecular orbital as depicted in Fig. 4.19.','[{"s": "The molecular orbital σ formed by the addition of atomic orbitals is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bonding molecular orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": "while the molecular orbital σ* formed by the subtraction of atomic orbital is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "antibonding molecular orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as depicted in Fig. 4.19.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7129,38,379,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-13-10',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image69.png'),
(7130,38,380,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.4.19 Formation of bonding (σ) and antibonding (σ*) molecular orbitals by the linear combination of atomic orbitals ψ A and ψ B centered on two atoms A and B respectively.','[{"s": "Fig.4.19 Formation of bonding (σ) and antibonding (σ*) molecular orbitals by the linear combination of atomic orbitals ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ψ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "centered on two atoms A and B respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7131,38,381,'paragraph',NULL,'Qualitatively, the formation of molecular orbitals can be understood in terms of the constructive or destructive interference of the electron waves of the combining atoms. In the formation of bonding molecular orbital, the two electron waves of the bonding atoms reinforce each other due to constructive interference while in the formation of antibonding molecular orbital, the electron waves cancel each other due to destructive interference. As a result, the electron density in a bonding molecular orbital is located between the nuclei of the bonded atoms because of which the repulsion between the nuclei is very less while in case of an antibonding molecular orbital, most of the electron density is located away from the space between the nuclei. Infact, there is a nodal plane (on which the electron density is zero) between the nuclei and hence the repulsion between the nuclei is high. Electrons placed in a bonding molecular orbital tend to hold the nuclei together and stabilise a molecule. Therefore, a bonding molecular orbital always possesses lower energy than either of the atomic orbitals that have combined to form it. In contrast, the electrons placed in the antibonding molecular orbital destabilise the molecule. This is because the mutual repulsion of the electrons in this orbital is more than the attraction between the electrons and the nuclei, which causes a net increase in energy.','[{"s": "Qualitatively, the formation of molecular orbitals can be understood in terms of the constructive or destructive interference of the electron waves of the combining atoms. In the formation of bonding molecular orbital, the two electron waves of the bonding atoms reinforce each other due to constructive interference while in the formation of antibonding molecular orbital, the electron waves cancel each other due to destructive interference. As a result, the electron density in a bonding molecular orbital is located between the nuclei of the bonded atoms because of which the repulsion between the nuclei is very less while in case of an antibonding molecular orbital, most of the electron density is located away from the space between the nuclei. Infact, there is a nodal plane (on which the electron density is zero) between the nuclei and hence the repulsion between the nuclei is high. Electrons placed in a bonding molecular orbital tend to hold the nuclei together and stabilise a molecule. Therefore, a bonding molecular orbital always possesses lower energy than either of the atomic orbitals that have combined to form it. In contrast, the electrons placed in the antibonding molecular orbital destabilise the molecule. This is because the mutual repulsion of the electrons in this orbital is more than the attraction between the electrons and the nuclei, which causes a net increase in energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7132,38,382,'paragraph',NULL,'It may be noted that the energy of the antibonding orbital is raised above the energy of the parent atomic orbitals that have combined and the energy of the bonding orbital has been lowered than the parent orbitals. The total energy of two molecular orbitals, however, remains the same as that of two original atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "It may be noted that the energy of the antibonding orbital is raised above the energy of the parent atomic orbitals that have combined and the energy of the bonding orbital has been lowered than the parent orbitals. The total energy of two molecular orbitals, however, remains the same as that of two original atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7133,38,383,'paragraph',NULL,'4.7.2 Conditions for the Combination of Atomic Orbitals','[{"s": "4.7.2 Conditions for the Combination of Atomic Orbitals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7134,38,384,'paragraph',NULL,'The linear combination of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals takes place only if the following conditions are satisfied:','[{"s": "The linear combination of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals takes place only if the following conditions are satisfied:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7135,38,385,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The combining atomic orbitals must have the same or nearly the same energy . This means that 1s orbital can combine with another 1s orbital but not with 2s orbital because the energy of 2s orbital is appreciably higher than that of 1s orbital. This is not true if the atoms are very different.','[{"s": "1. The combining atomic orbitals must have the same or nearly the same energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means that 1s orbital can combine with another 1s orbital but not with 2s orbital because the energy of 2s orbital is appreciably higher than that of 1s orbital. This is not true if the atoms are very different.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7136,38,386,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The combining atomic orbitals must have the same symmetry about the molecular axis . By convention z-axis is taken as the molecular axis. It is important to note that atomic orbitals having same or nearly the same energy will not combine if they do not have the same symmetry. For example, 2pz orbital of one atom can combine with 2pz orbital of the other atom but not with the 2px or 2py orbitals because of their different symmetries.','[{"s": "2. The combining atomic orbitals must have the same symmetry about the molecular axis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". By convention z-axis is taken as the molecular axis. It is important to note that atomic orbitals having same or nearly the same energy will not combine if they do not have the same symmetry. For example, 2pz orbital of one atom can combine with 2pz orbital of the other atom but not with the 2px or 2py orbitals because of their different symmetries.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7137,38,387,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The combining atomic orbitals must overlap to the maximum extent . Greater the extent of overlap, the greater will be the electron-density between the nuclei of a molecular orbital.','[{"s": "3. The combining atomic orbitals must overlap to the maximum extent", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Greater the extent of overlap, the greater will be the electron-density between the nuclei of a molecular orbital.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7138,38,388,'paragraph',NULL,'4.7.3 Types of Molecular Orbitals','[{"s": "4.7.3 Types of Molecular Orbitals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7139,38,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular orbitals of diatomic molecules are designated as σ (sigma), π (pi), δ (delta), etc.','[{"s": "Molecular orbitals of diatomic molecules are designated as σ (sigma), π (pi), δ (delta), etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7140,38,390,'paragraph',NULL,'In this nomenclature, the sigma ( σ ) molecular orbitals are symmetrical around the bond-axis while pi ( π ) molecular orbitals are not symmetrical . For example, the linear combination of 1s orbitals centered on two nuclei produces two molecular orbitals which are symmetrical around the bond-axis. Such molecular orbitals are of the σ type and are designated as σ1s and σ*1s [Fig. 4.20(a),page 124]. If internuclear axis is taken to be in the z-direction, it can be seen that a linear combination of 2p z - orbitals of two atoms also produces two sigma molecular orbitals designated as σ 2p z and σ *2p z . [Fig. 4.20(b)]','[{"s": "In this nomenclature, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sigma (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "σ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") molecular orbitals are symmetrical around the bond-axis while pi (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") molecular orbitals are not symmetrical", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, the linear combination of 1s orbitals centered on two nuclei produces two molecular orbitals which are symmetrical around the bond-axis. Such molecular orbitals are of the σ type and are designated as σ1s and σ*1s [Fig. 4.20(a),page 124].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If internuclear axis is taken to be in the z-direction, it can be seen that a linear combination of 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "- orbitals of two atoms also produces two sigma molecular orbitals designated as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "σ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "σ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "*2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". [Fig. 4.20(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7141,38,391,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular orbitals obtained from 2p x and 2p y orbitals are not symmetrical around the bond axis because of the presence of positive lobes above and negative lobes below the molecular plane. Such molecular orbitals, are labelled as π and π* [Fig. 4.20(c)]. A π bonding MO has larger electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis. The π* antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.','[{"s": "Molecular orbitals obtained from 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are not symmetrical around the bond axis because of the presence of positive lobes above and negative lobes below the molecular plane. Such molecular orbitals, are labelled as π and π* [Fig. 4.20(c)]. A π bonding MO has larger electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis. The π* antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7142,38,392,'paragraph',NULL,'4.7.4 Energy Level Diagram for Molecular Orbitals','[{"s": "4.7.4 Energy Level Diagram for Molecular Orbitals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7143,38,393,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen that 1s atomic orbitals on two atoms form two molecular orbitals designated as σ1s and σ*1s. In the same manner, the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals (eight atomic orbitals on two atoms) give rise to the following eight molecular orbitals:','[{"s": "We have seen that 1s atomic orbitals on two atoms form two molecular orbitals designated as σ1s and σ*1s. In the same manner, the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals (eight atomic orbitals on two atoms) give rise to the following eight molecular orbitals:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7144,38,394,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image19.png'),
(12185,44,955,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
