(7145,38,395,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.20 Contours and energies of bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals formed through combinations of (a) 1s atomic orbitals; (b) 2p z atomic orbitals and (c) 2p x atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.20 Contours and energies of bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals formed through combinations of (a) 1s atomic orbitals; (b) 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic orbitals and (c) 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7146,38,396,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-14-48',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image15.png'),
(7147,38,397,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy levels of these molecular orbitals have been determined experimentally from spectroscopic data for homonuclear diatomic molecules of second row elements of the periodic table. The increasing order of energies of various molecular orbitals for O 2 and F 2 is given below:','[{"s": "The energy levels of these molecular orbitals have been determined experimentally from spectroscopic data for homonuclear diatomic molecules of second row elements of the periodic table.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The increasing order of energies of various molecular orbitals for O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given below:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41929"]',NULL),
(7148,38,398,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-15-13',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image73.png'),
(7149,38,399,'paragraph',NULL,'However, this sequence of energy levels of molecular orbitals is not correct for the remaining molecules Li 2 , Be 2 , B 2 , C 2 , N 2 . For instance, it has been observed experimentally that for molecules such as B 2 , C 2 , N 2 , etc. the increasing order of energies of various molecular orbitals is','[{"s": "However, this sequence of energy levels of molecular orbitals is not correct for the remaining molecules Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For instance, it has been observed experimentally that for molecules such as B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc. the increasing order of energies of various molecular orbitals is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7150,38,400,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 22-15-33',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image71.png'),
(7151,38,401,'paragraph',NULL,'The important characteristic feature of this order is that the energy of σ 2p molecular orbital is higher than that of π2p x and π2p y molecular orbitals .','[{"s": "The important characteristic feature of this order is that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "energy of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "σ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "molecular orbital is higher than that of π2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and π2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecular orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7152,38,402,'heading',3,'4.7.5 Electronic Configuration and Molecular Behaviour',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7153,38,403,'paragraph',NULL,'The distribution of electrons among various molecular orbitals is called the electronic configuration of the molecule . From the electronic configuration of the molecule, it is possible to get important information about the molecule as discussed below.','[{"s": "The distribution of electrons among various molecular orbitals is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronic configuration of the molecule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From the electronic configuration of the molecule, it is possible to get important information about the molecule as discussed below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7154,38,404,'paragraph',NULL,'Stability of Molecules: If N b is the number of electrons occupying bonding orbitals and N a the number occupying the antibonding orbitals, then','[{"s": "Stability of Molecules:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the number of electrons occupying bonding orbitals and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the number occupying the antibonding orbitals, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7155,38,405,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the molecule is stable if N b is greater than N a , and','[{"s": "(i) the molecule is stable if N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is greater than N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7156,38,406,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the molecule is unstable if N b a .','[{"s": "(ii) the molecule is unstable if N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7157,38,407,'paragraph',NULL,'In (i) more bonding orbitals are occupied and so the bonding influence is stronger and a stable molecule results. In (ii) the antibonding influence is stronger and therefore the molecule is unstable.','[{"s": "In (i) more bonding orbitals are occupied and so the bonding influence is stronger and a stable molecule results. In (ii) the antibonding influence is stronger and therefore the molecule is unstable.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7158,38,408,'paragraph',NULL,'Bond order','[{"s": "Bond order", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7159,38,409,'paragraph',NULL,'Bond order (b.o.)3 is defined as one half the difference between the number of electrons present in the bonding and the antibonding orbitals i.e.,','[{"s": "Bond order (b.o.)3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is defined as one half the difference between the number of electrons present in the bonding and the antibonding orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e.,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41923", "41925", "41931"]',NULL),
(7160,38,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Bond order (b.o.) = ½ (N b –N a )','[{"s": "Bond order (b.o.) = ½ (N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10544,43,530,'paragraph',NULL,'HX(aq) + H 2 O(l) → H 3 O + (aq) + X – (aq)','[{"s": "HX(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12187,44,957,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12188,44,958,'paragraph',NULL,'(','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12189,44,959,'paragraph',NULL,'B','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7161,38,411,'paragraph',NULL,'The rules discussed above regarding the stability of the molecule can be restated in terms of bond order as follows: A positive bond order (i.e., N b > N a ) means a stable molecule while a negative (i.e., N b <N a b = N a ) bond order means an unstable molecule.','[{"s": "The rules discussed above regarding the stability of the molecule can be restated in terms of bond order as follows: A positive bond order (i.e., N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") means a stable molecule while a negative (i.e., N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") bond order means an unstable molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7162,38,412,'paragraph',NULL,'Nature of the bond','[{"s": "Nature of the bond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7163,38,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Integral bond order values of 1, 2 or 3 correspond to single, double or triple bonds respectively as studied in the classical concept.','[{"s": "Integral bond order values of 1, 2 or 3 correspond to single, double or triple bonds respectively as studied in the classical concept.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7164,38,414,'paragraph',NULL,'Bond-length','[{"s": "Bond-length", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7165,38,415,'paragraph',NULL,'The bond order between two atoms in a molecule may be taken as an approximate measure of the bond length . The bond length decreases as bond order increases.','[{"s": "The bond order between two atoms in a molecule may be taken as an approximate measure of the bond length", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The bond length decreases as bond order increases.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41948"]',NULL),
(7166,38,416,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetic nature','[{"s": "Magnetic nature", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41929"]',NULL),
(7167,38,417,'paragraph',NULL,'If all the molecular orbitals in a molecule are doubly occupied, the substance is diamagnetic (repelled by magnetic field). However if one or more molecular orbitals are singly occupied it is paramagnetic (attracted by magnetic field), e.g., O 2 molecule.','[{"s": "If all the molecular orbitals in a molecule are doubly occupied, the substance is diamagnetic (repelled by magnetic field). However if one or more molecular orbitals are singly occupied it is paramagnetic (attracted by magnetic field), e.g., O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7168,38,418,'heading',3,'4.8 BONDING IN SOME HOMONUCLEAR DIATOMIC MOLECULES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7169,38,419,'paragraph',NULL,'In this section we shall discuss bonding in some homonuclear diatomic molecules.','[{"s": "In this section we shall discuss bonding in some homonuclear diatomic molecules.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41949"]',NULL),
(7170,38,420,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Hydrogen molecule (H 2 ) : It is formed by the combination of two hydrogen atoms. Each hydrogen atom has one electron in 1s orbital. Therefore, in all there are two electrons in hydrogen molecule which are present in σ1s molecular orbital. So electronic configuration of hydrogen molecule is','[{"s": "1. Hydrogen molecule (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": It is formed by the combination of two hydrogen atoms. Each hydrogen atom has one electron in 1s orbital. Therefore, in all there are two electrons in hydrogen molecule which are present in σ1s molecular orbital. So electronic configuration of hydrogen molecule is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7171,38,421,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 : (σ1s) 2','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": (σ1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7172,38,422,'paragraph',NULL,'The bond order of H 2 molecule can be calculated as given below:','[{"s": "The bond order of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule can be calculated as given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7173,38,423,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 07-30-46',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image54.png'),
(7174,38,424,'paragraph',NULL,'This means that the two hydrogen atoms are bonded together by a single covalent bond. The bond dissociation energy of hydrogen molecule has been found to be 438 kJ mol –1 and bond length equal to 74 pm. Since no unpaired electron is present in hydrogen molecule, therefore, it is diamagnetic.','[{"s": "This means that the two hydrogen atoms are bonded together by a single covalent bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The bond dissociation energy of hydrogen molecule has been found to be 438 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and bond length equal to 74 pm. Since no unpaired electron is present in hydrogen molecule, therefore, it is diamagnetic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7175,38,425,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Helium molecule (He 2 ) : The electronic configuration of helium atom is 1s 2 . Each helium atom contains 2 electrons, therefore, in He 2 molecule there would be 4 electrons. These electrons will be accommodated in σ1s and σ*1s molecular orbitals leading to electronic configuration:','[{"s": "2. Helium molecule (He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The electronic configuration of helium atom is 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Each helium atom contains 2 electrons, therefore, in He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule there would be 4 electrons. These electrons will be accommodated in σ1s and σ*1s molecular orbitals leading to electronic configuration:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7176,38,426,'paragraph',NULL,'He 2 : (σ1s) 2 (σ*1s) 2','[{"s": "He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": (σ1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ*1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7177,38,427,'paragraph',NULL,'Bond order of He 2 is ½(2 – 2) = 0','[{"s": "Bond order of He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is ½(2 – 2) = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7178,38,428,'paragraph',NULL,'He 2 molecule is therefore unstable and does not exist.','[{"s": "He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule is therefore unstable and does not exist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7179,38,429,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, it can be shown that Be2 molecule (σ1s) 2 (σ*1s) 2 (σ2s) 2 (σ*2s) 2 also does not exist.','[{"s": "Similarly, it can be shown that Be2 molecule (σ1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ*1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ2s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ*2s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also does not exist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12190,44,960,'paragraph',NULL,'r','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7180,38,430,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Lithium molecule (Li 2 ) : The electronic configuration of lithium is 1s 2 , 2s 1 . There are six electrons in Li 2 . The electronic configuration of Li 2 molecule, therefore, is Li 2 : (σ1s) 2 (σ*1s) 2 (σ2s) 2','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Lithium molecule (Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The electronic configuration of lithium is 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are six electrons in Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The electronic configuration of Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule, therefore, is Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": (σ1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ*1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ2s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7181,38,431,'paragraph',NULL,'The above configuration is also written as KK(σ2s) 2 where KK represents the closed K shell structure (σ1s) 2 (σ*1s) 2 .','[{"s": "The above configuration is also written as KK(σ2s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where KK represents the closed K shell structure (σ1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(σ*1s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7182,38,432,'paragraph',NULL,'From the electronic configuration of Li 2 molecule it is clear that there are four electrons present in bonding molecular orbitals and two electrons present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Its bond order, therefore, is ½ (4 – 2) = 1. It means that Li 2 molecule is stable and since it has no unpaired electrons it should be diamagnetic. Indeed diamagnetic Li 2 molecules are known to exist in the vapour phase.','[{"s": "From the electronic configuration of Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule it is clear that there are four electrons present in bonding molecular orbitals and two electrons present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Its bond order, therefore, is ½ (4 – 2) = 1. It means that Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule is stable and since it has no unpaired electrons it should be diamagnetic. Indeed diamagnetic Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are known to exist in the vapour phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7183,38,433,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Carbon molecule (C 2 ): The electronic configuration of carbon is 1s 2 2s 2 2p 2 . There are twelve electrons in C 2 . The electronic configuration of C 2 molecule, therefore, is','[{"s": "4. Carbon molecule (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "):", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The electronic configuration of carbon is 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are twelve electrons in C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The electronic configuration of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule, therefore, is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7184,38,434,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 07-29-40',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image32.png'),
(7185,38,435,'paragraph',NULL,'The bond order of C 2 is ½ (8 – 4) = 2 and C 2 should be diamagnetic. Diamagnetic C 2 molecules have indeed been detected in vapour phase. It is important to note that double bond in C 2 consists of both pi bonds because of the presence of four electrons in two pi molecular orbitals. In most of the other molecules a double bond is made up of a sigma bond and a pi bond. In a similar fashion the bonding in N 2 molecule can be discussed.','[{"s": "The bond order of C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is ½ (8 – 4) = 2 and C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "should be diamagnetic.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Diamagnetic C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules have indeed been detected in vapour phase. It is important to note that double bond in C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "consists of both pi bonds because of the presence of four electrons in two pi molecular orbitals. In most of the other molecules a double bond is made up of a sigma bond and a pi bond. In a similar fashion the bonding in N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule can be discussed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41928"]',NULL),
(7186,38,436,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Oxygen molecule (O 2 ): The electronic configuration of oxygen atom is 1s 2 2s 2 2p 4 .4 Each oxygen atom has 8 electrons, hence, in O 2 molecule there are 16 electrons. The electronic configuration of O 2 molecule, therefore, is','[{"s": "5.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oxygen molecule (O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "):", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The electronic configuration of oxygen atom is 1s", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2s", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Each oxygen atom has 8 electrons, hence, in O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule there are 16 electrons. The electronic configuration of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule, therefore, is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41924", "41930", "41931", "41963"]',NULL),
(7187,38,437,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 07-28-40',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image16.png'),
(7188,38,438,'paragraph',NULL,'From the electronic configuration of O 2 molecule it is clear that ten electrons are present in bonding molecular orbitals and six electrons are present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Its bond order, therefore, is','[{"s": "From the electronic configuration of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule it is clear that ten electrons are present in bonding molecular orbitals and six electrons are present in antibonding molecular orbitals. Its bond order, therefore, is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
