(7194,38,444,'heading',3,'4.9 Hydrogen Bonding',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7195,38,445,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine are the highly electronegative elements. When they are attached to a hydrogen atom to form covalent bond, the electrons of the covalent bond are shifted towards the more electronegative atom. This partially positively charged hydrogen atom forms a bond with the other more electronegative atom. This bond is known as hydrogen bond and is weaker than the covalent bond. For example, in HF molecule, the hydrogen bond exists between hydrogen atom of one molecule and fluorine atom of another molecule as depicted below :','[{"s": "Nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine are the highly electronegative elements. When they are attached to a hydrogen atom to form covalent bond, the electrons of the covalent bond are shifted towards the more electronegative atom. This partially positively charged hydrogen atom forms a bond with the other more electronegative atom. This bond is known as hydrogen bond and is weaker than the covalent bond. For example, in HF molecule, the hydrogen bond exists between hydrogen atom of one molecule and fluorine atom of another molecule as depicted below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7196,38,446,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image26.png'),
(7197,38,447,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, hydrogen bond acts as a bridge between two atoms which holds one atom by covalent bond and the other by hydrogen bond. Hydrogen bond is represented by a dotted line (– – –) while a solid line represents the covalent bond. Thus, hydrogen bond can be defined as the attractive force which binds hydrogen atom of one molecule with the electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another molecule.','[{"s": "Here, hydrogen bond acts as a bridge between two atoms which holds one atom by covalent bond and the other by hydrogen bond. Hydrogen bond is represented by a dotted line (– – –) while a solid line represents the covalent bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, hydrogen bond can be defined as the attractive force which binds hydrogen atom of one molecule with the electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another molecule.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["290824"]',NULL),
(7198,38,448,'heading',4,'4.9.1 Cause of Formation of Hydrogen Bond',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7199,38,449,'paragraph',NULL,'When hydrogen is bonded to strongly electronegative element ‘X’, the electron pair shared between the two atoms moves far away from hydrogen atom. As a result the hydrogen atom becomes highly electropositive with respect to the other atom ‘X’. Since there is displacement of electrons towards X, the hydrogen acquires fractional positive charge (δ + ) while ‘X’ attain fractional negative charge (δ – ). This results in the formation of a polar molecule having electrostatic force of attraction which can be represented as :','[{"s": "When hydrogen is bonded to strongly electronegative element ‘X’, the electron pair shared between the two atoms moves far away from hydrogen atom. As a result the hydrogen atom becomes highly electropositive with respect to the other atom ‘X’. Since there is displacement of electrons towards X, the hydrogen acquires fractional positive charge (δ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") while ‘X’ attain fractional negative charge (δ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). This results in the formation of a polar molecule having electrostatic force of attraction which can be represented as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7200,38,450,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image65.png'),
(7201,38,451,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of H-bonding depends on the physical state of the compound. It is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state. Thus, the hydrogen bonds have strong influence on the structure and properties of the compounds.','[{"s": "The magnitude of H-bonding depends on the physical state of the compound. It is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state. Thus, the hydrogen bonds have strong influence on the structure and properties of the compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7202,38,452,'heading',4,'4.9.2 Types of H-Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7203,38,453,'paragraph',NULL,'There are two types of H-bonds','[{"s": "There are two types of H-bonds", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["41932"]',NULL),
(7204,38,454,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Intermolecular hydrogen bond','[{"s": "(i) Intermolecular hydrogen bond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7205,38,455,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Intramolecular hydrogen bond','[{"s": "(ii) Intramolecular hydrogen bond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7206,38,456,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) Intermolecular hydrogen bond : It is formed between two different molecules of the same or different compounds. For example, H-bond in case of HF molecule, alcohol or water molecules, etc.','[{"s": "(1) Intermolecular hydrogen bond :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is formed between two different molecules of the same or different compounds. For example, H-bond in case of HF molecule, alcohol or water molecules, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7207,38,457,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) Intramolecular hydrogen bond : It is formed when hydrogen atom is in between the two highly electronegative (F, O, N) atoms present within the same molecule. For example, in o-nitrophenol the hydrogen is in between the two oxygen atoms.','[{"s": "(2) Intramolecular hydrogen bond :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is formed when hydrogen atom is in between the two highly electronegative (F, O, N) atoms present within the same molecule. For example, in o-nitrophenol the hydrogen is in between the two oxygen atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7208,38,458,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Chemical_bonding-image28.png'),
(7209,38,459,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.22 Intramolecular hydrogen bonding in o-nitrophenol molecule','[{"s": "Fig. 4.22 Intramolecular hydrogen bonding in o-nitrophenol molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7210,38,460,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7211,38,461,'paragraph',NULL,'Kössel’s first insight into the mechanism of formation of electropositive and electronegative ions related the process to the attainment of noble gas configurations by the respective ions. Electrostatic attraction between ions is the cause for their stability. This gives the concept of electrovalency .','[{"s": "Kössel’s first insight into the mechanism of formation of electropositive and electronegative ions related the process to the attainment of noble gas configurations by the respective ions. Electrostatic attraction between ions is the cause for their stability. This gives the concept of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrovalency", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7212,38,462,'paragraph',NULL,'The first description of covalent bonding was provided by Lewis in terms of the sharing of electron pairs between atoms and he related the process to the attainment of noble gas configurations by reacting atoms as a result of sharing of electrons. The Lewis dot symbols show the number of valence electrons of the atoms of a given element and Lewis dot structures show pictorial representations of bonding in molecules.','[{"s": "The first description of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "covalent bonding", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was provided by Lewis in terms of the sharing of electron pairs between atoms and he related the process to the attainment of noble gas configurations by reacting atoms as a result of sharing of electrons. The Lewis dot symbols show the number of valence electrons of the atoms of a given element and Lewis dot structures show pictorial representations of bonding in molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7213,38,463,'paragraph',NULL,'An ionic compound is pictured as a three-dimensional aggregation of positive and negative ions in an ordered arrangement called the crystal lattice. In a crystalline solid there is a charge balance between the positive and negative ions. The crystal lattice is stabilized by the enthalpy of lattice formation .','[{"s": "An ionic compound is pictured as a three-dimensional aggregation of positive and negative ions in an ordered arrangement called the crystal lattice. In a crystalline solid there is a charge balance between the positive and negative ions. The crystal lattice is stabilized by the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "enthalpy of lattice formation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7214,38,464,'paragraph',NULL,'While a single covalent bond is formed by sharing of an electron pair between two atoms, multiple bonds result from the sharing of two or three electron pairs. Some bonded atoms have additional pairs of electrons not involved in bonding. These are called lone-pairs of electrons. A Lewis dot structure shows the arrangement of bonded pairs and lone pairs around each atom in a molecule. Important parameters, associated with chemical bonds, like: bond length, bond angle, bond enthalpy, bond order and bond polarity have significant effect on the properties of compounds.','[{"s": "While a single covalent bond is formed by sharing of an electron pair between two atoms, multiple bonds result from the sharing of two or three electron pairs. Some bonded atoms have additional pairs of electrons not involved in bonding. These are called lone-pairs of electrons. A Lewis dot structure shows the arrangement of bonded pairs and lone pairs around each atom in a molecule.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Important parameters, associated with chemical bonds, like: bond length, bond angle, bond enthalpy, bond order and bond polarity have significant effect on the properties of compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7215,38,465,'paragraph',NULL,'A number of molecules and polyatomic ions cannot be described accurately by a single Lewis structure and a number of descriptions (representations) based on the same skeletal structure are written and these taken together represent the molecule or ion. This is a very important and extremely useful concept called resonance . The contributing structures or canonical forms taken together constitute the resonance hybrid which represents the molecule or ion.','[{"s": "A number of molecules and polyatomic ions cannot be described accurately by a single Lewis structure and a number of descriptions (representations) based on the same skeletal structure are written and these taken together represent the molecule or ion. This is a very important and extremely useful concept called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resonance", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The contributing structures or canonical forms taken together constitute the resonance hybrid which represents the molecule or ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7216,38,466,'paragraph',NULL,'The VSEPR model used for predicting the geometrical shapes of molecules is based on the assumption that electron pairs repel each other and, therefore, tend to remain as far apart as possible. According to this model, molecular geometry is determined by repulsions between lone pairs and lone pairs ; lone pairs and bonding pairs and bonding pairs and bonding pairs. The order of these repulsions being : lp-lp > lp-bp > bp-bp','[{"s": "The VSEPR model used for predicting the geometrical shapes of molecules is based on the assumption that electron pairs repel each other and, therefore, tend to remain as far apart as possible. According to this model, molecular geometry is determined by repulsions between lone pairs and lone pairs ; lone pairs and bonding pairs and bonding pairs and bonding pairs. The order of these repulsions being : lp-lp > lp-bp > bp-bp", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12198,44,968,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) allyl (b) aryl (c) vinyl (d) secondary','[{"s": "(a) allyl (b) aryl (c) vinyl (d) secondary", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12199,44,969,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 13 Chlorobenzene is formed by reaction of chlorine with benzene in the','[{"s": "Q. 13 Chlorobenzene is formed by reaction of chlorine with benzene in the", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12200,44,970,'paragraph',NULL,'Presence of AICL','[{"s": "Presence of AICL", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7217,38,467,'paragraph',NULL,'The valence bond (VB) approach to covalent bonding is basically concerned with the energetics of covalent bond formation about which the Lewis and VSEPR models are silent. Basically the VB theory discusses bond formation in terms of overlap of orbitals. For example the formation of the H 2 molecule from two hydrogen atoms involves the overlap of the 1s orbitals of the two H atoms which are singly occupied. It is seen that the potential energy of the system gets lowered as the two H atoms come near to each other. At the equilibrium inter-nuclear distance (bond distance) the energy touches a minimum. Any attempt to bring the nuclei still closer results in a sudden increase in energy and consequent destabilization of the molecule. Because of orbital overlap the electron density between the nuclei increases which helps in bringing them closer. It is however seen that the actual bond enthalpy and bond length values are not obtained by overlap alone and other variables have to be taken into account.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence bond (VB) approach", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to covalent bonding is basically concerned with the energetics of covalent bond formation about which the Lewis and VSEPR models are silent. Basically the VB theory discusses bond formation in terms of overlap of orbitals. For example the formation of the H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule from two hydrogen atoms involves the overlap of the 1s orbitals of the two H atoms which are singly occupied. It is seen that the potential energy of the system gets lowered as the two H atoms come near to each other. At the equilibrium inter-nuclear distance (bond distance) the energy touches a minimum. Any attempt to bring the nuclei still closer results in a sudden increase in energy and consequent destabilization of the molecule. Because of orbital overlap the electron density between the nuclei increases which helps in bringing them closer. It is however seen that the actual bond enthalpy and bond length values are not obtained by overlap alone and other variables have to be taken into account.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7218,38,468,'paragraph',NULL,'For explaining the characteristic shapes of polyatomic molecules Pauling introduced the concept of hybridisation of atomic orbitals . sp,sp 2 , sp 3 hybridizations of atomic orbitals of Be, B,C, N and O are used to explain the formation and geometrical shapes of molecules like BeCl 2 , BCl 3 , CH 4 , NH 3 and H 2 O. T hey also explain the formation of multiple bonds in molecules like C 2 H 2 and C 2 H 4 .','[{"s": "For explaining the characteristic shapes of polyatomic molecules Pauling introduced the concept of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation of atomic orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sp,sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybridizations of atomic orbitals of Be, B,C, N and O are used to explain the formation and geometrical shapes of molecules like BeCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", BCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", CH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hey also explain the formation of multiple bonds in molecules like C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["41921"]',NULL),
(7219,38,469,'paragraph',NULL,'The molecular orbital (MO) theory describes bonding in terms of the combination and arrangment of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals that are associated with the molecule as a whole. The number of molecular orbitals are always equal to the number of atomic orbitals from which they are formed. Bonding molecular orbitals increase electron density between the nuclei and are lower in energy than the individual atomic orbitals. Antibonding molecular orbitals have a region of zero electron density between the nuclei and have more energy than the individual atomic orbitals.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecular orbital (MO) theory", "t": "text"}, {"s": "describes bonding in terms of the combination and arrangment of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals that are associated with the molecule as a whole. The number of molecular orbitals are always equal to the number of atomic orbitals from which they are formed. Bonding molecular orbitals increase electron density between the nuclei and are lower in energy than the individual atomic orbitals. Antibonding molecular orbitals have a region of zero electron density between the nuclei and have more energy than the individual atomic orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7220,38,470,'paragraph',NULL,'The electronic configuration of the molecules is written by filling electrons in the molecular orbitals in the order of increasing energy levels. As in the case of atoms, the Pauli exclusion principle and Hund’s rule are applicable for the filling of molecular orbitals. Molecules are said to be stable if the number of elctrons in bonding molecular orbitals is greater than that in antibonding molecular orbitals.','[{"s": "The electronic configuration of the molecules is written by filling electrons in the molecular orbitals in the order of increasing energy levels. As in the case of atoms, the Pauli exclusion principle and Hund’s rule are applicable for the filling of molecular orbitals. Molecules are said to be stable if the number of elctrons in bonding molecular orbitals is greater than that in antibonding molecular orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7221,38,471,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrogen bond is formed when a hydrogen atom finds itself between two highly electronegative atoms such as F, O and N. It may be intermolecular (existing between two or more molecules of the same or different substances) or intramolecular (present within the same molecule). Hydrogen bonds have a powerful effect on the structure and properties of many compounds.','[{"s": "Hydrogen bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is formed when a hydrogen atom finds itself between two highly electronegative atoms such as F, O and N. It may be intermolecular (existing between two or more molecules of the same or different substances) or intramolecular (present within the same molecule). Hydrogen bonds have a powerful effect on the structure and properties of many compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
