(7336,39,56,'paragraph',NULL,'In the preceding unit we have learnt that an electron in an atom is characterised by a set of four quantum numbers, and the principal quantum number (n ) defines the main energy level known as shell . We have also studied about the filling of electrons into different subshells, also referred to as orbitals (s, p, d, f) in an atom. The distribution of electrons into orbitals of an atom is called its electronic configuration . An element’s location in the Periodic Table reflects the quantum numbers of the last orbital filled. In this section we will observe a direct connection between the electronic configurations of the elements and the long form of the Periodic Table.','[{"s": "In the preceding unit we have learnt that an electron in an atom is characterised by a set of four quantum numbers, and the principal quantum number (n ) defines the main energy level known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shell", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We have also studied about the filling of electrons into different subshells, also referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s, p, d, f) in an atom. The distribution of electrons into orbitals of an atom is called its", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronic configuration", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". An element’s location in the Periodic Table reflects the quantum numbers of the last orbital filled. In this section we will observe a direct connection between the electronic configurations of the elements and the long form of the Periodic Table.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7337,39,57,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Electronic Configurations in Periods','[{"s": "(a) Electronic Configurations in Periods", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7338,39,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The period indicates the value of n for the outermost or valence shell. In other words, successive period in the Periodic Table is associated with the filling of the next higher principal energy level (n = 1, n = 2, etc.). It can be readily seen that the number of elements in each period is twice the number of atomic orbitals available in the energy level that is being filled. The first period (n = 1) starts with the filling of the lowest level (1s) and therefore has two elements — hydrogen (ls 1 ) and helium (ls 2 ) when the first shell (K) is completed. The second period (n = 2) starts with lithium and the third electron enters the 2s orbital. The next element, beryllium has four electrons and has the electronic configuration 1s 2 2s 2 . Starting from the next element boron, the 2p orbitals are filled with electrons when the L shell is completed at neon (2s 2 2p 6 transition series of elements. This starts from scandium (Z = 21) which has the electronic configuration 3d 1 4s 2 . The 3d orbitals are filled at zinc (Z=30) with electronic configuration 3d 10 4s 2 . The fourth period ends at krypton with the filling up of the 4p orbitals. Altogether we have 18 elements in this fourth period. The fifth period (n = 5) beginning with rubidium is similar to the fourth period and contains the 4d transition series starting at yttrium (Z = 39). This period ends at xenon with the filling up of the 5p orbitals. The sixth period (n = 6) contains 32 elements and successive electrons enter 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals, in the order — filling up of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) and ends at lutetium (Z = 71) to give the 4f- inner transition series which is called the lanthanoid series . The seventh period (n = 7) is similar to the sixth period with the successive filling up of the 7s, 5f, 6d and 7p orbitals and includes most of the man-made radioactive elements. This period will end at the element with atomic number 118 which would belong to the noble gas family. Filling up of the 5f orbitals after actinium (Z = 89) gives the 5f- inner transition series known as the actinoid series . The 4f- and 5f- inner transition series of elements are placed separately in the Periodic Table to maintain its structure and to preserve the principle of classification by keeping elements with similar properties in a single column.','[{"s": "The period indicates the value of n for the outermost or valence shell. In other words, successive period in the Periodic Table is associated with the filling of the next higher principal energy level (n = 1, n = 2, etc.). It can be readily seen that the number of elements in each period is twice the number of atomic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "available in the energy level that is being filled. The first period (n = 1) starts with the filling of the lowest level (1s) and therefore has two elements — hydrogen (ls", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and helium (ls", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") when the first shell (K) is completed. The second period (n = 2) starts with lithium and the third electron enters the 2s orbital. The next element, beryllium has four electrons and has the electronic configuration 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Starting from the next element boron, the 2p orbitals are filled with electrons when the L shell is completed at neon (2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "transition series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of elements. This starts from scandium (Z = 21) which has the electronic configuration 3d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The 3d orbitals are filled at zinc (Z=30) with electronic configuration 3d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The fourth period ends at krypton with the filling up of the 4p orbitals. Altogether we have 18 elements in this fourth period. The fifth period (n = 5) beginning with rubidium is similar to the fourth period and contains the 4d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transition series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "starting at yttrium (Z = 39). This period ends at xenon with the filling up of the 5p orbitals. The sixth period (n = 6) contains 32 elements and successive electrons enter 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals, in the order — filling up of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) and ends at lutetium (Z = 71) to give the 4f-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner transition series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lanthanoid series", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The seventh period (n = 7) is similar to the sixth period with the successive filling up of the 7s, 5f, 6d and 7p orbitals and includes most of the man-made radioactive elements. This period will end at the element with atomic number 118 which would belong to the noble gas family. Filling up of the 5f orbitals after actinium (Z = 89) gives the 5f-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner transition series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "actinoid series", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The 4f- and 5f-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner transition series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of elements are placed separately in the Periodic Table to maintain its structure and to preserve the principle of classification by keeping elements with similar properties in a single column.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7339,39,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.2','[{"s": "Problem 3.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7340,39,60,'paragraph',NULL,'How would you justify the presence of 18 elements in the 5 th period of the Periodic Table?','[{"s": "How would you justify the presence of 18 elements in the 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "period of the Periodic Table?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7341,39,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7342,39,62,'paragraph',NULL,'When n = 5, l = 0, 1, 2, 3. The order in which the energy of the available orbitals 4d, 5s and 5p increases is 5s < 4d < 5p. The total number of orbitals available are 9. The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated is 18; and therefore 18 elements are there in the 5 th period.','[{"s": "When n = 5, l = 0, 1, 2, 3. The order in which the energy of the available orbitals 4d, 5s and 5p increases is 5s < 4d < 5p. The total number of orbitals available are 9. The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated is 18; and therefore 18 elements are there in the 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "period.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7343,39,63,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Groupwise Electronic Configurations','[{"s": "(b) Groupwise Electronic Configurations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7344,39,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Elements in the same vertical column or group have similar valence shell electronic configurations, the same number of electrons in the outer orbitals, and similar properties. For example, the Group 1 elements (alkali metals) all have ns 1 valence shell electronic configuration as shown below.','[{"s": "Elements in the same vertical column or group have similar valence shell electronic configurations, the same number of electrons in the outer orbitals, and similar properties. For example, the Group 1 elements (alkali metals) all have ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence shell electronic configuration as shown below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7345,39,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus it can be seen that the properties of an element have periodic dependence upon its atomic number and not on relative atomic mass.','[{"s": "Thus it can be seen that the properties of an element have periodic dependence upon its atomic number and not on relative atomic mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7346,39,66,'heading',3,'3.6 ELECTRONIC CONFIGURATIONS AND TYPES OF ELEMENTS: s-, p-, d-, f- BLOCKS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7347,39,67,'paragraph',NULL,'The aufbau (build up) principle and the electronic configuration of atoms provide a theoretical foundation for the periodic classification. The elements in a vertical column of the Periodic Table constitute a group or family and exhibit similar chemical behaviour. This similarity arises because these elements have the same number and same distribution of electrons in their outermost orbitals. We can classify the elements into four blocks viz., s -block, p -block, d -block and f -block depending on the type of atomic orbitals that are being filled with electrons. This is illustrated in Fig. 3.3. We notice two exceptions to this categorisation. Strictly, helium belongs to the s-block but its positioning in the p-block along with other group 18 elements is justified because it has a completely filled valence shell (1s 2 ) and as a result, exhibits properties characteristic of other noble gases. The other exception is hydrogen. It has only one s-electron and hence can be placed in group 1 (alkali metals). It can also gain an electron to achieve a noble gas arrangement and hence it can behave similar to a group 17 (halogen family) elements. Because it is a special case, we shall place hydrogen separately at the top of the Periodic Table as shown in Fig. 3.2 and Fig. 3.3. We will briefly discuss the salient features of the four types of elements marked in the Periodic Table. More about these elements will be discussed later. During the description of their features certain terminology has been used which has been classified in section 3.7.','[{"s": "The aufbau (build up) principle and the electronic configuration of atoms provide a theoretical foundation for the periodic classification. The elements in a vertical column of the Periodic Table constitute a group or family and exhibit similar chemical behaviour. This similarity arises because these elements have the same number and same distribution of electrons in their outermost orbitals. We can classify the elements into four blocks viz., s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block", "t": "text"}, {"s": "depending on the type of atomic orbitals that are being filled with electrons. This is illustrated in Fig. 3.3. We notice two exceptions to this categorisation. Strictly, helium belongs to the s-block but its positioning in the p-block along with other group 18 elements is justified because it has a completely filled valence shell (1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and as a result, exhibits properties characteristic of other noble gases. The other exception is hydrogen. It has only one s-electron and hence can be placed in group 1 (alkali metals). It can also gain an electron to achieve a noble gas arrangement and hence it can behave similar to a group 17 (halogen family) elements. Because it is a special case, we shall place hydrogen separately at the top of the Periodic Table as shown in Fig. 3.2 and Fig. 3.3. We will briefly discuss the salient features of the four types of elements marked in the Periodic Table. More about these elements will be discussed later. During the description of their features certain terminology has been used which has been classified in section 3.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
