(7404,39,124,'paragraph',NULL,'3.6 (a)','[{"s": "3.6 (a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7405,39,125,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image6.png'),
(7406,39,126,'paragraph',NULL,'3.6 (b)','[{"s": "3.6 (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7407,39,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.6(a) First ionization enthalpies (∆iH) of elements of the second period as a function of atomic number (Z) and Fig. 3.6(b) ∆iH of alkali metals as a function of Z.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.6(a) First ionization enthalpies (∆iH) of elements of the second period as a function of atomic number (Z) and Fig. 3.6(b) ∆iH of alkali metals as a function of Z.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7408,39,128,'paragraph',NULL,'When we move from lithium to fluorine across the second period, successive electrons are added to orbitals in the same principal quantum level and the shielding of the nuclear charge by the inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus. Thus, across a period, increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding. Consequently, the outermost electrons are held more and more tightly and the ionization enthalpy increases across a period. As we go down a group, the outermost electron being increasingly farther from the nucleus, there is an increased shielding of the nuclear charge by the electrons in the inner levels. In this case, increase in shielding outweighs the increasing nuclear charge and the removal of the outermost electron requires less energy down a group.','[{"s": "When we move from lithium to fluorine across the second period, successive electrons are added to orbitals in the same principal quantum level and the shielding of the nuclear charge by the inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, across a period, increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding. Consequently, the outermost electrons are held more and more tightly and the ionization enthalpy increases across a period. As we go down a group, the outermost electron being increasingly farther from the nucleus, there is an increased shielding of the nuclear charge by the electrons in the inner levels. In this case, increase in shielding outweighs the increasing nuclear charge and the removal of the outermost electron requires less energy down a group.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31603"]',NULL),
(7409,39,129,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 3.6(a), you will also notice that the first ionization enthalpy of boron (Z = 5) is slightly less than that of beryllium (Z = 4) even though the former has a greater nuclear charge. When we consider the same principal quantum level, an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron. In beryllium, the electron removed during the ionization is an s-electron whereas the electron removed during ionization of boron is a p-electron. 3 The penetration of a 2s-electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron; hence the 2p electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of beryllium. Therefore, it is easier to remove the 2p-electron from boron compared to the removal of a 2s- electron from beryllium. Thus, boron has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium. Another “anomaly” is the smaller first ionization enthalpy of oxygen compared to nitrogen. This arises because in the nitrogen atom, three 2p-electrons reside in different atomic orbitals (Hund’s rule) whereas in the oxygen atom, two of the four 2p-electrons must occupy the same 2p-orbital resulting in an increased electron-electron repulsion . Consequently, it is easier to remove the fourth 2p-electron from oxygen than it is, to remove one of the three 2p-electrons from nitrogen.','[{"s": "From Fig. 3.6(a), you will also notice that the first ionization enthalpy of boron (Z = 5) is slightly less than that of beryllium (Z = 4) even though the former has a greater nuclear charge. When we consider the same principal quantum level, an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In beryllium, the electron removed during the ionization is an s-electron whereas the electron removed during ionization of boron is a p-electron.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The penetration of a 2s-electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron; hence the 2p electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of beryllium. Therefore, it is easier to remove the 2p-electron from boron compared to the removal of a 2s- electron from beryllium. Thus, boron has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium. Another “anomaly” is the smaller first ionization enthalpy of oxygen compared to nitrogen.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This arises because in the nitrogen atom, three 2p-electrons reside in different atomic orbitals (Hund’s rule) whereas in the oxygen atom, two of the four 2p-electrons must occupy the same 2p-orbital resulting in an increased electron-electron repulsion", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Consequently, it is easier to remove the fourth 2p-electron from oxygen than it is, to remove one of the three 2p-electrons from nitrogen.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31621", "31622", "31637", "31638"]',NULL),
(7410,39,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.6','[{"s": "Problem 3.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7411,39,131,'paragraph',NULL,'The first ionization enthalpy (∆i H ) values of the third period elements, Na, Mg and Si are respectively 496, 737 and 786 kJ mol –1 . Predict whether the first ∆i H value for Al will be more close to 575 or 760 kJ mol –1 ? Justify your answer.','[{"s": "The first ionization enthalpy (∆i H ) values of the third period elements, Na, Mg and Si are respectively 496, 737 and 786 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Predict whether the first ∆i H value for Al will be more close to 575 or 760 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? Justify your answer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7412,39,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7413,39,133,'paragraph',NULL,'It will be more close to 575 kJ mol –1.','[{"s": "It will be more close to 575 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7414,39,134,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Electron Gain Enthalpy','[{"s": "(d) Electron Gain Enthalpy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7415,39,135,'paragraph',NULL,'When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom (X) to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy (∆egH). Electron gain enthalpy provides a measure of the ease with which an atom adds an electron to form anion as represented by equation 3.3.','[{"s": "When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom (X) to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electron Gain Enthalpy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(∆egH). Electron gain enthalpy provides a measure of the ease with which an atom adds an electron to form anion as represented by equation 3.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7416,39,136,'paragraph',NULL,'X(g) + e – → X – (g) (3.3)','[{"s": "X(g) + e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) (3.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7417,39,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Depending on the element, the process of adding an electron to the atom can be either endothermic or exothermic. For many elements energy is released when an electron is added to the atom and the electron gain enthalpy is negative. For example, group 17 elements (the halogens) have very high negative electron gain enthalpies because they can attain stable noble gas electronic configurations by picking up an electron. On the other hand, noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpies because the electron has to enter the next higher principal quantum level leading to a very unstable electronic configuration. It may be noted that electron gain enthalpies have large negative values toward the upper right of the periodic table preceding the noble gases.','[{"s": "Depending on the element, the process of adding an electron to the atom can be either endothermic or exothermic. For many elements energy is released when an electron is added to the atom and the electron gain enthalpy is negative.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, group 17 elements (the halogens) have very high negative electron gain enthalpies because they can attain stable noble gas electronic configurations by picking up an electron.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "On the other hand, noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpies because the electron has to enter the next higher principal quantum level leading to a very unstable electronic configuration. It may be noted that electron gain enthalpies have large negative values toward the upper right of the periodic table preceding the noble gases.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31620"]',NULL),
(7418,39,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 3.7 Electron Gain Enthalpies * / (kJ mol –1 ) of Some Main Group Elements','[{"s": "Table 3.7 Electron Gain Enthalpies", "t": "text"}, {"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ (kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of Some Main Group Elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7419,39,139,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-31-37',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image31.png'),
(7420,39,140,'paragraph',NULL,'The variation in electron gain enthalpies of elements is less systematic than for ionization enthalpies. As a general rule, electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative with increase in the atomic number across a period. The effective nuclear charge increases from left to right across a period and consequently it will be easier to add an electron to a smaller atom since the added electron on an average would be closer to the positively charged nucleus. We should also expect electron gain enthalpy to become less negative as we go down a group because the size of the atom increases and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus. This is generally the case (Table 3.7). However, electron gain enthalpy of O or F is less negative than that of the succeeding element. This is because when an electron is added to O or F, the added electron goes to the smaller n = 2 quantum level and suffers significant repulsion from the other electrons present in this level. For the n = 3 quantum level (S or Cl), the added electron occupies a larger region of space and the electron-electron repulsion is much less.','[{"s": "The variation in electron gain enthalpies of elements is less systematic than for ionization enthalpies.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "As a general rule, electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative with increase in the atomic number across a period. The effective nuclear charge increases from left to right across a period and consequently it will be easier to add an electron to a smaller atom since the added electron on an average would be closer to the positively charged nucleus. We should also expect electron gain enthalpy to become less negative as we go down a group because the size of the atom increases and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus. This is generally the case (Table 3.7). However, electron gain enthalpy of O or F is less negative than that of the succeeding element. This is because when an electron is added to O or F, the added electron goes to the smaller n = 2 quantum level and suffers significant repulsion from the other electrons present in this level. For the n = 3 quantum level (S or Cl), the added electron occupies a larger region of space and the electron-electron repulsion is much less.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31619"]',NULL),
(7421,39,141,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.7','[{"s": "Problem 3.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7422,39,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Which of the following will have the most negative electron gain enthalpy and which the least negative? 2','[{"s": "Which of the following will have the most negative electron gain enthalpy and which the least negative?", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31618", "31619"]',NULL),
(7423,39,143,'paragraph',NULL,'P, S, Cl, F.','[{"s": "P, S, Cl, F.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7424,39,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Explain your answer.','[{"s": "Explain your answer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7425,39,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12234,44,1004,'paragraph',NULL,']','[{"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12235,44,1005,'paragraph',NULL,'−','[{"s": "−", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7426,39,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period as we move from left to right. Within a group, electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group . However, adding an electron to the 2p-orbital leads to greater repulsion than adding an electron to the larger 3p-orbital. Hence the element with most negative electron gain enthalpy is chlorine; the one with the least negative electron gain enthalpy is phosphorus.','[{"s": "Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period as we move from left to right.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Within a group, electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "However, adding an electron to the 2p-orbital leads to greater repulsion than adding an electron to the larger 3p-orbital. Hence the element with most negative electron gain enthalpy is chlorine; the one with the least negative electron gain enthalpy is phosphorus.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31639"]',NULL),
(7427,39,147,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Electronegativity','[{"s": "(e) Electronegativity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7428,39,148,'paragraph',NULL,'A qualitative measure of the ability of an atom in a chemical compound to attract shared electrons to itself is called electronegativity . Unlike ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, it is not a measureable quantity. However, a number of numerical scales of electronegativity of elements viz., Pauling scale, Mulliken-Jaffe scale, Allred-Rochow scale have been developed. The one which is the most widely used is the Pauling scale. Linus Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 assigned arbitrarily a value of 4.0 to fluorine, the element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons. Approximate values for the electronegativity of a few elements are given in Table 3.8(a)','[{"s": "A qualitative measure of the ability of an atom in a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chemical compound", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to attract shared electrons to itself is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronegativity", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Unlike ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, it is not a measureable quantity. However, a number of numerical scales of electronegativity of elements viz., Pauling scale, Mulliken-Jaffe scale, Allred-Rochow scale have been developed. The one which is the most widely used is the Pauling scale. Linus Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 assigned arbitrarily a value of 4.0 to fluorine, the element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons. Approximate values for the electronegativity of a few elements are given in Table 3.8(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7429,39,149,'paragraph',NULL,'* In many books, the negative of the enthalpy change for the process depicted in equation 3.3 is defined as the ELECTRON AFFINITY (A e ) of the atom under consideration. If energy is released when an electron is added to an atom, the electron affinity is taken as positive, contrary to thermodynamic convention. If energy has to be supplied to add an electron to an atom, then the electron affinity of the atom is assigned a negative sign. However, electron affinity is defined as absolute zero and, therefore at any other temperature (T) heat capacities of the reactants and the products have to be taken into account in ∆ eg H = –A e – 5/2 RT.','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In many books, the negative of the enthalpy change for the process depicted in equation 3.3 is defined as the ELECTRON AFFINITY (A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of the atom under consideration. If energy is released when an electron is added to an atom, the electron affinity is taken as positive, contrary to thermodynamic convention. If energy has to be supplied to add an electron to an atom, then the electron affinity of the atom is assigned a negative sign. However, electron affinity is defined as absolute zero and, therefore at any other temperature (T) heat capacities of the reactants and the products have to be taken into account in ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H = –A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 5/2 RT.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7430,39,150,'paragraph',NULL,'The electronegativity of any given element is not constant; it varies depending on the element to which it is bound. Though it is not a measurable quantity, it does provide a means of predicting the nature of force that holds a pair of atoms together – a relationship that you will explore later.','[{"s": "The electronegativity of any given element is not constant; it varies depending on the element to which it is bound. Though it is not a measurable quantity, it does provide a means of predicting the nature of force that holds a pair of atoms together – a relationship that you will explore later.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7431,39,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right (say from lithium to fluorine) and decrease down a group (say from fluorine to astatine) in the periodic table. How can these trends be explained? Can the electronegativity be related to atomic radii, which tend to decrease across each period from left to right, but increase down each group ? The attraction between the outer (or valence) electrons and the nucleus increases as the atomic radius decreases in a period. The electronegativity also increases. On the same account electronegativity values decrease with the increase in atomic radii down a group. The trend is similar to that of ionization enthalpy.','[{"s": "Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right (say from lithium to fluorine) and decrease down a group (say from fluorine to astatine) in the periodic table. How can these trends be explained? Can the electronegativity be related to atomic radii, which tend to decrease across each period from left to right, but increase down each group ? The attraction between the outer (or valence) electrons and the nucleus increases as the atomic radius decreases in a period. The electronegativity also increases.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On the same account electronegativity values decrease with the increase in atomic radii down a group.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The trend is similar to that of ionization enthalpy.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31639"]',NULL),
(7432,39,152,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image8.png'),
(7433,39,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.7 The periodic trends of elements in the periodic table','[{"s": "Fig. 3.7 The periodic trends of elements in the periodic table", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10547,43,533,'paragraph',NULL,'MOH(aq) → M + (aq) + OH – (aq) The hydroxyl ion also exists in the hydrated form in the aqueous solution. Arrhenius concept of acid and base, however, suffers from the limitation of being applicable only to aqueous solutions and also, does not account for the basicity of substances like, ammonia which do not possessa hydroxyl group.','[{"s": "MOH(aq) → M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) The hydroxyl ion also exists in the hydrated form in the aqueous solution. Arrhenius concept of acid and base, however, suffers from the limitation of being applicable only to aqueous solutions and also, does not account for the basicity of substances like, ammonia which do not possessa hydroxyl group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7434,39,154,'paragraph',NULL,'Knowing the relationship between electronegativity and atomic radius, can you now visualise the relationship between electronegativity and non-metallic properties? Non-metallic elements have strong tendency to gain electrons. Therefore, electronegativity is directly related to that non-metallic properties of elements. It can be further extended to say that the electronegativity is inversely related to the metallic properties of elements. Thus, the increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by an increase in non-metallic properties (or decrease in metallic properties) of elements. Similarly, the decrease in electronegativity down a group is accompanied by a decrease in non-metallic properties (or increase in metallic properties) of elements.','[{"s": "Knowing the relationship between electronegativity and atomic radius, can you now visualise the relationship between electronegativity and non-metallic properties? Non-metallic elements have strong tendency to gain electrons. Therefore, electronegativity is directly related to that non-metallic properties of elements. It can be further extended to say that the electronegativity is inversely related to the metallic properties of elements. Thus, the increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by an increase in non-metallic properties (or decrease in metallic properties) of elements. Similarly, the decrease in electronegativity down a group is accompanied by a decrease in non-metallic properties (or increase in metallic properties) of elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7435,39,155,'paragraph',NULL,'All these periodic trends are summarised in figure 3.7.','[{"s": "All these periodic trends are summarised in figure 3.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7436,39,156,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-42-27',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image26.png'),
(7437,39,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 3.8(a) Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) Across the Periods','[{"s": "Table 3.8(a) Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) Across the Periods", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7438,39,158,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-42-17',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image17.png'),
(7439,39,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 3.8(b) Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) Down a Family','[{"s": "Table 3.8(b) Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) Down a Family", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["31639"]',NULL),
(7440,39,160,'heading',4,'3.7.2 Periodic Trends in Chemical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7441,39,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Most of the trends in chemical properties of elements, such as diagonal relationships, inert pair effect, effects of lanthanoid contraction etc . will be dealt with along the discussion of each group in later units. In this section we shall study the periodicity of the valence state shown by elements and the anomalous properties of the second period elements (from lithium to fluorine).','[{"s": "Most of the trends in chemical properties of elements, such as diagonal relationships, inert pair effect, effects of lanthanoid contraction etc", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be dealt with along the discussion of each group in later units. In this section we shall study the periodicity of the valence state shown by elements and the anomalous properties of the second period elements (from lithium to fluorine).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31640"]',NULL),
(7442,39,162,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Periodicity of Valence or Oxidation States','[{"s": "(a) Periodicity of Valence or Oxidation States", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7443,39,163,'paragraph',NULL,'The valence is the most characteristic property of the elements and can be understood in terms of their electronic configurations. The valence of representative elements is usually (though not necessarily) equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals and / or equal to eight minus the number of outermost electrons as shown below.','[{"s": "The valence is the most characteristic property of the elements and can be understood in terms of their electronic configurations. The valence of representative elements is usually (though not necessarily) equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals and / or equal to eight minus the number of outermost electrons as shown below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7444,39,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Nowadays the term oxidation state is frequently used for valence. Consider the two oxygen containing compounds: OF 2 and Na 2 O. The order of electronegativity of the three elements involved in these compounds is F > O > Na. Each of the atoms of fluorine, with outer electronic configuration 2s 2 2p 5 , shares one electron with oxygen in the OF 2 molecule. Being highest electronegative element, fluorine is given oxidation state –1. Since there are two fluorine atoms in this molecule, oxygen with outer electronic configuration 2s 2 2p 4 shares two electrons with fluorine atoms and thereby exhibits oxidation state +2. In Na 2 O, oxygen being more electronegative accepts two electrons, one from each of the two sodium atoms and, thus, shows oxidation state –2. On the other hand sodium with electronic configuration 3s 1 loses one electron to oxygen and is given oxidation state +1. Thus, the oxidation state of an element in a particular compound can be defined as the charge acquired by its atom on the basis of electronegative consideration from other atoms in the molecule.','[{"s": "Nowadays the term oxidation state is frequently used for valence. Consider the two oxygen containing compounds: OF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O. The order of electronegativity of the three elements involved in these compounds is F > O > Na. Each of the atoms of fluorine, with outer electronic configuration 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", shares one electron with oxygen in the OF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule. Being highest electronegative element, fluorine is given oxidation state –1. Since there are two fluorine atoms in this molecule, oxygen with outer electronic configuration 2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shares two electrons with fluorine atoms and thereby exhibits oxidation state +2. In Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, oxygen being more electronegative accepts two electrons, one from each of the two sodium atoms and, thus, shows oxidation state –2. On the other hand sodium with electronic configuration 3s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "loses one electron to oxygen and is given oxidation state +1. Thus, the oxidation state of an element in a particular compound can be defined as the charge acquired by its atom on the basis of electronegative consideration from other atoms in the molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7445,39,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.8','[{"s": "Problem 3.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7446,39,166,'paragraph',NULL,'Using the Periodic Table, predict the formulas of compounds which might be formed by the following pairs of elements; (a) silicon and bromine (b) aluminium and sulphur.','[{"s": "Using the Periodic Table, predict the formulas of compounds which might be formed by the following pairs of elements; (a) silicon and bromine (b) aluminium and sulphur.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7447,39,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7448,39,168,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Silicon is group 14 element with a valence of 4; bromine belongs to the halogen family with a valence of 1. Hence the formula of the compound formed would be SiBr 4.','[{"s": "(a) Silicon is group 14 element with a valence of 4; bromine belongs to the halogen family with a valence of 1. Hence the formula of the compound formed would be SiBr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7449,39,169,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Aluminium belongs to group 13 with a valence of 3; sulphur belongs to group 16 elements with a valence of 2. Hence, the formula of the compound formed would be Al 2 S 3.','[{"s": "(b) Aluminium belongs to group 13 with a valence of 3; sulphur belongs to group 16 elements with a valence of 2. Hence, the formula of the compound formed would be Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7450,39,170,'paragraph',NULL,'Some periodic trends observed in the valence of elements (hydrides and oxides) are shown in Table 3.9. Other such periodic trends which occur in the chemical behaviour of the elements are discussed elsewhere in this book. There are many elements which exhibit variable valence. This is particularly characteristic of transition elements and actinoids, which we shall study later.','[{"s": "Some periodic trends observed in the valence of elements (hydrides and oxides) are shown in Table 3.9. Other such periodic trends which occur in the chemical behaviour of the elements are discussed elsewhere in this book. There are many elements which exhibit variable valence. This is particularly characteristic of transition elements and actinoids, which we shall study later.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7451,39,171,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-46-31',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image28.png'),
(7452,39,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 3.9 Periodic Trends in Valence of Elements as shown by the Formulas of Their Compounds','[{"s": "Table 3.9 Periodic Trends in Valence of Elements as shown by the Formulas of Their Compounds", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7453,39,173,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-49-01',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image29.png'),
(7454,39,174,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Anomalous Properties of Second Period Elements','[{"s": "(b) Anomalous Properties of Second Period Elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7455,39,175,'paragraph',NULL,'The first element of each of the groups 1 (lithium) and 2 (beryllium) and groups 13-17 (boron to fluorine) differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group. For example, lithium unlike other alkali metals, and beryllium unlike other alkaline earth metals, form compounds with pronounced covalent character; the other members of these groups predominantly form ionic compounds. In fact the behaviour of lithium and beryllium is more similar with the second element of the following group i.e., magnesium and aluminium, respectively. This sort of similarity is commonly referred to as diagonal relationship in the periodic properties.','[{"s": "The first element of each of the groups 1 (lithium) and 2 (beryllium) and groups 13-17 (boron to fluorine) differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group. For example, lithium unlike other alkali metals, and beryllium unlike other alkaline earth metals, form compounds with pronounced covalent character; the other members of these groups predominantly form ionic compounds. In fact the behaviour of lithium and beryllium is more similar with the second element of the following group i.e., magnesium and aluminium, respectively. This sort of similarity is commonly referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diagonal relationship", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the periodic properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7456,39,176,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 18-57-49',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Classification_of_Elements_and_Periodicity_in_Properties-image4.png'),
(7457,39,177,'paragraph',NULL,'What are the reasons for the different chemical behaviour of the first member of a group of elements in the s- and p- blocks compared to that of the subsequent members in the same group? The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity of the elements. In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). As a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4 (e.g., boron can only form [ BF 4 ] - , whereas the other members of the groups can expand their valence shell to accommodate more than four pairs of electrons e.g., aluminium [ AIF 4 ] - forms). Furthermore, the first member of p-block elements displays greater ability to form pπ – pπ multiple bonds to itself (e.g., C = C, C ≡ C, N = N, N ≡ Ν) and to other second period elements (e.g., C = O, C = N, C ≡ N, N = O) compared to subsequent members of the same group.','[{"s": "What are the reasons for the different chemical behaviour of the first member of a group of elements in the s- and p-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "blocks", "t": "text"}, {"s": "compared to that of the subsequent members in the same group? The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity of the elements. In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d). As a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4 (e.g., boron can only form [ BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", whereas the other members of the groups can expand their valence shell to accommodate more than four pairs of electrons e.g., aluminium [ AIF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forms). Furthermore, the first member of p-block elements displays greater ability to form pπ – pπ multiple bonds to itself (e.g., C = C, C ≡ C, N = N, N ≡ Ν) and to other second period elements (e.g., C = O, C = N, C ≡ N, N = O) compared to subsequent members of the same group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7458,39,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.9','[{"s": "Problem 3.9", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(7459,39,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Are the oxidation state and covalency of Al in [AlCl(H 2 O) 5 ] 2+ same ?','[{"s": "Are the oxidation state and covalency of Al in [AlCl(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "same ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7460,39,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7461,39,181,'paragraph',NULL,'No. The oxidation state of Al is +3 and the covalency is 6.','[{"s": "No. The oxidation state of Al is +3 and the covalency is 6.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7462,39,182,'heading',4,'3.7.3 Periodic Trends and Chemical Reactivity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7463,39,183,'paragraph',NULL,'We have observed the periodic trends in certain fundamental properties such as atomic and ionic radii, ionization enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and valence. We know by now that the periodicity is related to electronic configuration. That is, all chemical and physical properties are a manifestation of the electronic configuration of elements. We shall now try to explore relationships between these fundamental properties of elements with their chemical reactivity.','[{"s": "We have observed the periodic trends in certain fundamental properties such as atomic and ionic radii, ionization enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and valence.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We know by now that the periodicity is related to electronic configuration. That is, all chemical and physical properties are a manifestation of the electronic configuration of elements. We shall now try to explore relationships between these fundamental properties of elements with their chemical reactivity.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["31615"]',NULL),
(7464,39,184,'paragraph',NULL,'The atomic and ionic radii, as we know, generally decrease in a period from left to right. As a consequence, the ionization enthalpies generally increase (with some exceptions as outlined in section 3.7.1(a)) and electron gain enthalpies become more negative across a period. In other words, the ionization enthalpy of the extreme left element in a period is the least and the electron gain enthalpy of the element on the extreme right is the highest negative (note : noble gases having completely filled shells have rather positive electron gain enthalpy values). This results into high chemical reactivity at the two extremes and the lowest in the centre. Thus, the maximum chemical reactivity at the extreme left (among alkali metals) is exhibited by the loss of an electron leading to the formation of a cation and at the extreme right (among halogens) shown by the gain of an electron forming an anion. This property can be related with the reducing and oxidizing behaviour of the elements which you will learn later. However, here it can be directly related to the metallic and non-metallic character of elements. Thus, the metallic character of an element, which is highest at the extremely left decreases and the non-metallic character increases while moving from left to right across the period. The chemical reactivity of an element can be best shown by its reactions with oxygen and halogens. Here, we shall consider the reaction of the elements with oxygen only. Elements on two extremes of a period easily combine with oxygen to form oxides. The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (e.g., Na 2 O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (e.g., Cl 2 O 7 ). Oxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (e.g., Al 2 O 3 , As 2 O 3 ) or neutral (e.g., CO, NO, N 2 O). Amphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties.','[{"s": "The atomic and ionic radii, as we know, generally decrease in a period from left to right. As a consequence, the ionization enthalpies generally increase (with some exceptions as outlined in section 3.7.1(a)) and electron gain enthalpies become more negative across a period. In other words, the ionization enthalpy of the extreme left element in a period is the least and the electron gain enthalpy of the element on the extreme right is the highest negative (note : noble gases having completely filled shells have rather positive electron gain enthalpy values). This results into high chemical reactivity at the two extremes and the lowest in the centre. Thus, the maximum chemical reactivity at the extreme left (among alkali metals) is exhibited by the loss of an electron leading to the formation of a cation and at the extreme right (among halogens) shown by the gain of an electron forming an anion. This property can be related with the reducing and oxidizing behaviour of the elements which you will learn later. However, here it can be directly related to the metallic and non-metallic character of elements. Thus, the metallic character of an element, which is highest at the extremely left decreases and the non-metallic character increases while moving from left to right across the period. The chemical reactivity of an element can be best shown by its reactions with oxygen and halogens. Here, we shall consider the reaction of the elements with oxygen only. Elements on two extremes of a period easily combine with oxygen to form oxides. The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (e.g., Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (e.g., Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Oxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (e.g., Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", As", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or neutral (e.g., CO, NO, N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O). Amphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7465,39,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 3.10','[{"s": "Problem 3.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7466,39,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Show by a chemical reaction with water that Na 2 O is a basic oxide and Cl 2 O 7 is an acidic oxide.','[{"s": "Show by a chemical reaction with water that Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O is a basic oxide and Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an acidic oxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7467,39,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7468,39,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 2 O with water forms a strong base whereas Cl 2 O 7 forms strong acid.','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O with water forms a strong base whereas Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forms strong acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7469,39,189,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 2 O + H 2 O → 2NaOH','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → 2NaOH", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7470,39,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Cl 2 O 7 + H 2 O → 2HClO 4','[{"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → 2HClO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7471,39,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Their basic or acidic nature can be qualitatively tested with litmus paper.','[{"s": "Their basic or acidic nature can be qualitatively tested with litmus paper.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7472,39,192,'paragraph',NULL,'In a group, the increase in atomic and ionic radii with increase in atomic number generally results in a gradual decrease in ionization enthalpies and a regular decrease (with exception in some third period elements as shown in section 3.7.1(d)) in electron gain enthalpies in the case of main group elements. Thus, the metallic character increases down the group and non-metallic character decreases. This trend can be related with their reducing and oxidizing property which you will learn later. In the case of transition elements, however, a reverse trend is observed. This can be explained in terms of atomic size and ionization enthalpy.','[{"s": "In a group, the increase in atomic and ionic radii with increase in atomic number generally results in a gradual decrease in ionization enthalpies and a regular decrease (with exception in some third period elements as shown in section 3.7.1(d)) in electron gain enthalpies in the case of main group elements. Thus, the metallic character increases down the group and non-metallic character decreases. This trend can be related with their reducing and oxidizing property which you will learn later. In the case of transition elements, however, a reverse trend is observed. This can be explained in terms of atomic size and ionization enthalpy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7473,39,193,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7474,39,194,'paragraph',NULL,'In this Unit, you have studied the development of the Periodic Law and the Periodic Table . Mendeleev’s Periodic Table was based on atomic masses. Modern Periodic Table arranges the elements in the order of their atomic numbers in seven horizontal rows ( periods ) and eighteen vertical columns ( groups or families ). Atomic numbers in a period are consecutive, whereas in a group they increase in a pattern. Elements of the same group have similar valence shell electronic configuration and, therefore, exhibit similar chemical properties. However, the elements of the same period have incrementally increasing number of electrons from left to right, and, therefore, have different valencies. Four types of elements can be recognized in the periodic table on the basis of their electronic configurations. These are s -block, p -block, d -block and f -block elements. Hydrogen with one electron in the 1s orbital occupies a unique position in the periodic table. Metals comprise more than seventy eight per cent of the known elements. Non-metals , which are located at the top of the periodic table, are less than twenty in number. Elements which lie at the border line between metals and non-metals (e.g., Si, Ge, As) are called metalloids or semi-metals . Metallic character increases with increasing atomic number in a group whereas decreases from left to right in a period. The physical and chemical properties of elements vary periodically with their atomic numbers.','[{"s": "In this Unit, you have studied the development of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Periodic Law", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Periodic Table", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Mendeleev’s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Periodic Table", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was based on atomic masses. Modern", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Periodic Table", "t": "text"}, {"s": "arranges the elements in the order of their atomic numbers in seven horizontal rows (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "periods", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and eighteen vertical columns (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "families", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Atomic numbers in a period are consecutive, whereas in a group they increase in a pattern. Elements of the same group have similar", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence shell", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronic configuration and, therefore, exhibit similar chemical properties. However, the elements of the same period have incrementally increasing number of electrons from left to right, and, therefore, have different valencies. Four types of elements can be recognized in the periodic table on the basis of their electronic configurations. These are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block", "t": "text"}, {"s": "elements.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrogen", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with one electron in the 1s orbital occupies a unique position in the periodic table.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Metals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "comprise more than seventy eight per cent of the known elements.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Non-metals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which are located at the top of the periodic table, are less than twenty in number. Elements which lie at the border line between metals and non-metals (e.g., Si, Ge, As) are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "metalloids", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "semi-metals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Metallic character increases with increasing atomic number in a group whereas decreases from left to right in a period. The physical and chemical properties of elements vary periodically with their atomic numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7475,39,195,'paragraph',NULL,'Periodic trends are observed in atomic sizes, ionization enthalpies, electron gain enthalpies , electronegativity and valence. The atomic radii decrease while going from left to right in a period and increase with atomic number in a group. Ionization enthalpies generally increase across a period and decrease down a group. Electronegativity also shows a similar trend. Electron gain enthalpies, in general, become more negative across a period and less negative down a group. There is some periodicity in valence, for example, among representative elements, the valence is either equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals or eight minus this number. Chemical reactivity is highest at the two extremes of a period and is lowest in the centre. The reactivity on the left extreme of a period is because of the ease of electron loss (or low ionization enthalpy). Highly reactive elements do not occur in nature in free state; they usually occur in the combined form. Oxides formed of the elements on the left are basic and of the elements on the right are acidic in nature. Oxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric or neutral.','[{"s": "Periodic trends", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are observed in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic sizes, ionization enthalpies, electron gain enthalpies", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electronegativity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The atomic radii decrease while going from left to right in a period and increase with atomic number in a group. Ionization enthalpies generally increase across a period and decrease down a group. Electronegativity also shows a similar trend. Electron gain enthalpies, in general, become more negative across a period and less negative down a group. There is some periodicity in valence, for example, among representative elements, the valence is either equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals or eight minus this number.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical reactivity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is highest at the two extremes of a period and is lowest in the centre. The reactivity on the left extreme of a period is because of the ease of electron loss (or low ionization enthalpy). Highly reactive elements do not occur in nature in free state; they usually occur in the combined form. Oxides formed of the elements on the left are basic and of the elements on the right are acidic in nature. Oxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric or neutral.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
