(7877,40,13,'paragraph',NULL,'These observations, together with the results of conductivity measurements in solution can be explained if (i) six groups in all, either chloride ions or ammonia molecules or both, remain bonded to the cobalt ion during the reaction and (ii) the compounds are formulated as shown in Table 9.1, where the atoms within the square brackets form a single entity which does not dissociate under the reaction conditions. Werner proposed the term secondary valence for the number of groups bound directly to the metal ion; in each of these examples the secondary valences are six.','[{"s": "These observations, together with the results of conductivity measurements in solution can be explained if (i) six groups in all, either chloride ions or ammonia molecules or both, remain bonded to the cobalt ion during the reaction and (ii) the compounds are formulated as shown in Table 9.1, where the atoms within the square brackets form a single entity which does not dissociate under the reaction conditions. Werner proposed the term", "t": "text"}, {"s": "secondary valence", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the number of groups bound directly to the metal ion; in each of these examples the secondary valences are six.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7878,40,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 9.1: Formulation of Cobalt(III) Chloride-Ammonia Complexes','[{"s": "Table 9.1: Formulation of Cobalt(III) Chloride-Ammonia Complexes", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231376"]',NULL),
(7879,40,15,'image',NULL,'9a',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image27.png'),
(7880,40,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the last two compounds in Table 9.1 have identical empirical formula, CoCl 3 .4NH 3 , but distinct properties. Such compounds are termed as isomers. Werner in 1898, propounded his theory of coordination compounds. The main postulates are:','[{"s": "Note that the last two compounds in Table 9.1 have identical empirical formula, CoCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".4NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", but distinct properties.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Such compounds are termed as isomers. Werner in 1898, propounded his theory of coordination compounds. The main postulates are:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231377"]',NULL),
(7881,40,17,'paragraph',NULL,'1. In coordination compounds metals show two types of linkages (valences)-primary and secondary.','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In coordination compounds metals show two types of linkages (valences)-primary and secondary.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7882,40,18,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The primary valences are normally ionisable and are satisfied by negative ions.','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The primary valences are normally ionisable and are satisfied by negative ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7883,40,19,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The secondary valences are non ionisable. These are satisfied by neutral molecules or negative ions. The secondary valence is equal to the coordination number and is fixed for a metal.','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The secondary valences are non ionisable. These are satisfied by neutral molecules or negative ions.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The secondary valence is equal to the coordination number and is fixed for a metal.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231317"]',NULL),
(7884,40,20,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The ions/groups bound by the secondary linkages to the metal have characteristic spatial arrangements corresponding to different coordination numbers.','[{"s": "4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ions/groups bound by the secondary linkages to the metal have characteristic spatial arrangements corresponding to different coordination numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7885,40,21,'paragraph',NULL,'In modern formulations, such spatial arrangements are called coordination polyhedra . The species within the square bracket are coordination entities or complexes and the ions outside the square bracket are called counter ions.','[{"s": "In modern formulations, such spatial arrangements are called coordination", "t": "text"}, {"s": "polyhedra", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The species within the square bracket are coordination entities or complexes and the ions outside the square bracket are called counter ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7886,40,22,'paragraph',NULL,'He further postulated that octahedral, tetrahedral and square planar geometrical shapes are more common in coordination compounds of transition metals. Thus, [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ , [CoCl(NH 3 ) 5 ] 2+ and [CoCl 2 (NH 3 ) 4 ] + are octahedral entities, while [Ni(CO) 4 ] and [PtCl 4 ] 2– are tetrahedral and square planar, respectively.','[{"s": "He further postulated that octahedral, tetrahedral and square planar geometrical shapes are more common in coordination compounds of transition metals. Thus, [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [CoCl(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [CoCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are octahedral entities, while [Ni(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and [PtCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are tetrahedral and square planar, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7887,40,23,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.1','[{"s": "Example 9.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7888,40,24,'paragraph',NULL,'On the basis of the following observations made with aqueous solutions, assign secondary valences to metals in the following compounds:','[{"s": "On the basis of the following observations made with aqueous solutions, assign secondary valences to metals in the following compounds:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7889,40,25,'image',NULL,'9b',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image18.png'),
(7890,40,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7891,40,27,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Secondary 4 (ii) Secondary 6','[{"s": "(i) Secondary 4 (ii) Secondary 6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7892,40,28,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Secondary 6 (iv) Secondary 6 (v) Secondary 4','[{"s": "(iii) Secondary 6 (iv) Secondary 6 (v) Secondary 4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7893,40,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Difference between a double salt and a complex','[{"s": "Difference between a double salt and a complex", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7894,40,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Both double salts as well as complexes are formed by the combination of two or more stable compounds in stoichiometric ratio. However, they differ in the fact that double salts such as carnallite, KCl.MgCl 2 .6H 2 O, Mohr’s salt, FeSO 4 .(NH 4 ) 2 SO 4 .6H 2 O, potash alum, KAl(SO 4 ) 2 .12H 2 O, etc. dissociate into simple ions completely when dissolved in water. However, complex ions such as [Fe(CN) 6 ] 4– of K 4 [Fe(CN) 6 ] do not dissociate into Fe 2+ and CN – ions.','[{"s": "Both double salts as well as complexes are formed by the combination of two or more stable compounds in stoichiometric ratio. However, they differ in the fact that double salts such as carnallite, KCl.MgCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".6H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, Mohr’s salt, FeSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".6H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, potash alum, KAl(SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".12H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, etc. dissociate into simple ions completely when dissolved in water. However, complex ions such as [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] do not dissociate into Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7895,40,31,'image',NULL,'9c',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image2.png'),
(7896,40,32,'paragraph',NULL,'(1866-1919)','[{"s": "(1866-1919)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7897,40,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Werner was born on December 12, 1866, in Mülhouse, a small community in the French province of Alsace. His study of chemistry began in Karlsruhe (Germany) and continued in Zurich (Switzerland), where in his doctoral thesis in 1890, he explained the difference in properties of certain nitrogen containing organic substances on the basis of isomerism. He extended vant Hoff’s theory of tetrahedral carbon atom and modified it for nitrogen. Werner showed optical and electrical differences between complex compounds based on physical measurements. In fact, Werner was the first to discover optical activity in certain coordination compounds.','[{"s": "Werner", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was born on December 12, 1866, in Mülhouse, a small community in the French province of Alsace. His study of chemistry began in Karlsruhe (Germany) and continued in Zurich (Switzerland), where in his doctoral thesis in 1890, he explained the difference in properties of certain nitrogen containing organic substances on the basis of isomerism. He extended vant Hoff’s theory of tetrahedral carbon atom and modified it for nitrogen. Werner showed optical and electrical differences between complex compounds based on physical measurements. In fact, Werner was the first to discover optical activity in certain coordination compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12238,44,1008,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) vic-dihalide (b) gem-dihalide —(c) allylic halide (d) vinylic halide','[{"s": "(a) vic-dihalide (b) gem-dihalide —(c) allylic halide (d) vinylic halide", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7898,40,34,'paragraph',NULL,'He, at the age of 29 years became a full professor at Technische Hochschule in Zurich in 1895. Alfred Werner was a chemist and educationist. His accomplishments included the development of the theory of coordination compounds. This theory, in which Werner proposed revolutionary ideas about how atoms and molecules are linked together, was formulated in a span of only three years, from 1890 to 1893. The remainder of his career was spent gathering the experimental support required to validate his new ideas. Werner became the first Swiss chemist to win the Nobel Prize in 1913 for his work on the linkage of atoms and the coordination theory.','[{"s": "He, at the age of 29 years became a full professor at Technische Hochschule in Zurich in 1895. Alfred Werner was a chemist and educationist. His accomplishments included the development of the theory of coordination compounds. This theory, in which Werner proposed revolutionary ideas about how atoms and molecules are linked together, was formulated in a span of only three years, from 1890 to 1893. The remainder of his career was spent gathering the experimental support required to validate his new ideas. Werner became the first Swiss chemist to win the Nobel Prize in 1913 for his work on the linkage of atoms and the coordination theory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7899,40,35,'heading',3,'9.2 Definitions of Some Important Terms Pertaining to Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7900,40,36,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Coordination entity','[{"s": "(a) Coordination entity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7901,40,37,'paragraph',NULL,'A coordination entity constitutes a central metal atom or ion bonded to a fixed number of ions or molecules. For example, [CoCl 3 (NH 3 ) 3 ] is a coordination entity in which the cobalt ion is surrounded by three ammonia molecules and three chloride ions. Other examples are [Ni(CO) 4 ], [PtCl 2 (NH 3 ) 2 ], [Fe(CN) 6 ] 4– , [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ .','[{"s": "A coordination entity constitutes a central metal atom or ion bonded to a fixed number of ions or molecules. For example, [CoCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is a coordination entity in which the cobalt ion is surrounded by three ammonia molecules and three chloride ions. Other examples are [Ni(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], [PtCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7902,40,38,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Central atom/ion','[{"s": "(b) Central atom/ion", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7903,40,39,'paragraph',NULL,'In a coordination entity, the atom/ion to which a fixed number of ions/groups are bound in a definite geometrical arrangement around it, is called the central atom or ion. For example, the central atom/ion in the coordination entities: [NiCl 2 (H 2 O) 4 ], [CoCl(NH 3 ) 5 ] 2+ and [Fe(CN) 6 ] 3– are Ni 2+ , Co 3+ and Fe 3+ , respectively. These central atoms/ions are also referred to as Lewis acids .','[{"s": "In a coordination entity, the atom/ion to which a fixed number of ions/groups are bound in a definite geometrical arrangement around it, is called the central atom or ion. For example, the central atom/ion in the coordination entities: [NiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], [CoCl(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are Ni", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Co", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. These central atoms/ions are also referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Lewis acids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7904,40,40,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Ligands','[{"s": "(c) Ligands", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7905,40,41,'paragraph',NULL,'The ions or molecules bound to the central atom/ion in the coordination entity are called ligands. These may be simple ions such as Cl – , small molecules such as H 2 O or NH 3 , larger molecules such as H 2 NCH 2 CH 2 NH 2 or N(CH 2 CH 2 NH 2 ) 3 or even macromolecules, such as proteins.','[{"s": "The ions or molecules bound to the central atom/ion in the coordination entity are called ligands. These may be simple ions such as Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", small molecules such as H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O or NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", larger molecules such as H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or N(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or even macromolecules, such as proteins.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7906,40,42,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image24.png'),
(7907,40,43,'paragraph',NULL,'When a ligand is bound to a metal ion through a single donor atom, as with Cl – , H 2 O or NH 3 , the ligand is said to be unidentate . When a ligand can bind through two donor atoms as in H 2 NCH 2 CH 2 NH 2 (ethane-1,2-diamine) or C 2 O 4 2– (oxalate), the ligand is said to be didentate and when several donor atoms are present in a single ligand as in N(CH 2 CH 2 NH 2 ) 3 , the ligand is said to be polydentate . Ethylenediaminetetraacetate ion (EDTA 4– ) is an important hexadentate ligand. It can bind through two nitrogen and four oxygen atoms to a central metal ion.','[{"s": "When a ligand is bound to a metal ion through a single donor atom, as with Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O or NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the ligand is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unidentate", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When a ligand can bind through two donor atoms as in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ethane-1,2-diamine) or C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(oxalate), the ligand is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "didentate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and when several donor atoms are present in a single ligand as in N(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the ligand is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "polydentate", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ethylenediaminetetraacetate ion (EDTA", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") is an important hexadentate ligand.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It can bind through two nitrogen and four oxygen atoms to a central metal ion.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231323"]',NULL),
(7908,40,44,'image',NULL,'9d',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image1.png'),
(7909,40,45,'paragraph',NULL,'When a di- or polydentate ligand uses its two or more donor atoms simultaneously to bind a single metal ion, it is said to be a chelate ligand. The number of such ligating groups is called the denticity of the ligand. Such complexes, called chelate complexes tend to be more stable than similar complexes containing unidentate ligands (for reasons see Section 9.8). Ligand which has two different donor atoms and either of the two ligetes in the complex is called ambidentate ligand . Examples of such ligands are the NO 2 – and SCN – ions. NO 2 – ion can coordinate either through nitrogen or through oxygen to a central metal atom/ion.','[{"s": "When a di- or polydentate ligand uses its two or more donor atoms simultaneously to bind a single metal ion, it is said to be a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chelate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ligand. The number of such ligating groups is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denticity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the ligand. Such complexes, called chelate complexes tend to be more stable than similar complexes containing unidentate ligands (for reasons see Section 9.8). Ligand which has two different donor atoms and either of the two ligetes in the complex is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ambidentate ligand", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Examples of such ligands are the NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions. NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion can coordinate either through nitrogen or through oxygen to a central metal atom/ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7910,40,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, SCN – ion can coordinate through the sulphur or nitrogen atom.','[{"s": "Similarly, SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion can coordinate through the sulphur or nitrogen atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7911,40,47,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Coordination number','[{"s": "(d) Coordination number", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7912,40,48,'paragraph',NULL,'The coordination number (CN) of a metal ion in a complex can be defined as the number of ligand donor atoms to which the metal is directly bonded. For example, in the complex ions, [PtCl 6 ] 2– and [Ni(NH 3 ) 4 ] 2+ , the coordination number of Pt and Ni are 6 and 4 respectively. Similarly, in the complex ions, [Fe(C 2 O 4 ) 3 ] 3– and [Co(en) 3 ] 3+ , the coordination number of both, Fe and Co, is 6 because C 2 O 4 2– and en (ethane-1,2-diamine) are didentate ligands.','[{"s": "The coordination number (CN) of a metal ion in a complex can be defined as the number of ligand donor atoms to which the metal is directly bonded.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, in the complex ions, [PtCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [Ni(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the coordination number of Pt and Ni are 6 and 4 respectively. Similarly, in the complex ions, [Fe(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [Co(en)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the coordination number of both, Fe and Co, is 6 because C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and en (ethane-1,2-diamine) are didentate ligands.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231321"]',NULL),
(7913,40,49,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to note here that coordination number of the central atom/ion is determined only by the number of sigma bonds formed by the ligand with the central atom/ion. Pi bonds, if formed between the ligand and the central atom/ion, are not counted for this purpose.','[{"s": "It is important to note here that coordination number of the central atom/ion is determined only by the number of sigma bonds formed by the ligand with the central atom/ion. Pi bonds, if formed between the ligand and the central atom/ion, are not counted for this purpose.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7914,40,50,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Coordination sphere','[{"s": "(e) Coordination sphere", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7915,40,51,'paragraph',NULL,'The central atom/ion and the ligands attached to it are enclosed in square bracket and is collectively termed as the coordination sphere . The ionisable groups are written outside the bracket and are called counter ions. For example, in the complex K 4 [Fe(CN) 6 ], the coordination sphere is [Fe(CN) 6 ] 4– and the counter ion is K + .','[{"s": "The central atom/ion and the ligands attached to it are enclosed in square bracket and is collectively termed as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coordination sphere", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The ionisable groups are written outside the bracket and are called counter ions. For example, in the complex K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], the coordination sphere is [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the counter ion is K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7916,40,52,'paragraph',NULL,'(f) Coordination polyhedron','[{"s": "(f) Coordination polyhedron", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7917,40,53,'paragraph',NULL,'The spatial arrangement of the ligand atoms which are directly attached to the central atom/ion defines a coordination polyhedron about the central atom. The most common coordination polyhedra are octahedral, square planar and tetrahedral. For example, [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ is octahedral, [Ni(CO) 4 ] is tetrahedral and [PtCl 4 ] 2– is square planar. Fig. 9.1 shows the shapes of different coordination polyhedra.','[{"s": "The spatial arrangement of the ligand atoms which are directly attached to the central atom/ion defines a coordination polyhedron about the central atom. The most common coordination polyhedra are octahedral, square planar and tetrahedral. For example, [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is octahedral, [Ni(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is tetrahedral and [PtCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is square planar. Fig. 9.1 shows the shapes of different coordination polyhedra.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7918,40,54,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image28.png'),
(7919,40,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.1: Shapes of different coordination polyhedra. M represents the central atom/ion and L, a unidentate ligand.','[{"s": "Fig. 9.1: Shapes of different coordination polyhedra. M represents the central atom/ion and L, a unidentate ligand.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7920,40,56,'paragraph',NULL,'(g) Oxidation number of central atom','[{"s": "(g) Oxidation number of central atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7921,40,57,'paragraph',NULL,'The oxidation number of the central atom in a complex is defined as the charge it would carry if all the ligands are removed along with the electron pairs that are shared with the central atom. The oxidation number is represented by a Roman numeral in parenthesis following the name of the coordination entity. For example, oxidation number of copper in [Cu(CN) 4 ] 3– is +1 and it is written as Cu(I).','[{"s": "The oxidation number of the central atom in a complex is defined as the charge it would carry if all the ligands are removed along with the electron pairs that are shared with the central atom. The oxidation number is represented by a Roman numeral in parenthesis following the name of the coordination entity. For example, oxidation number of copper in [Cu(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is +1 and it is written as Cu(I).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7922,40,58,'paragraph',NULL,'(h) Homoleptic and heteroleptic complexes','[{"s": "(h) Homoleptic and heteroleptic complexes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7923,40,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Complexes in which a metal is bound to only one kind of donor groups, e.g. , [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ , are known as homoleptic. Complexes in which a metal is bound to more than one kind of donor groups, e.g. , [Co(NH 3 ) 4 Cl 2 ] + , are known as heteroleptic.','[{"s": "Complexes in which a metal is bound to only one kind of donor groups,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e.g.", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", are known as homoleptic. Complexes in which a metal is bound to more than one kind of donor groups,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e.g.", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", are known as heteroleptic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7924,40,60,'heading',3,'9.3 Nomenclature of Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7925,40,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Nomenclature is important in Coordination Chemistry because of the need to have an unambiguous method of describing formulas and writing systematic names, particularly when dealing with isomers. The formulas and names adopted for coordination entities are based on the recommendations of the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC).','[{"s": "Nomenclature is important in Coordination Chemistry because of the need to have an unambiguous method of describing formulas and writing systematic names, particularly when dealing with isomers. The formulas and names adopted for coordination entities are based on the recommendations of the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7926,40,62,'heading',4,'9.3.1 Formulas of Mononuclear Coordination Entities',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7927,40,63,'paragraph',NULL,'The formula of a compound is a shorthand tool used to provide basic information about the constitution of the compound in a concise and convenient manner. Mononuclear coordination entities contain a single central metal atom. The following rules are applied while writing the formulas:','[{"s": "The formula of a compound is a shorthand tool used to provide basic information about the constitution of the compound in a concise and convenient manner. Mononuclear coordination entities contain a single central metal atom. The following rules are applied while writing the formulas:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7928,40,64,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The central atom is listed first.','[{"s": "(i) The central atom is listed first.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7929,40,65,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The ligands are then listed in alphabetical order. The placement of a ligand in the list does not depend on its charge.','[{"s": "(ii) The ligands are then listed in alphabetical order. The placement of a ligand in the list does not depend on its charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7930,40,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Polydentate ligands are also listed alphabetically. In case of abbreviated ligand, the first letter of the abbreviation is used to determine the position of the ligand in the alphabetical order.','[{"s": "(iii) Polydentate ligands are also listed alphabetically. In case of abbreviated ligand, the first letter of the abbreviation is used to determine the position of the ligand in the alphabetical order.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7931,40,67,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) The formula for the entire coordination entity, whether charged or not, is enclosed in square brackets. When ligands are polyatomic, their formulas are enclosed in parentheses. Ligand abbreviations are also enclosed in parentheses.','[{"s": "(iv) The formula for the entire coordination entity, whether charged or not, is enclosed in square brackets. When ligands are polyatomic, their formulas are enclosed in parentheses. Ligand abbreviations are also enclosed in parentheses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7932,40,68,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) There should be no space between the ligands and the metal within a coordination sphere.','[{"s": "(v) There should be no space between the ligands and the metal within a coordination sphere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7933,40,69,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) When the formula of a charged coordination entity is to be written without that of the counter ion, the charge is indicated outside the square brackets as a right superscript with the number before the sign. For example, [Co(CN) 6 ] 3– , [Cr(H 2 O) 6 ] 3+ , etc.','[{"s": "(vi) When the formula of a charged coordination entity is to be written without that of the counter ion, the charge is indicated outside the square brackets as a right superscript with the number before the sign. For example, [Co(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Cr(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7934,40,70,'paragraph',NULL,'(vii) The charge of the cation(s) is balanced by the charge of the anion(s).','[{"s": "(vii) The charge of the cation(s) is balanced by the charge of the anion(s).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7935,40,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Note : The 2004 IUPAC draft recommends that ligands will be sorted alphabetically, irrespective of charge.','[{"s": "Note", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The 2004 IUPAC draft recommends that ligands will be sorted alphabetically, irrespective of charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7936,40,72,'heading',4,'9.3.2 Naming of Mononuclear Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7937,40,73,'paragraph',NULL,'The names of coordination compounds are derived by following the principles of additive nomenclature. Thus, the groups that surround the central atom must be identified in the name. They are listed as prefixes to the name of the central atom along with any appropriate multipliers. The following rules are used when naming coordination compounds:','[{"s": "The names of coordination compounds are derived by following the principles of additive nomenclature.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, the groups that surround the central atom must be identified in the name. They are listed as prefixes to the name of the central atom along with any appropriate multipliers. The following rules are used when naming coordination compounds:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231322"]',NULL),
(7938,40,74,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The cation is named first in both positively and negatively charged coordination entities.','[{"s": "(i) The cation is named first in both positively and negatively charged coordination entities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7939,40,75,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The ligands are named in an alphabetical order before the name of the central atom/ion. (This procedure is reversed from writing formula).','[{"s": "(ii) The ligands are named in an alphabetical order before the name of the central atom/ion. (This procedure is reversed from writing formula).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7940,40,76,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Names of the anionic ligands end in –o, those of neutral and cationic ligands are the same except aqua for H 2 O, ammine for NH 3 , carbonyl for CO and nitrosyl for NO. While writing the formula of coordination entity, these are enclosed in brackets ( ).','[{"s": "(iii) Names of the anionic ligands end in –o, those of neutral and cationic ligands are the same except aqua for H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, ammine for NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", carbonyl for CO and nitrosyl for NO. While writing the formula of coordination entity, these are enclosed in brackets ( ).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7941,40,77,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Prefixes mono, di, tri, etc., are used to indicate the number of the individual ligands in the coordination entity. When the names of the ligands include a numerical prefix, then the terms, bis , tris , tetrakis are used, the ligand to which they refer being placed in parentheses. For example, [NiCl 2 (PPh 3 ) 2 ] is named as dichloridobis(triphenylphosphine)nickel(II).','[{"s": "(iv) Prefixes mono, di, tri, etc., are used to indicate the number of the individual ligands in the coordination entity. When the names of the ligands include a numerical prefix, then the terms,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tris", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tetrakis", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are used, the ligand to which they refer being placed in parentheses. For example, [NiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(PPh", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is named as dichloridobis(triphenylphosphine)nickel(II).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7942,40,78,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Oxidation state of the metal in cation, anion or neutral coordination entity is indicated by Roman numeral in parenthesis.','[{"s": "(v) Oxidation state of the metal in cation, anion or neutral coordination entity is indicated by Roman numeral in parenthesis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7943,40,79,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) If the complex ion is a cation, the metal is named same as the element. For example, Co in a complex cation is called cobalt and Pt is called platinum. If the complex ion is an anion, the name of the metal ends with the suffix – ate. For example, Co in a complex anion, Coordination_Compounds-image23.png is called cobaltate. For some metals, the Latin names are used in the complex anions, e .g., ferrate for Fe.','[{"s": "(vi) If the complex ion is a cation, the metal is named same as the element. For example, Co in a complex cation is called cobalt and Pt is called platinum. If the complex ion is an anion, the name of the metal ends with the suffix – ate. For example, Co in a complex anion,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coordination_Compounds-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is called cobaltate. For some metals, the Latin names are used in the complex anions,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".g., ferrate for Fe.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7944,40,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Note: The 2004 IUPAC draft recommends that anionic ligands will end with–ido so that chloro would become chlorido, etc.','[{"s": "Note:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The 2004 IUPAC draft recommends that anionic ligands will end with–ido so that chloro would become chlorido, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7945,40,81,'paragraph',NULL,'(vii) The neutral complex molecule is named similar to that of the complex cation.','[{"s": "(vii) The neutral complex molecule is named similar to that of the complex cation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7946,40,82,'paragraph',NULL,'The following examples illustrate the nomenclature for coordination compounds.','[{"s": "The following examples illustrate the nomenclature for coordination compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7947,40,83,'paragraph',NULL,'1. [Cr(NH 3 ) 3 (H 2 O) 3 ]Cl 3 is named as:','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Cr(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is named as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7948,40,84,'paragraph',NULL,'triamminetriaquachromium(III) chloride','[{"s": "triamminetriaquachromium(III) chloride", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7949,40,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Explanation : The complex ion is inside the square bracket, which is a cation. The amine ligands are named before the aqua ligands according to alphabetical order. Since there are three chloride ions in the compound, the charge on the complex ion must be +3 (since the compound is electrically neutral). From the charge on the complex ion and the charge on the ligands, we can calculate the oxidation number of the metal. In this example, all the ligands are neutral molecules. Therefore, the oxidation number of chromium must be the same as the charge of the complex ion, +3.','[{"s": "Explanation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The complex ion is inside the square bracket, which is a cation. The amine ligands are named before the aqua ligands according to alphabetical order. Since there are three chloride ions in the compound, the charge on the complex ion must be +3 (since the compound is electrically neutral). From the charge on the complex ion and the charge on the ligands, we can calculate the oxidation number of the metal. In this example, all the ligands are neutral molecules. Therefore, the oxidation number of chromium must be the same as the charge of the complex ion, +3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7950,40,86,'paragraph',NULL,'2. [Co(H 2 NCH 2 CH 2 NH 2 ) 3 ] 2 (SO 4 ) 3 is named as:','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Co(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is named as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7951,40,87,'paragraph',NULL,'tris(ethane-1,2–diamine)cobalt(III) sulphate','[{"s": "tris(ethane-1,2–diamine)cobalt(III) sulphate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7952,40,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Explanation : The sulphate is the counter anion in this molecule. Since it takes 3 sulphates to bond with two complex cations, the charge on each complex cation must be +3. Further, ethane-1,2–diamine is a neutral molecule, so the oxidation number of cobalt in the complex ion must be +3. Remember that you never have to indicate the number of cations and anions in the name of an ionic compound.','[{"s": "Explanation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The sulphate is the counter anion in this molecule. Since it takes 3 sulphates to bond with two complex cations, the charge on each complex cation must be +3. Further, ethane-1,2–diamine is a neutral molecule, so the oxidation number of cobalt in the complex ion must be +3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Remember that you never have to indicate the number of cations and anions in the name of an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ionic compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7953,40,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Notice how the name of the metal differs in cation and anion even though they contain the same metal ions.','[{"s": "Notice how the name of the metal differs in cation and anion even though they contain the same metal ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7954,40,90,'paragraph',NULL,'3. [Ag(NH 3 ) 2 ][Ag(CN) 2 ] is named as:','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Ag(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "][Ag(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is named as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7955,40,91,'paragraph',NULL,'diamminesilver(I) dicyanidoargentate(I)','[{"s": "diamminesilver(I) dicyanidoargentate(I)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231323"]',NULL),
