(7988,40,124,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) [Pt(NH 3 ) 2 Cl(NH 2 CH 3 )]Cl','[{"s": "(vi) [Pt(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]Cl", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7989,40,125,'heading',3,'9.4 Isomerism in Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7990,40,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Isomers are two or more compounds that have the same chemical formula but a different arrangement of atoms. Because of the different arrangement of atoms, they differ in one or more physical or chemical properties. Two principal types of isomerism are known among coordination compounds. Each of which can be further subdivided.','[{"s": "Isomers are two or more compounds that have the same chemical formula but a different arrangement of atoms. Because of the different arrangement of atoms, they differ in one or more physical or chemical properties.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two principal types of isomerism are known among coordination compounds.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Each of which can be further subdivided.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231379"]',NULL),
(7991,40,127,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Stereoisomerism','[{"s": "(a) Stereoisomerism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7992,40,128,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Geometrical isomerism (ii) Optical isomerism','[{"s": "(i) Geometrical isomerism (ii) Optical isomerism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7993,40,129,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Structural isomerism','[{"s": "(b) Structural isomerism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7994,40,130,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Linkage isomerism (ii) Coordination isomerism','[{"s": "(i) Linkage isomerism (ii) Coordination isomerism", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231379"]',NULL),
(7995,40,131,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Ionisation isomerism (iv) Solvate isomerism','[{"s": "(iii) Ionisation isomerism (iv) Solvate isomerism", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7996,40,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Stereoisomers have the same chemical formula and chemical bonds but they have different spatial arrangement. Structural isomers have different bonds. A detailed account of these isomers are given below.','[{"s": "Stereoisomers have the same chemical formula and chemical bonds but they have different spatial arrangement.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structural isomers have different bonds.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A detailed account of these isomers are given below.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231325"]',NULL),
(7997,40,133,'heading',4,'9.4.1 Geometric Isomerism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(7998,40,134,'paragraph',NULL,'This type of isomerism arises in heteroleptic complexes due to different possible geometric arrangements of the ligands. 2 Important examples of this behaviour are found with coordination numbers 4 and 6. In a square planar complex of formula [MX 2 L 2 ] (X and L are unidentate), the two ligands X may be arranged adjacent to each other in a cis isomer, or opposite to each other in a trans isomer as depicted in Fig. 9.2.','[{"s": "This type of isomerism arises in heteroleptic complexes due to different possible geometric arrangements of the ligands.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Important examples of this behaviour are found with coordination numbers 4 and 6. In a square planar complex of formula [MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (X and L are unidentate), the two ligands X may be arranged adjacent to each other in a cis isomer, or opposite to each other in a trans isomer as depicted in Fig. 9.2.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231326", "231327"]',NULL),
(7999,40,135,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image8.png'),
(12239,44,1009,'paragraph',NULL,'Vinyl halides are the compounds where halogen atom is bonded directly to sp','[{"s": "Vinyl halides are the compounds where halogen atom is bonded directly to sp", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8000,40,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.2: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of Pt [NH 3 ) 2 Cl 2 ]','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9.2: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of Pt [NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231328"]',NULL),
(8001,40,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Other square planar complex of the type MABXL (where A, B, X, L are unidentates) shows three isomers-two cis and one trans . You may attempt to draw these structures. Such isomerism is not possible for a tetrahedral geometry but similar behaviour is possible in octahedral complexes of formula [MX 2 L 4 ] in which the two ligands X may be oriented cis or trans to each other (Fig. 9.3).','[{"s": "Other square planar complex of the type MABXL (where A, B, X, L are unidentates) shows three isomers-two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cis", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "trans", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". You may attempt to draw these structures. Such isomerism is not possible for a tetrahedral geometry but similar behaviour is possible in octahedral complexes of formula [MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] in which the two ligands X may be oriented cis or trans to each other (Fig. 9.3).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8002,40,138,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image30.png'),
(8003,40,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.3: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of [Co(NH 3 ) 4 Cl 2 ] +','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9.3: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of [Co(NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231329"]',NULL),
(8004,40,140,'paragraph',NULL,'This type of isomerism also arises when bidentate ligands L–L [ e.g., NH 2 CH 2 CH 2 NH 2 (en)] are present in complexes of formula [MX 2 (L–L) 2 ] (Fig. 9.4).','[{"s": "This type of isomerism also arises when bidentate ligands L–L [", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e.g.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(en)] are present in complexes of formula [MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(L–L)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (Fig. 9.4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8005,40,141,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image31.png'),
(8006,40,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.4: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of [CoCl 2 (en) 2 ]','[{"s": "Fig. 9.4: Geometrical isomers (cis and trans) of [CoCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(en)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8007,40,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Another type of geometrical isomerism occurs in octahedral coordination entities of the type [Ma 3 b 3 ] like [Co(NH 3 ) 3 (NO 2 ) 3 ]. 2 If three donor atoms of the same ligands occupy adjacent positions at the corners of an octahedral face, we have the facial (fac) isomer . When the positions are around the meridian of the octahedron, we get the meridional (mer) isomer (Fig. 9.5).','[{"s": "Another type of geometrical isomerism occurs in octahedral coordination entities of the type [Ma", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "b", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "] like [Co(NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(NO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "].", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If three donor atoms of the same ligands occupy adjacent positions at the corners of an octahedral face, we have the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "facial (fac) isomer", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When the positions are around the meridian of the octahedron, we get the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "meridional (mer) isomer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 9.5).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231305", "231330"]',NULL),
(8008,40,144,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image13.png'),
(8009,40,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.5 The facial (fac) and meridional (mer) isomers of [Co(NH 3 ) 3 (NO 2 ) 3 ]','[{"s": "Fig. 9.5 The facial (fac) and meridional (mer) isomers of [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8010,40,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.4','[{"s": "Example 9.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8011,40,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Why is geometrical isomerism not possible in tetrahedral complexes having two different types of unidentate ligands coordinated with the central metal ion ?','[{"s": "Why is geometrical isomerism not possible in tetrahedral complexes having two different types of unidentate ligands coordinated with the central metal ion ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8012,40,148,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8013,40,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometrical isomerism because the relative positions of the unidentate ligands attached to the central metal atom are the same with respect to each other.','[{"s": "Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometrical isomerism because the relative positions of the unidentate ligands attached to the central metal atom are the same with respect to each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
