(8035,40,171,'paragraph',NULL,'This type of isomerism arises from the interchange of ligands between cationic and anionic entities of different metal ions present in a complex. 2 An example is provided by [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ][Cr(CN) 6 ], in which the NH 3 ligands are bound to Co 3+ and the CN – ligands to Cr 3+ . In its coordination isomer [Cr(NH 3 ) 6 ][Co(CN) 6 ], the NH 3 ligands are bound to Cr 3+ and the CN – ligands to Co 3+ .','[{"s": "This type of isomerism arises from the interchange of ligands between cationic and anionic entities of different metal ions present in a complex.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "An example is provided by [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "][Cr(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], in which the NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ligands are bound to Co", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the CN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ligands to Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In its coordination isomer [Cr(NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "][Co(CN)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "], the NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ligands are bound to Cr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and the CN", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ligands to Co", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231365", "231367", "231368"]',NULL),
(8036,40,172,'heading',4,'9.4.5 Ionisation Isomerism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8037,40,173,'paragraph',NULL,'This form of isomerism arises when the counter ion in a complex salt is itself a potential ligand and can displace a ligand which can then become the counter ion. An example is provided by the ionisation isomers [Co(NH 3 ) 5 (SO 4 )]Br and [Co(NH 3 ) 5 Br]SO 4 .','[{"s": "This form of isomerism arises when the counter ion in a complex salt is itself a potential ligand and can displace a ligand which can then become the counter ion. An example is provided by the ionisation isomers [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]Br and [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br]SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8038,40,174,'heading',4,'9.4.6 Solvate Isomerism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8039,40,175,'paragraph',NULL,'This form of isomerism is known as ‘hydrate isomerism’ in case where water is involved as a solvent. This is similar to ionisation isomerism. Solvate isomers differ by whether or not a solvent molecule is directly bonded to the metal ion or merely present as free solvent molecules in the crystal lattice. An example is provided by the aqua complex [Cr(H 2 O) 6 ]Cl 3 (violet) and its solvate isomer [Cr(H 2 O) 5 Cl]Cl 2 .H 2 O (grey-green).','[{"s": "This form of isomerism is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘hydrate isomerism’", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in case where water is involved as a solvent. This is similar to ionisation isomerism. Solvate isomers differ by whether or not a solvent molecule is directly bonded to the metal ion or merely present as free solvent molecules in the crystal lattice. An example is provided by the aqua complex [Cr(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(violet) and its solvate isomer [Cr(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl]Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (grey-green).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8040,40,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8041,40,177,'paragraph',NULL,'9.3 Indicate the types of isomerism exhibited by the following complexes and draw the structures for these isomers:','[{"s": "9.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Indicate the types of isomerism exhibited by the following complexes and draw the structures for these isomers:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8042,40,178,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) K[Cr(H 2 O) 2 (C 2 O 4 ) 2 (ii) [Co(en) 3 ]Cl 3','[{"s": "(i) K[Cr(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ii) [Co(en)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8043,40,179,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) [Co(NH 3 ) 5 (NO 2 )](NO 3 ) 2 (iv) [Pt(NH 3 )(H 2 O)Cl 2 ]','[{"s": "(iii) [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")](NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(iv) [Pt(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8044,40,180,'paragraph',NULL,'9.4 Give evidence that [Co(NH 3 ) 5 Cl]SO 4 and [Co(NH 3 ) 5 (SO 4 )]Cl are ionisation isomers.','[{"s": "9.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Give evidence that [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl]SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]Cl are ionisation isomers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8045,40,181,'heading',3,'9.5 Bonding in Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8046,40,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Werner was the first to describe the bonding features in coordination compounds. But his theory could not answer basic questions like:','[{"s": "Werner was the first to describe the bonding features in coordination compounds. But his theory could not answer basic questions like:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8047,40,183,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Why only certain elements possess the remarkable property of forming coordination compounds?','[{"s": "(i) Why only certain elements possess the remarkable property of forming coordination compounds?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8048,40,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Why the bonds in coordination compounds have directional properties?','[{"s": "(ii) Why the bonds in coordination compounds have directional properties?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8049,40,185,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Why coordination compounds have characteristic magnetic and optical properties?','[{"s": "(iii) Why coordination compounds have characteristic magnetic and optical properties?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8050,40,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Many approaches have been put forth to explain the nature of bonding in coordination compounds viz. Valence Bond Theory (VBT), Crystal Field Theory (CFT), Ligand Field Theory (LFT) and Molecular Orbital Theory (MOT). We shall focus our attention on elementary treatment of the application of VBT and CFT to coordination compounds.','[{"s": "Many approaches have been put forth to explain the nature of bonding in coordination compounds", "t": "text"}, {"s": "viz.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Valence Bond Theory (VBT), Crystal Field Theory (CFT),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ligand Field Theory", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(LFT) and Molecular Orbital Theory (MOT). We shall focus our attention on elementary treatment of the application of VBT and CFT to coordination compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8051,40,187,'heading',4,'9.5.1 Valence Bond Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8052,40,188,'paragraph',NULL,'According to this theory, the metal atom or ion under the influence of ligands can use its (n-1) d , n s , n p or n s , n p , n d orbitals for hybridisation to yield a set of equivalent orbitals of definite geometry such as octahedral, tetrahedral, square planar and so on (Table 9.2). These hybridised orbitals are allowed to overlap with ligand orbitals that can donate electron pairs for bonding. This is illustrated by the following examples.','[{"s": "According to this theory, the metal atom or ion under the influence of ligands can use its (n-1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals for hybridisation to yield a set of equivalent orbitals of definite geometry such as octahedral, tetrahedral, square planar and so on (Table 9.2).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These hybridised orbitals are allowed to overlap with ligand orbitals that can donate electron pairs for bonding.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is illustrated by the following examples.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231369"]',NULL),
(8053,40,189,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 9.2: Number of Orbitals and Types of Hybridisations3','[{"s": "Table 9.2: Number of Orbitals and Types of Hybridisations3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231315", "231345", "231381"]',NULL),
(8054,40,190,'image',NULL,'9e',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image21.png'),
(8055,40,191,'paragraph',NULL,'It is usually possible to predict the geometry of a complex from the knowledge of its magnetic behaviour on the basis of the valence bond theory.','[{"s": "It is usually possible to predict the geometry of a complex from the knowledge of its magnetic behaviour on the basis of the valence bond theory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8056,40,192,'paragraph',NULL,'In the diamagnetic octahedral complex, [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ , the cobalt ion is in +3 oxidation state and has the electronic configuration 3 d 6 . The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram.','[{"s": "In the diamagnetic octahedral complex, [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the cobalt ion is in +3 oxidation state and has the electronic configuration 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8057,40,193,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image14.png'),
(8058,40,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Six pairs of electrons, one from each NH 3 molecule, occupy the six hybrid orbitals. Thus, the complex has octahedral geometry and is diamagnetic because of the absence of unpaired electron. 2 I n the formation of this complex, since the inner d orbital (3 d ) is used in hybridisation, the complex, [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ is called an inner orbital or low spin or spin paired complex . The paramagnetic octahedral complex, [CoF 6 ] 3– uses outer orbital (4 d ) in hybridisation ( sp 3 d 2 ). 3 It is thus called outer orbital or high spin or spin free complex . Thus:','[{"s": "Six pairs of electrons, one from each NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "molecule, occupy the six hybrid orbitals.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, the complex has octahedral geometry and is diamagnetic because of the absence of unpaired electron.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n the formation of this complex, since the inner", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is used in hybridisation, the complex, [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low spin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spin paired complex", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The paramagnetic octahedral complex, [CoF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "uses outer orbital (4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") in hybridisation (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is thus called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "outer orbital", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high spin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spin free complex", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231303", "231360", "231372", "231373", "231374", "231375"]',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(8059,40,195,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image25.png'),
(8060,40,196,'paragraph',NULL,'In tetrahedral complexes one s and three p orbitals are hybridised to form four equivalent orbitals oriented tetrahedrally. This is ill-ustrated below for [NiCl 4 ] 2- . Here nickel is in +2 oxidation state and the ion has the electronic configuration 3 d 8 . The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram.','[{"s": "In tetrahedral complexes one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and three", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are hybridised to form four equivalent orbitals oriented tetrahedrally. This is ill-ustrated below for [NiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2-", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here nickel is in +2 oxidation state and the ion has the electronic configuration 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8061,40,197,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image9.png'),
(8062,40,198,'paragraph',NULL,'Each Cl – ion donates a pair of electrons. The compound is paramagnetic since it contains two unpaired electrons. Similarly, [Ni(CO) 4 ] has tetrahedral geometry but is diamagnetic since nickel is in zero oxidation state and contains no unpaired electron. 2','[{"s": "Each Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion donates a pair of electrons. The compound is paramagnetic since it contains two unpaired electrons.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Similarly, [Ni(CO)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "] has tetrahedral geometry but is diamagnetic since nickel is in zero oxidation state and contains no unpaired electron.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231306", "231371"]',NULL),
(8063,40,199,'paragraph',NULL,'In the square planar complexes, the hybridisation involved is dsp 2 . An example is [Ni(CN) 4 ] 2– . Here nickel is in +2 oxidation state and has the electronic configuration 3 d 8 . The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram:','[{"s": "In the square planar complexes, the hybridisation involved is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "dsp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "An example is [Ni(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here nickel is in +2 oxidation state and has the electronic configuration 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The hybridisation scheme is as shown in diagram:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231370"]',NULL),
(8064,40,200,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image16.png'),
(8065,40,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Each of the hybridised orbitals receives a pair of electrons from a cyanide ion. The compound is diamagnetic as evident from the absence of unpaired electron.','[{"s": "Each of the hybridised orbitals receives a pair of electrons from a cyanide ion. The compound is diamagnetic as evident from the absence of unpaired electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8066,40,202,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to note that the hybrid orbitals do not actually exist. In fact, hybridisation is a mathematical manipulation of wave equation for the atomic orbitals involved.','[{"s": "It is important to note that the hybrid orbitals do not actually exist. In fact, hybridisation is a mathematical manipulation of wave equation for the atomic orbitals involved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8067,40,203,'heading',4,'9.5.2 Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8068,40,204,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnetic moment of coordination compounds can be measured by the magnetic susceptibility experiments. 3 The results can be used to obtain information about the number of unpaired electrons (page 228) and hence structures adopted by metal complexes.','[{"s": "The magnetic moment of coordination compounds can be measured by the magnetic susceptibility experiments.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The results can be used to obtain information about the number of unpaired electrons (page 228) and hence structures adopted by metal complexes.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231358", "231359", "231362", "231363"]',NULL),
(8069,40,205,'paragraph',NULL,'A critical study of the magnetic data of coordination compounds of metals of the first transition series reveals some complications. For metal ions with upto three electrons in the d orbitals, like Ti 3+ ( d 1 ); V 3+ ( d 2 ); Cr 3+ ( d 3 ); two vacant d orbitals are available for octahedral hybridisation with 4 s and 4 p orbitals. The magnetic behaviour of these free ions and their coordination entities is similar. When more than three 3 d electrons are present, the required pair of 3 d orbitals for octahedral hybridisation is not directly available (as a consequence of Hund’s rule). Thus, for d 4 (Cr 2+ , Mn 3+ ), d 5 (Mn 2+ , Fe 3+ ), d 6 (Fe 2+ , Co 3+ ) cases, a vacant pair of d orbitals results only by pairing of 3 d electrons which leaves two, one and zero unpaired electrons, respectively.','[{"s": "A critical study of the magnetic data of coordination compounds of metals of the first transition series reveals some complications.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For metal ions with upto three electrons in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals, like Ti", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "); V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "); Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "); two vacant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are available for octahedral hybridisation with 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. The magnetic behaviour of these free ions and their coordination entities is similar.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When more than three 3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "electrons are present, the required pair of 3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "orbitals for octahedral hybridisation is not directly available (as a consequence of Hund’s rule).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, for", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Cr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Mn", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "),", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Mn", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Fe", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "),", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Fe", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Co", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") cases, a vacant pair of", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "orbitals results only by pairing of 3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "electrons which leaves two, one and zero unpaired electrons, respectively.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231360", "231361", "231364"]',NULL),
(8070,40,206,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnetic data agree with maximum spin pairing in many cases, especially with coordination compounds containing d 6 ions. 2 However, with species containing d 4 and d 5 ions there are complications. [Mn(CN) 6 ] 3– has magnetic moment of two unpaired electrons while [MnCl 6 ] 3– has a paramagnetic moment of four unpaired electrons. [Fe(CN) 6 ] 3– has magnetic moment of a single unpaired electron while [FeF 6 ] 3– has a paramagnetic moment of five unpaired electrons. [CoF 6 ] 3– is paramagnetic with four unpaired electrons while [Co(C 2 O 4 ) 3 ] 3– is diamagnetic. This apparent anomaly is explained by valence bond theory in terms of formation of inner orbital and outer orbital coordination entities. [Mn(CN) 6 ] 3– , [Fe(CN) 6 ] 3– and [Co(C 2 O 4 ) 3 ] 3– are inner orbital complexes involving d 2 sp 3 hybridisation, the former two complexes are paramagnetic and the latter diamagnetic. On the other hand, [MnCl 6 ] 3– , [FeF 6 ] 3– and [CoF 6- ] 3– are outer orbital complexes involving sp 3 d 2 hybridisation and are paramagnetic corresponding to four, five and four unpaired electrons.','[{"s": "The magnetic data agree with maximum spin pairing in many cases, especially with coordination compounds containing", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "However, with species containing", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions there are complications. [Mn(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has magnetic moment of two unpaired electrons while [MnCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a paramagnetic moment of four unpaired electrons. [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has magnetic moment of a single unpaired electron while [FeF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a paramagnetic moment of five unpaired electrons. [CoF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is paramagnetic with four unpaired electrons while [Co(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is diamagnetic. This apparent anomaly is explained by valence bond theory in terms of formation of inner orbital and outer orbital coordination entities. [Mn(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and [Co(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are inner orbital complexes involving", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation, the former two complexes are paramagnetic and the latter diamagnetic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On the other hand, [MnCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", [FeF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and [CoF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6-", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "are outer orbital complexes involving", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sp", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hybridisation and are paramagnetic corresponding to four, five and four unpaired electrons.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231352", "231353", "231354"]',NULL),
(8071,40,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.7','[{"s": "Example 9.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8072,40,208,'paragraph',NULL,'The spin only magnetic moment of [MnBr 4 ] 2– is 5.9 BM. Predict the geometry of the complex ion ?','[{"s": "The spin only magnetic moment of [MnBr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 5.9 BM. Predict the geometry of the complex ion ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8073,40,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12252,44,1022,'paragraph',NULL,'Since In ethylidene chloride (H','[{"s": "Since In ethylidene chloride (H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12253,44,1023,'paragraph',NULL,'3','[{"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12254,44,1024,'paragraph',NULL,'3','[{"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12255,44,1025,'paragraph',NULL,'C—CHC!','[{"s": "C—CHC!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12256,44,1026,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12257,44,1027,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12258,44,1028,'paragraph',NULL,',) the two chlorine atoms are present on same carbon atom hence it is called gem-dihalide.','[{"s": ",) the two chlorine atoms are present on same carbon atom hence it is called gem-dihalide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8074,40,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the coordination number of Mn 2+ ion in the complex ion is 4, it will be either tetrahedral ( sp 3 hybridisation) or square planar ( dsp 2 hybridisation). But the fact that the magnetic moment of the complex ion is 5.9 BM, it should be tetrahedral in shape rather than square planar because of the presence of five unpaired electrons in the d orbitals.','[{"s": "Since the coordination number of Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion in the complex ion is 4, it will be either tetrahedral (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation) or square planar (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dsp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation). But the fact that the magnetic moment of the complex ion is 5.9 BM, it should be tetrahedral in shape rather than square planar because of the presence of five unpaired electrons in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8075,40,211,'heading',4,'9.5.3 Limitations of Valence Bond Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8076,40,212,'paragraph',NULL,'While the VB theory, to a larger extent, explains the formation, structures and magnetic behaviour of coordination compounds, it suffers from the following shortcomings:','[{"s": "While the VB theory, to a larger extent, explains the formation, structures and magnetic behaviour of coordination compounds, it suffers from the following shortcomings:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8077,40,213,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) It involves a number of assumptions.','[{"s": "(i) It involves a number of assumptions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8078,40,214,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) It does not give quantitative interpretation of magnetic data.','[{"s": "(ii) It does not give quantitative interpretation of magnetic data.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8079,40,215,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) It does not explain the colour exhibited by coordination compounds.','[{"s": "(iii) It does not explain the colour exhibited by coordination compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8080,40,216,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic or kinetic stabilities of coordination compounds.','[{"s": "(iv) It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic or kinetic stabilities of coordination compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8081,40,217,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) It does not make exact predictions regarding the tetrahedral and square planar structures of 4-coordinate complexes.','[{"s": "(v) It does not make exact predictions regarding the tetrahedral and square planar structures of 4-coordinate complexes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8082,40,218,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) It does not distinguish between weak and strong ligands.','[{"s": "(vi) It does not distinguish between weak and strong ligands.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8083,40,219,'heading',4,'9.5.4 Crystal Field Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8084,40,220,'paragraph',NULL,'The crystal field theory (CFT) is an electrostatic model which considers the metal-ligand bond to be ionic arising purely from electrostatic interactions between the metal ion and the ligand. Ligands are treated as point charges in case of anions or point dipoles in case of neutral molecules. The five d orbitals in an isolated gaseous metal atom/ion have same energy, i.e., they are degenerate. This degeneracy is maintained if a spherically symmetrical field of negative charges surrounds the metal atom/ion. However, when this negative field is due to ligands (either anions or the negative ends of dipolar molecules like NH 3 and H 2 O) in a complex, it becomes asymmetrical and the degeneracy of the d orbitals is lifted. It results in splitting of the d orbitals. The pattern of splitting depends upon the nature of the crystal field. Let us explain this splitting in different crystal fields.','[{"s": "The crystal field theory (CFT) is an electrostatic model which considers the metal-ligand bond to be ionic arising purely from electrostatic interactions between the metal ion and the ligand. Ligands are treated as point charges in case of anions or point dipoles in case of neutral molecules. The five", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in an isolated gaseous metal atom/ion have same energy,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "they are degenerate. This degeneracy is maintained if a spherically symmetrical field of negative charges surrounds the metal atom/ion. However, when this negative field is due to ligands (either anions or the negative ends of dipolar molecules like NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) in a complex, it becomes asymmetrical and the degeneracy of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals is lifted. It results in splitting of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. The pattern of splitting depends upon the nature of the crystal field. Let us explain this splitting in different crystal fields.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8085,40,221,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Crystal field splitting in octahedral coordination entities','[{"s": "(a) Crystal field splitting in octahedral coordination entities", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8086,40,222,'paragraph',NULL,'In an octahedral coordination entity with six ligands surrounding the metal atom/ion, there will be repulsion between the electrons in metal d orbitals and the electrons (or negative charges) of the ligands. Such a repulsion is more when the metal d orbital is directed towards the ligand than when it is away from the ligand. Thus, the d x2-y2','[{"s": "In an octahedral coordination entity with six ligands surrounding the metal atom/ion, there will be repulsion between the electrons in metal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals and the electrons (or negative charges) of the ligands. Such a repulsion is more when the metal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital is directed towards the ligand than when it is away from the ligand. Thus, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x2-y2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10549,43,535,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrogen ion by itself is a bare proton with very small size (~10 –15 m radius) and intense electric field, binds itself with the water molecule at one of the two available lone pairs on it giving H 3 O + . This species has been detected in many compounds (e.g., H 3 O + Cl – ) in the solid state. In aqueous solution the hydronium ion is further hydrated to give species like H 5 O 2 + , H 7 O 3 + and H 9 O 4 + . Similarly the hydroxyl ion is hydrated to give several ionic species like H 3 O 2 – , H 5 O 3 – and H 7 O 4 – etc.','[{"s": "Hydrogen ion by itself is a bare proton with very small size (~10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m radius) and intense electric field, binds itself with the water molecule at one of the two available lone pairs on it giving H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This species has been detected in many compounds (e.g., H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in the solid state. In aqueous solution the hydronium ion is further hydrated to give species like H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Similarly the hydroxyl ion is hydrated to give several ionic species like H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10550,43,536,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-15-14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image4.png'),
(10551,43,537,'heading',4,'7.10.2 The Brönsted-Lowry Acids and Bases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12259,44,1029,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 15 What is ‘A’ in the following reaction?','[{"s": "Q. 15 What is ‘A’ in the following reaction?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8087,40,223,'paragraph',NULL,'and d z2 orbitals which point towards the axes along the direction of the ligand will experience more repulsion and will be raised in energy; and the d xy , d yz and d xz orbitals which are directed between the axes will be lowered in energy relative to the average energy in the spherical crystal field. Thus, the degeneracy of the d orbitals has been removed due to ligand electron-metal electron repulsions in the octahedral complex to yield three orbitals of lower energy, t 2g set and two orbitals of higher energy, e g set. This splitting of the degenerate levels due to the presence of ligands in a definite geometry is termed as crystal field splitting and the energy separation is denoted by ∆ o (the subscript o is for octahedral) (Fig.9.8) . Thus, the energy of the two e g orbitals will increase by (3/5) ∆ o and that of the three t 2g will decrease by (2/5)∆ o .','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals which point towards the axes along the direction of the ligand will experience more repulsion and will be raised in energy; and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "xy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "yz", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "xz", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals which are directed between the axes will be lowered in energy relative to the average energy in the spherical crystal field. Thus, the degeneracy of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals has been removed due to ligand electron-metal electron repulsions in the octahedral complex to yield three orbitals of lower energy,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "set and two orbitals of higher energy,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "set.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This splitting of the degenerate levels due to the presence of ligands in a definite geometry is termed as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "crystal field splitting", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and the energy separation is denoted by ∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "o", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(the subscript o is for octahedral) (Fig.9.8)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, the energy of the two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals will increase by (3/5) ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and that of the three", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will decrease by (2/5)∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231351"]',NULL),
(8088,40,224,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image19.png'),
(8089,40,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.9.8: d orbital splitting in an octahedral crystal field','[{"s": "Fig.9.8: d orbital splitting in an octahedral crystal field", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8090,40,226,'paragraph',NULL,'The crystal field splitting, ∆ o , depends upon the field produced by the ligand and charge on the metal ion. Some ligands are able to produce strong fields in which case, the splitting will be large whereas others produce weak fields and consequently result in small splitting of d orbitals. In general, ligands can be arranged in a series in the order of increasing field strength as given below:','[{"s": "The crystal field splitting, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", depends upon the field produced by the ligand and charge on the metal ion. Some ligands are able to produce strong fields in which case, the splitting will be large whereas others produce weak fields and consequently result in small splitting of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. In general, ligands can be arranged in a series in the order of increasing field strength as given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8091,40,227,'paragraph',NULL,'I – < Br – < SCN – < Cl – < S 2– < F – < OH – < C 2 O 4 2– < H 2 O < NCS – < edta 4– < NH 3 < en < CN – < CO','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O < NCS", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< edta", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< en < CN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< CO", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8092,40,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Such a series is termed as spectrochemical series . 3 It is an experimentally determined series based on the absorption of light by complexes with different ligands. Let us assign electrons in the d orbitals of metal ion in octahedral coordination entities. Obviously, the single d electron occupies one of the lower energy t 2g orbitals. In d 2 and d 3 coordination entities, the d electrons occupy the t 2g orbitals singly in accordance with the Hund’s rule. For d 4 ions, two possible patterns of electron distribution arise: (i) the fourth electron could either enter the t 2g level and pair with an existing electron, or (ii) it could avoid paying the price of the pairing energy by occupying the e g level. Which of these possibilities occurs, depends on the relative magnitude of the crystal field splitting, ∆ o and the pairing energy, P (P represents the energy required for electron pairing in a single orbital). The two options are:','[{"s": "Such a series is termed as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "spectrochemical series", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is an experimentally determined series based on the absorption of light by complexes with different ligands. Let us assign electrons in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals of metal ion in octahedral coordination entities. Obviously, the single", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron occupies one of the lower energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coordination entities, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons occupy the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals singly in accordance with the Hund’s rule. For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions, two possible patterns of electron distribution arise: (i) the fourth electron could either enter the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level and pair with an existing electron, or (ii) it could avoid paying the price of the pairing energy by occupying the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level. Which of these possibilities occurs, depends on the relative magnitude of the crystal field splitting, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the pairing energy, P (P represents the energy required for electron pairing in a single orbital). The two options are:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231347", "231349", "231350"]',NULL),
(8093,40,229,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) If ∆ o < P, the fourth electron enters one of the e g orbitals giving the configuration Coordination_Compounds-image11.png . Ligands for which ∆ o < P are known as weak field ligands and form high spin complexes.','[{"s": "(i) If ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< P, the fourth electron enters one of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals giving the configuration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coordination_Compounds-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Ligands for which ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< P are known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "weak field ligands", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and form high spin complexes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12260,44,1030,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image92.png'),
(12261,44,1031,'paragraph',NULL,'(','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
