(12265,44,1035,'paragraph',NULL,'N','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8094,40,230,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) If ∆ o > P, it becomes more energetically favourable for the fourth electron to occupy a t 2g orbital with configuration t 2g 4 e g 0 . Ligands which produce this effect are known as strong field ligands and form low spin complexes.','[{"s": "(ii) If ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> P, it becomes more energetically favourable for the fourth electron to occupy a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital with configuration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ligands which produce this effect are known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "strong field ligands", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and form low spin complexes.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231348"]',NULL),
(8095,40,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Calculations show that d 4 to d 7 coordination entities are more stable for strong field as compared to weak field cases.','[{"s": "Calculations show that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coordination entities are more stable for strong field as compared to weak field cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8096,40,232,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Crystal field splitting in tetrahedral coordination entities','[{"s": "(b) Crystal field splitting in tetrahedral coordination entities", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8097,40,233,'paragraph',NULL,'In tetrahedral coordination entity formation, the d orbital splitting (Fig. 9.9) is inverted and is smaller as compared to the octahedral field splitting. For the same metal, the same ligands and metal-ligand distances, it can be shown that ∆ t = (4/9) ∆ 0 . Consequently, the orbital splitting energies are not sufficiently large for forcing pairing and, therefore, low spin configurations are rarely observed. The ‘g’ subscript is used for the octahedral and square planar complexes which have centre of symmetry. Since tetrahedral complexes lack symmetry, ‘g’ subscript is not used with energy levels.','[{"s": "In tetrahedral coordination entity formation, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital splitting (Fig. 9.9) is inverted and is smaller as compared to the octahedral field splitting. For the same metal, the same ligands and metal-ligand distances, it can be shown that ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (4/9) ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Consequently, the orbital splitting energies are not sufficiently large for forcing pairing and, therefore, low spin configurations are rarely observed. The ‘g’ subscript is used for the octahedral and square planar complexes which have centre of symmetry. Since tetrahedral complexes lack symmetry, ‘g’ subscript is not used with energy levels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8098,40,234,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image26.png'),
(8099,40,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.9.9: d orbital splitting in a tetrahedral crystal field.','[{"s": "Fig.9.9: d orbital splitting in a tetrahedral crystal field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8100,40,236,'heading',4,'9.5.5 Colour in Coordination Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8101,40,237,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous Unit, we learnt that one of the most distinctive properties of transition metal complexes is their wide range of colours. This means that some of the visible spectrum is being removed from white light as it passes through the sample, so the light that emerges is no longer white. The colour of the complex is complementary to that which is absorbed. The complementary colour is the colour generated from the wavelength left over; if green light is absorbed by the complex, it appears red. Table 9.3 gives the relationship of the different wavelength absorbed and the colour observed.','[{"s": "In the previous Unit, we learnt that one of the most distinctive properties of transition metal complexes is their wide range of colours.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This means that some of the visible spectrum is being removed from white light as it passes through the sample, so the light that emerges is no longer white. The colour of the complex is complementary to that which is absorbed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The complementary colour is the colour generated from the wavelength left over; if green light is absorbed by the complex, it appears red.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Table 9.3 gives the relationship of the different wavelength absorbed and the colour observed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231345", "231347"]',NULL),
(8102,40,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 9.3: Relationship between the Wavelength of Light absorbed and the Colour observed in some Coordination Entities','[{"s": "Table 9.3: Relationship between the Wavelength of Light absorbed and the Colour observed in some Coordination Entities", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231346"]',NULL),
(8103,40,239,'image',NULL,'9f',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image3.png'),
(8104,40,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The colour in the coordination compounds can be readily explained in terms of the crystal field theory. Consider, for example, the complex [Ti(H 2 O) 6 ] 3+ , which is violet in colour. This is an octahedral complex where the single electron (Ti 3+ is a 3 d 1 system) in the metal d orbital is in the t 2g level in the ground state of the complex. The next higher state available for the electron is the empty e g level. If light corresponding to the energy of blue-green region is absorbed by the complex, it would excite the electron from t 2g level to the e g level ( t 2g 1 e g 0 → t 2g 0 e g 1 ). Consequently, the complex appears violet in colour (Fig. 9.10). The crystal field theory attributes the colour of the coordination compounds to d-d transition of the electron. 4','[{"s": "The colour in the coordination compounds can be readily explained in terms of the crystal field theory. Consider, for example, the complex [Ti(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is violet in colour. This is an octahedral complex where the single electron (Ti", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "system) in the metal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital is in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level in the ground state of the complex. The next higher state available for the electron is the empty", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level. If light corresponding to the energy of blue-green region is absorbed by the complex, it would excite the electron from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Consequently, the complex appears violet in colour (Fig. 9.10).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The crystal field theory attributes the colour of the coordination compounds to", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d-d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "transition of the electron.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231342", "231343", "231344", "231345"]',NULL),
(8105,40,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image7.png'),
(8106,40,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.9.10: Transition of an electron in [Ti(H 2 O) 6 ] 3+','[{"s": "Fig.9.10: Transition of an electron in [Ti(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12266,44,1036,'paragraph',NULL,'1','[{"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8107,40,243,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to note that in the absence of ligand, crystal field splitting does not occur and hence the substance is colourless. For example, removal of water from [Ti(H 2 O) 6 ]Cl 3 on heating renders it colourless. Similarly, anhydrous CuSO 4 is white, but CuSO 4 .5H 2 O is blue in colour. The influence of the ligand on the colour of a complex may be illustrated by considering the [Ni(H 2 O) 6 ] 2+ complex, which forms when nickel(II) chloride is dissolved in water. If the didentate ligand, ethane-1,2-diamine(en) is progressively added in the molar ratios en:Ni, 1:1, 2:1, 3:1, the following series of reactions and their associated colour changes occur:','[{"s": "It is important to note that in the absence of ligand, crystal field splitting does not occur and hence the substance is colourless. For example, removal of water from [Ti(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on heating renders it colourless. Similarly, anhydrous CuSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is white, but CuSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".5H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O is blue in colour. The influence of the ligand on the colour of a complex may be illustrated by considering the [Ni(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "complex, which forms when nickel(II) chloride is dissolved in water. If the didentate ligand, ethane-1,2-diamine(en) is progressively added in the molar ratios en:Ni, 1:1, 2:1, 3:1, the following series of reactions and their associated colour changes occur:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8108,40,244,'paragraph',NULL,'[Ni(H 2 O) 6 ] 2+ (aq) + en (aq) = [Ni(H 2 O) 4 (en)] 2+ (aq) + 2H 2 O','[{"s": "[Ni(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + en (aq) = [Ni(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(en)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
