(8116,40,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.11 Aqueous solutions of complexes of nickel(II) with an increasing number of ethane-1, 2-diamine ligands.','[{"s": "Fig. 9.11 Aqueous solutions of complexes of nickel(II) with an increasing number of ethane-1, 2-diamine ligands.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8117,40,253,'paragraph',NULL,'Colour of Some Gem Stones','[{"s": "Colour of Some Gem Stones", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8118,40,254,'paragraph',NULL,'The colours produced by electronic transitions within the d orbitals of a transition metal ion occur frequently in everyday life. Ruby [Fig.9.12(a)] is aluminium oxide (Al 2 O 3 ) containing about 0.5-1% Cr 3+ ions ( d 3 ), which are randomly distributed in positions normally occupied by Al 3+ . We may view these chromium(III) species as octahedral chromium(III) complexes incorporated into the alumina lattice; d–d transitions at these centres give rise to the colour.','[{"s": "The colours produced by electronic transitions within the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals of a transition metal ion occur frequently in everyday life. Ruby [Fig.9.12(a)] is aluminium oxide (Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") containing about 0.5-1% Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), which are randomly distributed in positions normally occupied by Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We may view these chromium(III) species as octahedral chromium(III) complexes incorporated into the alumina lattice;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d–d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transitions at these centres give rise to the colour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8119,40,255,'paragraph',NULL,'In emerald [Fig.9.12(b)], Cr 3+ ions occupy octahedral sites in the mineral beryl (Be 3 Al 2 Si 6 O 18 ). The absorption bands seen in the ruby shift to longer wavelength, namely yellow-red and blue, causing emerald to transmit light in the green region.','[{"s": "In emerald [Fig.9.12(b)], Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions occupy octahedral sites in the mineral beryl (Be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The absorption bands seen in the ruby shift to longer wavelength, namely yellow-red and blue, causing emerald to transmit light in the green region.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8120,40,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Coordination_Compounds-image10.png (b) Coordination_Compounds-image29.png','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coordination_Compounds-image10.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coordination_Compounds-image29.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8121,40,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.9.12: (a) Ruby: this gemstone was found in marble from Mogok, Myanmar; (b) Emerald: this gemstone was found in Muzo, Columbia.','[{"s": "Fig.9.12: (a) Ruby: this gemstone was found in marble from Mogok, Myanmar; (b) Emerald: this gemstone was found in Muzo, Columbia.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8122,40,258,'heading',4,'9.5.6 Limitation of Crystal Field Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8123,40,259,'paragraph',NULL,'The crystal field model is successful in explaining the formation, structures, colour and magnetic properties of coordination compounds to a large extent. However, from the assumptions that the ligands are point charges, it follows that anionic ligands should exert the greatest splitting effect. The anionic ligands actually are found at the low end of the spectrochemical series. Further, it does not take into account the covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central atom. These are some of the weaknesses of CFT, which are explained by ligand field theory (LFT) and molecular orbital theory which are beyond the scope of the present study.','[{"s": "The crystal field model is successful in explaining the formation, structures, colour and magnetic properties of coordination compounds to a large extent. However, from the assumptions that the ligands are point charges, it follows that anionic ligands should exert the greatest splitting effect. The anionic ligands actually are found at the low end of the spectrochemical series. Further, it does not take into account the covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central atom. These are some of the weaknesses of CFT, which are explained by ligand field theory (LFT) and molecular orbital theory which are beyond the scope of the present study.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8124,40,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8125,40,261,'paragraph',NULL,'9.5 Explain on the basis of valence bond theory that [Ni(CN) 4 ] 2– ion with square planar structure is diamagnetic and the [NiCl 4 ] 2– ion with tetrahedral geometry is paramagnetic.','[{"s": "9.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain on the basis of valence bond theory that [Ni(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion with square planar structure is diamagnetic and the [NiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion with tetrahedral geometry is paramagnetic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8126,40,262,'paragraph',NULL,'9.6 [NiCl 4 ] 2– is paramagnetic while [Ni(CO) 4 ] is diamagnetic though both are tetrahedral. Why?','[{"s": "9.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[NiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is paramagnetic while [Ni(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is diamagnetic though both are tetrahedral. Why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8127,40,263,'paragraph',NULL,'9.7 [Fe(H 2 O) 6 ] 3+ is strongly paramagnetic whereas [Fe(CN) 6 ] 3– is weakly paramagnetic. Explain.','[{"s": "9.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fe(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is strongly paramagnetic whereas [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is weakly paramagnetic. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8128,40,264,'paragraph',NULL,'9.8 Explain [Co(NH 3 ) 6 ] 3+ is an inner orbital complex whereas [Ni(NH 3 ) 6 ] 2+ is an outer orbital complex.','[{"s": "9.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain [Co(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an inner orbital complex whereas [Ni(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an outer orbital complex.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8129,40,265,'paragraph',NULL,'9.9 Predict the number of unpaired electrons in the square planar [Pt(CN) 4 ] 2– ion.','[{"s": "9.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Predict the number of unpaired electrons in the square planar [Pt(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8130,40,266,'paragraph',NULL,'9.10 The hexaquo manganese(II) ion contains five unpaired electrons, while the hexacyanoion contains only one unpaired electron. Explain using Crystal Field Theory.','[{"s": "9.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The hexaquo manganese(II) ion contains five unpaired electrons, while the hexacyanoion contains only one unpaired electron. Explain using Crystal Field Theory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8131,40,267,'heading',3,'9.6 Bonding in Metal Carbonyls',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8132,40,268,'paragraph',NULL,'The homoleptic carbonyls (compounds containing carbonyl ligands only) are formed by most of the transition metals. These carbonyls have simple, well defined structures. Tetracarbonylnickel(0) is tetrahedral, pentacarbonyliron(0) is trigonalbipyramidal while hexacarbonyl chromium(0) is octahedral.','[{"s": "The homoleptic carbonyls (compounds containing carbonyl ligands only) are formed by most of the transition metals.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These carbonyls have simple, well defined structures.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Tetracarbonylnickel(0) is tetrahedral, pentacarbonyliron(0) is trigonalbipyramidal while hexacarbonyl chromium(0) is octahedral.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["231304"]',NULL),
(8133,40,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Decacarbonyldimanganese(0) is made up of two square pyramidal Mn(CO) 5 units joined by a Mn – Mn bond. Octacarbonyldicobalt(0) has a Co – Co bond bridged by two CO groups (Fig.9.13).','[{"s": "Decacarbonyldimanganese(0) is made up of two square pyramidal Mn(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "units joined by a Mn – Mn bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Octacarbonyldicobalt(0) has a Co – Co bond bridged by two CO groups (Fig.9.13).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231341"]',NULL),
(8134,40,270,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Coordination_Compounds-image22.png'),
(8135,40,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.13 Structures of some representative homoleptic metal carbonyls.','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9.13 Structures of some representative homoleptic metal carbonyls.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["231340"]',NULL),
