(8473,41,5,'paragraph',NULL,'After studying this Unit, you will be able to','[{"s": "After studying this Unit, you will be able to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8474,41,6,'paragraph',NULL,'The d- block of the periodic table contains the elements of the groups 3-12 in which the d orbitals are progressively filled in each of the four long periods. The f -block consists of elements in which 4 f and 5 f orbitals are progressively filled. They are placed in a separate panel at the bottom of the periodic table. The names transition metals and inner transition metals are often used to refer to the elements of d -and f -blocks respectively.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "block of the periodic table contains the elements of the groups 3-12 in which the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are progressively filled in each of the four long periods. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block consists of elements in which 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are progressively filled. They are placed in a separate panel at the bottom of the periodic table. The names", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transition metals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner transition", "t": "text"}, {"s": "metals are often used to refer to the elements of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-blocks respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8475,41,7,'paragraph',NULL,'There are mainly four series of the transition metals, 3 d series (Sc to Zn), 4 d series (Y to Cd), 5 d series (La and Hf to Hg) and 6 d series which has Ac and elements from Rf to Cn. The two series of the inner transition metals; 4 f (Ce to Lu) and 5 f (Th to Lr) are known as lanthanoids and actinoids respectively.','[{"s": "There are mainly four series of the transition metals, 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series (Sc to Zn), 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series (Y to Cd), 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series (La and Hf to Hg) and 6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series which has Ac and elements from Rf to Cn. The two series of the inner transition metals; 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Ce to Lu) and 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Th to Lr) are known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lanthanoids", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "actinoids", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8476,41,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Originally the name transition metals was derived from the fact that their chemical properties were transitional between those of s and p -block elements. Now according to IUPAC, transition metals are defined as metals which have incomplete d subshell either in neutral atom or in their ions. Zinc, cadmium and mercury of group 12 have full d 10 configuration in their ground state as well as in their common oxidation states and hence, are not regarded as transition metals. 3 However, being the end members of the 3 d , 4 d and 5 d transition series, respectively, their chemistry is studied along with the chemistry of the transition metals.','[{"s": "Originally the name transition metals was derived from the fact that their chemical properties were transitional between those of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block elements.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Now according to IUPAC, transition metals are defined as metals which have incomplete", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "subshell either in neutral atom or in their ions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Zinc, cadmium and mercury of group 12 have full", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "configuration in their ground state as well as in their common oxidation states and hence, are not regarded as transition metals.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "However, being the end members of the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transition series, respectively, their chemistry is studied along with the chemistry of the transition metals.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82438", "82474", "82475", "82476"]',NULL),
(12297,44,1067,'paragraph',NULL,'m','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12298,44,1068,'paragraph',NULL,'e','[{"s": "e", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12299,44,1069,'paragraph',NULL,'n','[{"s": "n", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12300,44,1070,'paragraph',NULL,'t','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12301,44,1071,'paragraph',NULL,'a','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8477,41,9,'paragraph',NULL,'The presence of partly filled d or f orbitals in their atoms makes transition elements different from that of the non-transition elements. Hence, transition elements and their compounds are studied separately. However, the usual theory of valence as applicable to the non-transition elements can be applied successfully to the transition elements also.','[{"s": "The presence of partly filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in their atoms makes transition elements different from that of the non-transition elements. Hence, transition elements and their compounds are studied separately. However, the usual theory of valence as applicable to the non-transition elements can be applied successfully to the transition elements also.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8478,41,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Various precious metals such as silver, gold and platinum and industrially important metals like iron, copper and titanium belong to the transition metals series.','[{"s": "Various precious metals such as silver, gold and platinum and industrially important metals like iron, copper and titanium belong to the transition metals series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8479,41,11,'paragraph',NULL,'In this Unit, we shall first deal with the electronic configuration, occurrence and general characteristics of transition elements with special emphasis on the trends in the properties of the first row (3 d ) transition metals along with the preparation and properties of some important compounds. This will be followed by consideration of certain general aspects such as electronic configurations, oxidation states and chemical reactivity of the inner transition metals.','[{"s": "In this Unit, we shall first deal with the electronic configuration, occurrence and general characteristics of transition elements with special emphasis on the trends in the properties of the first row (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") transition metals along with the preparation and properties of some important compounds. This will be followed by consideration of certain general aspects such as electronic configurations, oxidation states and chemical reactivity of the inner transition metals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8480,41,12,'paragraph',NULL,'THE TRANSITION ELEMENTS (d-BLOCK)','[{"s": "THE TRANSITION ELEMENTS (d-BLOCK)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8481,41,13,'heading',3,'8.1 Position in the Periodic Table',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8482,41,14,'paragraph',NULL,'The d –block occupies the large middle section of the periodic table flanked between s – and p – blocks in the periodic table. The d –orbitals of the penultimate energy level of atoms receive electrons giving rise to four rows of the transition metals, i.e., 3 d , 4 d , 5 d and 6 d . All these series of transition elements are shown in Table 8.1.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–block occupies the large middle section of the periodic table flanked between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– blocks in the periodic table. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–orbitals of the penultimate energy level of atoms receive electrons giving rise to four rows of the transition metals, i.e., 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". All these series of transition elements are shown in Table 8.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8483,41,15,'heading',3,'8.2 Electronic Configurations of the d-Block Elements',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8484,41,16,'paragraph',NULL,'In general the electronic configuration of outer orbitals of these elements is (n-1) d 1–10 n s 1–2 . The (n–1) stands for the inner d orbitals which may have one to ten electrons and the outermost n s orbital may have one or two electrons. However, this generalisation has several exceptions because of very little energy difference between (n-1) d and n s orbitals. Furthermore, half and completely filled sets of orbitals are relatively more stable. A consequence of this factor is reflected in the electronic configurations of Cr and Cu in the 3 d series. For example, consider the case of Cr, which has 3 d 5 4 s 1 configuration instead of 3 d 4 4 s 2 ; the energy gap between the two sets (3 d and 4 s ) of orbitals is small enough to prevent electron entering the 3 d orbitals. Similarly in case of Cu, the configuration is 3 d 10 4 s 1 and not 3 d 9 4 s 2 . The ground state electronic configurations of the outer orbitals of transition elements are given in Table 8.1.','[{"s": "In general the electronic configuration of outer orbitals of these elements is (n-1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The (n–1) stands for the inner", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals which may have one to ten electrons and the outermost n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital may have one or two electrons. However, this generalisation has several exceptions because of very little energy difference between (n-1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. Furthermore, half and completely filled sets of orbitals are relatively more stable. A consequence of this factor is reflected in the electronic configurations of Cr and Cu in the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series. For example, consider the case of Cr, which has 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration instead of 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; the energy gap between the two sets (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of orbitals is small enough to prevent electron entering the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. Similarly in case of Cu, the configuration is 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and not 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ground state electronic configurations of the outer orbitals of transition elements are given in Table 8.1.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82477"]',NULL),
(8485,41,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.1: Electronic Configurations of outer orbitals of the Transition Elements (ground state)','[{"s": "Table 8.1: Electronic Configurations of outer orbitals of the Transition Elements (ground state)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8486,41,18,'image',NULL,'8a',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image27.png'),
(8487,41,19,'image',NULL,'8b',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image25.png'),
(8488,41,20,'paragraph',NULL,'The electronic configurations of outer orbitals of Zn, Cd, Hg and Cn are represented by the general formula (n-1) d 10 n s 2 . The orbitals in these elements are completely filled in the ground state as well as in their common oxidation states. Therefore, they are not regarded as transition elements.','[{"s": "The electronic configurations of outer orbitals of Zn, Cd, Hg and Cn are represented by the general formula (n-1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The orbitals in these elements are completely filled in the ground state as well as in their common oxidation states. Therefore, they are not regarded as transition elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12302,44,1072,'paragraph',NULL,'t','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12303,44,1073,'paragraph',NULL,'i','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12304,44,1074,'paragraph',NULL,'o','[{"s": "o", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12305,44,1075,'paragraph',NULL,'n','[{"s": "n", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8489,41,21,'paragraph',NULL,'The d orbitals of the transition elements protrude to the periphery of an atom more than the other orbitals (i.e., s and p ), hence, they are more influenced by the surroundings as well as affect the atoms or molecules surrounding them. In some respects, ions of a given d n configuration (n = 1 – 9) have similar magnetic and electronic properties. With partly filled d orbitals these elements exhibit certain characteristic properties such as display of a variety of oxidation states, formation of coloured ions and entering into complex formation with a variety of ligands. 3','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals of the transition elements protrude to the periphery of an atom more than the other orbitals (i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), hence, they are more influenced by the surroundings as well as affect the atoms or molecules surrounding them. In some respects, ions of a given", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration (n = 1 – 9) have similar magnetic and electronic properties.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "With partly filled", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "orbitals these elements exhibit certain characteristic properties such as display of a variety of oxidation states, formation of coloured ions and entering into complex formation with a variety of ligands.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82439", "82478", "82479"]',NULL),
(8490,41,22,'paragraph',NULL,'The transition metals and their compounds also exhibit catalytic property and paramagnetic behaviour. All these characteristics have been discussed in detail later in this Unit.','[{"s": "The transition metals and their compounds also exhibit catalytic property and paramagnetic behaviour. All these characteristics have been discussed in detail later in this Unit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8491,41,23,'paragraph',NULL,'There are greater similarities in the properties of the transition elements of a horizontal row in contrast to the non-transition elements. However, some group similarities also exist. We shall first study the general characteristics and their trends in the horizontal rows (particularly 3 d row) and then consider some group similarities.','[{"s": "There are greater similarities in the properties of the transition elements of a horizontal row in contrast to the non-transition elements. However, some group similarities also exist. We shall first study the general characteristics and their trends in the horizontal rows (particularly 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "row) and then consider some group similarities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8492,41,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.1','[{"s": "Example 8.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8493,41,25,'paragraph',NULL,'On what ground can you say that scandium ( Z = 21) is a transition element but zinc ( Z = 30) is not?','[{"s": "On what ground can you say that scandium (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 21) is a transition element but zinc (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 30) is not?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8494,41,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8495,41,27,'paragraph',NULL,'On the basis of incompletely filled 3 d orbitals in case of scandium atom in its ground state (3 d 1 ), it is regarded as a transition element. On the other hand, zinc atom has completely filled d orbitals (3 d 10 ) in its ground state as well as in its oxidised state, hence it is not regarded as a transition element.','[{"s": "On the basis of incompletely filled 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in case of scandium atom in its ground state (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), it is regarded as a transition element. On the other hand, zinc atom has completely filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in its ground state as well as in its oxidised state, hence it is not regarded as a transition element.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8496,41,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8497,41,29,'paragraph',NULL,'8.1 Silver atom has completely filled d orbitals (4 d 10 ) in its ground state. How can you say that it is a transition element?','[{"s": "8.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Silver atom has completely filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals (4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in its ground state. How can you say that it is a transition element?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8498,41,30,'paragraph',NULL,'We will discuss the properties of elements of first transition series only in the following sections.','[{"s": "We will discuss the properties of elements of first transition series only in the following sections.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8499,41,31,'heading',3,'8.3 General Properties of the Transition Elements (d-Block)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8500,41,32,'heading',4,'8.3.1 Physical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8501,41,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Nearly all the transition elements display typical metallic properties such as high tensile strength, ductility, malleability, high thermal and electrical conductivity and metallic lustre. With the exceptions of Zn, Cd, Hg and Mn, they have one or more typical metallic structures at normal temperatures.','[{"s": "Nearly all the transition elements display typical metallic properties such as high tensile strength, ductility, malleability, high thermal and electrical conductivity and metallic lustre. With the exceptions of Zn, Cd, Hg and Mn, they have one or more typical metallic structures at normal temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8502,41,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Lattice Structures of Transition Metals','[{"s": "Lattice Structures of Transition Metals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8503,41,35,'image',NULL,'8c',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image1.png'),
(8504,41,36,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image30.png'),
(8505,41,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.1: Trends in melting points of transition elements','[{"s": "Fig. 8.1: Trends in melting points of transition elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12306,44,1076,'paragraph',NULL,'(','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12307,44,1077,'paragraph',NULL,'d','[{"s": "d", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12308,44,1078,'paragraph',NULL,')','[{"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12309,44,1079,'paragraph',NULL,'r','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12310,44,1080,'paragraph',NULL,'a','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12311,44,1081,'paragraph',NULL,'c','[{"s": "c", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12312,44,1082,'paragraph',NULL,'e','[{"s": "e", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8506,41,38,'paragraph',NULL,'The transition metals (with the exception of Zn, Cd and Hg) are very hard and have low volatility. Their melting and boiling points are high. Fig. 8.1 depicts the melting points of transition metals belonging to 3 d , 4 d and 5 d series. The high melting points of these metals are attributed to the involvement of greater number of electrons from (n-1) d in addition to the n s electrons in the interatomic metallic bonding. In any row the melting points of these metals rise to a maximum at d 5 except for anomalous values of Mn and Tc and fall regularly as the atomic number increases. They have high enthalpies of atomisation which are shown in Fig. 8.2. The maxima at about the middle of each series indicate that one unpaired electron per d orbital is particularly favourable for strong interatomic interaction. In general, greater the number of valence electrons, stronger is the resultant bonding. Since the enthalpy of atomisation is an important factor in determining the standard electrode potential of a metal, metals with very high enthalpy of atomisation (i.e., very high boiling point) tend to be noble in their reactions (see later for electrode potentials).','[{"s": "The transition metals (with the exception of Zn, Cd and Hg) are very hard and have low volatility. Their melting and boiling points are high. Fig. 8.1 depicts the melting points of transition metals belonging to 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series. The high melting points of these metals are attributed to the involvement of greater number of electrons from (n-1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in addition to the n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons in the interatomic metallic bonding. In any row the melting points of these metals rise to a maximum at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "except for anomalous values of Mn and Tc and fall regularly as the atomic number increases. They have high enthalpies of atomisation which are shown in Fig. 8.2. The maxima at about the middle of each series indicate that one unpaired electron per", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital is particularly favourable for strong interatomic interaction. In general, greater the number of valence electrons, stronger is the resultant bonding. Since the enthalpy of atomisation is an important factor in determining the standard electrode potential of a metal, metals with very high enthalpy of atomisation (i.e., very high boiling point) tend to be noble in their reactions (see later for electrode potentials).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8507,41,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Another generalisation that may be drawn from Fig. 8.2 is that the metals of the second and third series have greater enthalpies of atomisation than the corresponding elements of the first series; this is an important factor in accounting for the occurrence of much more frequent metal – metal bonding in compounds of the heavy transition metals.','[{"s": "Another generalisation that may be drawn from Fig. 8.2 is that the metals of the second and third series have greater enthalpies of atomisation than the corresponding elements of the first series; this is an important factor in accounting for the occurrence of much more frequent metal – metal bonding in compounds of the heavy transition metals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8508,41,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image17.png'),
(8509,41,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.2 Trends in enthalpies of atomisation of transition elements','[{"s": "Fig. 8.2 Trends in enthalpies of atomisation of transition elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8510,41,42,'heading',4,'8.3.2 Variation in Atomic and Ionic Sizes of Transition Metals',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8511,41,43,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive decrease in radius with increasing atomic number. This is because the new electron enters a d orbital each time the nuclear charge increases by unity. It may be recalled that the shielding effect of a d electron is not that effective, hence the net electrostatic attraction between the nuclear charge and the outermost electron increases and the ionic radius decreases. The same trend is observed in the atomic radii of a given series. However, the variation within a series is quite small. An interesting point emerges when atomic sizes of one series are compared with those of the corresponding elements in the other series. The curves in Fig. 8.3 show an increase from the first (3 d ) to the second (4 d ) series of the elements but the radii of the third (5 d ) series are virtually the same as those of the corresponding members of the second series. This phenomenon is associated with the intervention of the 4 f orbitals which must be filled before the 5 d series of elements begin. The filling of 4 f before 5 d orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number. The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third d series exhibit similar radii (e.g., Zr 160 pm, Hf 159 pm) and have very similar physical and chemical properties much more than that expected on the basis of usual family relationship.','[{"s": "In general, ions of the same charge in a given series show progressive decrease in radius with increasing atomic number. This is because the new electron enters a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital each time the nuclear charge increases by unity. It may be recalled that the shielding effect of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron is not that effective, hence the net electrostatic attraction between the nuclear charge and the outermost electron increases and the ionic radius decreases. The same trend is observed in the atomic radii of a given series. However, the variation within a series is quite small. An interesting point emerges when atomic sizes of one series are compared with those of the corresponding elements in the other series. The curves in Fig. 8.3 show an increase from the first (3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to the second (4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") series of the elements but the radii of the third (5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") series are virtually the same as those of the corresponding members of the second series. This phenomenon is associated with the intervention of the 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals which must be filled before the 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series of elements begin.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The filling of 4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "before 5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Lanthanoid contraction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The net result of the lanthanoid contraction is that the second and the third", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "series exhibit similar radii (e.g., Zr 160 pm, Hf 159 pm) and have very similar physical and chemical properties much more than that expected on the basis of usual family relationship.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82457", "82480"]',NULL),
(8512,41,44,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image15.png'),
(8513,41,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.3: Trends in atomic radii of transition elements','[{"s": "Fig. 8.3: Trends in atomic radii of transition elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8514,41,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The factor responsible for the lanthanoid contraction is somewhat similar to that observed in an ordinary transition series and is attributed to similar cause, i.e., the imperfect shielding of one electron by another in the same set of orbitals. However, the shielding of one 4 f electron by another is less than that of one d electron by another, and as the nuclear charge increases along the series, there is fairly regular decrease in the size of the entire 4 f n orbitals.','[{"s": "The factor responsible for the lanthanoid contraction is somewhat similar to that observed in an ordinary transition series and is attributed to similar cause, i.e., the imperfect shielding of one electron by another in the same set of orbitals. However, the shielding of one 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron by another is less than that of one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron by another, and as the nuclear charge increases along the series, there is fairly regular decrease in the size of the entire 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8515,41,47,'paragraph',NULL,'The decrease in metallic radius coupled with increase in atomic mass results in a general increase in the density of these elements. Thus, from titanium (Z = 22) to copper (Z = 29) the significant increase in the density may be noted (Table 8.2).','[{"s": "The decrease in metallic radius coupled with increase in atomic mass results in a general increase in the density of these elements. Thus, from titanium (Z = 22) to copper (Z = 29) the significant increase in the density may be noted (Table 8.2).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8516,41,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.2: Electronic Configurations and some other Properties of the First Series of Transition Elements3','[{"s": "Table 8.2: Electronic Configurations and some other Properties of the First Series of Transition Elements3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82416", "82426", "82434"]',NULL),
(8517,41,49,'image',NULL,'8d',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image9.png'),
(8518,41,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.2','[{"s": "Example 8.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8519,41,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Why do the transition elements exhibit higher enthalpies of atomisation?','[{"s": "Why do the transition elements exhibit higher enthalpies of atomisation?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8520,41,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8521,41,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Because of large number of unpaired electrons in their atoms they have stronger interatomic interaction and hence stronger bonding between atoms resulting in higher enthalpies of atomisation.','[{"s": "Because of large number of unpaired electrons in their atoms they have stronger interatomic interaction and hence stronger bonding between atoms resulting in higher enthalpies of atomisation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8522,41,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8523,41,55,'paragraph',NULL,'8.2 In the series Sc ( Z = 21) to Zn ( Z = 30), the enthalpy of atomisation of zinc is the lowest, i.e., 126 kJ mol –1 . Why?','[{"s": "8.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the series Sc (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 21) to Zn (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 30), the enthalpy of atomisation of zinc is the lowest, i.e., 126 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8524,41,56,'heading',4,'8.3.3 Ionisation Enthalpies',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8525,41,57,'paragraph',NULL,'There is an increase in ionisation enthalpy along each series of the transition elements from left to right due to an increase in nuclear charge which accompanies the filling of the inner d orbitals. Table 8.2 gives the values of the first three ionisation enthalpies of the first series of transition elements. These values show that the successive enthalpies of these elements do not increase as steeply as in the case of non-transition elements. The variation in ionisation enthalpy along a series of transition elements is much less in comparison to the variation along a period of non-transition elements. The first ionisation enthalpy, in general, increases, but the magnitude of the increase in the second and third ionisation enthalpies for the successive elements, is much higher along a series.','[{"s": "There is an increase in ionisation enthalpy along each series of the transition elements from left to right due to an increase in nuclear charge which accompanies the filling of the inner", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. Table 8.2 gives the values of the first three ionisation enthalpies of the first series of transition elements. These values show that the successive enthalpies of these elements do not increase as steeply as in the case of non-transition elements. The variation in ionisation enthalpy along a series of transition elements is much less in comparison to the variation along a period of non-transition elements. The first ionisation enthalpy, in general, increases, but the magnitude of the increase in the second and third ionisation enthalpies for the successive elements, is much higher along a series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8526,41,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The irregular trend in the first ionisation enthalpy of the metals of 3 d series, though of little chemical significance, can be accounted for by considering that the removal of one electron alters the relative energies of 4 s and 3 d orbitals. You have learnt that when d -block elements form ions, ns electrons are lost before ( n – 1) d electrons. As we move along the period in 3 d series, we see that nuclear charge increases from scandium to zinc but electrons are added to the orbital of inner subshell, i.e., 3 d orbitals. These 3 d electrons shield the 4 s electrons from the increasing nuclear charge somewhat more effectively than the outer shell electrons can shield one another. Therefore, the atomic radii decrease less rapidly. Thus, ionization energies increase only slightly along the 3 d series. The doubly or more highly charged ions have d n configurations with no 4 s electrons. A general trend of increasing values of second ionisation enthalpy is expected as the effective nuclear charge increases because one d electron does not shield another electron from the influence of nuclear charge because d -orbitals differ in direction. However, the trend of steady increase in second and third ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Mn 2+ and Fe 3+ respectively. In both the cases, ions have d 5 configuration. Similar breaks occur at corresponding elements in the later transition series.','[{"s": "The irregular trend in the first ionisation enthalpy of the metals of 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series, though of little chemical significance, can be accounted for by considering that the removal of one electron alters the relative energies of 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. You have learnt that when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block elements form ions,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons are lost before (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons. As we move along the period in 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series, we see that nuclear charge increases from scandium to zinc but electrons are added to the orbital of inner subshell, i.e., 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals. These 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons shield the 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons from the increasing nuclear charge somewhat more effectively than the outer shell electrons can shield one another. Therefore, the atomic radii decrease less rapidly. Thus, ionization energies increase only slightly along the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series. The doubly or more highly charged ions have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configurations with no 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons. A general trend of increasing values of second ionisation enthalpy is expected as the effective nuclear charge increases because one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron does not shield another electron from the influence of nuclear charge because", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-orbitals differ in direction. However, the trend of steady increase in second and third ionisation enthalpy breaks for the formation of Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. In both the cases, ions have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration. Similar breaks occur at corresponding elements in the later transition series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8527,41,59,'paragraph',NULL,'The interpretation of variation in ionisation enthalpy for an electronic configuration d n is as follows:','[{"s": "The interpretation of variation in ionisation enthalpy for an electronic configuration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n is as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8528,41,60,'paragraph',NULL,'The three terms responsible for the value of ionisation enthalpy are attraction of each electron towards nucleus, repulsion between the electrons and the exchange energy. Exchange energy is responsible for the stabilisation of energy state. Exchange energy is approximately proportional to the total number of possible pairs of parallel spins in the degenerate orbitals. When several electrons occupy a set of degenerate orbitals, the lowest energy state corresponds to the maximum possible extent of single occupation of orbital and parallel spins (Hunds rule). The loss of exchange energy increases the stability. As the stability increases, the ionisation becomes more difficult. There is no loss of exchange energy at d 6 configuration. Mn + has 3 d 5 4 s 1 configuration and configuration of Cr + is d 5 , therefore, ionisation enthalpy of Mn + is lower than Cr + . In the same way, Fe 2+ has d 6 configuration and Mn2+ has 3 d 5 configuration. Hence, ionisation enthalpy of Fe 2+ is lower than the Mn 2+ . In other words, we can say that the third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is lower than that of Mn.','[{"s": "The three terms responsible for the value of ionisation enthalpy are attraction of each electron towards nucleus, repulsion between the electrons and the exchange energy. Exchange energy is responsible for the stabilisation of energy state. Exchange energy is approximately proportional to the total number of possible pairs of parallel spins in the degenerate orbitals. When several electrons occupy a set of degenerate orbitals, the lowest energy state corresponds to the maximum possible extent of single occupation of orbital and parallel spins (Hunds rule). The loss of exchange energy increases the stability. As the stability increases, the ionisation becomes more difficult. There is no loss of exchange energy at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration. Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration and configuration of Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", therefore, ionisation enthalpy of Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is lower than Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the same way, Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration and Mn2+ has 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration. Hence, ionisation enthalpy of Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is lower than the Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In other words, we can say that the third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is lower than that of Mn.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82428"]',NULL),
(8529,41,61,'paragraph',NULL,'The lowest common oxidation state of these metals is +2. To form the M 2+ ions from the gaseous atoms, the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpy is required in addition to the enthalpy of atomisation. The dominant term is the second ionisation enthalpy which shows unusually high values for Cr and Cu where M + ions have the d 5 and d 10 configurations respectively. The value for Zn is correspondingly low as the ionisation causes the removal of 1 s electron which results in the formation of stable d 10 configuration. The trend in the third ionisation enthalpies is not complicated by the 4 s orbital factor and shows the greater difficulty of removing an electron from the d 5 (Mn 2+ ) and d 10 (Zn 2+ ) ions. In general, the third ionisation enthalpies are quite high. Also the high values for third ionisation enthalpies of copper, nickel and zinc indicate why it is difficult to obtain oxidation state greater than two for these elements.','[{"s": "The lowest common oxidation state of these metals is +2. To form the M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions from the gaseous atoms, the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpy is required in addition to the enthalpy of atomisation. The dominant term is the second ionisation enthalpy which shows unusually high values for Cr and Cu where M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions have the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configurations respectively. The value for Zn is correspondingly low as the ionisation causes the removal of 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron which results in the formation of stable", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The trend in the third ionisation enthalpies is not complicated by the 4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "s", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "orbital factor and shows the greater difficulty of removing an electron from the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Mn", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "d", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Zn", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") ions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In general, the third ionisation enthalpies are quite high. Also the high values for third ionisation enthalpies of copper, nickel and zinc indicate why it is difficult to obtain oxidation state greater than two for these elements.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82428"]',NULL),
(8530,41,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Although ionisation enthalpies give some guidance concerning the relative stabilities of oxidation states, this problem is very complex and not amenable to ready generalisation.','[{"s": "Although ionisation enthalpies give some guidance concerning the relative stabilities of oxidation states, this problem is very complex and not amenable to ready generalisation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8531,41,63,'heading',4,'8.3.4 Oxidation States',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8532,41,64,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the notable features of a transition elements is the great variety of oxidation states these may show in their compounds. Table 8.3 lists the common oxidation states of the first row transition elements.','[{"s": "One of the notable features of a transition elements is the great variety of oxidation states these may show in their compounds. Table 8.3 lists the common oxidation states of the first row transition elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8533,41,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.3: Oxidation States of the first row Transition Metals','[{"s": "Table 8.3: Oxidation States of the first row Transition Metals", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82476"]',NULL),
(8534,41,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(the most common ones are in bold types)','[{"s": "(the most common ones are in bold types)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8535,41,67,'image',NULL,'8e',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image23.png'),
(8536,41,68,'paragraph',NULL,'The elements which give the greatest number of oxidation states occur in or near the middle of the series. Manganese, for example, exhibits all the oxidation states from +2 to +7. The lesser number of oxidation states at the extreme ends stems from either too few electrons to lose or share (Sc, Ti) or too many d electrons (hence fewer orbitals available in which to share electrons with others) for higher valence (Cu, Zn). Thus, early in the series scandium(II) is virtually unknown and titanium (IV) is more stable than Ti(III) or Ti(II). At the other end, the only oxidation state of zinc is +2 (no d electrons are involved). The maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the s and d electrons upto manganese (Ti IV O 2 , V V O 2 + , Cr V1 O 4 2– , Mn VII O 4 – ) followed by a rather abrupt decrease in stability of higher oxidation states, so that the typical species to follow are Fe II,III , Co II,III , Ni II , Cu I,II , Zn II .','[{"s": "The elements which give the greatest number of oxidation states occur in or near the middle of the series. Manganese, for example, exhibits all the oxidation states from +2 to +7. The lesser number of oxidation states at the extreme ends stems from either too few electrons to lose or share (Sc, Ti) or too many", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons (hence fewer orbitals available in which to share electrons with others) for higher valence (Cu, Zn). Thus, early in the series scandium(II) is virtually unknown and titanium (IV) is more stable than Ti(III) or Ti(II). At the other end, the only oxidation state of zinc is +2 (no", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons are involved). The maximum oxidation states of reasonable stability correspond in value to the sum of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons upto manganese (Ti", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "VII", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") followed by a rather abrupt decrease in stability of higher oxidation states, so that the typical species to follow are Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II,III", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Co", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II,III", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Ni", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I,II", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8537,41,69,'paragraph',NULL,'The variability of oxidation states, a characteristic of transition elements, arises out of incomplete filling of d orbitals in such a way that their oxidation states differ from each other by unity, e.g., V II , V III , V IV , V V . This is in contrast with the variability of oxidation states of non transition elements where oxidation states normally differ by a unit of two.','[{"s": "The variability of oxidation states, a characteristic of transition elements, arises out of incomplete filling of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in such a way that their oxidation states differ from each other by unity, e.g., V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "III", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IV", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is in contrast with the variability of oxidation states of non transition elements where oxidation states normally differ by a unit of two.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82438"]',NULL),
(8538,41,70,'paragraph',NULL,'An interesting feature in the variability of oxidation states of the d –block elements is noticed among the groups (groups 4 through 10). Although in the p –block the lower oxidation states are favoured by the heavier members (due to inert pair effect), the opposite is true in the groups of d -block. For example, in group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are found to be more stable than Cr(VI). Thus Cr(VI) in the form of dichromate in acidic medium is a strong oxidising agent, whereas MoO 3 and WO 3 are not.','[{"s": "An interesting feature in the variability of oxidation states of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–block elements is noticed among the groups (groups 4 through 10). Although in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–block the lower oxidation states are favoured by the heavier members (due to inert pair effect), the opposite is true in the groups of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block. For example, in group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are found to be more stable than Cr(VI). Thus Cr(VI) in the form of dichromate in acidic medium is a strong oxidising agent, whereas MoO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and WO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are not.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8539,41,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Low oxidation states are found when a complex compound has ligands capable of π-acceptor character in addition to the σ-bonding. For example, in Ni(CO) 4 and Fe(CO) 5 , the oxidation state of nickel and iron is zero.','[{"s": "Low oxidation states are found when a complex compound has ligands capable of π-acceptor character in addition to the σ-bonding.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, in Ni(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Fe(CO)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the oxidation state of nickel and iron is zero.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82423"]',NULL),
(8540,41,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.3','[{"s": "Example 8.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8541,41,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Name a transition element which does not exhibit variable oxidation states.','[{"s": "Name a transition element which does not exhibit variable oxidation states.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8542,41,74,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8543,41,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Scandium ( Z = 21) does not exhibit variable oxidation states.','[{"s": "Scandium (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 21) does not exhibit variable oxidation states.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8544,41,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8545,41,77,'paragraph',NULL,'8.3 Which of the 3 d series of the transition metals exhibits the largest number of oxidation states and why?','[{"s": "8.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Which of the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series of the transition metals exhibits the largest number of oxidation states and why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
