(8547,41,79,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.4 contains the thermochemical parameters related to the transformation of the solid metal atoms to M 2+ ions in solution and their standard electrode potentials. The observed values of E Ɵ and those calculated using the data of Table 8.4 are compared in Fig. 8.4.','[{"s": "Table 8.4 contains the thermochemical parameters related to the transformation of the solid metal atoms to M", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ions in solution and their standard electrode potentials.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The observed values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and those calculated using the data of Table 8.4 are compared in Fig. 8.4.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82481"]',NULL),
(8548,41,80,'paragraph',NULL,'The unique behaviour of Cu, having a positive E Ɵ , accounts for its inability to liberate H 2 from acids. Only oxidising acids (nitric and hot concentrated sulphuric) react with Cu, the acids being reduced. The high energy to transform Cu(s) to Cu 2+ (aq) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy. The general trend towards less negative E Ɵ values across the series is related to the general increase in the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpies. It is interesting to note that the value of E Ɵ for Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the trend.','[{"s": "The unique behaviour of Cu, having a positive", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", accounts for its inability to liberate H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from acids. Only oxidising acids (nitric and hot concentrated sulphuric) react with Cu, the acids being reduced.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The high energy to transform Cu(s) to Cu", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(aq) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The general trend towards less negative", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values across the series is related to the general increase in the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpies. It is interesting to note that the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the trend.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["82482"]',NULL),
