(8560,41,92,'paragraph',NULL,'The stability of the half-filled d sub-shell in Mn 2+ and the completely filled d 10 configuration in Zn 2+ are related to their E Ɵ values, whereas E Ɵ for Ni is related to the highest negative ∆ hyd H Ɵ .','[{"s": "The stability of the half-filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sub-shell in Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the completely filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration in Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are related to their", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values, whereas", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for Ni is related to the highest negative ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8561,41,93,'heading',4,'8.3.6 Trends in the M3+/M2+ Standard Electrode Potentials',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12314,44,1084,'paragraph',NULL,'i','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8562,41,94,'paragraph',NULL,'An examination of the E Ɵ (M 3+ /M 2+ ) values (Table 8.2) shows the varying trends. The low value for Sc reflects the stability of Sc 3+ which has a noble gas configuration. The highest value for Zn is due to the removal of an electron from the stable d 10 configuration of Zn 2+ . The comparatively high value for Mn shows that Mn 2+ ( d 5 ) is particularly stable, whereas comparatively low value for Fe shows the extra stability of Fe 3+ ( d 5 ). The comparatively low value for V is related to the stability of V 2+ (half-filled t 2g level, Unit 9).','[{"s": "An examination of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") values (Table 8.2) shows the varying trends. The low value for Sc reflects the stability of Sc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which has a noble gas configuration. The highest value for Zn is due to the removal of an electron from the stable", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration of Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The comparatively high value for Mn shows that Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is particularly stable, whereas comparatively low value for Fe shows the extra stability of Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The comparatively low value for V is related to the stability of V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(half-filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "level, Unit 9).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8563,41,95,'heading',4,'8.3.7 Trends in Stability of Higher Oxidation States',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8564,41,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.5 shows the stable halides of the 3 d series of transition metals. The highest oxidation numbers are achieved in TiX 4 (tetrahalides), VF 5 and CrF 6 . The +7 state for Mn is not represented in simple halides but MnO 3 F is known, and beyond Mn no metal has a trihalide except FeX 3 and CoF 3 . The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to either higher lattice energy as in the case of CoF 3 , or higher bond enthalpy terms for the higher covalent compounds, e.g., VF 5 and CrF 6 .','[{"s": "Table 8.5 shows the stable halides of the 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "series of transition metals. The highest oxidation numbers are achieved in TiX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(tetrahalides), VF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CrF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The +7 state for Mn is not represented in simple halides but MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F is known, and beyond Mn no metal has a trihalide except FeX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CoF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The ability of fluorine to stabilise the highest oxidation state is due to either higher lattice energy as in the case of CoF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", or higher bond enthalpy terms for the higher covalent compounds, e.g., VF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CrF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8565,41,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.5: Formulas of Halides of 3 d Metals','[{"s": "Table 8.5: Formulas of Halides of 3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Metals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8566,41,98,'image',NULL,'8g',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image3.png'),
(8567,41,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Although V +5 is represented only by VF 5 , the other halides, however, undergo hydrolysis to give oxohalides, VOX 3 . Another feature of fluorides is their instability in the low oxidation states e.g., VX 2 (X = CI, Br or I) and the same applies to CuX. On the other hand, all Cu II halides are known except the iodide. In this case, Cu 2+ oxidises I – to I 2 :','[{"s": "Although V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is represented only by VF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the other halides, however, undergo hydrolysis to give oxohalides, VOX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Another feature of fluorides is their instability in the low oxidation states e.g., VX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(X = CI, Br or I) and the same applies to CuX. On the other hand, all Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "II", "t": "text"}, {"s": "halides are known except the iodide. In this case, Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "oxidises I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8568,41,100,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/D_and_F_Block_Elements-image34.png'),
(8569,41,101,'paragraph',NULL,'However, many copper (I) compounds are unstable in aqueous solution and undergo disproportionation.','[{"s": "However, many copper (I) compounds are unstable in aqueous solution and undergo disproportionation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8570,41,102,'paragraph',NULL,'2Cu + → Cu 2+ + Cu','[{"s": "2Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Cu", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8571,41,103,'paragraph',NULL,'The stability of Cu 2+ (aq) rather than Cu + (aq) is due to the much more negative ∆ hyd H Ɵ of Cu 2+ (aq) than Cu + , which more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.','[{"s": "The stability of Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) rather than Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) is due to the much more negative ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) than Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10552,43,538,'paragraph',NULL,'The Danish chemist, Johannes Brönsted and the English chemist, Thomas M. Lowry gave a more general definition of acids and bases. According to Brönsted-Lowry theory, acid is a substance that is capable of donating a hydrogen ion H + and bases are substances capable of accepting a hydrogen ion, H+ . In short, acids are proton donors and bases are proton acceptors.','[{"s": "The Danish chemist, Johannes Brönsted and the English chemist, Thomas M. Lowry gave a more general definition of acids and bases. According to Brönsted-Lowry theory,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acid is a substance that is capable of donating a hydrogen ion H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and bases are substances capable of accepting a hydrogen ion, H+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In short, acids are proton donors and bases are proton acceptors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10553,43,539,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the example of dissolution of NH 3 in H 2 O represented by the following equation:','[{"s": "Consider the example of dissolution of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O represented by the following equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10554,43,540,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-17-40',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image2.png'),
(12315,44,1085,'paragraph',NULL,'s','[{"s": "s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12316,44,1086,'paragraph',NULL,'a','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8572,41,104,'paragraph',NULL,'The ability of oxygen to stabilise the highest oxidation state is demonstrated in the oxides. The highest oxidation number in the oxides (Table 8.6) coincides with the group number and is attained in Sc 2 O 3 to Mn 2 O 7 . Beyond Group 7, no higher oxides of Fe above Fe 2 O 3 , are known, although ferrates (VI)(FeO 4 ) 2– , are formed in alkaline media but they readily decompose to Fe 2 O 3 and O 2 . Besides the oxides, oxocations stabilise V v as VO 2 + , V IV as VO 2+ and Ti IV as TiO 2+ . The ability of oxygen to stabilise these high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine. Thus the highest Mn fluoride is MnF 4 whereas the highest oxide is Mn 2 O 7 . The ability of oxygen to form multiple bonds to metals explains its superiority. In the covalent oxide Mn 2 O 7 , each Mn is tetrahedrally surrounded by O’s including a Mn–O–Mn bridge. The tetrahedral [MO 4 ] n- ions are known for V V , Cr Vl , Mn V , Mn Vl and Mn VII .','[{"s": "The ability of oxygen to stabilise the highest oxidation state is demonstrated in the oxides. The highest oxidation number in the oxides (Table 8.6) coincides with the group number and is attained in Sc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Beyond Group 7, no higher oxides of Fe above Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", are known, although ferrates (VI)(FeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", are formed in alkaline media but they readily decompose to Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Besides the oxides, oxocations stabilise V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as VO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as VO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Ti", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as TiO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The ability of oxygen to stabilise these high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine. Thus the highest Mn fluoride is MnF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "whereas the highest oxide is Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The ability of oxygen to form multiple bonds to metals explains its superiority. In the covalent oxide Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", each Mn is tetrahedrally surrounded by O’s including a Mn–O–Mn bridge. The tetrahedral [MO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions are known for V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Vl", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Vl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "VII", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8573,41,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.6: Oxides of 3d Metals','[{"s": "Table 8.6: Oxides of 3d Metals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
