(8576,41,108,'paragraph',NULL,'How would you account for the increasing oxidising power in the series VO 2 + < Cr 2 O 7 2– < MnO 4 – ?','[{"s": "How would you account for the increasing oxidising power in the series VO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8577,41,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8578,41,110,'paragraph',NULL,'This is due to the increasing stability of the lower species to which they are reduced.','[{"s": "This is due to the increasing stability of the lower species to which they are reduced.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8579,41,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8580,41,112,'paragraph',NULL,'8.5 How would you account for the irregular variation of ionisation enthalpies (first and second) in the first series of the transition elements?','[{"s": "8.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "How would you account for the irregular variation of ionisation enthalpies (first and second) in the first series of the transition elements?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8581,41,113,'heading',4,'8.3.8 Chemical Reactivity and EƟ Values',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8582,41,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Transition metals vary widely in their chemical reactivity. Many of them are sufficiently electropositive to dissolve in mineral acids, although a few are ‘noble’—that is, they are unaffected by single acids.','[{"s": "Transition metals vary widely in their chemical reactivity. Many of them are sufficiently electropositive to dissolve in mineral acids, although a few are ‘noble’—that is, they are unaffected by single acids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8583,41,115,'paragraph',NULL,'The metals of the first series with the exception of copper are relatively more reactive and are oxidised by 1M H + , though the actual rate at which these metals react with oxidising agents like hydrogen ion (H + ) is sometimes slow. For example, titanium and vanadium, in practice, are passive to dilute non oxidising acids at room temperature. The E Ɵ values for M 2+ /M (Table 8.2) indicate a decreasing tendency to form divalent cations across the series. This general trend towards less negative E Ɵ values is related to the increase in the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpies. It is interesting to note that the E Ɵ values for Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the general trend. Whereas the stabilities of half-filled d subshell ( d 5 ) in Mn 2+ and completely filled d subshell ( d 10 ) in zinc are related to their E e values; for nickel, E Ɵ value is related to the highest negative enthalpy of hydration.','[{"s": "The metals of the first series with the exception of copper are relatively more reactive and are oxidised by 1M H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", though the actual rate at which these metals react with oxidising agents like hydrogen ion (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is sometimes slow. For example, titanium and vanadium, in practice, are passive to dilute non oxidising acids at room temperature. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values for M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M (Table 8.2) indicate a decreasing tendency to form divalent cations across the series. This general trend towards less negative", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values is related to the increase in the sum of the first and second ionisation enthalpies. It is interesting to note that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values for Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the general trend. Whereas the stabilities of half-filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "subshell (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and completely filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "subshell (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in zinc are related to their", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values; for nickel,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "value is related to the highest negative enthalpy of hydration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8584,41,116,'paragraph',NULL,'An examination of the E Ɵ values for the redox couple M 3+ /M 2+ (Table 8.2) shows that Mn 3+ and Co 3+ ions are the strongest oxidising agents in aqueous solutions. The ions Ti 2+ , V 2+ and Cr 2+ are strong reducing agents and will liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid, e.g., 2','[{"s": "An examination of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values for the redox couple M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Table 8.2) shows that Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Co", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions are the strongest oxidising agents in aqueous solutions.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ions Ti", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and Cr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "are strong reducing agents and will liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid, e.g.,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["82483", "82484"]',NULL),
(12317,44,1087,'paragraph',NULL,'t','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12318,44,1088,'paragraph',NULL,'i','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(8585,41,117,'paragraph',NULL,'2 Cr 2+ (aq) + 2 H + (aq) → 2 Cr 3+ (aq) + H 2 (g)','[{"s": "2 Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → 2 Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
