(9386,42,164,'heading',3,'3.4 Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9387,42,165,'paragraph',NULL,'It is necessary to define a few terms before we consider the subject of conductance of electricity through electrolytic solutions. The electrical resistance is represented by the symbol ‘R’ and it is measured in ohm (Ω) which in terms of SI base units is equal to (kg m 2 )/( S 3 A 2 ). It can be measured with the help of a Wheatstone bridge with which you are familiar from your study of physics. The electrical resistance of any object is directly proportional to its length, l , and inversely proportional to its area of cross section, A . That is,','[{"s": "It is necessary to define a few terms before we consider the subject of conductance of electricity through electrolytic solutions. The electrical resistance is represented by the symbol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘R’", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and it is measured in ohm (Ω) which in terms of SI base units is equal to (kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). It can be measured with the help of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wheatstone bridge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with which you are familiar from your study of physics. The electrical resistance of any object is directly proportional to its length,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and inversely proportional to its area of cross section,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". That is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9388,42,166,'paragraph',NULL,'R ∝ I/A or R=ρ I/A (3.17)','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∝ I/A or R=ρ I/A (3.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9389,42,167,'paragraph',NULL,'The constant of proportionality, ρ (Greek, rho), is called resistivity (specific resistance). Its SI units are ohm metre (Ω m) and quite often its submultiple, ohm centimetre (Ω cm) is also used. IUPAC recommends the use of the term resistivity over specific resistance and hence in the rest of the book we shall use the term resistivity. Physically, the resistivity for a substance is its resistance when it is one metre long and its area of cross section is one m 2 . It can be seen that:','[{"s": "The constant of proportionality, ρ (Greek, rho), is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resistivity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(specific resistance). Its SI units are ohm metre (Ω m) and quite often its submultiple, ohm centimetre (Ω cm) is also used. IUPAC recommends the use of the term resistivity over specific resistance and hence in the rest of the book we shall use the term resistivity. Physically, the resistivity for a substance is its resistance when it is one metre long and its area of cross section is one m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It can be seen that:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9390,42,168,'paragraph',NULL,'1 Ω m = 100 Ω cm or 1 Ω cm = 0.01 Ω m','[{"s": "1 Ω m = 100 Ω cm or 1 Ω cm = 0.01 Ω m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9391,42,169,'paragraph',NULL,'The inverse of resistance, R , is called conductance , G , and we have the relation','[{"s": "The inverse of resistance,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conductance", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and we have the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9392,42,170,'paragraph',NULL,'G = 1/R= A/ρl=κ/Al (3.18)','[{"s": "G = 1/R= A/ρl=κ/Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9393,42,171,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit of conductance is siemens, represented by the symbol ‘S’ and is equal to ohm –1 (also known as mho) or Ω –1 . The inverse of resistivity, called conductivity (specific conductance) is represented by the symbol, κ (Greek, kappa). IUPAC has recommended the use of term conductivity over specific conductance and hence we shall use the term conductivity in the rest of the book. The SI units of conductivity are S m –1 but quite often, κ is expressed in S cm –1 . Conductivity of a material in S m –1 is its conductance when it is 1 m long and its area of cross section is 1 m 2 . It may be noted that 1 S cm –1 = 100 S m –1 .','[{"s": "The SI unit of conductance is siemens, represented by the symbol ‘S’ and is equal to ohm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(also known as mho) or Ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The inverse of resistivity, called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conductivity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(specific conductance) is represented by the symbol, κ (Greek, kappa). IUPAC has recommended the use of term conductivity over specific conductance and hence we shall use the term conductivity in the rest of the book. The SI units of conductivity are S m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but quite often, κ is expressed in S cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Conductivity of a material in S m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is its conductance when it is 1 m long and its area of cross section is 1 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It may be noted that 1 S cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 100 S m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
