(12345,44,1115,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9396,42,174,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be seen from Table 3.2 that the magnitude of conductivity varies a great deal and depends on the nature of the material. It also depends on the temperature and pressure at which the measurements are made. Materials are classified into conductors, insulators and semiconductors depending on the magnitude of their conductivity. Metals and their alloys have very large conductivity and are known as conductors. Certain non-metals like carbon-black, graphite and some organic polymers* are also electronically conducting. Substances like glass, ceramics, etc., having very low conductivity are known as insulators. Substances like silicon, doped silicon and gallium arsenide having conductivity between conductors and insulators are called semiconductors and are important electronic materials. Certain materials called superconductors by definition have zero resistivity or infinite conductivity. Earlier, only metals and their alloys at very low temperatures (0 to 15 K) were known to behave as superconductors, but nowadays a number of ceramic materials and mixed oxides are also known to show superconductivity at temperatures as high as 150 K.','[{"s": "It can be seen from Table 3.2 that the magnitude of conductivity varies a great deal and depends on the nature of the material. It also depends on the temperature and pressure at which the measurements are made. Materials are classified into conductors, insulators and semiconductors depending on the magnitude of their conductivity. Metals and their alloys have very large conductivity and are known as conductors. Certain non-metals like carbon-black, graphite and some organic polymers* are also electronically conducting. Substances like glass, ceramics, etc., having very low conductivity are known as insulators. Substances like silicon, doped silicon and gallium arsenide having conductivity between conductors and insulators are called semiconductors and are important electronic materials. Certain materials called superconductors by definition have zero resistivity or infinite conductivity. Earlier, only metals and their alloys at very low temperatures (0 to 15 K) were known to behave as superconductors, but nowadays a number of ceramic materials and mixed oxides are also known to show superconductivity at temperatures as high as 150 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9397,42,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrical conductance through metals is called metallic or electronic conductance and is due to the movement of electrons. The electronic conductance depends on','[{"s": "Electrical conductance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "through metals is called metallic or electronic conductance and is due to the movement of electrons. The electronic conductance depends on", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9398,42,176,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the nature and structure of the metal','[{"s": "(i) the nature and structure of the metal", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9399,42,177,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the number of valence electrons per atom','[{"s": "(ii) the number of valence electrons per atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9400,42,178,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) temperature (it decreases with increase of temperature).','[{"s": "(iii) temperature (it decreases with increase of temperature).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9401,42,179,'paragraph',NULL,'* Electronically conducting polymers – In 1977 MacDiarmid, Heeger and Shirakawa discovered that acetylene gas can be polymerised to produce a polymer, polyacetylene when exposed to vapours of iodine acquires metallic lustre and conductivity. Since then several organic conducting polymers have been made such as polyaniline, polypyrrole and polythiophene. These organic polymers which have properties like metals, being composed wholly of elements like carbon, hydrogen and occasionally nitrogen, oxygen or sulphur, are much lighter than normal metals and can be used for making light-weight batteries. Besides, they have the mechanical properties of polymers such as flexibility so that one can make electronic devices such as transistors that can bend like a sheet of plastic. For the discovery of conducting polymers, MacDiarmid, Heeger and Shirakawa were awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for the year 2000.','[{"s": "* Electronically conducting polymers – In 1977 MacDiarmid, Heeger and Shirakawa discovered that acetylene gas can be polymerised to produce a polymer, polyacetylene when exposed to vapours of iodine acquires metallic lustre and conductivity. Since then several organic conducting polymers have been made such as polyaniline, polypyrrole and polythiophene. These organic polymers which have properties like metals, being composed wholly of elements like carbon, hydrogen and occasionally nitrogen, oxygen or sulphur, are much lighter than normal metals and can be used for making light-weight batteries. Besides, they have the mechanical properties of polymers such as flexibility so that one can make electronic devices such as transistors that can bend like a sheet of plastic. For the discovery of conducting polymers, MacDiarmid, Heeger and Shirakawa were awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for the year 2000.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9402,42,180,'paragraph',NULL,'As the electrons enter at one end and go out through the other end, the composition of the metallic conductor remains unchanged. The mechanism of conductance through semiconductors is more complex.','[{"s": "As the electrons enter at one end and go out through the other end, the composition of the metallic conductor remains unchanged. The mechanism of conductance through semiconductors is more complex.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9403,42,181,'paragraph',NULL,'We already know (Class XI, Unit 7) that even very pure water has small amounts of hydrogen and hydroxyl ions (~10 –7 M) which lend it very low conductivity (3.5 × 10 –5 S m –1 ). When electrolytes are dissolved in water, they furnish their own ions in the solution hence its conductivity also increases. The conductance of electricity by ions present in the solutions is called electrolytic or ionic conductance . The conductivity of electrolytic (ionic) solutions depends on:','[{"s": "We already know (Class XI, Unit 7) that even very pure water has small amounts of hydrogen and hydroxyl ions (~10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M) which lend it very low conductivity (3.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). When electrolytes are dissolved in water, they furnish their own ions in the solution hence its conductivity also increases. The conductance of electricity by ions present in the solutions is called electrolytic or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ionic conductance", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The conductivity of electrolytic (ionic) solutions depends on:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9404,42,182,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the nature of the electrolyte added','[{"s": "(i) the nature of the electrolyte added", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9405,42,183,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) size of the ions produced and their solvation','[{"s": "(ii) size of the ions produced and their solvation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9406,42,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) the nature of the solvent and its viscosity','[{"s": "(iii) the nature of the solvent and its viscosity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9407,42,185,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) concentration of the electrolyte','[{"s": "(iv) concentration of the electrolyte", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9408,42,186,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) temperature (it increases with the increase of temperature).','[{"s": "(v) temperature (it increases with the increase of temperature).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12346,44,1116,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12347,44,1117,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9409,42,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Passage of direct current through ionic solution over a prolonged period can lead to change in its composition due to electrochemical reactions (Section 3.4.1).','[{"s": "Passage of direct current through ionic solution over a prolonged period can lead to change in its composition due to electrochemical reactions (Section 3.4.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9410,42,188,'heading',4,'3.4.1 Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9411,42,189,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that accurate measurement of an unknown resistance can be performed on a Wheatstone bridge. However, for measuring the resistance of an ionic solution we face two problems. Firstly, passing direct current (DC) changes the composition of the solution. Secondly, a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductor. The first difficulty is resolved by using an alternating current (AC) source of power. The second problem is solved by using a specially designed vessel called conductivity cell. It is available in several designs and two simple ones are shown in Fig. 3.4.','[{"s": "We know that accurate measurement of an unknown resistance can be performed on a Wheatstone bridge. However, for measuring the resistance of an ionic solution we face two problems. Firstly, passing direct current (DC) changes the composition of the solution. Secondly, a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductor. The first difficulty is resolved by using an alternating current (AC) source of power. The second problem is solved by using a specially designed vessel called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conductivity cell.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is available in several designs and two simple ones are shown in Fig. 3.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9412,42,190,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Electrochemistry-image9.png'),
(9413,42,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.4 Two different types of conductivity cells.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.4 Two different types of conductivity cells.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9414,42,192,'paragraph',NULL,'Basically it consists of two platinum electrodes coated with platinum black (finely divided metallic Pt is deposited on the electrodes electrochemically). These have area of cross section equal to ‘ A ’ and are separated by distance ‘ l ’. Therefore, solution confined between these electrodes is a column of length l and area of cross section A . The resistance of such a column of solution is then given by the equation:','[{"s": "Basically it consists of two platinum electrodes coated with platinum black (finely divided metallic Pt is deposited on the electrodes electrochemically). These have area of cross section equal to ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’ and are separated by distance ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’. Therefore, solution confined between these electrodes is a column of length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and area of cross section", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The resistance of such a column of solution is then given by the equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9415,42,193,'paragraph',NULL,'R = ρ Electrochemistry-image22.png = Electrochemistry-image38.png (3.17)','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrochemistry-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrochemistry-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9416,42,194,'paragraph',NULL,'The quantity l / A is called cell constant denoted by the symbol, G *. It depends on the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross-section and has the dimension of length –1 and can be calculated if we know l and A . Measurement of l and A is not only inconvenient but also unreliable. The cell constant is usually determined by measuring the resistance of the cell containing a solution whose conductivity is already known. For this purpose, we generally use KCl solutions whose conductivity is known accurately at various concentrations (Table 3.3) and at different temperatures. The cell constant, G *, is then given by the equation:','[{"s": "The quantity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called cell constant denoted by the symbol,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "*. It depends on the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross-section and has the dimension of length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and can be calculated if we know", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Measurement of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not only inconvenient but also unreliable. The cell constant is usually determined by measuring the resistance of the cell containing a solution whose conductivity is already known. For this purpose, we generally use KCl solutions whose conductivity is known accurately at various concentrations (Table 3.3) and at different temperatures. The cell constant,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "*, is then given by the equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9417,42,195,'paragraph',NULL,'G * = Electrochemistry-image22.png = R κ (3.18)','[{"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "* =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrochemistry-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= R κ (3.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
