(9481,42,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Λ m = Λ° m – A c ½ (3.23)','[{"s": "Λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Λ°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "½", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9482,42,260,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be seen that if we plot (Fig. 3.12) Λ m against c 1/2 , we obtain a straight line with intercept equal to Λ° m and slope equal to ‘– A ’. The value of the constant ‘ A ’ for a given solvent and temperature depends on the type of electrolyte i.e., the charges on the cation and anion produced on the dissociation of the electrolyte in the solution. Thus, NaCl, CaCl 2 , MgSO 4 are known as 1-1, 2-1 and 2-2 electrolytes respectively. All electrolytes of a particular type have the same value for ‘ A ’.','[{"s": "It can be seen that if we plot (Fig. 3.12) Λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "against", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we obtain a straight line with intercept equal to Λ°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and slope equal to ‘–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’. The value of the constant ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’ for a given solvent and temperature depends on the type of electrolyte i.e., the charges on the cation and anion produced on the dissociation of the electrolyte in the solution. Thus, NaCl, CaCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", MgSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are known as 1-1, 2-1 and 2-2 electrolytes respectively. All electrolytes of a particular type have the same value for ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
