(9542,42,320,'heading',3,'3.5 Electrolytic Cells and Electrolysis',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9543,42,321,'paragraph',NULL,'In an electrolytic cell external source of voltage is used to bring about a chemical reaction. The electrochemical processes are of great importance in the laboratory and the chemical industry. One of the simplest electrolytic cell consists of two copper strips dipping in an aqueous solution of copper sulphate. If a DC voltage is applied to the two electrodes, then Cu 2+ ions discharge at the cathode (negatively charged) and the following reaction takes place:','[{"s": "In an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrolytic cell", "t": "text"}, {"s": "external source of voltage is used to bring about a chemical reaction. The electrochemical processes are of great importance in the laboratory and the chemical industry. One of the simplest electrolytic cell consists of two copper strips dipping in an aqueous solution of copper sulphate. If a DC voltage is applied to the two electrodes, then Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions discharge at the cathode (negatively charged) and the following reaction takes place:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9544,42,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Cu 2+ (aq) + 2e – → Cu (s) (3.28)','[{"s": "Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ Cu (s) (3.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9545,42,323,'paragraph',NULL,'Copper metal is deposited on the cathode. At the anode, copper is converted into Cu 2+ ions by the reaction:','[{"s": "Copper metal is deposited on the cathode. At the anode, copper is converted into Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions by the reaction:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9546,42,324,'paragraph',NULL,'Cu(s) → Cu 2+ (s) + 2e – (3.29)','[{"s": "Cu(s) → Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9547,42,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus copper is dissolved (oxidised) at anode and deposited (reduced) at cathode. This is the basis for an industrial process in which impure copper is converted into copper of high purity. The impure copper is made an anode that dissolves on passing current and pure copper is deposited at the cathode. Many metals like Na, Mg, Al, etc. are produced on large scale by electrochemical reduction of their respective cations where no suitable chemical reducing agents are available for this purpose.','[{"s": "Thus copper is dissolved (oxidised) at anode and deposited (reduced) at cathode. This is the basis for an industrial process in which impure copper is converted into copper of high purity. The impure copper is made an anode that dissolves on passing current and pure copper is deposited at the cathode. Many metals like Na, Mg, Al, etc. are produced on large scale by electrochemical reduction of their respective cations where no suitable chemical reducing agents are available for this purpose.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9548,42,326,'paragraph',NULL,'Sodium and magnesium metals are produced by the electrolysis of their fused chlorides and aluminium is produced (Class XII, Unit 6) by electrolysis of aluminium oxide in presence of cryolite.','[{"s": "Sodium and magnesium metals are produced by the electrolysis of their fused chlorides and aluminium is produced (Class XII, Unit 6) by electrolysis of aluminium oxide in presence of cryolite.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9549,42,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Quantitative Aspects of Electrolysis','[{"s": "Quantitative Aspects of Electrolysis", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9550,42,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Michael Faraday was the first scientist who described the quantitative aspects of electrolysis. Now Faraday’s laws also flow from what has been discussed earlier.','[{"s": "Michael Faraday", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was the first scientist who described the quantitative aspects of electrolysis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Now Faraday’s laws also flow from what has been discussed earlier.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["171171"]',NULL),
(9551,42,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Faraday’s Laws of Electrolysis','[{"s": "Faraday’s Laws of Electrolysis", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9552,42,330,'paragraph',NULL,'After his extensive investigations on electrolysis of solutions and melts of electrolytes, Faraday published his results during 1833-34 in the form of the following well known Faraday’s two laws of electrolysis: 3','[{"s": "After his extensive investigations on electrolysis of solutions and melts of electrolytes, Faraday published his results during 1833-34 in the form of the following well known Faraday’s two laws of electrolysis:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9554,42,332,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Second Law: The amounts of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity passing through the electrolytic solution are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights (Atomic Mass of Metal ÷ Number of electrons required to reduce the cation)3.','[{"s": "(ii) Second Law: The amounts of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity passing through the electrolytic solution are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights (Atomic Mass of Metal ÷ Number of electrons required to reduce the cation)3.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["171146", "171148", "171151"]',NULL),
(9555,42,333,'paragraph',NULL,'There were no constant current sources available during Faraday’s times. The general practice was to put a coulometer (a standard electrolytic cell) for determining the quantity of electricity passed from the amount of metal (generally silver or copper) deposited or consumed. However, coulometers are now obsolete and we now have constant current ( I ) sources available and the quantity of electricity Q , passed is given by','[{"s": "There were no constant current sources available during Faraday’s times.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The general practice was to put a coulometer (a standard electrolytic cell) for determining the quantity of electricity passed from the amount of metal (generally silver or copper) deposited or consumed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "However, coulometers are now obsolete and we now have constant current (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") sources available and the quantity of electricity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", passed is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9556,42,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = It','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9557,42,335,'paragraph',NULL,'Q is in coloumbs when I is in ampere and t is in second.','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in coloumbs when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in ampere and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in second.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(9558,42,336,'paragraph',NULL,'The amount of electricity (or charge) required for oxidation or reduction depends on the stoichiometry of the electrode reaction. For example, in the reaction:','[{"s": "The amount of electricity (or charge) required for oxidation or reduction depends on the stoichiometry of the electrode reaction. For example, in the reaction:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
