(12377,44,1147,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 22’ Molecules whose mirror image is non-superimposable aver them are','[{"s": "Q. 22’ Molecules whose mirror image is non-superimposable aver them are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12378,44,1148,'paragraph',NULL,'known as chiral. Which of the following molecules is chiral in nature?','[{"s": "known as chiral. Which of the following molecules is chiral in nature?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10057,43,43,'paragraph',NULL,'At equilibrium the pressure exerted by the water molecules at a given temperature remains constant and is called the equilibrium vapour pressure of water (or just vapour pressure of water); vapour pressure of water increases with temperature. If the above experiment is repeated with methyl alcohol, acetone and ether, it is observed that different liquids have different equilibrium vapour pressures at the same temperature, and the liquid which has a higher vapour pressure is more volatile and has a lower boiling point.','[{"s": "At equilibrium the pressure exerted by the water molecules at a given temperature remains constant and is called the equilibrium vapour pressure of water (or just vapour pressure of water); vapour pressure of water increases with temperature. If the above experiment is repeated with methyl alcohol, acetone and ether, it is observed that different liquids have different equilibrium vapour pressures at the same temperature, and the liquid which has a higher vapour pressure is more volatile and has a lower boiling point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10058,43,44,'paragraph',NULL,'If we expose three watch glasses containing separately 1mL each of acetone, ethyl alcohol, and water to atmosphere and repeat the experiment with different volumes of the liquids in a warmer room, it is observed that in all such cases the liquid eventually disappears and the time taken for complete evaporation depends on (i) the nature of the liquid, (ii) the amount of the liquid and (iii) the temperature. When the watch glass is open to the atmosphere, the rate of evaporation remains constant but the molecules are dispersed into large volume of the room. As a consequence the rate of condensation from vapour to liquid state is much less than the rate of evaporation. These are open systems and it is not possible to reach equilibrium in an open system.','[{"s": "If we expose three watch glasses containing separately 1mL each of acetone, ethyl alcohol, and water to atmosphere and repeat the experiment with different volumes of the liquids in a warmer room, it is observed that in all such cases the liquid eventually disappears and the time taken for complete evaporation depends on (i) the nature of the liquid, (ii) the amount of the liquid and (iii) the temperature. When the watch glass is open to the atmosphere, the rate of evaporation remains constant but the molecules are dispersed into large volume of the room. As a consequence the rate of condensation from vapour to liquid state is much less than the rate of evaporation. These are open systems and it is not possible to reach equilibrium in an open system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10059,43,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Water and water vapour are in equilibrium position at atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar) and at 100°C in a closed vessel. The boiling point of water is 100°C at 1.013 bar pressure. For any pure liquid at one atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar), the temperature at which the liquid and vapours are at equilibrium is called normal boiling point of the liquid. Boiling point of the liquid depends on the atmospheric pressure. It depends on the altitude of the place; at high altitude the boiling point decreases.','[{"s": "Water and water vapour are in equilibrium position at atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar) and at 100°C in a closed vessel. The boiling point of water is 100°C at 1.013 bar pressure. For any pure liquid at one atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar), the temperature at which the liquid and vapours are at equilibrium is called normal boiling point of the liquid. Boiling point of the liquid depends on the atmospheric pressure. It depends on the altitude of the place; at high altitude the boiling point decreases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10060,43,46,'heading',4,'7.1.3 Solid – Vapour Equilibrium',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10061,43,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now consider the systems where solids sublime to vapour phase. If we place solid iodine in a closed vessel, after sometime the vessel gets filled up with violet vapour and the intensity of colour increases with time. After certain time the intensity of colour becomes constant and at this stage equilibrium is attained. Hence solid iodine sublimes to give iodine vapour and the iodine vapour condenses to give solid iodine. The equilibrium can be represented as,','[{"s": "Let us now consider the systems where solids sublime to vapour phase. If we place solid iodine in a closed vessel, after sometime the vessel gets filled up with violet vapour and the intensity of colour increases with time. After certain time the intensity of colour becomes constant and at this stage equilibrium is attained. Hence solid iodine sublimes to give iodine vapour and the iodine vapour condenses to give solid iodine. The equilibrium can be represented as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10062,43,48,'paragraph',NULL,'I 2 (solid) I 2 (vapour)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(solid) I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(vapour)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10063,43,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Other examples showing this kind of equilibrium are,','[{"s": "Other examples showing this kind of equilibrium are,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10064,43,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Camphor (solid) ⇋ Camphor (vapour)','[{"s": "Camphor (solid) ⇋ Camphor (vapour)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10065,43,51,'paragraph',NULL,'NH 4 Cl (solid) ⇋ NH 4 Cl (vapour)','[{"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl (solid) ⇋ NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl (vapour)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10066,43,52,'heading',4,'7.1.4 Equilibrium Involving Dissolution of Solid or Gases in Liquids',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10067,43,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Solids in liquids','[{"s": "Solids in liquids", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10068,43,54,'paragraph',NULL,'We know from our experience that we can dissolve only a limited amount of salt or sugar in a given amount of water at room temperature. If we make a thick sugar syrup solution by dissolving sugar at a higher temperature, sugar crystals separate out if we cool the syrup to the room temperature. We call it a saturated solution when no more of solute can be dissolved in it at a given temperature. The concentration of the solute in a saturated solution depends upon the temperature. In a saturated solution, a dynamic equilibrium exits between the solute molecules in the solid state and in the solution:','[{"s": "We know from our experience that we can dissolve only a limited amount of salt or sugar in a given amount of water at room temperature. If we make a thick sugar syrup solution by dissolving sugar at a higher temperature, sugar crystals separate out if we cool the syrup to the room temperature. We call it a saturated solution when no more of solute can be dissolved in it at a given temperature. The concentration of the solute in a saturated solution depends upon the temperature. In a saturated solution, a dynamic equilibrium exits between the solute molecules in the solid state and in the solution:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10069,43,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Sugar (solution) Sugar (solid), and','[{"s": "Sugar (solution) Sugar (solid), and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10070,43,56,'paragraph',NULL,'the rate of dissolution of sugar = rate of crystallisation of sugar.','[{"s": "the rate of dissolution of sugar = rate of crystallisation of sugar.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10071,43,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Equality of the two rates and dynamic nature of equilibrium has been confirmed with the help of radioactive sugar. If we drop some radioactive sugar into saturated solution of non-radioactive sugar, then after some time radioactivity is observed both in the solution and in the solid sugar. Initially there were no radioactive sugar molecules in the solution but due to dynamic nature of equilibrium, there is exchange between the radioactive and non-radioactive sugar molecules between the two phases. The ratio of the radioactive to non-radioactive molecules in the solution increases till it attains a constant value.','[{"s": "Equality of the two rates and dynamic nature of equilibrium has been confirmed with the help of radioactive sugar. If we drop some radioactive sugar into saturated solution of non-radioactive sugar, then after some time radioactivity is observed both in the solution and in the solid sugar. Initially there were no radioactive sugar molecules in the solution but due to dynamic nature of equilibrium, there is exchange between the radioactive and non-radioactive sugar molecules between the two phases. The ratio of the radioactive to non-radioactive molecules in the solution increases till it attains a constant value.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10072,43,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Gases in liquids','[{"s": "Gases in liquids", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10073,43,59,'paragraph',NULL,'When a soda water bottle is opened, some of the carbon dioxide gas dissolved in it fizzes out rapidly. The phenomenon arises due to difference in solubility of carbon dioxide at different pressures. There is equilibrium between the molecules in the gaseous state and the molecules dissolved in the liquid under pressure i.e.,','[{"s": "When a soda water bottle is opened, some of the carbon dioxide gas dissolved in it fizzes out rapidly. The phenomenon arises due to difference in solubility of carbon dioxide at different pressures. There is equilibrium between the molecules in the gaseous state and the molecules dissolved in the liquid under pressure i.e.,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10074,43,60,'paragraph',NULL,'CO 2 (gas) Equilibrium-image1.png CO2 (in solution)','[{"s": "CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(gas)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "CO2 (in solution)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10075,43,61,'paragraph',NULL,'This equilibrium is governed by Henry’s law, which states that the mass of a gas dissolved in a given mass of a solvent at any temperature is proportional to the pressure of the gas above the solvent. This amount decreases with increase of temperature. The soda water bottle is sealed under pressure of gas when its solubility in water is high. As soon as the bottle is opened, some of the dissolved carbon dioxide gas escapes to reach a new equilibrium condition required for the lower pressure, namely its partial pressure in the atmosphere. This is how the soda water in bottle when left open to the air for some time, turns ‘flat’. It can be generalised that:','[{"s": "This equilibrium is governed by Henry’s law,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which states that the mass of a gas dissolved in a given mass of a solvent at any temperature is proportional to the pressure of the gas above the solvent.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This amount decreases with increase of temperature. The soda water bottle is sealed under pressure of gas when its solubility in water is high. As soon as the bottle is opened, some of the dissolved carbon dioxide gas escapes to reach a new equilibrium condition required for the lower pressure, namely its partial pressure in the atmosphere. This is how the soda water in bottle when left open to the air for some time, turns ‘flat’. It can be generalised that:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10076,43,62,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) For solid Equilibrium-image1.png','[{"s": "(i) For solid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10077,43,63,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) For liquid Equilibrium-image1.png vapour equilibrium, the vapour pressure is constant at a given temperature.','[{"s": "(ii) For liquid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "vapour equilibrium, the vapour pressure is constant at a given temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10078,43,64,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) For dissolution of solids in liquids, the solubility is constant at a given temperature.','[{"s": "(iii) For dissolution of solids in liquids, the solubility is constant at a given temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10079,43,65,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) For dissolution of gases in liquids, the concentration of a gas in liquid is proportional to the pressure (concentration) of the gas over the liquid. These observations are summarised in Table 7.1','[{"s": "(iv) For dissolution of gases in liquids, the concentration of a gas in liquid is proportional to the pressure (concentration) of the gas over the liquid. These observations are summarised in Table 7.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10080,43,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.1 Some Features of Physical Equilibria','[{"s": "Table 7.1 Some Features of Physical Equilibria", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10081,43,67,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 18-55-31',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image32.png'),
(10082,43,68,'heading',4,'7.1.5 General Characteristics of Equilibria Involving Physical Processes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10083,43,69,'paragraph',NULL,'For the physical processes discussed above, following characteristics are common to the system at equilibrium:','[{"s": "For the physical processes discussed above, following characteristics are common to the system at equilibrium:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10084,43,70,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature.','[{"s": "(i) Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10085,43,71,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Both the opposing processes occur at the same rate and there is a dynamic but stable condition.','[{"s": "(ii) Both the opposing processes occur at the same rate and there is a dynamic but stable condition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10086,43,72,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) All measurable properties of the system remain constant.','[{"s": "(iii) All measurable properties of the system remain constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10087,43,73,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) When equilibrium is attained for a physical process, it is characterised by constant value of one of its parameters at a given temperature. Table 7.1 lists such quantities.','[{"s": "(iv) When equilibrium is attained for a physical process, it is characterised by constant value of one of its parameters at a given temperature. Table 7.1 lists such quantities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10088,43,74,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) The magnitude of such quantities at any stage indicates the extent to which the physical process has proceeded before reaching equilibrium.','[{"s": "(v) The magnitude of such quantities at any stage indicates the extent to which the physical process has proceeded before reaching equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10089,43,75,'heading',3,'7.2 EQUILIBRIUM IN CHEMICAL PROCESSES – DYNAMIC EQUILIBRIUM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10090,43,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Analogous to the physical systems chemical reactions also attain a state of equilibrium. These reactions can occur both in forward and backward directions. When the rates of the forward and reverse reactions become equal, the concentrations of the reactants and the products remain constant. This is the stage of chemical equilibrium. This equilibrium is dynamic in nature as it consists of a forward reaction in which the reactants give product(s) and reverse reaction in which product(s) gives the original reactants.','[{"s": "Analogous to the physical systems chemical reactions also attain a state of equilibrium. These reactions can occur both in forward and backward directions. When the rates of the forward and reverse reactions become equal, the concentrations of the reactants and the products remain constant. This is the stage of chemical equilibrium. This equilibrium is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dynamic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in nature as it consists of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forward", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction in which the reactants give product(s) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reverse", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction in which product(s) gives the original reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10091,43,77,'paragraph',NULL,'For a better comprehension, let us consider a general case of a reversible reaction,','[{"s": "For a better comprehension, let us consider a general case of a reversible reaction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10092,43,78,'paragraph',NULL,'A + B Equilibrium-image1.png C + D','[{"s": "A + B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "C + D", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10093,43,79,'paragraph',NULL,'With passage of time, there is accumulation of the products C and D and depletion of the reactants A and B (Fig. 7.2). This leads to a decrease in the rate of forward reaction and an increase in he rate of the reverse reaction,','[{"s": "With passage of time, there is accumulation of the products C and D and depletion of the reactants A and B (Fig. 7.2). This leads to a decrease in the rate of forward reaction and an increase in he rate of the reverse reaction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10094,43,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Eventually, the two reactions occur at the same rate and the system reaches a state of equilibrium.','[{"s": "Eventually, the two reactions occur at the same rate and the system reaches a state of equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10095,43,81,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image31.png'),
(10096,43,82,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.2 Attainment of chemical equilibrium.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.2 Attainment of chemical equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10097,43,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, the reaction can reach the state of equilibrium even if we start with only C and D; that is, no A and B being present initially, as the equilibrium can be reached from either direction.','[{"s": "Similarly, the reaction can reach the state of equilibrium even if we start with only C and D; that is, no A and B being present initially, as the equilibrium can be reached from either direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10098,43,84,'paragraph',NULL,'The dynamic nature of chemical equilibrium can be demonstrated in the synthesis of ammonia by Haber’s process. In a series of experiments, Haber started with known amounts of dinitrogen and dihydrogen maintained at high temperature and pressure and at regular intervals determined the amount of ammonia present. He was successful in determining also the concentration of unreacted dihydrogen and dinitrogen. Fig. 7.4 (page 191) shows that after a certain time the composition of the mixture remains the same even though some of the reactants are still present. This constancy in composition indicates that the reaction has reached equilibrium. In order to understand the dynamic nature of the reaction, synthesis of ammonia is carried out with exactly the same starting conditions (of partial pressure and temperature) but using D 2 (deuterium) in place of H 2 . The reaction mixtures starting either with H 2 or D 2 reach equilibrium with the same composition, except that D 2 and ND 3 are present instead of H 2 and NH 3 2 , N 2 , NH 3 and D 2 , N 2 , ND 3 ) are mixed together and left for a while. Later, when this mixture is analysed, it is found that the concentration of ammonia is just the same as before. However, when this mixture is analysed by a mass spectrometer, it is found that ammonia and all deuterium containing forms of ammonia (NH 3 , NH 2 D, NHD 2 and ND 3 2 , HD and D 2 ) are present. Thus one can conclude that scrambling of H and D atoms in the molecules must result from a continuation of the forward and reverse reactions in the mixture. If the reaction had simply stopped when they reached equilibrium, then there would have been no mixing of isotopes in this way.','[{"s": "The dynamic nature of chemical equilibrium can be demonstrated in the synthesis of ammonia by Haber’s process. In a series of experiments, Haber started with known amounts of dinitrogen and dihydrogen maintained at high temperature and pressure and at regular intervals determined the amount of ammonia present. He was successful in determining also the concentration of unreacted dihydrogen and dinitrogen. Fig. 7.4 (page 191) shows that after a certain time the composition of the mixture remains the same even though some of the reactants are still present. This constancy in composition indicates that the reaction has reached equilibrium. In order to understand the dynamic nature of the reaction, synthesis of ammonia is carried out with exactly the same starting conditions (of partial pressure and temperature) but using D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(deuterium) in place of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The reaction mixtures starting either with H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reach equilibrium with the same composition, except that D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ND", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are present instead of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ND", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are mixed together and left for a while. Later, when this mixture is analysed, it is found that the concentration of ammonia is just the same as before. However, when this mixture is analysed by a mass spectrometer, it is found that ammonia and all deuterium containing forms of ammonia (NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D, NHD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ND", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", HD and D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are present. Thus one can conclude that scrambling of H and D atoms in the molecules must result from a continuation of the forward and reverse reactions in the mixture. If the reaction had simply stopped when they reached equilibrium, then there would have been no mixing of isotopes in this way.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10099,43,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Dynamic Equilibrium – A Student’s Activity','[{"s": "Dynamic Equilibrium – A Student’s Activity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10100,43,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Equilibrium whether in a physical or in a chemical system, is always of dynamic nature. This can be demonstrated by the use of radioactive isotopes. This is not feasible in a school laboratory. However this concept can be easily comprehended by performing the following activity. The activity can be performed in a group of 5 or 6 students.','[{"s": "Equilibrium whether in a physical or in a chemical system, is always of dynamic nature. This can be demonstrated by the use of radioactive isotopes. This is not feasible in a school laboratory. However this concept can be easily comprehended by performing the following activity. The activity can be performed in a group of 5 or 6 students.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10101,43,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Take two 100mL measuring cylinders (marked as 1 and 2) and two glass tubes each of 30 cm length. Diameter of the tubes may be same or different in the range of 3-5mm. Fill nearly half of the measuring cylinder-1 with coloured water (for this purpose add a crystal of potassium permanganate to water) and keep second cylinder (number 2) empty.','[{"s": "Take two 100mL measuring cylinders (marked as 1 and 2) and two glass tubes each of 30 cm length. Diameter of the tubes may be same or different in the range of 3-5mm. Fill nearly half of the measuring cylinder-1 with coloured water (for this purpose add a crystal of potassium permanganate to water) and keep second cylinder (number 2) empty.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10102,43,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Put one tube in cylinder 1 and second in cylinder 2. Immerse one tube in cylinder 1, close its upper tip with a finger and transfer the coloured water contained in its lower portion to cylinder 2. Using second tube, kept in 2 nd cylinder, transfer the coloured water in a similar manner from cylinder 2 to cylinder 1. In this way keep on transferring coloured water using the two glass tubes from cylinder 1 to 2 and from 2 to 1 till you notice that the level of coloured water in both the cylinders becomes constant.','[{"s": "Put one tube in cylinder 1 and second in cylinder 2. Immerse one tube in cylinder 1, close its upper tip with a finger and transfer the coloured water contained in its lower portion to cylinder 2. Using second tube, kept in 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cylinder, transfer the coloured water in a similar manner from cylinder 2 to cylinder 1. In this way keep on transferring coloured water using the two glass tubes from cylinder 1 to 2 and from 2 to 1 till you notice that the level of coloured water in both the cylinders becomes constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12379,44,1149,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) 2 -bromobutane (b) 1-bromobutane','[{"s": "(a) 2 -bromobutane (b) 1-bromobutane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10103,43,89,'paragraph',NULL,'If you continue intertransferring coloured solution between the cylinders, there will not be any further change in the levels of coloured water in two cylinders. If we take analogy of ‘level’ of coloured water with ‘concentration’ of reactants and products in the two cylinders, we can say the process of transfer, which continues even after the constancy of level, is indicative of dynamic nature of the process. If we repeat the experiment taking two tubes of different diameters we find that at equilibrium the level of coloured water in two cylinders is different. How far diameters are responsible for change in levels in two cylinders? Empty cylinder (2) is an indicator of no product in it at the beginning.','[{"s": "If you continue intertransferring coloured solution between the cylinders, there will not be any further change in the levels of coloured water in two cylinders. If we take analogy of ‘level’ of coloured water with ‘concentration’ of reactants and products in the two cylinders, we can say the process of transfer, which continues even after the constancy of level, is indicative of dynamic nature of the process. If we repeat the experiment taking two tubes of different diameters we find that at equilibrium the level of coloured water in two cylinders is different. How far diameters are responsible for change in levels in two cylinders? Empty cylinder (2) is an indicator of no product in it at the beginning.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10104,43,90,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image66.png'),
(10105,43,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.7.3 Demonstrating dynamic nature of equilibrium. (a) initial stage (b) final stage after the equilibrium is attained.','[{"s": "Fig.7.3 Demonstrating dynamic nature of equilibrium. (a) initial stage (b) final stage after the equilibrium is attained.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10106,43,92,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image11.png'),
(10107,43,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.4 Depiction of equilibrium for the reaction','[{"s": "Fig 7.4 Depiction of equilibrium for the reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10108,43,94,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 19-00-29',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image33.png'),
(10109,43,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Use of isotope (deuterium) in the formation of ammonia clearly indicates that chemical reactions reach a state of dynamic equilibrium in which the rates of forward and reverse reactions are equal and there is no net change in composition.','[{"s": "Use of isotope (deuterium) in the formation of ammonia clearly indicates that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chemical reactions reach a state of dynamic equilibrium in which the rates of forward and reverse reactions are equal and there is no net change in composition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10110,43,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Equilibrium can be attained from both sides, whether we start reaction by taking, H 2 (g) and N 2 (g) and get NH 3 (g) or by taking NH 3 (g) and decomposing it into N 2 (g) and H 2 (g).','[{"s": "Equilibrium can be attained from both sides, whether we start reaction by taking, H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and get NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) or by taking NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and decomposing it into N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10111,43,97,'paragraph',NULL,'N 2 (g) + 3H 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png 2NH 3 (g)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 3H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10112,43,98,'paragraph',NULL,'2NH 3 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png N 2 (g) + 3H 2 (g)','[{"s": "2NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 3H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10113,43,99,'paragraph',NULL,'2 (g) + I 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png 2HI(g). If we start with equal initial concentration of H 2 and I 2 , the reaction proceeds in the forward direction and the concentration of H 2 and I 2 decreases while that of HI increases, until all of these become constant at equilibrium (Fig. 7.5). We can also start with HI alone and make the reaction to proceed in the reverse direction; the concentration of HI will decrease and concentration of H 2 and I 2 will increase until they all become constant when equilibrium is reached (Fig.7.5). If total number of H and I atoms are same in a given volume, the same equilibrium mixture is obtained whether we start it from pure reactants or pure product.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2HI(g). If we start with equal initial concentration of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reaction proceeds in the forward direction and the concentration of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases while that of HI increases, until all of these become constant at equilibrium (Fig. 7.5). We can also start with HI alone and make the reaction to proceed in the reverse direction; the concentration of HI will decrease and concentration of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will increase until they all become constant when equilibrium is reached (Fig.7.5). If total number of H and I atoms are same in a given volume, the same equilibrium mixture is obtained whether we start it from pure reactants or pure product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
