(10315,43,301,'heading',3,'7.6 APPLICATIONS OF EQUILIBRIUM CONSTANTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10316,43,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Before considering the applications of equilibrium constants, let us summarise the important features of equilibrium constants as follows:','[{"s": "Before considering the applications of equilibrium constants, let us summarise the important features of equilibrium constants as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10317,43,303,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Expression for equilibrium constant is applicable only when concentrations of the reactants and products have attained constant value at equilibrium state.','[{"s": "1. Expression for equilibrium constant is applicable only when concentrations of the reactants and products have attained constant value at equilibrium state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10318,43,304,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The value of equilibrium constant is independent of initial concentrations of the reactants and products.','[{"s": "2. The value of equilibrium constant is independent of initial concentrations of the reactants and products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10319,43,305,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Equilibrium constant is temperature dependent having one unique value for a particular reaction represented by a balanced equation at a given temperature.','[{"s": "3. Equilibrium constant is temperature dependent having one unique value for a particular reaction represented by a balanced equation at a given temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10320,43,306,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The equilibrium constant for the reverse reaction is equal to the inverse of the equilibrium constant for the forward reaction .','[{"s": "4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The equilibrium constant for the reverse reaction is equal to the inverse of the equilibrium constant for the forward reaction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71125", "71129"]',NULL),
(10321,43,307,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The equilibrium constant K for a reaction is related to the equilibrium constant of the corresponding reaction, whose equation is obtained by multiplying or dividing the equation for the original reaction by a small integer.','[{"s": "5. The equilibrium constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a reaction is related to the equilibrium constant of the corresponding reaction, whose equation is obtained by multiplying or dividing the equation for the original reaction by a small integer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10322,43,308,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider applications of equilibrium constant to:','[{"s": "Let us consider applications of equilibrium constant to:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10323,43,309,'paragraph',NULL,'• predict the extent of a reaction on the basis of its magnitude,','[{"s": "• predict the extent of a reaction on the basis of its magnitude,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10324,43,310,'paragraph',NULL,'• predict the direction of the reaction, and','[{"s": "• predict the direction of the reaction, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10325,43,311,'paragraph',NULL,'• calculate equilibrium concentrations.','[{"s": "• calculate equilibrium concentrations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10326,43,312,'heading',4,'7.6.1 Predicting the Extent of a Reaction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10327,43,313,'paragraph',NULL,'The numerical value of the equilibrium constant for a reaction indicates the extent of the reaction . But it is important to note that an equilibrium constant does not give any information about the rate at which the equilibrium is reached. The magnitude of K c or K p is directly proportional to the concentrations of products (as these appear in the numerator of equilibrium constant expression) and inversely proportional to the concentrations of the reactants (these appear in the denominator). This implies that a high value of K is suggestive of a high concentration of products and vice-versa.','[{"s": "The numerical value of the equilibrium constant for a reaction indicates the extent of the reaction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "But it is important to note that an equilibrium constant does not give any information about the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at which the equilibrium is reached. The magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is directly proportional to the concentrations of products (as these appear in the numerator of equilibrium constant expression) and inversely proportional to the concentrations of the reactants (these appear in the denominator). This implies that a high value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is suggestive of a high concentration of products and vice-versa.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71141"]',NULL),
(10328,43,314,'paragraph',NULL,'We can make the following generalisations concerning the composition of equilibrium mixtures:','[{"s": "We can make the following generalisations concerning the composition of equilibrium mixtures:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10329,43,315,'paragraph',NULL,'• If K c > 10 3 , products predominate over reactants, i.e., if K c is very large, the reaction proceeds nearly to completion. Consider the following examples:','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", products predominate over reactants, i.e., if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very large, the reaction proceeds nearly to completion. Consider the following examples:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10330,43,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The reaction of H 2 with O 2 K c = 2.4 × 10 47 .','[{"s": "(a) The reaction of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "47", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10331,43,317,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) H 2 (g) + Cl 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png K c = 4.0 × 10 31 .','[{"s": "(b) H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "31", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10332,43,318,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) H 2 (g) + Br 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png K c = 5.4 × 10 18','[{"s": "(c) H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5.4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "18", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12404,44,1174,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12405,44,1175,'paragraph',NULL,'—','[{"s": "—", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10333,43,319,'paragraph',NULL,'• If K c < 10 –3 , reactants predominate over products, i.e., if K c is very small, the reaction proceeds rarely. Consider the following examples:','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", reactants predominate over products, i.e., if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very small, the reaction proceeds rarely. Consider the following examples:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10334,43,320,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The decomposition of H 2 O into H 2 and O 2 at 500 K has a very small equilibrium constant, K c = 4.1 × 10 –48','[{"s": "(a) The decomposition of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O into H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 500 K has a very small equilibrium constant,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–48", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10335,43,321,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) N 2 (g) + O 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png K c = 4.8 ×10 –31 .','[{"s": "(b) N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.8 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–31", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10336,43,322,'paragraph',NULL,'• If K c is in the range of 10 –3 to 10 3 , appreciable concentrations of both reactants and products are present. Consider the following examples:','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", appreciable concentrations of both reactants and products are present. Consider the following examples:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10337,43,323,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) For reaction of H 2 with I 2 to give HI,','[{"s": "(a) For reaction of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to give HI,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10338,43,324,'paragraph',NULL,'K c = 57.0 at 700K.','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 57.0 at 700K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10339,43,325,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Also, gas phase decomposition of N 2 O 4 to NO 2 is another reaction with a value of K c = 4.64 × 10 –3 at 25°C which is neither too small nor too large. Hence, equilibrium mixtures contain appreciable concentrations of both N 2 O 4 and NO 2 .','[{"s": "(b) Also, gas phase decomposition of N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is another reaction with a value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.64 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 25°C which is neither too small nor too large. Hence, equilibrium mixtures contain appreciable concentrations of both N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10340,43,326,'paragraph',NULL,'These generarlisations are illustrated in Fig. 7.6','[{"s": "These generarlisations are illustrated in Fig. 7.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10341,43,327,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image38.png'),
(10342,43,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.7.6 Dependence of extent of reaction on Kc','[{"s": "Fig.7.6 Dependence of extent of reaction on Kc", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10343,43,329,'heading',4,'7.6.2 Predicting the Direction of the Reaction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10344,43,330,'paragraph',NULL,'The equilibrium constant helps in predicting the direction in which a given reaction will proceed at any stage. For this purpose, we calculate the reaction quotient Q. The reaction quotient, Q ( Q c with molar concentrations and Q P with partial pressures) is defined in the same way as the equilibrium constant K c except that the concentrations in Q c','[{"s": "The equilibrium constant helps in predicting the direction in which a given reaction will proceed at any stage. For this purpose, we calculate the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction quotient", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q. The reaction quotient,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with molar concentrations and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with partial pressures) is defined in the same way as the equilibrium constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "except that the concentrations in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10345,43,331,'paragraph',NULL,'a A + b B Equilibrium-image1.png c C + d D (7.19)','[{"s": "a A + b B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "c C + d D (7.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10346,43,332,'paragraph',NULL,'Q c = [C] c [D] d / [A] a [B] b (7.20)','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [C]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[D]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10347,43,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Then,','[{"s": "Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10348,43,334,'paragraph',NULL,'If Q c > K c , the reaction will proceed in the direction of reactants (reverse reaction).','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reaction will proceed in the direction of reactants (reverse reaction).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10349,43,335,'paragraph',NULL,'If Q c < K c , the reaction will proceed in the direction of the products (forward reaction).','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reaction will proceed in the direction of the products (forward reaction).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12406,44,1176,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12407,44,1177,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10350,43,336,'paragraph',NULL,'If Q c = K c , the reaction mixture is already at equilibrium.','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reaction mixture is already at equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10351,43,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the gaseous reaction of H 2 with I 2 ,','[{"s": "Consider the gaseous reaction of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10352,43,338,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 (g) + I 2 (g) Equilibrium-image1.png 2HI(g); K c = 57.0 at 700 K.','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2HI(g);", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 57.0 at 700 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10353,43,339,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose we have molar concentrations [H 2 ] t =0.10M, [I 2 ] t = 0.20 M and [HI] t = 0.40 M.','[{"s": "Suppose we have molar concentrations [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=0.10M, [I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.20 M and [HI]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.40 M.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10354,43,340,'paragraph',NULL,'(the subscript t on the concentration symbols means that the concentrations were measured at some arbitrary time t, not necessarily at equilibrium).','[{"s": "(the subscript t on the concentration symbols means that the concentrations were measured at some arbitrary time t, not necessarily at equilibrium).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10355,43,341,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the reaction quotient, Q c at this stage of the reaction is given by,','[{"s": "Thus, the reaction quotient,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at this stage of the reaction is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10356,43,342,'paragraph',NULL,'Q c = [HI] t 2 / [H 2 ] t [I 2 ] t = (0.40) 2 / (0.10)×(0.20) = 8.0','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [HI]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (0.40)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ (0.10)×(0.20) = 8.0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10357,43,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, in this case, Q c (8.0) does not equal K c (57.0), so the mixture of H 2 (g), I 2 (g) and HI(g) is not at equilibrium; that is, more H 2 (g) and I 2 (g) will react to form more HI(g) and their concentrations will decrease till Q c = K c .','[{"s": "Now, in this case,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.0) does not equal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(57.0), so the mixture of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g), I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and HI(g) is not at equilibrium; that is, more H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) will react to form more HI(g) and their concentrations will decrease till", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10358,43,344,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction quotient, Q c is useful in predicting the direction of reaction by comparing the values of Q c and K c .','[{"s": "The reaction quotient,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is useful in predicting the direction of reaction by comparing the values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10359,43,345,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, we can make the following generalisations concerning the direction of the reaction (Fig. 7.7) :','[{"s": "Thus, we can make the following generalisations concerning the direction of the reaction (Fig. 7.7) :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10360,43,346,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image21.png'),
(10361,43,347,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.7 Predicting the direction of the reaction','[{"s": "Fig. 7.7 Predicting the direction of the reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10362,43,348,'paragraph',NULL,'• If Q c < K c , net reaction goes from left to right','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", net reaction goes from left to right", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10363,43,349,'paragraph',NULL,'• If Q c > K c , net reaction goes from right to left.','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", net reaction goes from right to left.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10364,43,350,'paragraph',NULL,'• If Q c = K c , no net reaction occurs.','[{"s": "• If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", no net reaction occurs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10365,43,351,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of K c for the reaction','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "The value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10366,43,352,'paragraph',NULL,'2A Equilibrium-image1.png B + C is 2 × 10 –3 –4 M. In which direction the reaction will proceed?','[{"s": "2A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "B + C is 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M. In which direction the reaction will proceed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10367,43,353,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10368,43,354,'paragraph',NULL,'For the reaction the reaction quotient Q c is given by,','[{"s": "For the reaction the reaction quotient", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10369,43,355,'paragraph',NULL,'Q c = [B][C]/ [A] 2','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [B][C]/ [A]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10370,43,356,'paragraph',NULL,'as [A] = [B] = [C] = 3 × 10 –4 M','[{"s": "as [A] = [B] = [C] = 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10371,43,357,'paragraph',NULL,'Q c = (3 ×10 –4 )(3 × 10 –4 ) / (3 ×10 –4 ) 2 = 1','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (3 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") / (3 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10372,43,358,'paragraph',NULL,'as Q c > K c so the reaction will proceed in the reverse direction.','[{"s": "as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "so the reaction will proceed in the reverse direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10373,43,359,'heading',4,'7.6.3 Calculating Equilibrium Concentrations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10374,43,360,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of a problem in which we know the initial concentrations but do not know any of the equilibrium concentrations, the following three steps shall be followed:','[{"s": "In case of a problem in which we know the initial concentrations but do not know any of the equilibrium concentrations, the following three steps shall be followed:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10375,43,361,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1. Write the balanced equation for the reaction.','[{"s": "Step 1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write the balanced equation for the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10376,43,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2. Under the balanced equation, make a table that lists for each substance involved in the reaction:','[{"s": "Step 2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Under the balanced equation, make a table that lists for each substance involved in the reaction:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10377,43,363,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the initial concentration,','[{"s": "(a) the initial concentration,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10378,43,364,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the change in concentration on going to equilibrium, and','[{"s": "(b) the change in concentration on going to equilibrium, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10379,43,365,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) the equilibrium concentration.','[{"s": "(c) the equilibrium concentration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10380,43,366,'paragraph',NULL,'In constructing the table, define x as the concentration (mol/L) of one of the substances that reacts on going to equilibrium, then use the stoichiometry of the reaction to determine the concentrations of the other substances in terms of x.','[{"s": "In constructing the table, define x as the concentration (mol/L) of one of the substances that reacts on going to equilibrium, then use the stoichiometry of the reaction to determine the concentrations of the other substances in terms of x.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10381,43,367,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3. Substitute the equilibrium concentrations into the equilibrium equation for the reaction and solve for x. If you are to solve a quadratic equation choose the mathematical solution that makes chemical sense.','[{"s": "Step 3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Substitute the equilibrium concentrations into the equilibrium equation for the reaction and solve for x. If you are to solve a quadratic equation choose the mathematical solution that makes chemical sense.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10382,43,368,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4. Calculate the equilibrium concentrations from the calculated value of x.','[{"s": "Step 4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Calculate the equilibrium concentrations from the calculated value of x.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10383,43,369,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5. Check your results by substituting them into the equilibrium equation.','[{"s": "Step 5.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Check your results by substituting them into the equilibrium equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
