(10486,43,472,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 19-43-59',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image44.png'),
(10487,43,473,'paragraph',NULL,'A reddish colour appears on adding two drops of 0.002 M potassium thiocynate solution to 1 mL of 0.2 M iron(III) nitrate solution due to the formation of [Fe(SCN)] 2+ . The intensity of the red colour becomes constant on attaining equilibrium. This equilibrium can be shifted in either forward or reverse directions depending on our choice of adding a reactant or a product. The equilibrium can be shifted in the opposite direction by adding reagents that remove Fe 3+ or SCN – ions. For example, oxalic acid (H 2 C 2 O 4 ), reacts with Fe 3+ ions to form the stable complex ion [Fe(C 2 O 4 ) 3 ] 3– , thus decreasing the concentration of free Fe 3+ (aq). In accordance with the Le Chatelier’s principle, the concentration stress of removed Fe 3+ is relieved by dissociation of [Fe(SCN)] 2+ to replenish the Fe 3+ ions. Because the concentration of [Fe(SCN)] 2+ decreases, the intensity of red colour decreases.','[{"s": "A reddish colour appears on adding two drops of 0.002 M potassium thiocynate solution to 1 mL of 0.2 M iron(III) nitrate solution due to the formation of [Fe(SCN)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The intensity of the red colour becomes constant on attaining equilibrium. This equilibrium can be shifted in either forward or reverse directions depending on our choice of adding a reactant or a product. The equilibrium can be shifted in the opposite direction by adding reagents that remove Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions. For example, oxalic acid (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), reacts with Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to form the stable complex ion [Fe(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", thus decreasing the concentration of free Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq). In accordance with the Le Chatelier’s principle, the concentration stress of removed Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is relieved by dissociation of [Fe(SCN)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to replenish the Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions. Because the concentration of [Fe(SCN)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases, the intensity of red colour decreases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10488,43,474,'paragraph',NULL,'Addition of aq. HgCl 2 also decreases red colour because Hg 2+ reacts with SCN – ions to form stable complex ion [Hg(SCN) 4 ] 2– . Removal of free SCN – (aq) shifts the equilibrium in equation (7.24) from right to left to replenish SCN – ions. Addition of potassium thiocyanate on the other hand increases the colour intensity of the solution as it shift the equilibrium to right.','[{"s": "Addition of aq. HgCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also decreases red colour because Hg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reacts with SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to form stable complex ion [Hg(SCN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Removal of free SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) shifts the equilibrium in equation (7.24) from right to left to replenish SCN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions. Addition of potassium thiocyanate on the other hand increases the colour intensity of the solution as it shift the equilibrium to right.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10489,43,475,'heading',4,'7.8.2 Effect of Pressure Change',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10490,43,476,'paragraph',NULL,'A pressure change obtained by changing the volume can affect the yield of products in case of a gaseous reaction where the total number of moles of gaseous reactants and total number of moles of gaseous products are different. In applying Le Chatelier’s principle to a heterogeneous equilibrium the effect of pressure changes on solids and liquids can be ignored because the volume (and concentration) of a solution/liquid is nearly independent of pressure.','[{"s": "A pressure change obtained by changing the volume can affect the yield of products in case of a gaseous reaction where the total number of moles of gaseous reactants and total number of moles of gaseous products are different.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In applying Le Chatelier’s principle to a heterogeneous equilibrium the effect of pressure changes on solids and liquids can be ignored because the volume (and concentration) of a solution/liquid is nearly independent of pressure.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71147"]',NULL),
(10491,43,477,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the reaction,','[{"s": "Consider the reaction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
