(10493,43,479,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, 4 mol of gaseous reactants (CO + 3H 2 ) become 2 mol of gaseous products (CH 4 + H 2 pV = constant). The partial pressure and therefore, concentration of reactants and products have changed and the mixture is no longer at equilibrium. The direction in which the reaction goes to re-establish equilibrium can be predicted by applying the Le Chatelier’s principle. Since pressure has doubled, the equilibrium now shifts in the forward direction, a direction in which the number of moles of the gas or pressure decreases (we know pressure is proportional to moles of the gas). This can also be understood by using reaction quotient, Q c . Let [CO], [H 2 ], [CH 4 ] and [H 2 O] be the molar concentrations at equilibrium for methanation reaction. When volume of the reaction mixture is halved, the partial pressure and the concentration are doubled. We obtain the reaction quotient by replacing each equilibrium concentration by double its value.','[{"s": "Here, 4 mol of gaseous reactants (CO + 3H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") become 2 mol of gaseous products (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "pV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= constant). The partial pressure and therefore, concentration of reactants and products have changed and the mixture is no longer at equilibrium. The direction in which the reaction goes to re-establish equilibrium can be predicted by applying the Le Chatelier’s principle. Since pressure has doubled, the equilibrium now shifts in the forward direction, a direction in which the number of moles of the gas or pressure decreases (we know pressure is proportional to moles of the gas). This can also be understood by using reaction quotient,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let [CO], [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], [CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O] be the molar concentrations at equilibrium for methanation reaction. When volume of the reaction mixture is halved, the partial pressure and the concentration are doubled. We obtain the reaction quotient by replacing each equilibrium concentration by double its value.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10494,43,480,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 19-44-42',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image9.png'),
(10495,43,481,'paragraph',NULL,'As Q c < K c , the reaction proceeds in the forward direction.','[{"s": "As", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reaction proceeds in the forward direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10496,43,482,'paragraph',NULL,'In reaction C(s) + CO 2 (g) 2CO(g), when pressure is increased, the reaction goes in the reverse direction because the number of moles of gas increases in the forward direction.','[{"s": "In reaction C(s) + CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) 2CO(g), when pressure is increased, the reaction goes in the reverse direction because the number of moles of gas increases in the forward direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10497,43,483,'heading',4,'7.8.3 Effect of Inert Gas Addition',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10498,43,484,'paragraph',NULL,'If the volume is kept constant and an inert gas such as argon is added which does not take part in the reaction, the equilibrium remains undisturbed. It is because the addition of an inert gas at constant volume does not change the partial pressures or the molar concentrations of the substance involved in the reaction. The reaction quotient changes only if the added gas is a reactant or product involved in the reaction.','[{"s": "If the volume is kept constant and an inert gas such as argon is added which does not take part in the reaction, the equilibrium remains undisturbed. It is because the addition of an inert gas at constant volume does not change the partial pressures or the molar concentrations of the substance involved in the reaction. The reaction quotient changes only if the added gas is a reactant or product involved in the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10499,43,485,'heading',4,'7.8.4 Effect of Temperature Change',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10500,43,486,'paragraph',NULL,'Whenever an equilibrium is disturbed by a change in the concentration, pressure or volume, the composition of the equilibrium mixture changes because the reaction quotient, Q c no longer equals the equilibrium constant, K c . However, when a change in temperature occurs, the value of equilibrium constant, K c is changed.','[{"s": "Whenever an equilibrium is disturbed by a change in the concentration, pressure or volume, the composition of the equilibrium mixture changes because the reaction quotient,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "no longer equals the equilibrium constant,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However, when a change in temperature occurs, the value of equilibrium constant,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is changed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10501,43,487,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, the temperature dependence of the equilibrium constant depends on the sign of ∆ H for the reaction.','[{"s": "In general, the temperature dependence of the equilibrium constant depends on the sign of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10502,43,488,'paragraph',NULL,'• The equilibrium constant for an exothermic reaction (negative ∆ H ) decreases as the temperature increases.','[{"s": "• The equilibrium constant for an exothermic reaction (negative ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") decreases as the temperature increases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10503,43,489,'paragraph',NULL,'• The equilibrium constant for an endothermic reaction (positive ∆ H ) increases as the temperature increases.','[{"s": "• The equilibrium constant for an endothermic reaction (positive ∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") increases as the temperature increases.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["71152"]',NULL),
(10504,43,490,'paragraph',NULL,'Temperature changes affect the equilibrium constant and rates of reactions.','[{"s": "Temperature changes affect the equilibrium constant and rates of reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
