(10560,43,546,'paragraph',NULL,'Svante Arrhenius','[{"s": "Svante Arrhenius", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10561,43,547,'paragraph',NULL,'(1859-1927)','[{"s": "(1859-1927)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10562,43,548,'paragraph',NULL,'was born near Uppsala, Sweden. He presented his thesis, on the conductivities of electrolyte solutions, to the University of Uppsala in 1884. For the next five years he travelled extensively and visited a number of research centers in Europe. In 1895 he was appointed professor of physics at the newly formed University of Stockholm, serving its rector from 1897 to 1902. From 1905 until his death he was Director of physical chemistry at the Nobel Institute in Stockholm. He continued to work for many years on electrolytic solutions. In 1899 he discussed the temperature dependence of reaction rates on the basis of an equation, now usually known as Arrhenius equation.','[{"s": "was born near Uppsala, Sweden. He presented his thesis, on the conductivities of electrolyte solutions, to the University of Uppsala in 1884. For the next five years he travelled extensively and visited a number of research centers in Europe. In 1895 he was appointed professor of physics at the newly formed University of Stockholm, serving its rector from 1897 to 1902. From 1905 until his death he was Director of physical chemistry at the Nobel Institute in Stockholm. He continued to work for many years on electrolytic solutions. In 1899 he discussed the temperature dependence of reaction rates on the basis of an equation, now usually known as Arrhenius equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10563,43,549,'paragraph',NULL,'He worked in a variety of fields, and made important contributions to immunochemistry, cosmology, the origin of life, and the causes of ice age. He was the first to discuss the ‘green house effect’ calling by that name. He received Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1903 for his theory of electrolytic dissociation and its use in the development of chemistry.','[{"s": "He worked in a variety of fields, and made important contributions to immunochemistry, cosmology, the origin of life, and the causes of ice age. He was the first to discuss the ‘green house effect’ calling by that name. He received Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1903 for his theory of electrolytic dissociation and its use in the development of chemistry.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10564,43,550,'paragraph',NULL,'It is interesting to observe the dual role of water as an acid and a base. In case of reaction with HCl water acts as a base while in case of ammonia it acts as an acid by donating a proton.','[{"s": "It is interesting to observe the dual role of water as an acid and a base. In case of reaction with HCl water acts as a base while in case of ammonia it acts as an acid by donating a proton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10565,43,551,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.12','[{"s": "Problem 7.12", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10566,43,552,'paragraph',NULL,'What will be the conjugate bases for the following Brönsted acids: HF, H 2 SO 4 and HCO 3 – ?','[{"s": "What will be the conjugate bases for the following Brönsted acids: HF, H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10567,43,553,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10568,43,554,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image46.png'),
(12437,44,1207,'paragraph',NULL,'e','[{"s": "e", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12438,44,1208,'paragraph',NULL,'(','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10569,43,555,'paragraph',NULL,'The conjugate bases should have one proton less in each case and therefore the corresponding conjugate bases are: F – , HSO 4 – and CO 3 2– respectively.','[{"s": "The conjugate bases should have one proton less in each case and therefore the corresponding conjugate bases are: F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", HSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10570,43,556,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.13','[{"s": "Problem 7.13", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10571,43,557,'paragraph',NULL,'Write the conjugate acids for the following Brönsted bases: NH 2 – , NH 3 and HCOO – .','[{"s": "Write the conjugate acids for the following Brönsted bases: NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HCOO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10572,43,558,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10573,43,559,'paragraph',NULL,'The conjugate acid should have one extra proton in each case and therefore the corresponding conjugate acids are: NH 3 , NH 4 + and HCOOH respectively.','[{"s": "The conjugate acid should have one extra proton in each case and therefore the corresponding conjugate acids are: NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HCOOH respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10574,43,560,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.14','[{"s": "Problem 7.14", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10575,43,561,'paragraph',NULL,'The species: H 2 O, HCO 3 – , HSO 4 – and NH 3 can act both as Bronsted acids and bases. For each case give the corresponding conjugate acid and conjugate base.','[{"s": "The species: H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O, HCO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", HSO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "can act both as Bronsted acids and bases.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For each case give the corresponding conjugate acid and conjugate base.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71158"]',NULL),
(10576,43,562,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10577,43,563,'paragraph',NULL,'The answer is given in the following Table:','[{"s": "The answer is given in the following Table:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10578,43,564,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-22-54',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image69.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(10579,43,565,'heading',4,'7.10.3 Lewis Acids and Bases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10580,43,566,'paragraph',NULL,'G.N. Lewis in 1923 defined an acid as a species which accpets electron pair and base which donates an electron pair. As far as bases are concerned, there is not much difference between Brönsted-Lowry and Lewis concepts, as the base provides a lone pair in both the cases. However, in Lewis concept many acids do not have proton. A typical example is reaction of electron deficient species BF 3 with NH 3 .','[{"s": "G.N. Lewis in 1923 defined an acid as a species which accpets electron pair and base which donates an electron pair.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "As far as bases are concerned, there is not much difference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between Brönsted-Lowry and Lewis concepts, as the base provides a lone pair in both the cases. However, in Lewis concept many acids do not have proton. A typical example is reaction of electron deficient species BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71239"]',NULL),
(10581,43,567,'paragraph',NULL,'BF 3 does not have a proton but still acts as an acid and reacts with NH 3 by accepting its lone pair of electron s. The reaction can be represented by,','[{"s": "BF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "does not have a proton but still acts as an acid and reacts with NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "by accepting its lone pair of electron", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "s.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The reaction can be represented by,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71164"]',NULL),
(10582,43,568,'paragraph',NULL,'BF 3 + :NH 3 → BF 3 :NH 3','[{"s": "BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ :NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10583,43,569,'paragraph',NULL,'Electron deficient species like AlCl 3 , Co 3+ , Mg 2+ , etc. can act as Lewis acids while species like H 2 O, NH 3 , OH – etc. which can donate a pair of electrons, can act as Lewis bases. 3','[{"s": "Electron deficient species like AlCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Co", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can act as Lewis acids while species like H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O, NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", OH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "etc. which can donate a pair of electrons, can act as Lewis bases.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["71160"]',NULL),
(10584,43,570,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.15','[{"s": "Problem 7.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10585,43,571,'paragraph',NULL,'Classify the following species into Lewis acids and Lewis bases and show how these act as such:','[{"s": "Classify the following species into Lewis acids and Lewis bases and show how these act as such:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10586,43,572,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) HO – (b)F – (c) H + (d) BCl 3','[{"s": "(a) HO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b)F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(c) H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(d) BCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10587,43,573,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10588,43,574,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Hydroxyl ion is a Lewis base as it can donate an electron lone pair (:OH – ).','[{"s": "(a) Hydroxyl ion is a Lewis base as it can donate an electron lone pair (:OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12439,44,1209,'paragraph',NULL,'i','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12440,44,1210,'paragraph',NULL,')','[{"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10589,43,575,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Flouride ion acts as a Lewis base as it can donate any one of its four electron lone pairs.','[{"s": "(b) Flouride ion acts as a Lewis base as it can donate any one of its four electron lone pairs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10590,43,576,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A proton is a Lewis acid as it can accept a lone pair of electrons from bases like hydroxyl ion and fluoride ion.','[{"s": "(c) A proton is a Lewis acid as it can accept a lone pair of electrons from bases like hydroxyl ion and fluoride ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10591,43,577,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) BCl 3 acts as a Lewis acid as it can accept a lone pair of electrons from species like ammonia or amine molecules.','[{"s": "(d) BCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acts as a Lewis acid as it can accept a lone pair of electrons from species like ammonia or amine molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10592,43,578,'heading',3,'7.11 IONIZATION OF ACIDS AND BASES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10593,43,579,'paragraph',NULL,'Arrhenius concept of acids and bases becomes useful in case of ionization of acids and bases as mostly ionizations in chemical and biological systems occur in aqueous medium. Strong acids like perchloric acid (HClO 4 ), hydrochloric acid (HCl), hydrobromic acid (HBr), hyrdoiodic acid (HI), nitric acid (HNO 3 ) and sulphuric acid (H 2 SO 4 ) are termed strong because they are almost completely dissociated into their constituent ions in an aqueous medium, thereby acting as proton (H + ) donors. Similarly, strong bases like lithium hydroxide (LiOH), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), potassium hydroxide (KOH), caesium hydroxide (CsOH) and barium hydroxide Ba(OH) 2 are almost completely dissociated into ions in an aqueous medium giving hydroxyl ions, OH – . According to Arrhenius concept they are strong acids and bases as they are able to completely dissociate and produce H 3 O + and OH – ions respectively in the medium. Alternatively, the strength of an acid or base may also be gauged in terms of Brönsted-Lowry concept of acids and bases, wherein a strong acid means a good proton donor and a strong base implies a good proton acceptor. Consider, the acid-base dissociation equilibrium of a weak acid HA,','[{"s": "Arrhenius concept of acids and bases becomes useful in case of ionization of acids and bases as mostly ionizations in chemical and biological systems occur in aqueous medium.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Strong acids like perchloric acid (HClO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "), hydrochloric acid (HCl), hydrobromic acid (HBr), hyrdoiodic acid (HI), nitric acid (HNO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and sulphuric acid (H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") are termed", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "strong", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "because they are almost completely dissociated into their constituent ions in an aqueous medium, thereby acting as proton (H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") donors.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Similarly, strong bases like lithium hydroxide (LiOH), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), potassium hydroxide (KOH), caesium hydroxide (CsOH) and barium hydroxide Ba(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are almost completely dissociated into ions in an aqueous medium giving hydroxyl ions, OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to Arrhenius concept they are strong acids and bases as they are able to completely dissociate and produce H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions respectively in the medium. Alternatively, the strength of an acid or base may also be gauged in terms of Brönsted-Lowry concept of acids and bases, wherein a strong acid means a good proton donor and a strong base implies a good proton acceptor. Consider, the acid-base dissociation equilibrium of a weak acid HA,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71240"]',NULL),
(10594,43,580,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-24-14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image8.png'),
(10595,43,581,'paragraph',NULL,'In section 7.10.2 we saw that acid (or base) dissociation equilibrium is dynamic involving a transfer of proton in forward and reverse directions. Now, the question arises that if the equilibrium is dynamic then with passage of time which direction is favoured? What is the driving force behind it? In order to answer these questions we shall deal into the issue of comparing the strengths of the two acids (or bases) involved in the dissociation equilibrium. Consider the two acids HA and H 3 O + present in the above mentioned acid-dissociation equilibrium. We have to see which amongst them is a stronger proton donor. Whichever exceeds in its tendency of donating a proton over the other shall be termed as the stronger acid and the equilibrium will shift in the direction of weaker acid. Say, if HA is a stronger acid than H 3 O + , then HA will donate protons and not H 3 O + , and the solution will mainly contain A – and H 3 O + ions. The equilibrium moves in the direction of formation of weaker acid and weaker base because the stronger acid donates a proton to the stronger base .','[{"s": "In section 7.10.2 we saw that acid (or base) dissociation equilibrium is dynamic involving a transfer of proton in forward and reverse directions. Now, the question arises that if the equilibrium is dynamic then with passage of time which direction is favoured? What is the driving force behind it? In order to answer these questions we shall deal into the issue of comparing the strengths of the two acids (or bases) involved in the dissociation equilibrium. Consider the two acids HA and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "present in the above mentioned acid-dissociation equilibrium. We have to see which amongst them is a stronger proton donor. Whichever exceeds in its tendency of donating a proton over the other shall be termed as the stronger acid and the equilibrium will shift in the direction of weaker acid. Say, if HA is a stronger acid than H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then HA will donate protons and not H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and the solution will mainly contain A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions. The equilibrium moves in the direction of formation of weaker acid and weaker base because", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the stronger acid donates a proton to the stronger base", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10596,43,582,'paragraph',NULL,'It follows that as a strong acid dissociates completely in water, the resulting base formed would be very weak i.e., strong acids have very weak conjugate bases. Strong acids like perchloric acid (HClO 4 ), hydrochloric acid (HCl), hydrobromic acid (HBr), hydroiodic acid (HI), nitric acid (HNO 3 ) and sulphuric acid (H 2 SO 4 ) will give conjugate base ions ClO 4 – , Cl, Br – , I – , NO 3 – and HSO 4 – , which are much weaker bases than H 2 O. Similarly a very strong base would give a very weak conjugate acid. On the other hand, a weak acid say HA is only partially dissociated in aqueous medium and thus, the solution mainly contains undissociated HA molecules. Typical weak acids are nitrous acid (HNO 2 ), hydrofluoric acid (HF) and acetic acid (CH 3 COOH). It should be noted that the weak acids have very strong conjugate bases . For example, NH 2 – , O 2– and H – are very good proton acceptors and thus, much stronger bases than H 2 O.','[{"s": "It follows that as a strong acid dissociates completely in water, the resulting base formed would be very weak i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "strong acids have very weak conjugate bases.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Strong acids like perchloric acid (HClO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), hydrochloric acid (HCl), hydrobromic acid (HBr), hydroiodic acid (HI), nitric acid (HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and sulphuric acid (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") will give conjugate base ions ClO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cl, Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which are much weaker bases than H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O. Similarly a very strong base would give a very weak conjugate acid. On the other hand, a weak acid say HA is only partially dissociated in aqueous medium and thus, the solution mainly contains undissociated HA molecules. Typical weak acids are nitrous acid (HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), hydrofluoric acid (HF) and acetic acid (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "COOH). It should be noted that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "weak acids have very strong conjugate bases", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are very good proton acceptors and thus, much stronger bases than H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10597,43,583,'paragraph',NULL,'Certain water soluble organic compounds like phenolphthalein and bromothymol blue behave as weak acids and exhibit different colours in their acid (HIn) and conjugate base (In – ) forms.','[{"s": "Certain water soluble organic compounds like phenolphthalein and bromothymol blue behave as weak acids and exhibit different colours in their acid (HIn) and conjugate base (In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") forms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10598,43,584,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-33-07',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image5.png'),
(12441,44,1211,'paragraph',NULL,'B','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12442,44,1212,'paragraph',NULL,'r','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10599,43,585,'paragraph',NULL,'Such compounds are useful as indicators in acid-base titrations, and finding out H + ion concentration.','[{"s": "Such compounds are useful as indicators in acid-base titrations, and finding out H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion concentration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10600,43,586,'heading',4,'7.11.1 The Ionization Constant of Water and its Ionic Product',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10601,43,587,'paragraph',NULL,'Some substances like water are unique in their ability of acting both as an acid and a base. We have seen this in case of water in section 7.10.2. In presence of an acid, HA it accepts a proton and acts as the base while in the presence of a base, B – it acts as an acid by donating a proton. In pure water, one H 2 O molecule donates proton and acts as an acid and another water molecules accepts a proton and acts as a base at the same time. The following equilibrium exists:','[{"s": "Some substances like water are unique in their ability of acting both as an acid and a base. We have seen this in case of water in section 7.10.2. In presence of an acid, HA it accepts a proton and acts as the base while in the presence of a base, B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it acts as an acid by donating a proton. In pure water, one H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecule donates proton and acts as an acid and another water molecules accepts a proton and acts as a base at the same time. The following equilibrium exists:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10602,43,588,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-35-51',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image71.png'),
(10603,43,589,'paragraph',NULL,'The dissociation constant is represented by,','[{"s": "The dissociation constant is represented by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10604,43,590,'paragraph',NULL,'K = [H 3 O + ] [OH – ] / [H 2 O] (7.26)','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] / [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O] (7.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10605,43,591,'paragraph',NULL,'The concentration of water is omitted from the denominator as water is a pure liquid and its concentration remains constant. [H 2 O] is incorporated within the equilibrium constant to give a new constant, K w , which is called the ionic product of water .','[{"s": "The concentration of water is omitted from the denominator as water is a pure liquid and its concentration remains constant. [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O] is incorporated within the equilibrium constant to give a new constant,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ionic product of water", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10606,43,592,'paragraph',NULL,'K w = [H + ][OH – ] (7.27)','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "][OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (7.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10607,43,593,'paragraph',NULL,'The concentration of H + has been found out experimentally as 1.0 × 10 –7 M at 298 K. And, as dissociation of water produces equal number of H + and OH – ions, the concentration of hydroxyl ions, [OH – ] = [H + ] = 1.0 × 10 –7 M. Thus, the value of K w at 298K,','[{"s": "The concentration of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has been found out experimentally as 1.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M at 298 K. And, as dissociation of water produces equal number of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions, the concentration of hydroxyl ions, [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = 1.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M. Thus, the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 298K,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10608,43,594,'paragraph',NULL,'K w = [H 3 O + ][OH – ] = (1 × 10 –7 ) 2 = 1 × 10 –14 M 2 (7.28)','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "][OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = (1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10609,43,595,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of K w is temperature dependent as it is an equilibrium constant.','[{"s": "The value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is temperature dependent as it is an equilibrium constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10610,43,596,'paragraph',NULL,'The density of pure water is 1000 g / L and its molar mass is 18.0 g /mol. From this the molarity of pure water can be given as,','[{"s": "The density of pure water is 1000 g / L and its molar mass is 18.0 g /mol. From this the molarity of pure water can be given as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10611,43,597,'paragraph',NULL,'[H 2 O] = (1000 g /L)(1 mol/18.0 g) = 55.55 M.','[{"s": "[H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O] = (1000 g /L)(1 mol/18.0 g) = 55.55 M.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10612,43,598,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, the ratio of dissociated water to that of undissociated water can be given as:','[{"s": "Therefore, the ratio of dissociated water to that of undissociated water can be given as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10613,43,599,'paragraph',NULL,'10 –7 / (55.55) = 1.8 × 10 –9 or ~ 2 in 10 –9 (thus, equilibrium lies mainly towards undissociated water)','[{"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ (55.55) = 1.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or ~ 2 in 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(thus, equilibrium lies mainly towards undissociated water)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10614,43,600,'paragraph',NULL,'We can distinguish acidic, neutral and basic aqueous solutions by the relative values of the H 3 O + and OH – concentrations:','[{"s": "We can distinguish acidic, neutral and basic aqueous solutions by the relative values of the H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "concentrations:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["71177"]',NULL),
(12443,44,1213,'paragraph',NULL,'(','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10615,43,601,'paragraph',NULL,'Acidic: [H 3 O + ] > [OH – ]','[{"s": "Acidic: [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] > [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10616,43,602,'paragraph',NULL,'Neutral: [H 3 O + ] = [OH – ]','[{"s": "Neutral: [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10617,43,603,'paragraph',NULL,'Basic : [H 3 O + ] < [OH – ]','[{"s": "Basic : [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] < [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10618,43,604,'heading',4,'7.11.2 The pH Scale',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10619,43,605,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydronium ion concentration in molarity is more conveniently expressed on a logarithmic scale known as the pH scale . The pH of a solution is defined as the negative logarithm to base 10 of the activity Equilibrium-image36.png of hydrogen ion. In dilute solutions (< 0.01 M), activity of hydrogen ion (H + ) is equal in magnitude to molarity represented by [H + ]. It should be noted that activity has no units and is defined as:','[{"s": "Hydronium ion concentration in molarity is more conveniently expressed on a logarithmic scale known as the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "pH scale", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The pH of a solution is defined as the negative logarithm to base 10 of the activity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equilibrium-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 20-36-44"}, {"s": "of hydrogen ion. In dilute solutions (< 0.01 M), activity of hydrogen ion (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is equal in magnitude to molarity represented by [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. It should be noted that activity has no units and is defined as:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71187"]',NULL),
(10620,43,606,'paragraph',NULL,'Equilibrium-image16.png = [H + ] / mol L –1','[{"s": "Equilibrium-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] / mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10621,43,607,'paragraph',NULL,'From the definition of pH, the following can be written,','[{"s": "From the definition of pH, the following can be written,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10622,43,608,'paragraph',NULL,'pH = – log a H+ = – log {[H + ] / mol L –1 }','[{"s": "pH = – log a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – log {[H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] / mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10623,43,609,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, an acidic solution of HCl (10 –2 M) will have a pH = 2. Similarly, a basic solution of NaOH having [OH – ] =10 –4 M and [H 3 O + –10 [H + ] = 10 –7 M. Hence, the pH of pure water is given as:','[{"s": "Thus, an acidic solution of HCl (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M) will have a pH = 2. Similarly, a basic solution of NaOH having [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] =10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M and [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "[H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "] = 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "M. Hence, the pH of pure water is given as:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["71180"]',NULL),
(10624,43,610,'paragraph',NULL,'pH = –log(10 –7 ) = 7','[{"s": "pH = –log(10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10625,43,611,'paragraph',NULL,'Acidic solutions possess a concentration of hydrogen ions, [H + ] > 10 –7 M, while basic solutions possess a concentration of hydrogen ions, [H + ] < 10 –7 M. thus, we can summarise that','[{"s": "Acidic solutions possess a concentration of hydrogen ions, [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] > 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M, while basic solutions possess a concentration of hydrogen ions, [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] < 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M. thus, we can summarise that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10626,43,612,'paragraph',NULL,'Acidic solution has pH < 7','[{"s": "Acidic solution has pH < 7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10627,43,613,'paragraph',NULL,'Basic solution has pH > 7','[{"s": "Basic solution has pH > 7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10628,43,614,'paragraph',NULL,'Neutral solution has pH = 7','[{"s": "Neutral solution has pH = 7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10629,43,615,'paragraph',NULL,'Now again, consider the equation (7.28) at 298 K','[{"s": "Now again, consider the equation (7.28) at 298 K", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10630,43,616,'paragraph',NULL,'K w = [H 3 O + ] [OH – ] = 10 –14','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10631,43,617,'paragraph',NULL,'Taking negative logarithm on both sides of equation, we obtain','[{"s": "Taking negative logarithm on both sides of equation, we obtain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10632,43,618,'paragraph',NULL,'–log K w = – log {[H 3 O + ] [OH – ]}','[{"s": "–log", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – log {[H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]}", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10633,43,619,'paragraph',NULL,'= – log [H 3 O + ] – log [OH – ]','[{"s": "= – log [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] – log [OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10634,43,620,'paragraph',NULL,'= – log 10 –14','[{"s": "= – log 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10635,43,621,'paragraph',NULL,'p K w = pH + pOH = 14 (7.29)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= pH + pOH = 14 (7.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10636,43,622,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that although K w may change with temperature the variations in pH with temperature are so small that we often ignore it.','[{"s": "Note that although", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may change with temperature the variations in pH with temperature are so small that we often ignore it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10637,43,623,'paragraph',NULL,'p K w is a very important quantity for aqueous solutions and controls the relative concentrations of hydrogen and hydroxyl ions as their product is a constant. It should be noted that as the pH scale is logarithmic, a change in pH by just one unit also means change in [H + ] by a factor of 10. Similarly, when the hydrogen ion concentration, [H + ] changes by a factor of 100, the value of pH changes by 2 units. Now you can realise why the change in pH with temperature is often ignored.','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a very important quantity for aqueous solutions and controls the relative concentrations of hydrogen and hydroxyl ions as their product is a constant. It should be noted that as the pH scale is logarithmic, a change in pH by just one unit also means change in [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] by a factor of 10. Similarly, when the hydrogen ion concentration, [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] changes by a factor of 100, the value of pH changes by 2 units. Now you can realise why the change in pH with temperature is often ignored.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10638,43,624,'paragraph',NULL,'Measurement of pH of a solution is very essential as its value should be known when dealing with biological and cosmetic applications. The pH of a solution can be found roughly with the help of pH paper that has different colour in solutions of different pH. Now-a-days pH paper is available with four strips on it. The different strips have different colours (Fig. 7.11) at the same pH. The pH in the range of 1-14 can be determined with an accuracy of ~0.5 using pH paper.','[{"s": "Measurement of pH of a solution is very essential as its value should be known when dealing with biological and cosmetic applications. The pH of a solution can be found roughly with the help of pH paper that has different colour in solutions of different pH. Now-a-days pH paper is available with four strips on it. The different strips have different colours (Fig. 7.11) at the same pH. The pH in the range of 1-14 can be determined with an accuracy of ~0.5 using pH paper.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10639,43,625,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Equilibrium-image57.png'),
(10640,43,626,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.7.11 pH-paper with four strips that may have different colours at the same pH','[{"s": "Fig.7.11 pH-paper with four strips that may have different colours at the same pH", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10641,43,627,'paragraph',NULL,'For greater accuracy pH meters are used. pH meter is a device that measures the pH-dependent electrical potential of the test solution within 0.001 precision. pH meters of the size of a writing pen are now available in the market. The pH of some very common substances are given in Table 7.5 (page 212).','[{"s": "For greater accuracy pH meters are used. pH meter is a device that measures the pH-dependent electrical potential of the test solution within 0.001 precision.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pH meters of the size of a writing pen are now available in the market.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The pH of some very common substances are given in Table 7.5 (page 212).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["71174"]',NULL),
(10642,43,628,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.16','[{"s": "Problem 7.16", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10643,43,629,'paragraph',NULL,'The concentration of hydrogen ion in a sample of soft drink is 3.8 × 10 –3 M. what is its pH ?','[{"s": "The concentration of hydrogen ion in a sample of soft drink is 3.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M. what is its pH ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10644,43,630,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10645,43,631,'paragraph',NULL,'pH = – log[3.8 × 10 –3 ]','[{"s": "pH = – log[3.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10646,43,632,'paragraph',NULL,'= – {log[3.8] + log[10 –3 ]}','[{"s": "= – {log[3.8] + log[10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]}", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10647,43,633,'paragraph',NULL,'= – {(0.58) + (– 3.0)} = – { – 2.42} = 2.42','[{"s": "= – {(0.58) + (– 3.0)} = – { – 2.42} = 2.42", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10648,43,634,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, the pH of the soft drink is 2.42 and it can be inferred that it is acidic.','[{"s": "Therefore, the pH of the soft drink is 2.42 and it can be inferred that it is acidic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10649,43,635,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 7.17','[{"s": "Problem 7.17", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
